	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Pipidae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101175255">
	<b:glossaryEntry>South American toad; incubates its young in pits in the skin of its back</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101175415">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in some classifications the family of the genus Xenopus which is otherwise included in the family Pipidae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101175599">
	<b:glossaryEntry>African clawed frog; in some classifications made the type genus of a separate family Xenopodidae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101175804">
	<b:glossaryEntry>native to Africa; established in the United States as result of release of laboratory and aquarium animals</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101176012"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a South American toad"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101176106">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an order of amphibians including caecilians (in former classifications it was called order Apoda)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101176319"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coextensive with the order Gymnophiona: legless amphibians"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101176494">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of the small slender limbless burrowing wormlike amphibians of the order Gymnophiona; inhabit moist soil in tropical regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101176709">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extinct amphibians typically resembling heavy-bodied salamanders or crocodiles and having a solid flattened skull and conical teeth; Devonian through Triassic</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101177083"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an amphibian of the superorder Labyrinthodontia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101177210">
	<b:glossaryEntry>formerly a suborder of Stegocephalia; amphibia having vertebrae whose component elements are fused into a single piece: "most vertebrates are stereospondylous"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101177472">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in former classifications a division of Amphibia comprising all pre-Jurassic and some later extinct large salamandriform amphibia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101177702">
	<b:glossaryEntry>formerly a suborder of Stegocephalia; large Carboniferous and Permian amphibians having vertebrae in which some elements remain separate</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101177939"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a family of reptiles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101178849"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of reptiles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101181845">
	<b:glossaryEntry>class of cold-blooded air-breathing vertebrates with completely ossified skeleton and a body usually covered with scales or horny plates; once the dominant land animals</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101182199">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any cold-blooded vertebrate of the class Reptilia including tortoises turtles snakes lizards alligators crocodiles and extinct forms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101182488"
	 b:glossaryEntry="oldest known reptiles: turtles; extinct Permian forms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101182673">
	<b:glossaryEntry>primitive reptile having no opening in the temporal region of the skull; all extinct except turtles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101182899">
	<b:glossaryEntry>reptile having a pair of openings in the skull behind each eye</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101183141">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used in former classifications to include all living reptiles except turtles; superseded by the two subclasses Lepidosauria and Archosauria</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101183355"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tortoises and turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101183685"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a reptile of the order Chelonia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101183829">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various aquatic and land reptiles having a bony shell and flipper-like limbs for swimming</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101184195"
	 b:glossaryEntry="green turtles; hawksbills"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101184427">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various large turtles with limbs modified into flippers; widely distributed in warm seas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101184685"
	 b:glossaryEntry="green turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101184808">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large tropical turtle with greenish flesh used for turtle soup</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101184965"
	 b:glossaryEntry="loggerhead turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101185091">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very large carnivorous sea turtle; wide ranging in warm open seas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101185270"
	 b:glossaryEntry="ridleys"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101185395"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a marine turtle"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101185518">
	<b:glossaryEntry>gray sea turtle of the Atlantic and Gulf coasts of North America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101185700">
	<b:glossaryEntry>olive-colored sea turtle of tropical Pacific and Indian and South Atlantic oceans</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101185881"
	 b:glossaryEntry="hawksbills"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101186009">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pugnacious tropical sea turtle with a hawk-like beak; source of food and the best tortoiseshell</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101186257"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sea turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101186391"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Dermochelyidae: leatherback turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101186559">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wide ranging marine turtle with flexible leathery carapace; largest living turtle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101186779"
	 b:glossaryEntry="snapping turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101186950"
	 b:glossaryEntry="large aggressive freshwater turtle with powerful jaws"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101187120"
	 b:glossaryEntry="snapping turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101187246">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large-headed turtle with powerful hooked jaws found in or near water; prone to bite</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101187449"
	 b:glossaryEntry="alligator snapping turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101187591">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large species having three ridges on its back; found in southeastern United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101187808"
	 b:glossaryEntry="mud turtles; musk turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101187973"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Kinosternidae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101188120">
	<b:glossaryEntry>bottom-dwelling freshwater turtle inhabiting muddy rivers of North and Central America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101188300"
	 b:glossaryEntry="musk turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101188430"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small freshwater turtle having a strong musky odor"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101188568"
	 b:glossaryEntry="box and water turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101188776">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various edible North American web-footed turtles living in fresh or brackish water</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101188957"
	 b:glossaryEntry="American terrapins"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101189089"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of marshes along Atlantic and Gulf coasts of United States"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101189255"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sliders; red-bellied terrapin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101189434">
	<b:glossaryEntry>freshwater turtle of Chesapeake bay tributaries having red markings on the lower shell</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101189663">
	<b:glossaryEntry>freshwater turtle of United States and South America; frequently raised commercially; some young sold as pets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101189890">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large river turtle of the southern United States and northern Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101190066"
	 b:glossaryEntry="box turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101190189">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chiefly terrestrial turtle of North America; shell can be closed tightly</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101190370">
	<b:glossaryEntry>primarily a prairie turtle of western United States and northern Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101190525"
	 b:glossaryEntry="painted turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101190652">
	<b:glossaryEntry>freshwater turtles having bright yellow and red markings; common in the eastern United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101190881"
	 b:glossaryEntry="land tortoises"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101191090">
	<b:glossaryEntry>usually herbivorous land turtles having clawed elephant-like limbs; worldwide in arid area except Australia and Antarctica</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101191376"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Testudinidae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101191533"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small land tortoise of southern Europe"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101191671"
	 b:glossaryEntry="giant tortoises"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101191800"
	 b:glossaryEntry="very large tortoises of the Galapagos and Seychelles islands"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101191959"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gopher tortoises"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101192123"
	 b:glossaryEntry="burrowing edible land tortoise of southeastern North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101192310">
	<b:glossaryEntry>possible new genus for desert and Texas tortoises based on recent research</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101192477">
	<b:glossaryEntry>burrowing tortoise of the arid western United States and northern Mexico; may be reclassified as a member of genus Xerobates</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101192703">
	<b:glossaryEntry>close relative to the desert tortoise; may be reclassified as a member of genus Xerobates</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101192872"
	 b:glossaryEntry="soft-shelled turtles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101193030">
	<b:glossaryEntry>voracious aquatic turtle with a flat flexible shell covered by a leathery skin; can inflict painful bites</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101193273"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Trionychidae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101193430">
	<b:glossaryEntry>river turtle of western United States with a warty shell; prefers quiet water</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101193609"
	 b:glossaryEntry="river turtle of Mississippi basin; prefers running water"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101193765"
	 b:glossaryEntry="diapsid reptiles: lizards; snakes; tuataras"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101193948"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tuataras; extinct forms from middle Triassic"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101194116"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coextensive with the order Rhynchocephalia: tuataras"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101194280">
	<b:glossaryEntry>only extant member of the order Rhynchocephalia of large spiny lizard-like diapsid reptiles of coastal islands off New Zealand</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101194501"
	 b:glossaryEntry="diapsid reptiles: snakes and lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101194666"
	 b:glossaryEntry="true lizards; including chameleons and geckos"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101195178">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various reptiles of the suborder Sauria which includes lizards; in former classifications included also the crocodiles and dinosaurs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101195408">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relatively long-bodied reptile with usually two pairs of legs and a tapering tail</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101195794"
	 b:glossaryEntry="geckos"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101195934">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various small chiefly tropical and usually nocturnal insectivorous terrestrial lizards typically with immovable eyelids; completely harmless</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101196169"
	 b:glossaryEntry="flying geckos"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101196296">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having membranous expansions along sides of body and limbs and tail</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101196485"
	 b:glossaryEntry="banded geckos"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101196608">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several geckos with dard bands across the body and differing from typical geckos in having movable eyelids; of United States southwest and gulf coast to Florida</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101196853"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Australian and Tasmanian lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101197002">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Pygopodidae; snake-shaped pleurodont lizard with no forelimbs and only rudimentary hind limbs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101197202"
	 b:glossaryEntry="New World lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101197644">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lizards of the New World and Madagascar and some Pacific islands; typically having a long tail and bright throat patch in males</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101198131"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Iguanidae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101198255">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large herbivorous tropical American arboreal lizards with a spiny crest along the back; used as human food in Central and South America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101198494"
	 b:glossaryEntry="marine iguanas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101198628">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shore-dwelling seaweed-eating lizard of the Galapagos Islands</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101198794"
	 b:glossaryEntry="desert iguanas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101198924">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small long-tailed lizard of arid areas of southwestern United States and northwestern Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101199118"
	 b:glossaryEntry="chuckwallas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101199224">
	<b:glossaryEntry>rock-dwelling herbivorous lizard of arid parts of southwestern United States and Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101199388"
	 b:glossaryEntry="zebra-tailed lizard"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101199523">
	<b:glossaryEntry>swift lizard with long black-banded tail and long legs; of deserts of United States and Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101199753"
	 b:glossaryEntry="fringe-toed lizard"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101199871">
	<b:glossaryEntry>with long pointed scales around toes; of deserts of United States and Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101200044"
	 b:glossaryEntry="earless lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101200173">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several slender lizards without external ear openings: of plains of western United States and Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101200360"
	 b:glossaryEntry="collard lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101200491">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several robust long-tailed lizards with collars of two dark bands; of central and western United States and northern Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101200702"
	 b:glossaryEntry="leopard lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101200827">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several large lizards with many dark spots; of western United States and northern Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101201002"
	 b:glossaryEntry="spiny lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101201129">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous lizards with overlapping ridged pointed scales; of North and Central America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101201335">
	<b:glossaryEntry>spiny lizard often seen basking on fences in the United States and northern Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101201512"
	 b:glossaryEntry="common western lizard; seen on logs or rocks"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101201670">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small active lizard of United States and north to British Columbia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101201840">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a ground dweller that prefers open ground and scattered low bushes; of United States west between Rocky and Sierra Nevada Mountains</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101202057"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a reptile genus of Iguanidae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101202185">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of the most abundant lizards in the arid western United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101202370"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a reptile genus of Iguanidae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101202510">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a climbing lizard of western United States and northern Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101202669"
	 b:glossaryEntry="horned lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101202797">
	<b:glossaryEntry>insectivorous lizard with hornlike spines on the head and spiny scales on the body; of western North America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101203029"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of arid and semiarid open country"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101203150"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a reptile genus of Iguanidae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101203292">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small crested arboreal lizard able to run on its hind legs; of tropical America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101203445"
	 b:glossaryEntry="New World chameleons"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101203571">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small arboreal tropical American insectivorous lizards with the ability to change skin color</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101203777"
	 b:glossaryEntry="worm lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101203893"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Amphisbaenidae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101204058">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a lizard of the genus Amphisbaena; harmless wormlike limbless lizard of warm or tropical regions having concealed eyes and ears and a short blunt tail</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101204285"
	 b:glossaryEntry="night lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101204415">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small secretive nocturnal lizard of southwestern North America and Cuba; bear live young</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101204581"
	 b:glossaryEntry="skinks"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101204757"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of Scincidae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101204869"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a reptile genus of Scincidae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101204990">
	<b:glossaryEntry>alert agile lizard with reduced limbs and an elongated body covered with shiny scales; more dependent on moisture than most lizards; found in tropical regions worldwide</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101205292"
	 b:glossaryEntry="New World skinks"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101205435">
	<b:glossaryEntry>found in western North American grasslands and open woodlands</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101205598">
	<b:glossaryEntry>frequents oak and pine habitats in rocky mountainous areas of United States southwest and Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101205798"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small family of spiny ovoviviparous African lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101205964">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Cordylidae; spiny lizards somewhat resembling tiny crocodiles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101206134"
	 b:glossaryEntry="whiptails; etc."/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101206296">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tropical New World lizard with a long tail and large rectangular scales on the belly and a long tail</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101206536"
	 b:glossaryEntry="whiptails"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101206665"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of numerous very agile and alert New World lizards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101206901"
	 b:glossaryEntry="very swift lizard of eastern and central United States"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101207078">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having distinct longitudinal stripes: of Colorado Plateau from Arizona to western Colorado</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101207261">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having longitudinal stripes overlaid with light spots; upland lizard of United States southwest and Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101207467">
	<b:glossaryEntry>active lizard having a network of dusky dark markings; of semiarid areas from Oregon and Idaho to Baja California</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101207666">
	<b:glossaryEntry>markings are darker and more marked than in western whiptail; from southeastern Colorado to eastern Chihuahua</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101207867"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tejus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;101207986">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large (to 3 feet) blackish yellow-banded South American lizard; raid henhouses; used as food</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
