</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107695476">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a general of Alexander the Great and king of Macedonia; lost one eye; killed in a battle at Ipsus (382-301 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107695696">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Roman general under Julius Caesar in the Gallic wars; repudiated his wife for the Egyptian queen Cleopatra; they were defeated by Octavian at Actium (83-30 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107695958">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Italian (Roman Catholic) theologian remembered for his attempt to reconcile faith and reason in a comprehensive theology (1225-1274)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107696199">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an ancient Greek grammarian remembered for his commentary on the Iliad and Odyssey (ca. 217-145 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107696356">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an ancient Greek astronomer who was one of the first to propose a heliocentric theory of the universe (ca. 270 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107696537">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an ancient Greek dramatist remembered for his comedies (448-380 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107696663">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of the greatest of the ancient Athenian philosophers (384-322 BC); pupil of Plato; teacher of Alexander the Great</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107696873">
	<b:glossaryEntry>German hero; leader at the battle of Teutoburger Wald in 9 AD (circa 18 BC - 19 AD)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107697047"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the first man to set foot on the moon (July 20, 1969)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107697173">
	<b:glossaryEntry>king of Persia who sanctioned the practice of Judaism in Jerusalem (?-424 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107697327">
	<b:glossaryEntry>king of Persia who subdued numerous revolutions and made peace with Sparta (?-359 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107697490">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a legendary king of the Britons (possibly based on a historical figure in the 6th century but the story has been retold too many times to be sure); said to have led the Knights of the Round Table at Camelot</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107697763">
	<b:glossaryEntry>elected Vice President and became 21st President of the United States when Garfield was assassinated (1830-1886)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107697970">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the first Saxon ruler who extended his kingdom to include nearly all of England (895-939)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107698133">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(406-453) king of the Huns; the most successful barbarian invader of the Roman Empire</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107698311"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1907-1973"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107698392">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of the Fathers of the Roman Catholic Church; after a dramatic conversion to Christianity he became Bishop of Hippo in North Africa (354-430)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107698610"
	 b:glossaryEntry="63 BC - 14 AD"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107698777"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1685-1750) German composer and organist"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107698910"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1561-1626"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107699043">
	<b:glossaryEntry>French novelist; he portrays the complexity of 19th century French society (1799-1850)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107699217"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1860-1937) Scottish dramatist"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107699350">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a French poet noted for macabre imagery and evocative language (1821-1867)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107699530">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1118-1170) archbishop of Canterbury from 1162 to 1170; murdered following his opposition to Henry II concerning Henry''s attempts to control the clergy</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107699794">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an author born in Ireland who lived in France; wrote plays for the Theater of the Absurd (1906-1989)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107699983">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Italian monk who founded the Benedictine order about 540 (480-547)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107700121"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1847-1922) American inventor of the telephone"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107700259"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1770-1827) German composer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107700381">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Old Testament) the last king of Babylon; he was warned of his doom by divine handwriting on the wall that was interpreted by Daniel</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107700570"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1685-1753"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107700688">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1815-1898) German statesman under whose leadership Germany was united</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107700873">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1754-1817) British admiral; was captain of the H.M.S. Bounty when the crew mutinied in 1789</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107701049">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1575-1624) German mystic and theosophist who founded modern theosophy; influenced George Fox</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107701278">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the second wife of Henry VIII of England and mother of Elizabeth I; was executed on a charge of adultery (1507-1536)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107701461">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1783-1830) Venezuelan statesman who led the revolt of South American colonies against Spanish rule; founded Bolivia in 1825</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107701689"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1899-1957) American film actor"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107701826">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1815-1864) English mathematician; creator of Boolean algebra</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107701954"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1734-1820) an American pioneer and guide and explorer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107702075">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Confederate general during the American Civil War who was defeated by Grant in the battle of Chattanooga (1817-1876)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107702259"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1833-1897) German composer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107702357"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Irish abbess; a patron saint of Ireland"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107702569">
	<b:glossaryEntry>King of Scotland from 1306 to 1329; defeated the English army under Edward II at Bannockburn and gained recognition of Scottish independence</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107702791">
	<b:glossaryEntry>statesman of ancient Rome who (with Cassius) led a conspiracy to assassinate Julius Caesar (85-42 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107702986"
	 b:glossaryEntry="15th President of the United States (1791-1868)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107703105">
	<b:glossaryEntry>British general in the American Revolution who lost the battle of Saratoga in 1777 (1722-1792)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107703270">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Vice President under Reagan and 41st President of the United States (1924- )</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107703440"
	 b:glossaryEntry="conqueror of Gaul and master of Italy (100-44 BC)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107703616">
	<b:glossaryEntry>French-born Swiss theologian whose tenets defined Presbyterianism (1509-1564)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107703804"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1835-1919) American industrialist and philanthropist"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107703948">
	<b:glossaryEntry>English author; Charles Dodgson was an Oxford don of mathematics who is remembered for the children''s stories he wrote under the pen name Lewis Carroll (1832-1898)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107704255"
	 b:glossaryEntry="39th President of the United States (1924- )"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107704387">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1725-1798) an Italian adventurer who wrote vivid accounts of his sexual encounters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107704552">
	<b:glossaryEntry>prime mover in the conspiracy against Julius Caesar (died in 42 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107704716"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1729-1796"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107704803">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1485-1536) first wife of Henry VIII of England; Henry''s divorce from her marked an initial step in the English Reformation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107704992">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Spanish writer best remembered for "Don Quixote" which satirizes chivalry and influenced the development of the novel form (1547-1616)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107705222">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(742-814) king of the Franks; conqueror of the Lombards and Saxons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107705384">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1340-1400) English poet remembered as author of the Canterbury Tales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107705526"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Russian dramatist (1860-1904)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107705669"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1810-1849) composer and pianist"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107705799">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Christian martyr of the 3rd century; the patron saint of travellers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107705946">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1874-1965) British Conservative statesman; British leader during World War II; received Nobel Prize for literature in 1953</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107706187">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Roman statesman and orator remembered for his mastery of Latin prose (106-43 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107706390">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1240-1302) painter of the Florentine school; anticipated the move from Byzantine to naturalistic art</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107706564"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(69-30 BC) queen of Egypt"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107706663"
	 b:glossaryEntry="22nd and 24th President of the United States (1837-1908)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107706794"
	 b:glossaryEntry="42nd President of the United States (1946- )"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107706935">
	<b:glossaryEntry>king of the Franks and founder of the Frankish monarchy (466-511)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107707082"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1772-1834"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107707173"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1451-1506"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107707299"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Chinese philosopher (circa 551-478 BC)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107707406"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1859-1930) English author who created Sherlock Holmes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107707581">
	<b:glossaryEntry>elected Vice President and succeeded as 30th President of the United States when Harding died in 1923 (1872-1933)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107707767">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Polish astronomer who produced a workable model of the solar system with the sun in the center (1473-1543)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107707971">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1485-1547) Spanish conquistador who defeated the Aztecs and conquered Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107708178"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1599-1658"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107708278">
	<b:glossaryEntry>English chemist and physicist who formulated atomic theory and the law of partial pressures (1766-1844)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107708466">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Old Testament) a youth who was taken into the court of Nebuchadnezzar and given divine protection when thrown into a den of lions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107708649">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an Italian poet famous for `The Divine Comedy''--a journey through hell and purgatory and paradise guided by Virgil and his idealized Beatrice (1265-1321)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107708872">
	<b:glossaryEntry>English natural scientist whose `On the Origin of Species'' formulated a theory of evolution by natural selection (1809-1882)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107709090">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the 2nd king of the Israelites; as a young shepherd he fought Goliath (a giant Philistine warrior) and killed him by hitting him in the head with a stone flung from a sling; many of the Psalms are attributed to David</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107709376">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Old Testament) Samson''s Philistine mistress who betrayed him by cutting off his hair</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107709515">
	<b:glossaryEntry>son of Antigonus Cyclops and king of Macedonia; he and his father were defeated at the battle of Ipsus (337-283 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107709742"
	 b:glossaryEntry="circa 385-322 BC"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107709852"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1596-1650"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107709952"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1859-1952"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107710044"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1572-1631"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107710136"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Russian novelist (1821-1881)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107710405">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Athenian lawmaker (7th century) whose code of laws prescribed death for almost every offense</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107710567">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1859-1935) French army officer of Jewish descent whose false imprisonment for treason in 1894 raised issues of anti-semitism that dominated French politics until his release in 1906</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107710820"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1882-1944"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107710916"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1821-1910) founder of Christian Science in 1866"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107711033"
	 b:glossaryEntry="American inventor (1847-1931)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107711152">
	<b:glossaryEntry>king of the English from 1042 to 1066; reign marked by conflict with the Normans</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107711308">
	<b:glossaryEntry>son of Edward III of England; defeated the French at Crecy and Poitiers in the Hundred Years'' War (1330-1376)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107711485">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1239-1307) King of England from 1272 to 1307; conquered Wales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107711602">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1284-1327) King of England from 1307 to 1327 and son of Edward I; was defeated at Bannockburn by the Scots led by Robert the Bruce; was deposed and died in prison</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107711821">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1312-1377) son of Edward II and King of England from 1327-1377; his claim to the French throne provoked the Hundred Years'' War; his reign was marked by an epidemic of the Black Plague and by the emergence of the Commons as the powerful arm of Parliament</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107712132">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1442-1483) King of England from 1461 to 1470 and from 1471 to 1483; was dethroned in 1470 but regained the throne in 1471 by his victory at the battle of Tewkesbury</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107712353">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1470-1483) King of England who was crowned at the age of 13 on the death of his father Edward IV but was immediately confined to the Tower of London where he and his younger brother were murdered</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107712604">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1537-1553) King of England and Ireland from 1547 to 1553; son of Henry VIII and Jane Seymour; died of tuberculosis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107712775">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1841-1910) King of England from 1901 to 1910; son of Victoria; famous for his elegant sporting ways</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107712948">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1894-1972) King of England and Ireland in 1936; his marriage to Wallis Warfield Simpson created a constitutional crisis leading to his abdication</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107713170">
	<b:glossaryEntry>physicist who formulated the special theory of relativity and the general theory of relativity (1879-1955)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107713349">
	<b:glossaryEntry>as Supreme Commander of the Allied Expeditionary Force he supervised the invasion of Normandy and the defeat of Nazi Germany; 34th President of the United States (1890-1961)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107713672">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Hebrew prophet in the Old Testament who opposed the worship of idols; he was persecuted for rebuking Ahab and Jezebel (king and queen of Israel); he was taken up to heaven in a chariot of fire</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107713937">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Queen of England from 1558 to 1603; daughter of Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn (1533-1603)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107714093"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1926-"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107714171"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wrote The Communist Manifesto with Marx in 1848 (1820-1895)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107714302">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(341-270 BC) Greek philosopher who believed that the world is a random combination of atoms and that pleasure is the highest good</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107714504">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Dutch Renaissance scholar and Roman Catholic theologian who attacked the theology of Martin Luther (1466-1536)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107714723">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Greek mathematician and astronomer who estimated the circumference of the earth and the distances to the moon and sun (276-194 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107714930">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Old Testament) the eldest son of Isaac who would have inherited the Covenant that God made with Abraham and that Abraham passed on to Isaac; he traded his birthright to his brother Jacob for a mess of pottage</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107715208">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Old Testament) a beautiful Jewess chosen by the king of Persia to be his queen; she stopped a plot to massacre all the Jews in Persia (an event celebrated by Jews as the feast of Purim)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107715465"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Greek geometer (3rd century BC)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107715567">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the English physicist nd chemist who discovered electromagnetic induction (1791-1867)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107715742">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1897-1962) novelist who wrote about people in the southern U.S.</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107715880">
	<b:glossaryEntry>French mathematician who founded number theory; contributed (with Pascal) to the theory of probability (1601-1665)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107716066">
	<b:glossaryEntry>elected Vice President and became the 13th President of the United States when Taylor died in office (1800-1874)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107716252">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Roman statesman and general who built the Flaminian Way; was defeated by Hannibal at Trasimene (died 217 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107716470"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1863-1947"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107716543">
	<b:glossaryEntry>38th President of the United States; appointed Vice President and succeeded Nixon when Nixon resigned (1913- )</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107716757">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1624-1691) English religious leader who founded the Society of Friends</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107716891">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1181-1226) Italian monk who founded the Franciscan order of friars</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107717103">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1123-1190) Holy Roman Emperor from 1152 to 1190; conceded supremacy to the pope; drowned leading the Third Crusade</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107717312">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1194-1250) Holy Roman Emperor; led the Sixth Crusade and crowned himself king of Jerusalem</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107717462">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1712-1786) king of Prussia from 1740 to 1786; brought Prussia military prestige by winning the War of the Austrian Succession and the Seven Years'' War</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107717694"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1856-1939"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107717771">
	<b:glossaryEntry>US poet famous for his lyrical poems on country life in New England (1874-1963)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107717936">
	<b:glossaryEntry>American inventor who designed the first commercially successful steamboat and the first steam warship (1765-1815)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107718119"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1727-1788"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107718209">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Arthurian legend) the most virtuous knight of the Round Table; was able to see the Holy Grail</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107718371">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Italian astronomer and mathematician; demonstrated that different weights descend at the same rate; perfected the refracting telescope that enabled him to make many discoveries (1564-1642)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107718631">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1869-1948) political and spiritual leader during India''s struggle with Great Britain for home rule; an advocate of passive resistance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107718882">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1917-1984) daughter of Nehru who served as Prime Minister of India from 1966 to 1977</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107719063">
	<b:glossaryEntry>20th President of the United States; assassinated by a frustrated office-seeker (1831-1881)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107719271">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Italian patriot whose conquest of Sicily and Naples led to the formation of the Italian state (1807-1882)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107719471"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1848-1903"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107719549"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1777-1855"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107719651"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(Arthurian legend) one of the knights of the Round Table"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107719773"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Mongol conqueror (died in 1227)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107719893">
	<b:glossaryEntry>German physicist who developed the Geiger counter (1882-1945)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107720021">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1660-1727) Elector of Hanover and the first Hanoverian King of Great Britain and Ireland from 1714 to 1727</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107720183">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1683-1760) King of Great Britain and Elector of Hanover from 1727 to 1760</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107720313">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1738-1820) King of Great Britain and Ireland from 1760 to 1820; the American colonies were lost during his reign; he became insane in 1811 and his son (later George IV) acted as regent until 1820</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107720566">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1762-1830) King of Great Britain and Ireland and Hanover from 1820 to 1830; his attempt to divorce his estranged wife undermined the prestige of the crown</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107720777">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1865-1936) King of Great Britain and Ireland and emperor of India from 1910 to 1936; gave up his German title during World War I</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107720961">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1895-1952) King of Great Britain and Ireland and emperor of India from 1936 to 1947; he succeeded Edward VIII</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107721127">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Christian martyr; patron saint of England; hero of the legend of Saint George and the Dragon in which he slew a dragon and saved a princess (?-303)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107721342">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a librettist who was a collaborator with Sir Arthur Sullivan (1836-1911)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107721552">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made the first orbital rocket-powered flight by a US astronaut in 1962; later in US Senate</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107721725">
	<b:glossaryEntry>according to legend she rode naked through Coventry in order to persuade her husband not to tax the townspeople so heavily (1040-1080)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107721926">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1749-1832) German poet and novelist and dramatist who lived in Weimar</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107722114">
	<b:glossaryEntry>18th President of the United States; commander of the Union armies in the American Civil War (1822-1885)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107722322">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(circa 540-604) the pope for whom Gregorian chants were named</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107722487"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1572-1585) the pope who introduced the modern calendar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107722630">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the older of the two Grimm brothers remembered best for their fairy stories; also author of Grimm''s Law describing consonant changes in Germanic languages (1785-1863)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107722892">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the younger of the two Grimm brothers remembered best for their fairy stories (1786-1859)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107723054"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a United States architect born in Germany (1883-1969)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107723178">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Arthurian legend) wife of King Arthur; in some versions of the legend she became Lancelot''s lover and that led to the end of the Knights of the Round Table</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107723402"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1892-1975) Emperor of Ethiopia; worshipped by Rastafarians"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107723557">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1685-1759) a prolific German composer noted for his oratorios</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107723723">
	<b:glossaryEntry>general who commanded the Carthaginian army in the Second Punic War; crossed the Alps and defeated the Romans but was recalled to defend Carthage and was defeated</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107723958">
	<b:glossaryEntry>29th President of the United States; two of his appointees were involved in the Teapot Dome scandal (1865-1823)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107724166">
	<b:glossaryEntry>9th President of the United States; caught pneumonia during his inauguration and died shortly after (1773-1841)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107724357"
	 b:glossaryEntry="23rd President of the United States (1833-1901)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107724479">
	<b:glossaryEntry>general who commanded a Carthaginian army in Spain; joined his brother Hannibal in Italy and was killed by the Romans at the battle of Metaurus River (died 207 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107724716"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1732-1809"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107724792"
	 b:glossaryEntry="19th President of the United States (1822-1893)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107724941"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1770-1831"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107725034"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1821-1894"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107725182">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an American writer of fiction who won the Nobel Prize for literature in 1954 (1899-1961)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107725345">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1791-1878) American physicist who studied electromagnetic phenomena</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107725480">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1068-1135) King of England from 1100 to 1135; youngest son of William the Conqueror; conquered Normandy in 1106</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107725664">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1133-1189) first Plantagenet King of England; instituted judicial and financial reforms; quarreled with archbishop Becket concerning the authority of the crown over the church</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107725895">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1207-1272) son of King John and King of England from 1216 to 1272; his incompetence led to baronial opposition led by Simon de Montfort</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107726087">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1367-1413) the first Lancastrian King of England from 1399 to 1413; deposed Richard II and suppressed rebellions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107726289">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1387-1422) son of Henry IV and King of England from 1413 to 1422; reopened the Hundred Years'' War and defeated the French at Agincourt</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107726478">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1421-1471) son of Henry V who as an infant succeeded his father and was King of England from 1422 to 1461; he was taken prisoner in 1460 and Edward IV was proclaimed king; he was rescued and regained the throne in 1470 but was recaptured and murdered in the Tower of London</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107726807">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1457-1509) first Tudor king of England from 1485 to 1509; head of the house of Lancaster in the War of the Roses; defeated Richard III at Bosworth Field and was proclaimed king; married the daughter of Edward IV and so united the houses of York and Lancaster</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107727136">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1491-1547) son of Henry VII and King of England from 1509 to 1547; his divorce from Catherine of Aragon resulted in his break with the Catholic Church in 1534 and the start of the English Reformation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107727393">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(73-4 BC) king of Judea who (according to the New Testament) tried to kill Jesus by ordering the death of all children under age two in Bethlehem</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107727608">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the ancient Greek known as the father of history; his accounts of the wars between the Greeks and Persians are the first known examples of historical writing (425-485 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107727834">
	<b:glossaryEntry>German physicist who was the first to produce electromagnetic waves artificially (1857-1894)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107728020">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a native American chieftain in the 16th century who argued for peace with the European settlers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107728188">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(circa 460-377 BC) medical practitioner who is regarded as the father of medicine; author of the Hippocratic Oath</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107728377"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1889-1945) German dictator during World War II"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107728505">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Greek epic poet (circa 850 B.C.) who is believed to have written the Iliad and the Odyssey</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107728665">
	<b:glossaryEntry>31st President of the United States; in 1929 the stock market crashed and the economy collapsed and Hoover was defeated for re-election by Franklin Roosevelt (1874-1964)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107728927"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a Hebrew prophet of the 8th century BC"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107729035"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1802-1885) leader of the romantic movement in France"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107729209"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1825-1895"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107729309">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Norwegian author who wrote plays on social and political themes (1828-1906)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107729471">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the second Old Testament patriarch; son of Abraham and Sarah; father of Jacob and Esau</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107729627">
	<b:glossaryEntry>7th president of the US; successfully defended New Orleans from the British in 1815; (1767-1845)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107729826">
	<b:glossaryEntry>disciple of Jesus; brother of John; author of The Epistle of James in the New Testament</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107730016"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1843-1916"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107730091"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1842-1910"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107730186">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1566-1625) King of England and Ireland from 1603 to 1925 and (as James VI) of Scotland from 1567 to 1625; alienated Parliament by claiming the divine right of kings</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107730405">
	<b:glossaryEntry>King of England and Ireland and Scotland; overthrown in 1688 (1633-1701)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107730532">
	<b:glossaryEntry>3rd President of the United States; chief drafter of the Declaration of Independence; made the Louisiana Purchase in 1803 and sent out the Lewis and Clark Expedition (1743-1826)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107730784"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Roman Catholic theologian (1585-1638)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107730914">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an Israelite prophet in the Old Testament who is remembered for his angry lamentations (jeremiads) about the wickedness of his people</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107731120">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a prophet of the first century (circa 8 BC - 29 AD); to Christians he was both God and man--the Messiah sent to save the human race from the sin it inherited through the Fall of Man</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107731479">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(9th century BC) wife of Ahab who was king of Israel; according to the Old Testament she was a cruel immoral queen who fostered the worship of Baal and tried to kill Elijah and other prophets of Israel</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107731752">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Jewish hero in the Old Testament who maintained his faith in God in spite of afflictions that tested him</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107731908"
	 b:glossaryEntry="disciple of Jesus; author of the 4th Gospel"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107732050">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1167-1216) youngest son of Henry II and King of England from 1199 to 1216; succeeded to the throne on the death of his brother Richard I; lost his French possessions; in 1215 John was compelled by the barons to sign the Magna Carta</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107732349">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(New Testament) a preacher and hermit and forerunner of Jesus (whom he baptized); was beheaded by Herod at the request of Salome</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107732558">
	<b:glossaryEntry>17th President of the United States; was elected Vice President and succeeded Lincoln when Lincoln was assassinated; was impeached but acquitted by one vote (1808-1875)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107732797">
	<b:glossaryEntry>36th President of the United States; was elected Vice President and succeeded Kennedy when Kennedy was assassinated (1908-1973)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107733019">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Old Testament) Jonah did not wish to become a prophet so God caused a great storm to throw him overboard from a ship; he was saved by being swallowed by a whale that vomited him out onto dry land</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107733285">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(New Testament) husband of Mary and (in Christian belief) the foster father of Jesus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107733440">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Old Testament) the 11th son of Jacob and one of the 12 patriarchs of Israel; Jacob gave Joseph a coat of many colors, which made his brother jealous and the sold him into slavery in Egypt</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107733699">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Moses'' successor who led the Israelites into the Promised Land; best remembered for his destruction of Jericho</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107733888">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the Apostle who betrayed Jesus to his enemies for 30 pieces of silver</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107734026"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1875-1961"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107734117"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1883-1924"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107734192"
	 b:glossaryEntry="German idealist philosopher (1724-1804)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107734298">
	<b:glossaryEntry>35th President of the United States; established the Peace Corps; assassinated in Dallas (1917-1963)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107734493">
	<b:glossaryEntry>German astronomer who first stated laws of planetary motion (1571-1630)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107734650">
	<b:glossaryEntry>English economist who advocated the use of government monetary and fiscal policy to maintain full employment without inflation (1883-1946)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107734863">
	<b:glossaryEntry>A Scottish sea captain who was hired to protect British shipping in the Indian Ocean and then was accused of piracy and hanged (1645-1701)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107735082"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1929-1968"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107735181"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1865-1936"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107735262"
	 b:glossaryEntry="founder of the Mongol dynasty in China (1216-1294)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107735370">
	<b:glossaryEntry>French naturalist who proposed that evolution resulted from the inheritance of acquired characteristics (1744-1829)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107735602">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Arthurian legend) one of the knights of the Round Table; friend of King Arthur until (according to some versions of the legend) he became the lover of Arthur''s wife Guinevere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107735847"
	 b:glossaryEntry="British paleontologist who worked in Tanzania (1903-1972)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107736002"
	 b:glossaryEntry="British artist and writer of nonsense verse (1812-1888)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107736140">
	<b:glossaryEntry>American general who led the Confederate armies in the American Civil War (1807-1870)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107736310"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1646-1716"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107736459">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Russian founder of the Bolsheviks and leader of the Russian Revolution and first head of the USSR (1870-1924)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107736679"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1452-1519"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107736817">
	<b:glossaryEntry>king of Sparta and hero of the battle of Thermopylae where is was killed by the Persians (d. 480 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107737009">
	<b:glossaryEntry>16th President of the United States; saved the Union during the Civil War and emancipated the slaves; was assassinated by Booth (1809-1865)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107737238"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1707-1778"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107737321"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1827-1912"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107737399"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1632-1704"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107737473"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1807-1882"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107737568">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1601-1643) King of France from 1610 to 1643 who relied heavily on the advice of Cardinal Richelieu</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107737724">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1638-1715) King of France from 1643 to 1715; his long reign was marked by the expansion of French influence in Europe and by the magnificence of his court and the Palace of Versailles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107737993">
	<b:glossaryEntry>German theologian who led the Reformation; believed that salvation is granted on the basis of faith rather than deeds (1483-1546)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107738191">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Spartan commander who defeated the Athenians in the final battle of the Peloponnesian War (d. 395 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107738365">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Macedonian general under Alexander the Great; with Seleucus he defeated Antigonus and Demetrius at the battle of Ipsus (circa 355-281 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107738577">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1469-1527) a statesman of Florence who advocated a strong central government</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107738734">
	<b:glossaryEntry>4th President of the United States; member of the Constitutional Congress and recorder at the Constitutional Convention in 1776; helped frame the Bill of Rights (1751-1836)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107738976"
	 b:glossaryEntry="circa 1480-1521"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107739067"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1925-1965"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107739167">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an English economist who argued that increases in population would outgrow increases in the means of subsistence (1766-1834)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107739386"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Chinese communist leader (1893-1976)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107739499">
	<b:glossaryEntry>American jurist; as chief justice of the Supreme Court he established the principles of United States constitutional law (1755-1835)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107739720">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sinful woman Jesus healed of evil spirits; she became a follower of Jesus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107739854">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the mother of Jesus; Christians refer to her as the Virgin Mary; she is especially honored by Roman Catholics</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107740069">
	<b:glossaryEntry>founder of modern communism; wrote The Communist Manifesto with Engels in 1848; wrote Das Kapital in 1867 (1818-1883)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107740249">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Dutch dancer who was executed by the French as a German spy in World War I (1876-1917)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107740417"
	 b:glossaryEntry="disciple of Jesus; author of the first Gospel"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107740580">
	<b:glossaryEntry>25th President of the United States; was assassinated by an anarchist (1843-1901)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107740735"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1819-1891"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107740817"
	 b:glossaryEntry="founder of the science of genetics (1822-1884)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107740973">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Russian chemist who devised the first periodic table of the chemical elements (1834-1907)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107741161">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Arthurian legend) the magician who acted as King Arthur''s advisor</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107741280"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1475-1564"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107741384">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ancient king who expanded his kingdom by defeating the Romans but was later driven out by Pompey (132-63 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107741591"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(570-632) the prophet who founded Islam"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107741716"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1622-1673"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107741779">
	<b:glossaryEntry>5th President of the United States; author of the "Monroe Doctrine" (1758-1831)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107741926">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1208-1265) an English nobleman who led the baronial rebellion against Henry III</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107742101">
	<b:glossaryEntry>soldier in the American Revolutionary War who defeated the British in the Battle of Cowpens, South Carolina (1736-1802)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107742289">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a buccaneer who raided Spanish colonies in the West Indies for the English</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107742443"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1837-1913"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107742541">
	<b:glossaryEntry>U.S. biologist who formulated the chromosome theory of heredity</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107742678">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the ancient prophet whose writings were revealed to Joseph Smith who founded the Mormon Church</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107742825"
	 b:glossaryEntry="founded the Mormon Church in 1830"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107742917">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the Hebrew prophet who led the Israelites from Egypt across the Red sea on a journey known as the Exodus; Moses received the Ten Commandments from God on Mount Sinai</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107743152"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1756-1791"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107743240"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1883-1945"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107743334">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the mother-in-law of Ruth whose story is told in the Book of Ruth in the Old Testament</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107743480">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Scottish mathematician; invented logarithms; introduced the use of the decimal point in writing numbers (1550-1617)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107743662"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1769-1821"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107743778">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Egyptian statesman who nationalized the Suez Canal (1918-1870)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107743914"
	 b:glossaryEntry="American political cartoonist (1840-1902)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107744020">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1889-1964) Indian statesman and leader with Gandhi in the struggle for home rule; was the first Prime Minister of the Republic of India from 1947 to 1964</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107744245">
	<b:glossaryEntry>English mathematician and physicist; remembered for developing the calculus and for his law of gravitation and his three laws of motion (1642-1727)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107744497"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1820-1910"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107744587">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Vice President under Eisenhower and 37th President of the United States; resigned after the Watergate scandal in 1974 (1913-1994)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107744827">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the Hebrew patriarch who saved himself and his family and the animals by building an ark in which they survived 40 days and 40 nights of rain; the story of Noah and the flood is told in the Book of Genesis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107745083"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1787-1854) the German physicist who formulated Ohm''s Law"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107745208">
	<b:glossaryEntry>American physicist who directed the project at Los Alamos that developed the first atomic bomb (1904-1967)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107745393">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Indian grammarian whose grammatical rules for Sanskrit are the first known example of descriptive linguistics (circa 400 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107745570"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1892-1968"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107745651">
	<b:glossaryEntry>British historian noted for ridicule of bureaucracies (1909- )</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107745822">
	<b:glossaryEntry>French mathematician and philosopher; invented an adding machine; contributed (with Fermat) to the theory of probability (1623-1662)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107746041">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1822-1895) French chemist and biologist whose discovery that fermentation is caused by microorganisms resulted in the process of pasteurization</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107746273">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a patron saint of Ireland; an English missionary to Ireland in the 5th century</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107746435">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Christian missionary to the Gentiles; author of several Epistles in the New Testament; even though Paul was not present at the Last Supper he is considered an apostle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107746756">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Russian physiologist who observed conditioned salivary responses in dogs (1849-1936)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107746931"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1839-1914) a pioneer of pragmatism"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107747080">
	<b:glossaryEntry>disciple of Jesus; regarded by Catholics as the vicar of Christ on earth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107747255"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an Italian poet famous for love lyrics (1304-1374)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107747392"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1896-1980"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107747461"
	 b:glossaryEntry="14th President of the United States (1804-1869)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107747579"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1881-1973"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107747676"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Greek lyric poet (518-438 BC)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107747758">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ancient Athenian philosopher (428-347 BC); pupil of Socrates; teacher of Aristotle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107747911">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the Greek biographer who wrote Parallel Lives (a collection of biographies of famous Greeks and Romans that Shakespeare used in writing his plays); (46?-120 AD)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107748126">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Powhatan Indian woman (the daughter of Powhatan) who befriended the English at Jamestown and is said to have saved Captain John Smith''s life (1595-1617)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107748363">
	<b:glossaryEntry>11th President of the United States; his expansionism led to the Mexican War and the annexation of California and much of the southwest (1795-1849)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107748592">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Venetian traveler who explored Asia in the 13th century and served Kublai Khan (1254-1324)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107748746">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Roman general and statesman who quarrelled with Caesar and fled to Egypt where he was murdered</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107748973">
	<b:glossaryEntry>famous chief of the Ottawa who led an unsuccessful rebellion against the British (1715-1769)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107749120">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chief of the Powhatan nad founder of the Powhatan confederacy of tribes in Eastern Virginia; father of Pocahontas (1550?-1618)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107749317">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Greek mythology) the last king of Troy; father of Hector and Paris and Cassandra</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107749450"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1871-1922) French novelist"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107749546">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(2nd century AD) proposed a geocentric system of astronomy that was undisputed until Copernicus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107749735">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Greek mythology) the king of Cyprus who fell in love with the statue of a woman (Galatea) he had created</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107749896">
	<b:glossaryEntry>king of Epirus; defeated the Romans in two battles in spite of staggering losses (319-272 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107750043"
	 b:glossaryEntry="circa 580-500 BC"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107750152"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1908-"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107750237">
	<b:glossaryEntry>author of satirical attacks on medieval scholasticism (1494-1553)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107750377"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1639-1699"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107750453"
	 b:glossaryEntry="40th President of the United States (1911- )"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107750589">
	<b:glossaryEntry>French physicist who invented the alcohol thermometer (1683-1757)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107750744"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1851-1902"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107750818"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1606-1669"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107750954">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1157-1199) son of Henry II and King of England from 1189 to 1199; a leader of the Third Crusade; on his way home from the crusade he was captured and held prisoner in the Holy Roman Empire until England ransomed him in 1194</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107751310">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1367-1400) King of England from 1377 to 1399; he suppressed the Peasant''s Revolt in 1381 but his reign was marked by popular discontent and baronial opposition in Parliament and he was forced to abdicate in 1399</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107751579">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1452-1485) King of England from 1483 to 1485; seized the throne from his nephew Edward V who was confined to the Tower of London and murdered; his reign ended when he was defeated by Henry Tudor (later Henry VII) at the battle of Bosworth Field</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107751882">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1585-1642) French prelate and statesman; principal minister to Louis XIII</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107752095"
	 b:glossaryEntry="pioneer of non-Euclidean geometry (1826-1866)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107752229">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Welsh pirate credited with having taken more than 400 ships (1682-1722)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107752378">
	<b:glossaryEntry>German field marshal noted for brilliant generalship in North Africa during World War II (1891-1944)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107752563">
	<b:glossaryEntry>26th President of the United States; hero of the Spanish-American War; Panama Canal was built during his administration; said "Speak softly but carry a big stick" (1858-1919)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107752814">
	<b:glossaryEntry>32nd President of the United States; elected four times; instituted New Deal to counter the great depression and led country during World War II (1882-1945)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107753081"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wife of Franklin Roosevelt (1884-1962)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107753238">
	<b:glossaryEntry>said to have made the first American flag at the request of George Washington (1752-1836)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107753412">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1828-1882) English poet and painter who was a leader of the Pre-Raphaelites</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107753568"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1792-1868) Italian composer remembered for his operas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107753706"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1712-1778"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107753812">
	<b:glossaryEntry>German field marshal in World War II who directed the conquest of Poland and led the Ardennes counteroffensive (1875-1953)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107754039">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a German-born English leader of the Royalist forces during the English Civil War (1619-1682)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107754200"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1872-1970"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107754349">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the great-grandmother of King David whose story is told in the Book of Ruth in the Old Testament</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107754496">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1895-1948) US professional baseball player famous for hitting home runs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107754669">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1740-1814) French soldier and writer whose descriptions of sexual perversion gave rise to the term `sadism''</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107754899">
	<b:glossaryEntry>woman whose dancing beguiled Herod into giving her the head of John the Baptist</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107755031">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1884-1939) anthropologist and linguist; studied languages of North American Indians</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107755200">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the Greek lyric poet of Lesbos; much admired although only fragments of her poetry have been preserved (6th century BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107755390">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a French marshall who distinguished himself in the War of the Austrian Succession</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107755561">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1887-1961) Austrian physicist who discovered the wave equation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107755702">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Roman general who commanded the invasion of Carthage in the second Punic War and defeated Hannibal at Zama (circa 237-183 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107756031">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Macedonian general who accompanied Alexander the Great into Asia; founded a line of kings who reigned in Asia Minor until 65 BC (358-281 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107756278"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(circa 4 BC - 65 AD) an adviser to Nero"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107756448">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1509-1537) the third wife of Henry VIII and mother of Edward VI</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107756581"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1564-1616"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107756720"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1856-1950"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107756831"
	 b:glossaryEntry="made the first US suborbital rocket-powered flight in 1961"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107756958">
	<b:glossaryEntry>American divorcee whose marriage to Edward VIII created a constitutional crisis leading to his abdication</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107757158">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a chief of the Teton Dakota Sioux (1831-1890); took up arms against settlers in the northern Great Plains and against US Army troops; he was present at the battle of Little Bighorn (1876) when the Sioux massacred General Custer''s troops</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107757471"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1904-1990"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107757575">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ancient Athenian philosopher(470-399 BC); teacher of Plato and Xenophon</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107757719"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a US bandmaster and composer of military marches (1854-1932)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107757869"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1552-1599) English poet"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107757964"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Russian Soviet leader (1879-1953)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107758102"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1882-1971"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107758193"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Roman general and dictator (138-78 BC)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107758332"
	 b:glossaryEntry="English composer (1842-1900)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107758481"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an Anglo-Irish satirist (1667-1745)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107758585">
	<b:glossaryEntry>27th President of the United States and late chief justice of the US Supreme Court (1857-1930)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107758752">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Mongolian ruler of Samarkand who led his nomadic hordes to conquer an area from Turkey to Mongolia (1336-1405)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107758940">
	<b:glossaryEntry>12th President of the United States; died in office (1784-1850)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107759073">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an English pirate who operated in the Caribbean and off the Atlantic coast of North America (died in 1718)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107759284">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a famous chief of the Shawnee who tried to unite Indian tribes against the increasing white settlement (1768-1813)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107759453"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1817-1862"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107759538">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the Apostle who would not believe the resurrection of Jesus until he saw Jesus with his own eyes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107759735">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ancient Greek historian remembered for his history of the Peloponnesian War (460-395 BC)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107759880"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1490-1576) painter of the Venetian school"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107759994">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Russian author remembered for two novels: `War and Peace'' and `Anna Karenina'' (1828-1910)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107760187"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Italian physicist who invented the mercury barometer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107760321">
	<b:glossaryEntry>elected Vice President in Roosevelt''s 4th term; became 33rd President of the United States on Roosevelt''s death in 1945 and was elected President in 1948; authorized the use of atomic bombs against Japan (1884-1972)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107760622">
	<b:glossaryEntry>elected Vice President and became the 10th President of the United States when Harrison died (1790-1862)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107760791">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Christian Bishop who translated the Bible from Greek into Gothic (311-382)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107760990"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1883-1955"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107761071"
	 b:glossaryEntry="8th President of the United States (1782-1862)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107761192"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1853-1890"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107761275">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Florentine explorer of the eastern coast of North America (circa 1485-1528)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107761468">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Flemish surgeon who is considered the father of modern anatomy (1514-1564)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107761618">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Florentine navigator who explored the coast of South America; America was named in his honor (1454-1512)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107761817">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1819-1901) queen of Great Britain and Ireland and Empress of India</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107761939"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(70-19 BC) a Roman poet; author of the epic poem `Aeneid''"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107762083"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1694-1778) French writer; author of `Candide''"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107762208">
	<b:glossaryEntry>United States mathematician who contributed to the development of atom bombs and of stored-program digital computers (1903-1957)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107762413"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1813-1883) German composer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107762535">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(1676-1745) English Whig statesman who (under George I) was effectively the first British prime minister</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107762731">
	<b:glossaryEntry>1st President of the United States; commander-in-chief of the Continental Army during the American Revolution (1732-1799)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107762947"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1758-1843) American lexicographer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107763050"
	 b:glossaryEntry="founder of Methodism (1703-1791)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107763149">
	<b:glossaryEntry>28th President of the United States; led US in World War I and secured the formation of the League of Nations (1856-1924)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107763364"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1889-1951"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107763488"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1770-1850) a romantic British poet"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107763601"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1865-1939"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107763703"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(ca 335-263 BC) Greek philosopher who found the Stoic school"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107763849">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(ca 495-430 BC) Greek philosopher who formulated paradoxes that defended the belief that motion and change are illusory</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107764052"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(1840-1902) French novelist and critic; defender of Dreyfus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107764175">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(circa 628-551 BC) Persian prophet who founded Zoroastrianism</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107764306"
	 b:glossaryEntry="all non-artificial phenomena"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107764480">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the phenomenon of a person or thing rising into the air by apparently supernatural means</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107764625">
	<b:glossaryEntry>after death the soul begins a new cycle of existence in another human of animal body</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107764780">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a natural phenomenon involving chemistry (as changes to atoms or molecules)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107765047">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the phenomenon of an element existing in two or more physical forms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107765184">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chemical process in which one atom or ion or group changes places with another</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107765335">
	<b:glossaryEntry>change in the electrical conductivity of a substance as a result of absorbing electromagnetic radiation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107765520">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the disappearance of electrical resistance at very low temperatures</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107765651">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an exchange produced by the chemical action of radiant energy (especially light)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107765800">
	<b:glossaryEntry>emission of photoelectrons (especially from a metallic surface)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107765923"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the formation of crystals"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107766046"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a powdery deposit on a surface"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107766144">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a phenomenon that follows and is caused by some previous phenomenon; "the magnetic effect was greater when the rod was lengthwise"; "his decision had depressing consequences for business"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107766722"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any result that follows its cause after an interval"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107766831">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the consequences of an event (especially a catastrophic event); "the aftermath of war"; "in the wake of the accident no one knew how many had been injured"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107767060">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the shattering or crushing effect of a sudden release of energy as in an explosion</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107767197"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a secondary and sometimes unexpected consequence"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107767320">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the result of alteration or modification; "there were marked changes in the lining of the lungs"; "there had been no change in the mountains"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107767514">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(politics) the consequence of one popular candidate in an election drawing votes for other members of the same party; "he was elected senator on the president''s coattails"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107767760">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an appreciable consequence (especially a lessening); "it made a dent in my bank account"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107767899">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the consequence of an effort or activity; "they gathered a harvest of examples"; "a harvest of love"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107768053">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a forceful consequence; "the book had an important impact on my thinking"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107768179">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the effect of one thing (or person) on another; "the influence of mechanical action"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107768355">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a means of exerting influence or gaining advantage; "he could get no purchase on the situation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107768505">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tendency or force that influences events; "the winds of change"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107768621"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a natural consequence of development"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107768742">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a consequence of someone''s efforts or of a particular set of circumstances; "skill is the product of hours of practice"; "his reaction was the product of hunger and fatigue"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107768969">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a remote or indirect consequence of some action; "his declaration had unforeseen repercussions"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107769123">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a result; "this situation developed in response to events in Africa"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107769264">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a response that reveals a person''s feelings or attitude; "he was pleased by the audience''s reaction to his performance"; "John feared his mother''s reaction when she saw the broken lamp"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107769504"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any unwanted secondary effect"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107769591"
	 b:glossaryEntry='something used to achieve a purpose: "an engine of change"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107769702">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a natural phenomenon involving the structure or composition of the earth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107769986">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an unknown and unpredictable phenomenon that causes an event to result one way rather than another; "bad luck caused his downfall"; "it was a chance meeting"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107770258">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an unknown and unpredictable phenomenon that leads to a favorable outcome; "it was my good luck to be there"; "they say luck is a lady"; "it was as if fortune guided his hand"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107770512"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a natural phenomenon involving living plants and animals"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107770957">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a natural phenomenon involving the physics of matter and energy</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107771626">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an optical phenomenon resulting from the failure of a lens or mirror to produce a good image</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107771845">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a hypothetical organic phenomenon by which living organisms are created from nonliving matter</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107772046">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a dark band in the spectrum of white light that has been transmitted through a substance that exhibits absorption at selective wavelengths</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107772246"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an ordered array of the components of an emission or wave"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107772503">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the spectrum of electromagnetic radiation that has passed through a medium that absorbed radiation of certain wavelengths</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107772690">
	<b:glossaryEntry>electromagnetic radiation that can produce photochemical reactions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107772907">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the energy that an atomic system must acquire before a process (such as an emission or reaction) can occur</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107773077"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a cloud of solid or liquid particles in a gas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107773230">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the force attracting atoms to each other and binding them together in a molecule: "basic dyes have an affinity for wool and silk"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107773436">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a local region of low pressure or descending air that causes a plane to lose height suddenly</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107773585">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the flow of air that is driven backwards by an aircraft propeller</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107773744"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a relatively well-defined prevailing wind"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107773859">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fan-shaped deposit where a fast flowing stream flattens out</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107773995"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the radiation of alpha particles during radioactive decay"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107774126">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the normal brainwave in the electroencephalogram of a person who is awake but relaxed; occurs with a frequency of 8-12 hertz</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107774322">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an electric current that reverses direction sinusoidally; "In the US most household current is AC at 60 cycles per second"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107774533">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the alternation of two or more different forms in the life cycle of a plant or animal</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107774739">
	<b:glossaryEntry>energy derived from sources that do not use up natural resources or harm the environment</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107774928"
	 b:glossaryEntry="alternation of sexual and asexual generations"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107775043"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the strength of a electrical current measured in amperes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107775154"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an annual formation of wood in plants as they grow"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107775276">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wind in the upper atmosphere blowing above but in the opposite direction from the trade winds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107775426">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an optical illusion of motion produced by viewing a rapid succession of still pictures of a moving object; "the cinema relies on apparent motion"; "the succession of flashing lights gave an illusion of movement"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107775739"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a physical phenomenon associated with the atmosphere"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107776148"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the energy released by a nuclear reaction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107776268"
	 b:glossaryEntry="nuclear energy regarded as a source of power"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107776403"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the force by which one object attracts another"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107776633">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(immunology) the attraction between an antigen and an antibody</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107776750"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the force by which bodies repel one another"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107776903">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the outermost region of the sun''s atmosphere; visible during a solar eclipse</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107777061">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an atmospheric phenomenon consisting of bands of light caused by charged solar particles following the earth''s magnetic lines of force</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107777303"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the aurora of the southern hemisphere"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107777421"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the aurora of the northern hemisphere"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107777538"
	 b:glossaryEntry="self-induced fluorescence"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107777626">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[an unpredictable outcome that is unfortunate; "if I didn''t have bad luck I wouldn''t have any luck at all"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107777807"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a column of light (as from a beacon)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107778023">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a group of nearly parallel lines of electromagnetic radiation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107778153"
	 b:glossaryEntry="radiation of beta particles during radioactive decay"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107778298">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the normal brainwave in the encephalogram of a person who is awake and alert; occurs with a frequency between 12 and 30 hertz</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107778493">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the energy required to separate particles from a molecule or atom or nucleus; equals the mass defect</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107778674"
	 b:glossaryEntry="electric phenomena in animals or plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107778792"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a dry cold north wind in SE France"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107778884">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the pressure of the circulating blood against the walls of the blood vessels; results from the systole of the left ventricle of the heart; "adult blood pressure is considered normal at 120/80 where the first number is the systolic pressure and the second is the diastolic pressure"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107779299">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the blood pressure (as measured by a sphygmomanometer) during the contraction of the left ventricle of the heart</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107779475">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the blood pressure (as measured by a sphygmomanometer) after the contraction of the heart while the chambers of the heart refill with blood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107779679">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the pressure of the circulating blood on the arteries; "arterial pressure is the product of cardiac output and vascular resistance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107779874"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the layer of slower flow of a fluid past a surface"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107779985">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(neurophysiology) rapid fluctuations of voltage between parts of the cerebral cortex that are detectable with an electroencephalograph</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107780281"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind moving at less than 1 knot; 0 on the Beaufort scale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107780411">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a slight wind (usually refreshing); "the breeze was cooled by the lake"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107780708"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a cooling breeze from the sea (during the daytime)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107780815">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a slight movement of the air; "there wasn''t a breath of air in the room"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107780940"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind moving 1-3 knots; 1 on the Beaufort scale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107781061"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind moving 4-7 knots; 2 on the Beaufort scale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107781185"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind moving 8-12 knots; 3 on the Beaufort scale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107781311"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind moving 13-18 knots; 4 on the Beaufort scale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107781440"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind moving 19-24 knots; 5 on the Beaufort scale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107781566"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind moving 25-31 knots; 6 on the Beaufort scale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107781693">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the random motion of small particles suspended in a gas or liquid</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107781850">
	<b:glossaryEntry>discharge between electrodes creating visible streamers of ionized particles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107781988"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the light provided by a burning candle"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107782099">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an electrical phenomenon whereby an electric charge is stored</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107782251"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the reciprocal of capacitance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107782359">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a phenomenon associated with surface tension and resulting in the elevation or depression of liquids in capillaries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107782551"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a sudden violent change in the earth''s surface"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107782667">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the gradual movement and formation of continents (as described by plate tectonics)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107782813">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the outward force on a body moving in a curved path around another body</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107782966">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the inward force on a body moving in a curved path around another body</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107783118">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the formless and disordered state of matter before the creation of the cosmos</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107783247">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the quantity of unbalanced electricity in a body; "the battery needed a fresh charge"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107783439">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a deficiency of electrons; having a higher electric potential</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107783587">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a surplus of electrons; having a lower electric potential</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107783731">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marks on a glaciated rock caused by the movement of a glacier</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107783851"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an electrical force linking atoms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107784041"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the phenomenon of forming chemical bonds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107784135">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the intermolecular force that holds together the molecules in a solid or liquid</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107784269">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a chemical bond that involves sharing a pair of electrons between atoms in a molecule</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107784432">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a chemical bond in which one atom loses an electron to form a positive ion and the other atom gains to electron to form a negative ion</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107784665">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a covalent bond in which both electrons are provided by one of the atoms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107784813">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a chemical bond in which electrons are shared over many nuclei and electronic conduction occurs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107784968">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a chemical bond consisting of a hydrogen atom between two electronegative atoms (e.g., oxygen or nitrogen) with one side be a covalent bond and the other being an ionic bond</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107785201">
	<b:glossaryEntry>that part of the energy in a substance that can be released by a chemical reaction</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107785345"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a warm dry wind blowing down the E slopes of the Rockies"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107785483">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a dusty wind from the Sahara that blows toward the W coast of Africa during the winter</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107785625"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an optical aberration in which the image has colored fringes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107785752"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the movement of blood through the heart and blood vessels"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107785922">
	<b:glossaryEntry>circulation that supplies blood to all the body except to the lungs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107786056"
	 b:glossaryEntry="circulation of blood between the heart and the lungs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107786176">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any collection of particles (e.g., smoke or dust) or gases that is visible</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107786410"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a period of unusually cold weather"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107786558">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a force due to the earth''s rotation; acts on a body in motion (airplane or projectile)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107786705"
	 b:glossaryEntry="weather cold enough to cause freezing"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107786803"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one or more circles of light seen around a luminous object"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107786914">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an electrical discharge accompanied by ionization of surrounding atmosphere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107787189">
	<b:glossaryEntry>clouds of particles or gases occurring throughout interstellar space</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107787334"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a could of dust in the air"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107787417">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a large cloud of rubble and dust shaped like a mushroom and rising into the sky after an explosion (especially of a nuclear bomb)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107787642">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a faint patch of light in the night sky that appears opposite the sun; a reflection of sunlight by micrometeoric material in space</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107787844">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a side bond that links two adjacent chains of atoms in a complex molecule</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107787990"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind blowing across the path or a ship or aircraft"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107788096">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a warm dry wind that blows down the northern slopes of the Alps</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107788218"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind blowing opposite to the path of a ship or aircraft"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107788328">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wind blowing in the same direction as the path of a ship or aircraft</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107788451">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a flow of electricity through a conductor; "the current was measured in amperes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107788622"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a violent rotating windstorm"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107788758"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the circulation of cytoplasm within a cell"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107788867"
	 b:glossaryEntry="light during the daytime"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107788946">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the permanent end of all life functions in an organism or part of an organism; "the animal died a painful death"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107789164"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the organic phenomenon of rotting"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107789265">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the phenomenon of spontaneous changes in the nucleus of an atom</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107789400">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) release of material by splitting open of an organ or tissue; (botany) natural bursting open at maturity of a fruit or other reproductive body to release seeds or spores; (surgery) bursting open of a surgically closed wound</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107789725">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the normal brainwave in the encephalogram of a person in deep dreamless sleep; occurs with high voltage and low frequency (1 to 4 hertz)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107789933"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the phenomenon of sediment or gravel accumulating"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107790084">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a deposit of valuable ore occurring within definite boundaries separating it from surrounding rocks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107790241"
	 b:glossaryEntry="atmospheric moisture that has condensed because of cold"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107790404">
	<b:glossaryEntry>condensation of moisture on a cold surface; "the cold glasses were streaked with sweat"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107790543">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pleochroism of a crystal so that it exhibits two different colors when viewed from two different directions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107790706">
	<b:glossaryEntry>when light passes sharp edges or goes through narrow slits the rays are deflected and produce fringes of light and dark bands</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107790907"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an electric current that flows in one direction steadily"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107791047">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a belt of calms and light winds between the northern and southern trade winds of the Atlantic and Pacific</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107791222"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a force that moves something along"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107791330">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a miniature whirlwind strong enough to whip dust and leaves and litter into the air</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107791470"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a windstorm that lifts up clouds of dust or sand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107791597"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a wind from the east"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107791711"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a wind from the northwest"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107791811"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a strong wind from the southwest"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107791914"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a strong wind from the southeast"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107792017">
	<b:glossaryEntry>potential energy that is stored when a body is deformed (as in a coiled spring)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107792184"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a physical phenomenon involving electricity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107792565"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the product of voltage and current"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107792707"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the power output of a generator or power plant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107792804"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a field of force surrounding a charged particle"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107792912"
	 b:glossaryEntry="heating of an insulator by a high-frequency electric field"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107793035">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a form of energy associated with moving electrons and protons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107793262"
	 b:glossaryEntry="electricity produced by chemical action"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107793357">
	<b:glossaryEntry>energy made available by the flow of electric charge through a conductor</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107793543">
	<b:glossaryEntry>radiation consisting of waves of energy associated with electric and magnetic fields resulting from the acceleration of an electric charge</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107793939">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an electromagnetic wave generated by oscillations in an electric circuit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107794071"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the entire frequency range of electromagnetic waves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107794554">
	<b:glossaryEntry>spectrum of electromagnetic radiation emitted by a self-luminous source</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107794689">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) the capacity of a physical system to do work; the units of energy are joules or ergs; "energy can take a wide variety of forms"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107795077">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) the rate of doing work; measured in watts (= joules/second)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107795234">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the energy equivalent to the mass of a particle at rest in an inertial frame of reference; equal to the rest mass times the square of the speed of light</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107795444">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) a manifestation of energy; the transfer of energy from one physical system to another expressed as the product of a force and the distance through which it moves a body in the direction of that force; "work equals force times distance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107795740">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the opposite of entropy: increasing order (as resulting from growth and development)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107795896">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(thermodynamics) a measure of the amount of energy in a system that is available for doing work; entropy increases as matter and energy in the universe degrade to an ultimate state of inert uniformity</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107796186">
	<b:glossaryEntry>entropy calculated from the probability that a state could be reached by chance alone</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107796340">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a secondary phenomenon that is a by-product of another phenomenon</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107796465">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(relativity theory) a phenomenon located at a single point in space-time; the fundamental observational entity in relativity theory</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107796648">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the rising of a body of water and its overflowing onto normally dry land</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107796830">
	<b:glossaryEntry>flooding caused by a tumultuous breakup of ice in a river during the spring or summer</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107796969"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a sudden local flood of great volume and short duration"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107797100">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a wall of water rushing ahead of the flood; "we were lucky to be safe when the floodheads hit"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107797250">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a point of convergence of light (or other radiation) or a point from which it diverges</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107797402">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(ecology) a community of organisms where each member is eaten in turn by another member</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107797565">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(ecology) a hierarchy of food chains with the principle predator at the top; each level preys on the level below</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107797736">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(ecology) a community of organisms where there are several interrelated food chains</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107797906"
	 b:glossaryEntry="moderate weather; suitable for outdoor activities"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107798041">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a mirage in the Strait of Messina (attributed to the Arthurian sorcerer Morgan le Fay)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107798186">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a chemical phenomenon in which an organic molecule splits into simpler substances</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107798336">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the space around a body within which it can exert force on another similar body not in contact with it</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107798575">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an imaginary line in a field of force; direction of the line at any point is the direction of the force at that point</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107798801"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a line of force in an electrical field"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107798910"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a line of force in a magnetic field"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107799014"
	 b:glossaryEntry="precipitation in very small drops"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107799104"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the light of a fire (especially in a fireplace)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107799207">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a storm in which violent winds are drawn into the column of hot air rising over a severely bombed area</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107799365">
	<b:glossaryEntry>light emitted during absorption of radiation of some other (invisible) wavelength</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107799541"
	 b:glossaryEntry="droplets of water vapor suspended in the air near the ground"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107799705"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a large mass of fog on the sea (as seen from a distance)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107799815">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the physical influence that produces a change in a physical quantity; "force equals mass times acceleration"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107800281">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a form of lightning that moves rapidly in a zigzag path with one end divided (fork-like)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107800450"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a pale light sometimes seen at night over marshy ground"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107800619">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the resistance encountered when one body is moved in contact with another</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107800793">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the wearing down of rock particles by friction due to water or wind or ice</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107800957">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the friction between a body and the surface on which it moves (as between an automobile tire and the road)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107801145">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the atmospheric phenomenon created at the boundary between two different air masses</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107801334"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the front of an advancing mass of warmer air"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107801435"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the front of an advancing mass of colder air"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107801568"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a cold front along which squalls or thunderstorms are likely"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107801686">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a composite front when colder air surrounds a mass of warm air and forces it aloft</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107801841">
	<b:glossaryEntry>warming that results when solar radiation is trapped by the atmosphere; caused by atmospheric gases that allow sunshine to pass through but absorb heat that is radiated back from the warmed surface of the earth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107802115">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the layer of air near the earth is cooler than an overlying layer</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107802236">
	<b:glossaryEntry>upthrust of ground or pavement caused by the freezing of moist soil</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107802377">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a strong wind moving 45-90 knots; force 7 to 10 on Beaufort scale</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107802565"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind moving 32-38 knots; 7 on the Beaufort scale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107802704"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind moving 39-46 knots; 8 on the Beaufort scale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107802828"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind moving 47-54 knots; 9 on the Beaufort scale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107802953"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wind moving 55-63 knots; 10 on the Beaufort scale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107803078">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a violent weather condition with winds 64-72 knots (11 on the Beaufort scale) and precipitation and thunder and lightening</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107803468">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the central area or point of lowest barometric pressure within a storm</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107803597"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a storm blowing from the northeast"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107803701">
	<b:glossaryEntry>electromagnetic radiation emitted during radioactive decay and having an extremely short wavelength</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107803893"
	 b:glossaryEntry="light yielded by the combustion of illuminating gas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107803999">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the amount of electromagnetic radiation leaving or arriving at a point on a surface</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107804172"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a stroke of luck"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107804267"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a field of force surrounding a body of finite mass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107804383">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[the force of attraction between all masses in the universe; especially the attraction of the earth''s mass for bodies near its surface; "gravitation cannot be held responsible for people falling in love"--Albert Einstein]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107804737">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the gravity of the sun; "solar gravity creates extreme pressures and temperatures"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107804879"
	 b:glossaryEntry="smoke created by the firing of guns"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107804970">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a strong current of air; "the tree was bent almost double by the gust"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107805160"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a violent gusty wind"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107805234"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a blast of wind laden with sand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107805321">
	<b:glossaryEntry>precipitation of ice pellets when there are strong rising air currents</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107805461"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a storm during which hail falls"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107805548"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a grayish light (as at dawn or dusk or in dim interiors)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107805661">
	<b:glossaryEntry>atmospheric moisture or dust or smoke that causes reduced visibility</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107805780">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a form of energy that is transferred by a difference in temperature</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107806002"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a period of unusually hot weather"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107806153"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an extremely hot day"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107806228"
	 b:glossaryEntry="oppressively hot and humid weather"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107806319">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a severe tropical cyclone usually with heavy rains and winds moving a 73-136 knots (12 on the Beaufort scale)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107806503"
	 b:glossaryEntry="electricity produced by water power"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107806601">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a storm with freezing rain that leaves everything glazed with ice</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107806737">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the phenomenon of light emission by a body as its temperature is raised</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107806875">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the striking of a light beam on a surface; "he measured the angle of incidence of the reflected light"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107807033">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an electrical phenomenon whereby an electromotive force (EMF) is generated in a closed circuit by a change in the flow of current</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107807254">
	<b:glossaryEntry>generation of electromotive forces in each other by two adjacent circuits</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107807390">
	<b:glossaryEntry>generation of an electromotive force (EMF) in a circuit by changing the current in that circuit; usually measured in henries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107807575">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) the tendency of a body to maintain is state of rest or uniform motion unless acted upon by an external force</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107807765"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the tendency of a body to resist angular acceleration"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107807882">
	<b:glossaryEntry>electromagnetic radiation with wavelengths longer than visible light but shorter than radio waves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107808092"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the spectrum of infrared radiation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107808228">
	<b:glossaryEntry>propulsion by means of the discharge a jet of fluid toward the rear</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107808356">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a high-speed high-altitude airstream blowing from west to east near the top of the troposphere; has important effects of the formation of weather fronts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107808565">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the mechanical energy that a body has by virtue of its motion</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107808687">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the heat required for a fluid substance to break up into simpler constituents</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107808831">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the heat evolved or absorbed during the formation of one mole of a substance from its component elements</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107808999">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the heat evolved or absorbed when one mole of a substance is dissolved in a large volume of a solvent</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107809163">
	<b:glossaryEntry>heat absorbed or radiated during a change of phase at a constant temperature and pressure</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107809400">
	<b:glossaryEntry>heat liberated by a unit mass of gas at its boiling point as it condenses into a liquid; "the heat of condensation is equal to the heat of vaporization"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107809619">
	<b:glossaryEntry>heat absorbed by a unit mass of a solid at its melting point in order to convert the solid into a liquid at the same temperature; "the heat of fusion is equal to the heat of solidification"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107809869">
	<b:glossaryEntry>heat liberated by a unit mass of liquid at its freezing point when it solidifies</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107810018">
	<b:glossaryEntry>heat absorbed by a unit mass of material when it changes from a solid to a gaseous state</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107810172">
	<b:glossaryEntry>heat absorbed by a unit mass of a material at its boiling point in order to convert the material into a gas at the same temperature</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107810393">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the heat required to raise the temperature of one gram of a substance one degree centigrade</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107810544"
	 b:glossaryEntry="light from a lamp"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107810617"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an easterly wind in the western Mediterranean area"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107810722">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the mechanical advantage gained by being in a position to use a lever</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107810857">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the organic phenomenon that distinguishes living organisms from nonliving ones; "there is no life on the moon"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107811054">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characteristic life processes and phenomena of living organisms: "the biology of viruses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107811197"
	 b:glossaryEntry="life sustained in the presence of air or oxygen"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107811301">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a series of stages through which an organism passes between recurrences of a primary stage</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107811448">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) electromagnetic radiation that can produce a visual sensation; "the light was filtered through a soft glass window"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107812040">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an acronym for Emissions of Light and Very low frequency perturbations due to Electromagnetic pulse Sources; extremely bright extremely short (less than a msec) electrical flashes forming a huge ring (up to 400 km diameter) in the ionosphere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107812333">
	<b:glossaryEntry>atmospheric discharges (lasting 10 msec) bursting from the tops of giant storm clouds in blue cones that widen as they flash upward</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107812527">
	<b:glossaryEntry>emission of light due to electricity discharging from cloud to cloud or from cloud to earth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107812710">
	<b:glossaryEntry>atmospheric electricity (lasting 10 msec) appearing as globular flashes of red (pink to blood-red) light rising to heights of 60 miles (sometimes seen together with elves)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107812949"
	 b:glossaryEntry="electrical discharges in the atmosphere"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107813148"
	 b:glossaryEntry="light not due to incandescence; occurs at low temperatures"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107813301">
	<b:glossaryEntry>luminescence produced by physiological processes (as in the firefly)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107813431">
	<b:glossaryEntry>luminescence resulting from a chemical reaction as the oxidation of luciferin in fireflies</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107813585"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the energy associated with visible light"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107813687">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the magnetic field of a planet; the volume around the planet in which charged particles are subject more to the planet''s magnetic field than to the solar magnetic field</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107813915"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the magnetic field of the sun"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107814030">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the lines of force surrounding a permanent magnet or a moving charged particle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107814228">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a single pulsation of an oscillation produced by adding two waves of different frequencies; has a frequency equal to the difference between the two oscillations</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107814439">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a vibration of large amplitude produced by a relatively small vibration near the same frequency of vibration as the natural frequency of the resonating system</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107814653"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(physics) vibration produced by resonance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107814762">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an excited state of a stable particle causing a sharp maximum in the probability of absorption of electromagnetic radiation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107814977">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the resonance absorption of a gamma ray by a nucleus identical to the nucleus that emitted the gamma ray</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107815145">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resonance of electrons or atoms or molecules or nuclei to radiation frequencies as a result of space quantization in a magnetic field</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107815361"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resonance of protons to radiation in a magnetic field"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107815486">
	<b:glossaryEntry>attraction for iron; associated with electric currents as well as magnets; characterized by fields of force</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107815796">
	<b:glossaryEntry>magnetism produced by an electric current; "electromagnetism was discovered when it was observed that a copper wire carrying an electric current can magnetize pieces of iron or steel near it"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107816050">
	<b:glossaryEntry>magnetic field creates parallel but opposing spins; varies with temperature</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107816208">
	<b:glossaryEntry>materials like copper or bismuth become magnetized in a magnetic field with a polarity opposite to the magnetic force; unlike iron they are slightly repelled by a magnet</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107816436">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a phenomenon in ferrites where there can be incomplete cancellation of antiferromagnetic arranged spins giving a net magnetic moment</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107816629">
	<b:glossaryEntry>materials like iron (nickel or cobalt) become magnetized in a magnetic field and retain their magnetism when the field is removed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107816819">
	<b:glossaryEntry>materials like aluminum or platinum become magnetized in a magnetic field but it disappears when the field is removed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107816996">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a physical phenomenon associated with the equilibrium or motion of objects</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107817228">
	<b:glossaryEntry>mechanical vibrations transmitted by an elastic medium; "falling trees make a sound in the forest even when no one is there to hear them"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107817417"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the path followed by a moving object"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107817555"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the trajectory of an object in free flight"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107817677">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(spaceflight) a trajectory that passes close to a planetary body in order to gain energy from its gravitational field</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107817855">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the amount by which the mass of an atomic nucleus is less than the sum of the masses of its constituent particles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107818044"
	 b:glossaryEntry="energy in a mechanical form"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107818171"
	 b:glossaryEntry="warm weather following a freeze; snow and ice melt"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107818302"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a very short electromagnetic wave"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107818410">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the sun visible at midnight (inside the arctic or antarctic circles)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107818537"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a thin fog with condensation near the ground"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107818632"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a strong north wind that blows in France during the winter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107818744">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the moment of a couple is the product of its force and the distance between its opposing forces</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107818929"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the moment of a dipole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107819047"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the dipole moment in an electric dipole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107819155">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) a current loop gives rise to a magnetic field characteristic of a magnetic dipole; "An orbiting electron in an atom will have a magnetic dipole moment"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107819385">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the torque exerted on a magnet or dipole when it is placed in a magnetic field</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107819546">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an optical illusion in which atmospheric refraction by a layer of hot air distorts or inverts reflections of distant objects</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107819741">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a seasonal wind in S Asia; blows from the SW (bringing rain) in summer and from the NE in winter</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107819891"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any wind that changes direction with the seasons"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107820011"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a ray of moonlight"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107820113">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[the light of the moon; "moonlight is the smuggler''s enemy"; "the moon was bright enough to read by"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107820306"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the light of the stars"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107820384">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the rays of the sun; "the shingles were weathered by the sun and wind"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107820545"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a ray of sunlight"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107820644"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a beam of light generated by a laser"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107820737">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a collimated flow of particles (atoms or electrons or molecules)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107820879">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a beam of ions moving in the same direction at the same speed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107821008"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the normal degeneration and death of living cells"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107821115">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the localized death of living cells (as from the interruption of blood supply)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107821262">
	<b:glossaryEntry>respiration and other reflexes are absent; consciousness is gone; organs can be removed for transplantation before the heartbeat stops</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107821471"
	 b:glossaryEntry="radiation of neutrons (as by a neutron bomb)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107821579"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a circle of light around the sun or moon"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107821688">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a luminous halo parallel to the horizon at the altitude of the sun; caused by ice crystals in the atmosphere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107821887">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bright spot on the parhelic circle; caused by diffraction by ice crystals</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107822038"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a wind from the north"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107822189"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the steady flow of ocean water in a prevailing direction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107822359">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a warm ocean current that flows from the Gulf of Mexico northward through the Atlantic Ocean</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107822509">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a warm ocean current that flows NE off the coast of Japan into the N Pacific</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107822656">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(oceanography) a weak warm ocean current that flows S off the coast of Ecuador at Christmas time</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107822824">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a more intense El Nino that occurs every few years when the welling up of cold nutrient-rich water does not occur; kills plankton and fish and affects weather patterns</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107823066"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not permitting the passage of light"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107823186">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an optical phenomenon that results in a false or deceptive visual impression</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107823361"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a physical phenomenon related to or involving light"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107823657"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a heavy thick yellow fog"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107823749">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fluorescence that persists after the bombarding radiation has ceased</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107823881"
	 b:glossaryEntry="electricity produced by mechanical pressure on a crystal"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107824023">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the phenomenon of different colors appearing when certain crystals are viewed from different directions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107824220">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) the appearance of two or more distinctly different forms in the life cycle of some organisms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107824381">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the phenomenon in which waves of light or other radiation are restricted in direction of vibration</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107824553">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the existence of two or more forms of individuals within the same species (independent of sex differences)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107824718">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(chemistry) the existence of different kinds of crystal of the same chemical compound</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107824877">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the difference in electrical potential between two points in a circuit expressed in volts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107825060">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the mechanical energy that a body has by virtue of its position</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107825204">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the falling to earth of rain or snow or hail or sleet or mist</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107825463">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the force applied to a unit area of surface; measured in pascals (SI unit) or in dynes (cgs unit); "the compressed gas exerts an increased pressure"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107825868"
	 b:glossaryEntry='the pressure exerted by a fluid; "a head of steam"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107825969"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the pressure exerted by the atmosphere"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107826125">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the air pressure maintained in the air-tight compartment of an aircraft</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107826263"
	 b:glossaryEntry="atmospheric pressure as indicated by a barometer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107826377">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the pressure at a given point in a liquid measured in terms of the vertical height of a column of the liquid needed to produce the same pressure</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107826584">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pressure that keeps oil on the moving parts of an internal-combustion engine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107826719">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physical chemistry) the pressure exerted by a solution necessary to prevent osmosis into that solution when it is separated from the pure solvent by a semipermeable membrane</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107826956">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the difference between the instantaneous pressure at a point in a sound field and the average pressure at that point</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107827164">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the minute pressure exerted on a surface normal to the direction of propagation of a wave</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107827369">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the predominant wind direction; "the prevailing wind is from the southwest"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107827560"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a propelling force"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107827653"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a short light gust of air"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107827751">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the force used in pulling; "the pull of the moon"; "the pull of the current"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107827878">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the force used in pushing; "the push of the water on the walls of the tank"; "the thrust of the jet engines"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107828046">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(mechanics) the equal and opposite force that is produced when any force is applied to a body; "every action has an equal and opposite reaction"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107828245">
	<b:glossaryEntry>energy that is transmitted in the form of (electromagnetic) radiation; energy that exists in the absence of matter</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107828438">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the physical phenomenon of radiating energy in the form of waves or particles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107828697">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a stream of atomic or subatomic particles that may be charged positively (e.g. alpha particles) or negatively (e.g. beta particles) or not at all (e.g. neutrons)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107828926"
	 b:glossaryEntry="radiation resulting from radioactive decay"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107829039">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an electromagnetic wave with a wavelength between 0.5 cm to 30,000 m</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107829308">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a radio wave that is reflected back to earth by the ionosphere or a communications satellite; permits transmission around the curve of the earth''s surface</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107829535"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a sky wave that is reflected by the ionosphere"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107829644"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a radio wave propagated on or near the earth''s surface"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107829756"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a radio wave used to transmit and receive messages"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107829865">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the entire spectrum of electromagnetic frequencies used for communications; includes frequencies used for radio and radar and television</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107830127">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a radio wave that can be modulated in order to transmit a signal</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107830260">
	<b:glossaryEntry>water falling in drops from vapor condensed in the atmosphere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107830474"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a drop of rain"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107830562"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a storm with rain"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107830671"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a violent rainstorm near the time of an equinox"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107830795"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a short rainstorm accompanied by thunder and lightning"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107830909"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a heavy rain"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107831030"
	 b:glossaryEntry="very light rain; stronger than mist but less than a shower"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107831151">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a brief period of precipitation; "the game was interrupted by a brief shower"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107831313">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the phenomenon of a propagating wave (light or sound) being thrown back from a surface</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107831504">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the change in direction of a propagating wave (light or sound) when passing from one medium to another</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107831681">
	<b:glossaryEntry>splitting a ray into two parallel rays polarized perpendicularly</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107831825"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any mechanical force that tends to retard or oppose motion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107831994">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a material''s capacity to conduct electricity; measured as the reciprocal of electrical resistance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107832149">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a material''s opposition to the flow of electric current; measured in ohms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107832388"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ohmic resistance of a conductor"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107832476">
	<b:glossaryEntry>opposition to the flow of electric current resulting from inductance and capacitance (rather than resistance)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107832641">
	<b:glossaryEntry>opposition to the flow of sound through a surface; acoustic resistance is the real component of acoustic impedance and acoustic reactance is the imaginary component</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107832913">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) opposition to magnetic flux (analogous to electric resistance)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107833042"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the phenomenon of resistance to motion through a fluid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107833201">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the increase in aerodynamic drag as an airplane approaches the speed of sound</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107833354"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the retarding force of air friction on a moving object"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107833462">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the phenomenon of vitality and freshness being restored; "the annual rejuvenation of the landscape"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107833631">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the ability of a microscope or telescope to measure the angular separation of images that are close together</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107833814">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(computer science) the number of pixels per square inch on a computer-generated display; the greater the resolution, the better the picture</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107834010">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a light shower that falls in some locations and not others nearby</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107834156">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an association between genes in sex chromosomes that makes some characteristics appear more frequently in one sex than in the other</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107834345">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) a deformation of an object in which parallel planes remain parallel but are shifted in a direction parallel to themselves; "the shear changed the quadrilateral into a parallelogram"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107834588">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a transient shower of meteors when a meteor swarm enters the earth''s atmosphere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107834762"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a radio wave with a wavelength less than 100 meters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107834870">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a violent hot sand-laden wind on the deserts of Arabia and North Africa</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107835012">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the tendency of high-frequency alternating current to distribute near the surface of a conductor</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107835166"
	 b:glossaryEntry="partially melted snow (or a mixture of rain and snow)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107835271"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a cloud of fine particles suspended in a gas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107835410"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a stifling cloud of smoke"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107835489">
	<b:glossaryEntry>precipitation falling from clouds in the form of ice crystals</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107835667">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a light brief snowfall and gust of wind (or something resembling that); "he had to close the window against the flurries"; "there was a flurry of chicken feathers"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107835897">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an arctic atmospheric condition with clouds over snow produce a uniform whiteness and objects are difficult to see; occurs when the light reflected off the snow equals the light coming through the clouds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107836155"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a crystal of snow"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107836293"
	 b:glossaryEntry="light rain that evaporates before it reaches the ground"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107836400"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small crystals of ice"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107836593"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a storm with widespread snowfall accompanied by strong winds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107836720">
	<b:glossaryEntry>energy from the sun that is converted into thermal or electrical energy</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107836864"
	 b:glossaryEntry="radiation from the sun"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107836984">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a sudden eruption of hydrogen gas from the sun''s surface; associated with sunspots and radio interference</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107837155"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a stream of protons moving radially from the sun"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107837298">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a cooler darker spot appearing periodically on the surface of the sun; associated with a strong magnetic field</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107837471"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a wind from the south"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107837571">
	<b:glossaryEntry>electrical conduction through a gas in an applied electric field</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107837777"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the passage of electricity through a conductor"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107837945"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the transmission of heat or electricity or sound"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107838083"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the movement of a wave through a medium"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107838216">
	<b:glossaryEntry>change in the apparent frequency of a wave as observer and source move toward or away from each other</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107838394"
	 b:glossaryEntry="all the points just reached by a wave as it propagates"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107838524"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a optical aberration resulting in a distorted image"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107838642"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(economics) any indirect effect of public expenditure"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107838751"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sudden violent winds; often accompanied by precipitation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107838878"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a squall advancing along a front that forms a definite line"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107838995">
	<b:glossaryEntry>electrical signals produced by unwanted sources (atmospherics or receiver noise or unwanted transmitters)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107839169"
	 b:glossaryEntry="electricity produced by friction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107839266"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a flow of electric charge"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107839379"
	 b:glossaryEntry="electricity produced by heat (as in a thermocouple)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107839494"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(physics) force that produces strain on a physical body"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107839638">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) a stress that produces an elongation of an elastic physical body; "the direction of maximum tension moves asymptotically toward the direction of the shear"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107839857">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) deformation of a physical body under the action of applied forces</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107840003"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the degree of tension or stress at which something breaks"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107840121"
	 b:glossaryEntry="too much strain"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107840193"
	 b:glossaryEntry='light that streams; "streamers of flames"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107840308"
	 b:glossaryEntry="light from a torch or torches"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107840394">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the diffused light from the sky when the sun is below the horizon but its rays are refracted by the atmosphere of the earth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107840572">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) the transfer of energy between elementary particles or between an elementary particle and a field or between fields; mediated by gauge bosons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107840879">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an interaction between charged elementary particles that is intermediate in strength between the strong and weak interactions; mediated by photons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107841099">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a weak interaction between particles that results from their mass; mediated by gravitons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107841259">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) the interaction that binds protons and neutrons together in the nuclei of atoms; mediated by gluons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107841462">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) an interaction between elementary particles involving neutrinos or antineutrinos that is responsible for certain kinds of radioactive decay; mediated by intermediate vector bosons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107841727"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a force over an area produced by a pressure difference"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107841835">
	<b:glossaryEntry>atmospheric phenomena accompanying the daily appearance of the sun</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107841955">
	<b:glossaryEntry>atmospheric phenomena accompanying the daily disappearance of the sun</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107842077"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a glow sometimes seen in the sky after sunset"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107842178">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a phenomenon at the surface of a liquid caused by intermolecular forces</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107842347">
	<b:glossaryEntry>surface tension at the surface separating two non-miscilbe liquids</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107842509">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the straight line configuration of 3 celestial bodies (as the sun and earth and moon) in a gravitational system</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107842673"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(literary) a violent wind; "a tempest swept over the island"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107842787"
	 b:glossaryEntry="rising current of warm air"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107842867">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an electric current produced between two electrodes as a result of electrons emitted by thermionic emission</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107843039">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the normal brainwave in the encephalogram of a person who is awake but relaxed and drowsy; occurs with low frequency and low amplitude</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107843245"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a discharge of lightning accompanied by thunder"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107843377">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a storm resulting from strong rising air currents; heavy rain or hail along with thunder and lightning</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107843574">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a localized and violently destructive windstorm occurring over land characterized by a funnel-shaped cloud extending toward the ground</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107843790"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a twisting force"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107843887">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[an unpredictable phenomenon; "it''s a toss-up whether he will win or lose"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107844028">
	<b:glossaryEntry>steady winds blowing from east to west above and below the equator; "they rode the trade winds going west"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107844199">
	<b:glossaryEntry>winds blowing from west to east and lying above the trade winds in the tropics</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107844350">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a cold dry wind that blows south out of the mountains into Italy and the western Mediterranean</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107844514"
	 b:glossaryEntry="permitting the free passage of electromagnetic radiation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107844662">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pleochroism of a crystal so that it exhibits three different colors when viewed from three different directions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107844830"
	 b:glossaryEntry="unstable flow of a liquid or gas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107844950">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tropical cyclone occurring in the W Pacific or Indian oceans</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107845066"
	 b:glossaryEntry="flow in which the velocity at any point varies erratically"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107845203">
	<b:glossaryEntry>turbulent water with swells of considerable size; "heavy seas"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107845333">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a sea in which the waves are running directly against the course of the ship</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107845464"
	 b:glossaryEntry="flow in which the velocity at any point is relatively steady"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107845586">
	<b:glossaryEntry>nonturbulent flow in parallel layers that have different velocities</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107845712">
	<b:glossaryEntry>radiation lying in the ultraviolet range; wave lengths shorter than light but longer than X rays</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107845942"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the spectrum of ultraviolet radiation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107846065"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a current of air (usually coming into a room or vehicle)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107846251"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an strong upward air current"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107846333"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a strong downward air current"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107846418">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relatively weak attraction between neutral atoms and molecules arising from polarization induced in each particle by the presence of other particles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107846634">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the pressure exerted by a vapor; often understood to mean saturated vapor pressure (the vapor pressure of a vapor in contact with its liquid form)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107846859"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a reflected optical image (as seen in a plane mirror)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107846972">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the distribution of colors produced when light is dispersed by a prism</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107847141">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the rate at which energy is drawn from a source that produces a flow of electricity in a circuit; expressed in volts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107847339"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the force that produces magnetic flux"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107847442">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) a hypothetical force (not physical or chemical) once thought to cause the evolution and development of organisms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107847633"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the phenomena associated with volcanic activity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107847736"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the power to do work that is latent in a head of water"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107847847">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tornado passing over water and picking up a column of water and mist</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107847974">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the meteorological conditions: temperature and wind and clouds and precipitation; "they were hoping for good weather"; "every day we have weather conditions and yesterday was no exception"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107848440">
	<b:glossaryEntry>violent or severe weather (viewed as caused by the action of the four elements); "they felt the full fury of the elements"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107848617"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a wind from the west"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107848713">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a more or less vertical column of air whirling around itself as it moves over the surface of the Earth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107848889">
	<b:glossaryEntry>air moving (sometimes with considerable force) from an area of high pressure to an area of low pressure; "trees bent under the fierce winds"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107849549"
	 b:glossaryEntry="power derived from the wind (as by windmills)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107849669"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a storm consisting of violent winds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107849832">
	<b:glossaryEntry>electromagnetic radiation of short wavelength produced when high-speed electrons strike a solid target</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107850015">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the scattering of X rays by the atoms of a crystal; the diffraction pattern shows structure of the crystal</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107850185">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a luminous tract in the sky; a reflection of sunlight from cosmic dust in the plane of the ecliptic; visible just before sunrise and just after sunset</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107850396">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a single frequency (or very narrow band) of radiation in a spectrum</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107850533">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the phenomenon of floating (remaining on the surface of a liquid)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107850667">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the apparent displacement of an object as seen from two different points that are not on a line with the object</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107850869">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the parallax of a celestial body using two points in the earth''s orbit around the sun as the baseline</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107851074"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the heliocentric parallax of a star"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107851172">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the parallax of a celestial body using two points on the surface of the earth as the earth rotates</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107851373">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the maximum parallax observed when the celestial body is at the horizon</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107851510"
	 b:glossaryEntry="taxonomic kingdom comprising all living or extinct plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107851883">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used only in former classifications: comprising what is now considered a heterogeneous assemblage of flowerless and seedless organisms: algae; bacteria; fungi; lichens</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107852127">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of a group of cryptogamic organisms consisting principally of a thallus and thus showing no differentiation into stem and root and leaf</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107852324"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of various plant parts that resemble buttons"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107852425">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a plant body without true stems or roots or leaves or vascular system; characteristic of the thallophytes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107852602">
	<b:glossaryEntry>thin crusty lichen thallus; adheres closely to or is embedded in the surface on which it grows</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107852759">
	<b:glossaryEntry>umbrellalike fruiting structure forming the top of a stalked fleshy fungus such as a mushroom</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107852930">
	<b:glossaryEntry>hood or cap-like part covering the calyx of certain plants: e.g. the California poppy</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107853070">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cup-like structure around the base of the stalk of certain fungi</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107853205"
	 b:glossaryEntry="mature fruiting body of an ascomycetous fungus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107853397">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small cushion- or blister-like asexual fruiting body consisting of a mat of hyphae produced on a host by some fungi</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107853568">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the fruiting body of a basidiomycete which bears its spores on special cells</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107853721"
	 b:glossaryEntry="outer layer of the spore-bearing organ in many fungi"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107853828">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an ascocarp having the spore-bearing layer of cells (the hymenium) on a broad disklike receptacle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107853978"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a cup-shaped ascocarp in many lichens and ascomycetous fungi"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107854113">
	<b:glossaryEntry>closed spore-bearing structure of some fungi (especially Aspergillaceae and Erysiphaceae) from which spores are released only by decay or disintegration</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107854340">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an organ or body resembling a stalk especially the stalklike outgrowth of the thallus of certain lichens on which the ascocarp is borne</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107854548"
	 b:glossaryEntry="stalk of a moss capsule"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107854622">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in former classifications: comprising plants with a vascular system including ferns and fern allies as well as seed plants</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107854865"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the order of plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107856275"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any plant grown for its beauty or ornamental value"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107856382">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any flowerless plant such as a fern (pteridophyte) or moss (bryophyte) in which growth occurs only at the tip of the main stem</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107856562"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a plant that reproduces or is reproduced by apomixis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107856668"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a plant that lives in or on water"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107856755">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a division of nonflowering plants characterized by rhizoids rather than true roots and having little or no organized vascular tissue and showing alternation of generations between gamete-bearing forms and spore-bearing forms; comprises true mosses (Bryopsida) and liverworts (Hepaticopsida) and hornworts (Anthoceropsida)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107857286"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of numerous plants of the division Bryophyta"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107857465"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tiny leafy-stemmed flowerless plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107857606"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a family of mosses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107857827"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of mosses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107858064">
	<b:glossaryEntry>hornworts: in some classification systems included in the class Hepaticopsida</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107858282">
	<b:glossaryEntry>hornworts; liverworts having a thalloid gametophyte; in some classification systems included in the class Hepaticopsida</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107858523"
	 b:glossaryEntry="hornworts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107858657"
	 b:glossaryEntry="hornworts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107858761"
	 b:glossaryEntry="liverworts with slender hornlike 2-valved capsules"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107858885">
	<b:glossaryEntry>true mosses: bryophytes having leafy rather than thalloid gametophytes: comprises orders Andreaeales; Bryales; Dicranales; Eubryales; Sphagnales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107859239">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a moss in which the main axis is terminated by the archegonium (and hence the capsule)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107859417">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a moss having the archegonium or antheridium on a short side branch rather than the main stalk</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107859607"
	 b:glossaryEntry="comprises a single genus: Andreaea"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107859757">
	<b:glossaryEntry>brown or blackish Alpine mosses having a dehiscent capsule with 4 longitudinal slits</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107859932">
	<b:glossaryEntry>category used in some classification systems for mosses having the spore case separated from the capsule wall by a hollow intercellular space</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107860162">
	<b:glossaryEntry>widely distributed order of mosses with erect gametophores and sporophytes at the tips of stems</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107860371">
	<b:glossaryEntry>mosses having costate leaves and long-stalked capsules with cleft peristome</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107860563"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of Dicranaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107860679">
	<b:glossaryEntry>mosses with perennial erect gametophores and stems with rows of leaves and drooping capsules</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107860902"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a family of acrocarpous mosses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107861043">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Bryaceae: mosses distinguished by mostly erect and tufted gametophytes and symmetrical short-necked capsules</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107861254">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family of erect mosses with club-shaped paraphyses and the hexagonal cells of the upper leaf surface; sometimes treated as a subfamily of Bryaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107861511"
	 b:glossaryEntry="mosses similar to those of genus Bryum but larger"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107861645">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coextensive with the genus Sphagnum; in some classifications isolated in a separate subclass</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107861851">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a large genus constituting the order Sphagnales: atypical mosses of temperate bogs with leaves that can hold much water</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107862069">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various pale or ashy mosses of the genus Sphagnum whose decomposed remains form peat</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107862273">
	<b:glossaryEntry>liverworts: comprises orders Anthocerotales; Jungermanniales; Marchantiales; Sphaerocarpales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107862572">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous small green nonvascular plants of the class Hepaticopsida growing in wet places and resembling green seaweeds or leafy mosses</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107862842"
	 b:glossaryEntry="large order of chiefly tropical liverworts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107863027">
	<b:glossaryEntry>mosslike liverwort with tiny scalelike leaves; usually epiphytic</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107863185"
	 b:glossaryEntry="comprising the leafy members of the order Jungermanniales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107863350"
	 b:glossaryEntry="liverworts with gametophyte differentiated internally"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107863523">
	<b:glossaryEntry>liverworts with prostrate and usually dichotomously branched thalli</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107863713">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of Marchantiaceae; liverworts that reproduce asexually by gemmae and have stalked antheridiophores</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107863936"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a common liverwort"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107864052"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small order sometimes included in the order Jungermanniales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107864235">
	<b:glossaryEntry>liverworts with bilaterally symmetrical gametophytes; sometimes placed in the order Jungermanniales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107864461">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of Sphaerocarpaceae; liverworts with small many-lobed usually orbicular thallus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107864690">
	<b:glossaryEntry>containing all the vascular plants that do not bear seeds: ferns, horsetails, club mosses, and whisk ferns; in some classifications considered a subdivision of Tracheophyta</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107865135"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of Carboniferous fossil ferns"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107865271">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Carboniferous fossil fern characterized by a regular arrangement of the leaflets resembling a comb</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107865446"
	 b:glossaryEntry="plants having vascular tissue and reproducing by spores"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107865636">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous flowerless and seedless vascular plants having true roots from a rhizome and fronds that uncurl upward; reproduce by spores</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107867448"
	 b:glossaryEntry="pteridophytes of other classes than Filicopsida"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107867623">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small usually single-celled reproductive body produced especially by certain bacteria and algae and fungi and nonflowering plants</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107868038"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a sexually produced fungal spore borne on a basidium"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107868149">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a small asexual spore that develops inside the cell of some bacteria and algae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107868283"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a nonmotile spore of red algae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107868389">
	<b:glossaryEntry>thick-walled asexual resting spore of certain fungi and algae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107868509"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an asexually produced fungal spore formed on a conidiophore"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107868638"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a specialized fungal hypha that produces conidia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107868745">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a thick-walled sexual spore that develops from a fertilized oosphere in some algae and fungi</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107868891"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used especially of lower plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107868977"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one of the four asexual spores produced within a sporangium"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107869093">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an asexual spore of some algae and fungi that moves by means of flagella</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107869220">
	<b:glossaryEntry>dust-like asexual spore of ferns; once thought to be seeds and to make the possessor invisible</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107869389">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fleshy spore-bearing inner mass of e.g. a puffball or stinkhorn</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107869523">
	<b:glossaryEntry>spore-bearing layer of cells in certain fungi containing asci or basidia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107869669"
	 b:glossaryEntry="flask-shaped asexual structure containing conidia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107869774">
	<b:glossaryEntry>specialized leaf branch in certain aquatic ferns that encloses the sori or clusters of sporangia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107869939">
	<b:glossaryEntry>seed plants; comprises the Angiospermae (or Magnoliophyta) and Gymnospermae (or Gymnospermophyta); in some classification systems Spermatophyta is coordinate with Pteridophyta (spore producing plants having vascular tissue and roots) and Bryophyta (spore producing plants lacking vascular tissue and roots)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107870387">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in former classification systems: one of two major plant divisions, including all seed-bearing plants; superseded by the division Spermatophyta</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107870626">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in former classification systems: one of two major plant divisions, including all plants that do not bear seeds: ferns, mosses, algae, fungi</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107870861">
	<b:glossaryEntry>formerly recognized taxonomic group including all flowerless and seedless plants that reproduce by means of spores: ferns, mosses, algae, fungi</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107871078"
	 b:glossaryEntry="plant that reproduces by means of seeds not spores"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107871286"
	 b:glossaryEntry="young plant or tree grown from a seed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107871378"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any seed plant yielding balsam"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107871480">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a plant that completes its entire life cycle within the space of a year</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107871604">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a plant having a life cycle that normally takes two seasons from germination to death to complete; flowering biennials usually bloom and fruit in the second season</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107871822"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a plant lasting for three seasons or more"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107871937"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a plant originally cultivated but now growing wild"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107872040"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a family of gymnosperms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107872452"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of gymnosperms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107873672">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family of flowering plants having a single cotyledon (embryonic leaf) in the seed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107874446">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family of monocotyledonous plants of the subclass Liliidae; mostly herbs usually with petaloid sepals and petals and compound pistils</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107875116">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of flowering plants having a single cotyledon (embryonic leaf) in the seed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107879487">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of monocotyledonous plants comprising mostly herbs having usually petaloid sepals and petals and compound pistils</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107881006">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family of flowering plants having two cotyledons (embryonic leaves) in the seed which usually appear at germination</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107882445">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family of dicotyledonous flowering plants regarded as among the most primitive of extant angiosperms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107882921">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family of mostly woody dicotyledonous flowering plants with flowers often unisexual and often borne in catkins</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107883227">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family of relatively early dicotyledonous plants including mostly flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107883572">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family of more or less advanced dicotyledonous trees and shrubs and herbs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107884417">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family of more or less advanced dicotyledonous herbs and some trees and shrubs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107884869"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a family of dicotyledonous plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107885598">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of flowering plants having two cotyledons (embryonic leaves) in the seed which usually appear at germination</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107890004">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of dicotyledonous flowering plants regarded as among the most primitive of extant angiosperms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107891270">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of mostly woody relatively primitive dicotyledonous flowering plants with flowers often unisexual and often borne in catkins</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107891757">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of relatively early dicotyledonous plants including mostly flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107893468">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of more or less advanced dicotyledonous trees and shrubs and herbs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107897335">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of more or less advanced dicotyledonous herbs and some trees and shrubs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107903239"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of dicotyledonous plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107908610"
	 b:glossaryEntry="includes lichen families"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107909973"
	 b:glossaryEntry="includes lichen genera"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107912467"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the order of fungi"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107913103">
	<b:glossaryEntry>plants having naked seeds not enclosed in an ovary; in some systems considered a class (Gymnospermae) and in others a division (Gymnospermophyta); comprises three subdivisions (or classes): Cycadophytina (class Cycadopsida) and Gnetophytina (class Gnetopsida) and Coniferophytina (class Coniferopsida); in some classifications the Coniferophytina are divided into three groups: Pinophytina (class Pinopsida) and Ginkgophytina (class Ginkgopsida) and Taxophytina (class Taxopsida)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107913899">
	<b:glossaryEntry>plants of the class Gymnospermae having seeds not enclosed in an ovary</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107914117">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an ancestral fossil type from which modern gymnosperms are thought to have derived</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107914259">
	<b:glossaryEntry>gymnospermous flowering plants; supposed link between conifers and angiosperms; in some systems classified as as class (Gnetopsida) and in others as a subdivision (Gnetophytina or Gnetophyta)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107914619">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chiefly tropical or xerophytic woody plants; practically unknown as fossils but considered close to the ancestral line of angiosperms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107914900">
	<b:glossaryEntry>plants having small unisexual flowers and fleshy or winged fruit: in some classifications includes the genera Ephedra and Welwitschia as well as Gnetum</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107915164">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Gnetaceae; small trees or shrubs usually with climbing jointed stems and terminal spikes of flowers with orange-red seeds clustered in rough cones</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107915425">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small tropical tree with tiered branches and divaricate branchlets having broad glossy dark green leaves; exploited for its edible young leaves and seeds that provide a fine flour</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107915692"
	 b:glossaryEntry="ephedras: in some classifications included in the Gnetaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107915868">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type and sole genus of Ephedraceae: tropical and subtropical evergreen shrubby or creeping plants native to dry and inhospitable regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107916102">
	<b:glossaryEntry>jointed and nearly leafless desert shrub having reduced scalelike leaves and reddish fleshy seeds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107916302"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Chinese ephedra yielding ephedrine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107916407"
	 b:glossaryEntry="in some classifications included in the Gnetaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107916581"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type and sole genus of Welwitschiaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107916740">
	<b:glossaryEntry>curious plant of arid regions of southwestern Africa having a yard-high and yard-wide turniplike trunk with a deep taproot and two large persistent woody straplike leaves growing from the base; living relic of a flora long disappeared; some may be 700-5000 years old</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107917107">
	<b:glossaryEntry>palmlike gymnosperms: includes the surviving order Cycadales and several extinct orders; possibly not a natural group; in some systems considered a class (Cycadopsida) and in others a subdivision (Cycadophytina or Cycadophyta)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107917552">
	<b:glossaryEntry>primitive tropical gymnosperms abundant in the Mesozoic, now reduced to a few scattered tropical forms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107917785">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any tropical gymnosperm of the order Cycadales; having unbranched stems with a crown of fernlike leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107918102">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ancient palmlike plants closely related to ferns in that fertilization is by means of spermatozoids</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107918331">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of Cycadaceae: genus of widely distributed Old World evergreen tropical trees having pinnate leaves and columnar stems covered with persistent bases of old leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107918608"
	 b:glossaryEntry="dwarf palmlike cycad of Japan that yields sago"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107918764"
	 b:glossaryEntry="southeastern Indian cycad with palmlike foliage"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107918918">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a family of cycads often included in the family Cycadaceae: zamias</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107919188">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of small evergreen tropical and subtropocal American cycads</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107919349">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various cycads of the genus Zamia; among the smallest and most verdant cycads</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107919522">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small tough woody zamia of Florida and West Indies and Cuba; roots and half-buried stems yield an arrowroot</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107919735">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of Mexican cycads; sometimes classified in family Cycadaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107919909">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a small cycad of the genus Ceratozamia having a short scaly woody trunk and fernlike foliage and woody cones; Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107920102">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of arborescent cycads of Mexico and Central America; sometimes classified in family Cycadaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107920304">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any cycad of the genus Dioon; handsome palmlike cycads with robust crowns of leaves and rugged trunks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107920476">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of arborescent African cycads; sometimes classified in family_Cycadaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107920677">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous cycads of the genus Encephalartos having stout cylindrical trunks and a terminal crown of long often spiny pinnate leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107920893">
	<b:glossaryEntry>South African cycad; the farinaceous pith of the fruit used as food</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107921061">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of large evergreen Australian cycads; sometimes classified in family Cycadaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107921285">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any treelike cycad of the genus Macrozamia having erect trunks and pinnate leaves and large cones with sometimes edible nuts; Australia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107921496"
	 b:glossaryEntry="large attractive palmlike evergreen cycad of New South Wales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107921653"
	 b:glossaryEntry="large attractive palmlike evergreen cycad of New South Wales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107921810"
	 b:glossaryEntry="fossil gymnospermous plants of the Carboniferous"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107921978"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a family of fossil gymnospermous plants of the Carboniferous"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107922161"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type of the Bennettitales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107922283">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extinct gymnosperms most of Carboniferous to Jurassic: seed ferns and allies</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107922489">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fossil gymnospermous trees or climbing plants from the Devonian: seed ferns</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107922732">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used in some classification systems: a group of extinct fossil gymnosperms coextensive with the order Cycadofilicales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107922981"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of fossil seed ferns of the Carboniferous"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107923127">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an extinct seed-producing fernlike plant of the order Cycadofilicales (or group Pteridospermae)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107923293">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cone-bearing gymnosperms dating from the Carboniferous period; most are substantial trees; includes the classes Pinopsida (subdivision Pinophytina) and Ginkgopsida (subdivision Ginkgophytina) and Taxopsida (subdivision Taxophytina) which in turn include the surviving orders Coniferales and Taxales (yews) and sometimes Ginkgoales as well as extinct orders such as Cordaitales (of the Carboniferous and Permian) and Volztiales (of the Permian-Jurassic)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107924005">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extinct plants having tall arborescent trunks comparable to or more advanced than cycads; known from the Pennsylvanian; probably extinct since the Mesozoic</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107924276">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chiefly Paleozoic plants; Cordaites is the chief and typical genus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107924461">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall Paleozoic trees superficially resembling modern screw pines; structurally intermediate in some ways between cycads and conifers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107924686">
	<b:glossaryEntry>most conifers: in some systems classified as a class (Pinopsida) and in others as a subdivision (Pinophytina); used in some classifications for one of five subdivisions of Gymnospermophyta</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107925007">
	<b:glossaryEntry>profusely branching and chiefly evergreen trees and some shrubs having narrow or needlelike leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107925354"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a family of Pinaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107925670"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Pinaceae: large genus of true pines"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107925865"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a coniferous tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107926500">
	<b:glossaryEntry>straight-grained durable and often resinous white to yellowish timber of any of numerous trees of the genus Pinus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107926737">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pine lumber with many knots; used especially for paneling and furniture</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107926866"
	 b:glossaryEntry="soft white wood of white pine trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107926977"
	 b:glossaryEntry="hard yellowish wood of a yellow pine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107927090"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of several low-growing pines of western North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107927299"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of several pinons bearing edible nutlike seeds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107927477"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small 2- or 3-needled pinon of Mexico and southern Texas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107927647">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small compact 2-needled pinon of southwestern United States; important as a nut pine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107927813">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pinon of southwestern United States having solitary needles and often many stems; important as a nut pine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107928035">
	<b:glossaryEntry>2- or 3-needled pinon mostly of northwestern California coast</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107928187">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very small tree similar to Rocky_mountain_pinon but having a single needle per fascicle; similar to Parry''s pinyon in range</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107928408">
	<b:glossaryEntry>5-needled pinon of southern California and northern Baja California having (sometimes 3- or 4-needled showing hybridization from Pinus californiarum)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107928655">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large 2-needled pine of southeastern United States with light soft wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107928799"
	 b:glossaryEntry="large 2-needled timber pine of southeastern Europe"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107928920">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large 3-needled pine of the eastern United States and southeastern Canada; closely related to the pond pine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107929121">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large 3-needled pine of sandy swamps of southeastern United States; needles longer than those of the northern pitch pine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107929314">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized 2-needled pine of southern Europe having a spreading crown; widely cultivated for its sweet almond-like seeds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107929562">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large 5-needled European pine; yields cembra nuts and a resinous exudate</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107929776"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the seed of the Swiss pine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107929890">
	<b:glossaryEntry>low shrubby pine of central Europe with short bright green needles in bunches of two</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107930116">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small slow-growing pine of western United States similar to the bristlecone pine; chocolate brown bark in plates and short needles in bunches of 5; crown conic but becoming rough and twisted; oldest plant in the world growing to 5000 years in cold semi-desert mountain tops</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107930465">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several 5-needled pines with white wood and smooth usually light gray bark when young; especially the eastern white pine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107930758">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall-growing pine of eastern North America; bark is brown with longitudinal fissures when mature; valued as a timber tree</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107930998">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall pine of western North America with stout blue-green needles; bark is gray-brown with rectangular plates when mature</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107931231">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-size pine of northwestern Mexico; bark is dark brown and furrowed when mature</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107931406">
	<b:glossaryEntry>western North American pine with long needles and very flexible limbs and dark-gray furrowed bark</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107931578">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small pine of western North America; having smooth gray-white bark and soft brittle wood; similar to limber pine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107931789"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of various pines having yellow wood"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107931959">
	<b:glossaryEntry>common and widely distributed tall timber pine of western North America having dark green needles in bunches of 2 to 5 and thick bark with dark brown plates when mature</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107932252">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall symmetrical pine of western North America having long blue-green needles in bunches of 3 and elongated cones on spreading somewhat pendulous branches; sometimes classified as a variety of ponderosa pine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107932565">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shrubby 2-needled pine of coastal northwestern United States; red to yellow-brown bark fissured into small squares</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107932796"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tall subspecies of lodgepole pine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107932924">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall spreading 3-needled pine of southeastern United States having reddish-brown fissured bark and a full bushy upper head</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107933140">
	<b:glossaryEntry>slender medium-sized 2-needled pine of eastern North America; with yellow-green needles and scaly gray to red-brown fissured bark</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107933343">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several pines that prefer or endure moist situations such as loblolly pine or longleaf pine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107933498">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large 3-needled pine of southeastern United States having very long needles and gnarled twisted limbs; bark is red-brown deeply ridgedged; an important timber tree</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107933790">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large pine of southrn United States having short needles in bunches of 2-3 and red-brown bark when mature</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107934015">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pine of eastern North America having long needles in bunches of two and reddish bark</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107934191">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium large 2-needled pine of northern Europe and Asia having flaking red-brown bark</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107934379">
	<b:glossaryEntry>common small shrubby pine of the eastern United States having straggling often twisted or branches and short needles in bunches of 2</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107934617">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall California pine with long needles in bunches of 3, a dense crown, and dark brown deeply fissured bark</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107934799">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small slow-growing upland pine of western United States (Rocky Mountains) having dense branches with fissured rust-brown bark and short needles in bunches of 5 and thorn-tipped cone scales; among the oldest living things some over 4500 years old</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107935158">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small 2-needled upland pine of the eastern United States (Appalachians) having dark brown flaking bark and thorn-tipped cone scales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107935401">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized 3-needled pine of United States Pacific coast having a prominent knob on each scale of the cone</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107935587">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pine native to Japan and Korea having a wide-spreading irregular crown when mature; grown as an ornamental</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107935798">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large Japanese ornamental having long needles in bunches of 2; widely planted in United States because of its resistance to salt and smog</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107936033">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized 5-needled pine of southwestern California having long cylindrical cones</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107936255"
	 b:glossaryEntry="larches"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107936366">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous conifers of the genus Larix all having deciduous needlelike leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107936641"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wood of a larch tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107936732">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized larch of Canada and northern United States including Alaska having a broad conic crown and rust-brown scaly bark</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107936960">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall larch of western North America have pale green sharply pointed leaves and oblong cones; an important timber tree</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107937192">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized larch of North American Rocky Mountains; closely related to Larix_occidentalis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107937361">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall European tree having a slender conic crown, flat needlelike leaves, and hairy cone scales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107937532">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized larch of northeastern Russia and Siberia having narrowly conic crown and soft narrow bright-green leaves; used in cultivation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107937764"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one species: golden larch"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107937905">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Chinese larchlike deciduous conifer with golden yellow leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107938067"
	 b:glossaryEntry="true firs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107938180">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various evergreen trees of the genus Abies; chiefly of upland areas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107938419"
	 b:glossaryEntry="nonresinous wood of a fir tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107938536">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various true firs having leaves white or silvery white beneath</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107938788">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium to tall fir of western North America having a conic crown and branches in tiers; leaves smell of orange when crushed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107939054">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall timber tree of central and southern Europe having a regular crown and gray bark</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107939234">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium to tall fir of central to western United States having a narrow erect crown and soft wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107939457">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized fir of northeastern North America; leaves smell of balsam when crushed; much used for pulpwood and Christmas trees</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107939691">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small fast-growing but short-lived fir of southern Alleghenies similar to balsam fir but with very short leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107939875">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lofty fir of the Pacific coast of northwestern America having long curving branches and deep green leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107940098">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-tall Rocky Mountain timber tree having a narrow-conic tocolumnar crown</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107940267">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a pyramidal fir of southwestern California having spiny pointed leaves and cone scales with long spines</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107940484"
	 b:glossaryEntry="true cedars"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107940601"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any cedar of the genus Cedrus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107940800">
	<b:glossaryEntry>durable aromatic wood of any of numerous cedar trees; especially wood of the red cedar often used for cedar chests</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107941052"
	 b:glossaryEntry="fragrant reddish wood of any of various red cedar trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107941181"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wood of a pencil cedar tree; used for making pencils"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107941311">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cedar of Lebanon and northwestern Syria that attains great age and height</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107941463">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall East Indian cedar having spreading branches with nodding tips; highly valued for its appearance as well as its timber</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107941688">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall Algerian evergreen of Atlas mountains with blue-green leaves; widely planted as an ornamental</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107941863"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of temperate and arctic evergreen trees (see spruce)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107942027"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any coniferous tree of the genus Picea"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107942318">
	<b:glossaryEntry>light soft moderately strong wood of spruce trees; used especially for timbers and millwork</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107942481">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall pyramidal spruce native to northern Europe having dark green foliage on spreading branches with pendulous branchlets and long pendulous cones</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107942701">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized spruce of California and Oregon having pendulous branches</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107942869">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall spruce of Rocky Mountains and British Columbia with blue-green needles and acutely conic crown; wood used for rough lumber and boxes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107943110">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized spruce of northeastern North America having short blue-green leaves and slender cones</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107943282">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small spruce of boggy areas of northeastern North America having spreading branches with dense foliage; inferior wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107943488">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall spruce of northern Europe and Asia; resembles Norway spruce</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107943630">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen tree of the Caucasus and Asia Minor used as an ornamental having pendulous branchlets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107943806">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall spruce with blue-green needles and dense conic crown; older trees become columnar with lower branches sweeping downward</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107944047">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized spruce of eastern North America; chief lumber spruce of the area; source of pulpwood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107944249"
	 b:glossaryEntry="hemlock; hemlock fir; hemlock spruce"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107944389"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an evergreen tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107944585">
	<b:glossaryEntry>soft coarse splintery wood of a hemlock tree especially the western hemlock</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107944733">
	<b:glossaryEntry>common forest tree of the eastern United States and Canada; used especially for pulpwood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107944935">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized evergreen of southeastern United States having spreading branches and widely diverging cone scales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107945129">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large evergreen of western United States; wood much harder than Canadian hemlock</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107945308">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall evergreen of western North America; commercially important timber tree</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107945505"
	 b:glossaryEntry="douglas fir; closely related to genera Larix and Cathaya"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107945677">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall evergreen timber tree of western North America having resinous wood and short needles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107945899"
	 b:glossaryEntry="strong durable timber of a douglas fir"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107946014">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lofty douglas fir of northwestern North America having short needles and egg-shaped cones</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107946268"
	 b:glossaryEntry="douglas fir of California having cones 4-8 inches long"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107946432"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one species; related to Pseudotsuga and Larix"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107946575">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Chinese evergreen conifer discovered in 1955; not yet cultivated elsewhere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107946722"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cypresses and junipers and many cedars"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107947238">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous trees of the family Cupressaceae that resemble cedars</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107947572"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of Cupressaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107947710">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous evergreen conifers of the genus Cupressus of north temperate regions having dark scalelike leaves and rounded cones</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107948092">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wood of any of various cypress trees especially of the genus Cupressus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107948361">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small sometimes shrubby tree native to California; often used as an ornamental; in some classification systems includes the Pygmy cypress and the Santa Cruz cypress</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107948607">
	<b:glossaryEntry>rare small cypress native to northern California; sometimes considered the same species as gowen cypress</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107948821">
	<b:glossaryEntry>rare California cypress taller than but closely related to gowen cypress and sometimes considered the same species</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107949056"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Arizona timber tree with bluish silvery foliage"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107949187">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relatively low wide-spreading endemic on Guadalupe Island; cultivated for its bluish foliage</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107949369">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall California cypress endemic on Monterey Bay; widely used for ornament as well as reforestation and shelterbelt planting</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107949578">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall spreading evergreen native to Mexico having drooping branches; believed to have been introduced into Portugal from Goa its Indian territory</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107949843">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall Eurasian cypress with thin gray bark and ascending branches</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107950018"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of Athrotaxes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107950153">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen of Tasmanian mountains having sharp-pointed leaves the curve inward</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107950339"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one species; formerly included in genus Libocedrus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107950507">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small well-shaped South American evergreen having coppery bark and pretty foliage</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107950692">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen monoecious coniferous trees or shrubs: cypress pines</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107950866">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several evergreen trees or shrubs of Australia and northern New Caledonia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107951096"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Australian cypress pine having globular cones"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107951232">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Australian tree with small flattened scales as leaves and numerous dark brown seed; valued for its timber and resin</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107951476"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small tree or shrub of southern Australia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107951626"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Australian cypress pine with fibrous inner bark"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107951759">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall evergreens of western North America and eastern Asia; formerly included in genus Libocedrus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107951969">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall tree of North American Pacific coast having cypress-like foliage and cinnamon-red bark</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107952197"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of Chamaecyparis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107952379">
	<b:glossaryEntry>slow-growing medium-sized cedar of east coast of the United States; resembles American arborvitae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107952660">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large timber tree of western North America with trunk diameter to 12 feet and height to 200 feet</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107952936"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the wood of the Port Orford cedar tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107953057">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall evergreen of North American Pacific coast often cultivated for ornament</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107953289"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Japanese cedar; sugi"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107953425">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall evergreen of Japan and China yielding valuable soft wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107953610"
	 b:glossaryEntry="junipers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107953787"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coniferous shrub or small tree with berrylike cones"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107954056">
	<b:glossaryEntry>berrylike fruit of a plant of the genus Juniperus especially the berrylike cone of the common juniper</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107954236">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several junipers with wood suitable for making pencils</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107954468">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small juniper found east of Rocky Mountains having a conic crown, brown bark that peels in shreds, and small sharp needles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107954703">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ornamental densely pyramidal juniper of Bermuda; fairly large for a juniper</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107954880"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tropical African timber tree with fragrant wood"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107955031">
	<b:glossaryEntry>juniper of swampy coastal regions of southeastern United States; similar to eastern red cedar</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107955212"
	 b:glossaryEntry="procumbant or spreading juniper"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107955330">
	<b:glossaryEntry>densely branching shrub or small tree having pungent blue berries used to flavor gin; widespread in northern hemisphere; only conifer on coasts of Iceland and Greenland</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107955599"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a procumbant variety of th common juniper"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107955745">
	<b:glossaryEntry>low to prostrate shrub of Canada and northern United States; bronzed purple in winter</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107955918">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small tree of western Texas and mountains of Mexico having spreading branches with drooping branchlets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107956122"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cypresses that resemble cedars"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107956304">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several attractive trees of southwestern South America and New Zealand and New Caledonia having glossy evergreen leaves and scented wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107956527"
	 b:glossaryEntry="New Zealand timber tree resembling the cypress"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107956666"
	 b:glossaryEntry="evergreen tree of New Zealand resembling the kawaka"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107956830">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coniferous trees; traditionally considered an independent family though recently included in Cupressaceae in some classification systems</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107957084">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of deciduous conifers comprising both living and fossil forms; 1 extant species: dawn redwood of China; variously classified as member of Pinaceae or Taxodiaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107957353">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large fast-growing Chinese monoecious tree having flat bright-green deciduous leaves and small globular cones; commonly cultivated in United States as an ornamental; known as a fossil before being discovered in China</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107957692">
	<b:glossaryEntry>redwoods; until recently considered a genus of a separate family Taxodiaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107957866">
	<b:glossaryEntry>either of two huge coniferous California trees that reach a height of 300 feet; sometimes placed in the Taxodiaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107958119">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the soft reddish wood of either of two species of sequoia trees</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107958255">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lofty evergreen of United States coastal foothills from Oregon to Big Sur</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107958451">
	<b:glossaryEntry>giant sequoias; sometimes included in the genus Sequoia; until recently placed in the Taxodiaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107958670">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extremely lofty evergreen of southern end of western foothills of Sierra Nevada in California; largest living organism</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107958964"
	 b:glossaryEntry="bald cypress; swamp cypress"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107959139">
	<b:glossaryEntry>common cypress of southeastern United States having trunk expanded at base; found in coastal swamps and flooding river bottoms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107959419">
	<b:glossaryEntry>smaller than and often included in the closely related Taxodium distichum</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107959606"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cypress of river valleys of Mexican highlands"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107959798">
	<b:glossaryEntry>literally "old man of the water": a giant specimen of great age and girth at Santa Maria del Tule</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107959951"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sandarac tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107960061">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large coniferous evergreen tree of North Africa and Spain having flattened branches and scalelike leaves yielding a hard fragrant wood; bark yields a resin used in varnishes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107960393">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a brittle and faintly aromatic translucent resin used in varnishes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107960545">
	<b:glossaryEntry>durable fragrant wood; used in building (as in the roof of Cordova Cathedral)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107960709"
	 b:glossaryEntry="red cedar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107960860">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several Asian and North American conifers of the genera Thuja and Thujopsis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107961071"
	 b:glossaryEntry="large valuable arborvitae of northwestern United States"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107961251">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small evergreen of eastern North America having tiny scalelike leaves on flattened branchlets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107961487">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Asiatic shrub or small tree widely planted in United States and Europe; in some classifications assigned to its own genus Platycladus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107961749"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one species; has close similarity to genus Thuja"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107961909">
	<b:glossaryEntry>slow-growing medium-large Japanese evergreen used as an ornamental</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107962078"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of Keteleeria"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107962200"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Asiatic conifers resembling firs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107962308">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall evergreen cone-bearing trees of South America and Australia with broad leathery leaves; in some classifications included in the Pinaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107962627">
	<b:glossaryEntry>newly discovered (1994) pine thought to have been long extinct; Australia; genus and species names not yet assigned</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107962820"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of Araucaria"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107962940">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several tall South American or Australian trees with large cones and edible seeds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107963193">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large Chilean evergreen conifer having intertwined branches and bearing edible nuts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107963370">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen of Australia and Norfolk Island in the South Pacific</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107963543">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very tall evergreen of New Caledonia and the New Hebrides similar to norfolk island pine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107963720">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Australian conifer bearing two-inch seeds tasting like roasted chestnuts; among the aborigines the tree is hereditary property protected by law</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107963981">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pine of Australia and New Guinea; yields a valuable light even-textured wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107964157"
	 b:glossaryEntry="kauri pine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107964332">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various trees of the genus Agathis; yield dammar resin</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107964574">
	<b:glossaryEntry>white close-grained wood of a tree of the genus Agathis especially Agathis australis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107964729">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall timber tree of New Zealand having white straight-grained wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107964894">
	<b:glossaryEntry>native to the Moluccas and Philippines; a source of dammar resin</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107965065">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Australian timber tree resembling the kauri but having wood much lighter in weight and softer</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107965294">
	<b:glossaryEntry>New Zealand tree with glossy leaves and scaly reddish-brown bark</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107965454"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a family of Cephalotaxaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107965643"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the genus of Cephalotaxus (see plum-yews)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107965802">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several yewlike evergreen trees and shrubs of eastern Asia having large seeds enclosed in a fleshy envelope; sometimes cultivated as ornamentals</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107966027"
	 b:glossaryEntry="nutmeg-yews"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107966165">
	<b:glossaryEntry>California evergreen having a fruit resembling a nutmeg but with a strong turpentine flavor</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107966374">
	<b:glossaryEntry>rare small evergreen of northern Florida; its glossy green leaves have an unpleasant fetid smell when crushed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107966612"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a family of Phyllocladaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107966764"
	 b:glossaryEntry="celery pine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107966893">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Australasian evergreen conifer having a graceful head of celery-like foliage composed of phyllodes borne in the axils of scalelike leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107967161"
	 b:glossaryEntry="medium-tall celery pine of Tasmania"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107967308"
	 b:glossaryEntry="medium-tall celery pine of New Zealand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107967431"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small shrubby celery pine of New Zealand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107967559">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various trees having yellowish wood or yielding a yellow extract</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107967760"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of various gymnospermous trees having yellow wood"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107967920"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of various angiospermous trees having yellow wood"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107968134"
	 b:glossaryEntry="yellow-colored wood of any of various yellowwood trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107968264">
	<b:glossaryEntry>gymnosperms with simple persistent needlelike or scalelike leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107968736">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen trees or shrubs; sometimes classified as member of the family Taxaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107969044">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any S-hemisphere evergreen of the genus Podocarpus having a pulpy fruit with one hard seed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107969208"
	 b:glossaryEntry="West Indian evergreen with medium to long leaves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107969367">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large Australian tree with straight-grained yellow wood that turns brown on exposure</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107969569"
	 b:glossaryEntry="South African tree or shrub having a rounded crown"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107969744">
	<b:glossaryEntry>erect or shrubby tree of Africa having ridged dark gray bark and rigid glossy medium to long leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107969957"
	 b:glossaryEntry="low wide-spreading coniferous shrub of New Zealand mountains"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107970117">
	<b:glossaryEntry>valuable timber tree of New Zealand yielding hard reddish wood used for furniture and bridges and wharves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107970314">
	<b:glossaryEntry>dioecious evergreen trees or shrubs; equatorial to southern and southeastern Africa: yellowwood; similar to trees or genus Podocarpus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107970561"
	 b:glossaryEntry="medium-sized tree of South Africa"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107970719">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen coniferous shrubs or trees of New Zealand to Malaysia and Philippines</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107970895">
	<b:glossaryEntry>New Zealand evergreen valued for its light easily worked wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107971115"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Australasian evergreen trees or shrubs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107971284"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tall New Zealand timber tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107971429">
	<b:glossaryEntry>New Zealand silver pine of conical habit with long slender flexuous branches; adapted to cold wet summers and high altitudes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107971647">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sickle pines: dioecious evergreen tropical trees and shrubs having sickle-shaped leaves; similar to Dacrycarpus in habit; Malaysia and Philippines to New Guinea and New ledonia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107971962"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small tropical rain forest tree of Indonesia and Malaysia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107972130">
	<b:glossaryEntry>rainforest tree or shrub of New Caledonia having a conic crown and pale green sickle-shaped leaves; host species for the rare parasite yew</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107972382">
	<b:glossaryEntry>dioecious trees or shrubs of New Zealand; similar in habit to Dacrydium</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107972567"
	 b:glossaryEntry="New Zealand shrub"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107972728">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of dioecious evergreen trees of New Zealand and Tasmania; similar to genus Dacrydium</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107972938"
	 b:glossaryEntry="timber tree of New Zealand having shiny white wood"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107973087">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Tasmanian timber tree with yellow aromatic wavy-grained wood used for carving and ship building; sometimes placed in genus Dacrydium</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107973344"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small usually shrubby conifers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107973513">
	<b:glossaryEntry>about the hardiest Podocarpaceae species; prostrate spreading shrub similar to mountain rimu; mountains of southern Chile</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107973735">
	<b:glossaryEntry>low-growning to prostrate shrub with slender trailing branches; New Zealand</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107973939">
	<b:glossaryEntry>2 species of small evergreen shrubs of Australia and Tasmania</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107974118"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small shrub or Tasmania having short stiff branches"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107974279">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of Asian evergreen trees having columnar crowns and distinguished by leaves lacking a midrib; eastern Asia including India and Philippines and New Guinea</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107974550">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium-sized tree having glossy lanceolate leaves; southern China to Taiwan and southern Japan</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107974728"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1 species: parasite yew"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107974867">
	<b:glossaryEntry>rare and endangered monoecious parasitic conifer of New Caledonia; parasitic on Falcatifolium taxoides</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107975067">
	<b:glossaryEntry>mostly dioecious evergreen conifers; leaves are softer than in Podocarpus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107975294"
	 b:glossaryEntry="New Zealand conifer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107975445"
	 b:glossaryEntry="New Zealand conifer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107975593"
	 b:glossaryEntry="South American evergreen tree or shrub"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107975756">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of tropical evergreen dioecious shrubs or trees of Oceania and tropical South America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107975952"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1 species: Prince Albert''s yew"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107976130">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small yew having attractive foliage and partially weeping branches cultivated as an ornamental; mountains of southern Chile</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107976382"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1 species"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107976507">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a large fast-growing monoecious tropical evergreen tree having large glossy lanceolate leaves; of rain forests of Sumatra and Philippines to northern Queensland</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107976789">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family comprising a single genus that until recently was considered part of Taxodiaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107977000">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type and sole genus of Sciadoptyaceae; Japanese umbrella pines</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107977177">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall evergreen having a symmetrical spreading crown and needles growing in umbrellalike whorls at ends of twigs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107977403">
	<b:glossaryEntry>yews: in some systems classified as a class (Taxopsida) and in others as a subdivision (Taxophytina) used in some classifications for one of five subdivisions of Gymnospermophyta</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107977714"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coextensive with the family Taxaceae: yews"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107977846"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sometimes classified as member of order Taxales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107978074"
	 b:glossaryEntry="yews"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107978239">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous evergreen trees or shrubs having red cup-shaped berries and flattened needlelike leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107978574">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wood of a yew; especially the durable fine-grained light brown or red wood of the English yew valued for cabinetwork and archery bows</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107978776">
	<b:glossaryEntry>predominant yew in Europe; extraordinarily long-lived and slow growing; one of the oldest species in the world</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107978995">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small or medium irregularly branched tree of the Pacific coast of North America; yields fine hard close-grained wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107979238">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shrubby hardy evergreen of China and Japan having lustrous dark green foliage; cultivated in the eastern United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107979452">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small bushy yew of northern Florida having spreading branches and very narrow leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107979631"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a gymnosperm genus having one species: New Caledonian yew"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107979804">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large yew native to New Caledonia; cultivated in eastern Australia and New Zealand and Hawaii</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107980003"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one species"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107980130">
	<b:glossaryEntry>yew of southeastern China, differing from the Old World yew in having white berries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107980316">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ginkgos: in some systems classified as a class (Ginkgopsida) and in others as a subdivision (Ginkgophytina or Ginkgophyta); used in some classifications for one of five subdivisions of Gymnospermophyta</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107980691">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coextensive with the family Ginkgoaceae: plants that first appeared in the Permian and now represented by a single surviving species; often included in Coniferales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107980968">
	<b:glossaryEntry>constituting the order Ginkgoales; includes the genus Ginkgo and extinct forms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107981179"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sole surviving genus of the Ginkgoaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107981296">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deciduous dioecious Chinese tree having fan-shaped leaves and fleshy yellow seeds; exists almost exclusively in cultivation especially as an ornamental street tree</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107981555">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used in former classifications to include all ferns and flowering plants and divided into the three classes Filicinae and Gymnospermae and Angiospermae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107981807">
	<b:glossaryEntry>comprising flowering plants that produce seeds enclosed in an ovary; in some systems considered a class (Angiospermae) and in others a division (Magnoliophyta or Anthophyta)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107982250"
	 b:glossaryEntry="plants having seeds in a closed ovary"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107982508"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tree bearing fruit enclosed in a shell or involucre or husk"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107982623">
	<b:glossaryEntry>comprising seed plants that produce an embryo with paired cotyledons and net-veined leaves; divided into six (not always well distinguished) subclasses (or superorders): Magnoliidae and Hamamelidae (considered primitive); Caryophyllidae (an early and distinctive offshoot); and three more or less advanced groups: Dilleniidae; Rosidae; Asteridae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107983696"
	 b:glossaryEntry="flowering plant with two cotyledons"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107983885">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a group of families or trees and shrubs and herbs having well-developed perianths and apocarpous ovaries and generally regarded as the most primitive extant flowering plants; contains 36 families including Magnoliaceae and Ranunculaceae; sometimes classified as a superorder</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107984335">
	<b:glossaryEntry>comprising seed plants that produce an embryo with a single cotyledon and parallel-veined leaves: includes grasses and lilies and palms and orchids; divided into four subclasses or superorders: Alismatidae; Arecidae; Commelinidae; and Liliidae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107984890"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a monocotyledonous flowering plant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107985025">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of four subclasses or superorders of Monocotyledones; comprises about 500 species in 14 families of aquatic and semi-aquatic herbs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107985259">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of four subclasses or superorder of Monocotyledones; comprises about 6400 species in 5 families of trees and shrubs and terrestrial herbs and a few free-floating aquatics including: Palmae; Araceae; Pandanaceae: and Lemnaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107985639">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of four subclasses or superorders of Monocotyledones; comprises about 19,000 species in 25 families of mostly terrestrial herbs especially of moist places including: Cyperaceae; Gramineae; Bromeliaceae; and Zingiberaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107986022">
	<b:glossaryEntry>reproductive organ of angiosperm plants especially one having showy or colorful parts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107986419">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a diminutive flower, especially one that is part of a composite flower</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107986542"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a plant cultivated for its blooms or blossoms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107988836"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wild or uncultivated flowering plant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107990473"
	 b:glossaryEntry="flower having no petals"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107990559">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a shortened compact cluster of flowers so arranged that the whole gives the effect of a single flower as in clover or members of the family Compositae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107990786">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the flowering part of a plant or arrangement of flowers on a stalk</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107991057">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small flower with a flat strap-shaped corolla usually occupying the peripheral rings of a composite flower</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107991252"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a cylindrical spikelike inflorescence"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107991350"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a partially opened flower"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107991443"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the bud of a rose"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107991514"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the male reproductive organ of a flower"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107991644">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the part of the stamen that contains pollen; usually borne on a stalk</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107991785">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fine powder produced by the anthers of seed-bearing plants; fine grains contain male gametes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107991950"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the parts of a plant involved in its reproduction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107992572">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the female ovule-bearing part of a flower composed of ovary and style and stigma</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107992809"
	 b:glossaryEntry="consists of one carpel"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107992891"
	 b:glossaryEntry="consists of two or more fused carpels"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107992990">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the narrow elongated part of the pistil between the ovary and the stigma</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107993152"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the apical end of the style where pollen is deposited"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107993277"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a simple pistil or one element of a compound pistil"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107993400"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the stalk of a stamen"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107993476"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the stalk of a plant ovule or seed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107993576">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the scar on certain seeds marking its point of attachment to the funicle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107993700"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the organ that bears the ovules of a flower"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107993833">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a small body that contains the female germ cell of a plant; develops into a seed after fertilization</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107994132">
	<b:glossaryEntry>basal part of a plant ovule opposite the micropyle; where integument and nucellus are joined</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107994297"
	 b:glossaryEntry="central part of a plant ovule; contains the embryo sac"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107994425">
	<b:glossaryEntry>minute opening in the wall of an ovule through which the pollen tube enters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107994575"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a partly inverted ovule turned back 90 degrees on its stalk"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107994699">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a completely inverted ovule turned back 180 degrees on its stalk</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107994826">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a curved ovule with the micropyle almost touching the funiculus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107994956"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a completely straight ovule with the micropyle at the apex"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107995079"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a minute epidermal pore in a leaf or stem"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107995179">
	<b:glossaryEntry>slender tubular outgrown from a pollen grain that penetrates the ovule and releases male gametes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107995333">
	<b:glossaryEntry>that part of the ovary of a flowering plant where the ovules form</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107995472">
	<b:glossaryEntry>arrangement of the ovules in the placenta and of the placentas in the ovary</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107995732">
	<b:glossaryEntry>where one or few ovules develop at the top of a simple or compound ovary</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107995870">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ovules are borne at or around the center of a compound ovary on an axis formed from joined septa</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107996031">
	<b:glossaryEntry>where one or few ovules develop at the base of a simple or compound ovary</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107996169">
	<b:glossaryEntry>where ovules develop on a central column in a compound ovary lacking septa or with septa at base only</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107996342">
	<b:glossaryEntry>with ovules on thin extensions of the placentae into a compound ovary</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107996480">
	<b:glossaryEntry>with ovules borne on the wall along the ventral suture of a simple ovary</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107996643">
	<b:glossaryEntry>where ovules develop on the wall or slight outgrowths of the wall forming broken partitions within a compound ovary</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107996826"
	 b:glossaryEntry="protective outer layer of seeds of flowering plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107996971">
	<b:glossaryEntry>nutritive tissue surrounding the embryo within seeds of flowering plants</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107997118">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small asexual reproductive structure in e.g. liverworts and mosses that detaches from the parent and develops into a new individual</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107997301"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cone-shaped mass of ovule- or spore-bearing scales or bracts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107997528"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the seed-producing cone of a pine tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107997640"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the seed-producing cone of a fir tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107997751"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a partition or wall especially in an ovary"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107997846">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the hard usually fibrous outer layer of some fruits especially nuts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107997983"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the shell around the kernel of a nut"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107998074">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a gland (often a protuberance or depression) that secretes nectar</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107998207">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a mature fertilized plant ovule consisting of an embryo and its food source and having a protective coat or testa</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107998428"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ripened and variously modified walls of a plant ovary"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107998645">
	<b:glossaryEntry>outermost layer of the pericarp of fruits as the skin of a peach or grape</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107998782"
	 b:glossaryEntry="inner usually woody layer of the pericarp or a fruit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107998889">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a dry dehiscent seed vessel or the spore-containing structure of e.g. mosses</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107999092"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a capsule divided into two cells or compartments"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107999204"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the rounded seed-bearing capsule of a cotton or flax plant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107999313">
	<b:glossaryEntry>narrow elongated seed capsule peculiar to the family Cruciferae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107999458"
	 b:glossaryEntry="short broad silique occurring in some cruciferous plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107999568">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) fringe of toothlike appendages surrounding the mouth of a moss capsule</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107999722">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a rootlike attachment in parasitic plants that penetrates and obtains food from the host</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107999867"
	 b:glossaryEntry="embryonic leaf in seed-bearing plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;107999972">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a minute rudimentary plant contained within a seed or an archegonium</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108000093"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a plant that bears fruit once and dies"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108000225">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the spore-producing individual or phase in the life cycle of a plant having alternation of generations</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108000384">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the gamete-producing individual or phase in the life cycle of a plant having alternation of generations</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108000545">
	<b:glossaryEntry>smaller of the two types of spore produced in heterosporous plants; develops in the pollen sac into a male gametophyte</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108000721">
	<b:glossaryEntry>larger of the two types of spore produced in heterosporous plants; develops in ovule into a female gametophyte</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108000887">
	<b:glossaryEntry>primitive cell or group of cells from which a mother cell develops</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108001025">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cell from which another cell of an organism (usually of a different sort) develops: "a sperm cell develops from a sperm mother cell"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108001233"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cell from which a spore develops"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108001329">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a female sex organ occurring in mosses, ferns, and most gymnosperms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108001455">
	<b:glossaryEntry>hard shiny gray seed of a bonduc tree; used for making e.g. jewelry</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108001625"
	 b:glossaryEntry="hard pearly seeds of an Asiatic grass; often used as beads"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108001741"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of several seeds that yield oil"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108001920"
	 b:glossaryEntry="seed of castor-oil plant; source of castor oil"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108002043"
	 b:glossaryEntry="seed of cotton plants; source of cottonseed oil"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108002185"
	 b:glossaryEntry="seed of candlenut tree; source of soil used in varnishes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108002316">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the single central seed in some fruits such as peaches and cherries enclosed in a hard woody shell</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108002472"
	 b:glossaryEntry="part of the perianth that is usually brightly colored"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108002614"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one of the green parts that form the calyx of a flower"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108002757">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a projection like a chin formed by the sepals and base of the column in some orchids</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108002913"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a modified leaf that is part of a flower"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108003066">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the petals of a flower collectively forming an inner floral envelope or layer of the perianth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108003251">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the sepals of a flower collectively forming the outer floral envelope or layer of the perianth enclosing a developing bud; usually green</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108003513">
	<b:glossaryEntry>persistent enlarged calyx at base of e.g. a strawberry or raspberry</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108003631">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a group of bracts simulating a calyx as in a carnation or hibiscus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108003788">
	<b:glossaryEntry>collective term for the outer parts of a flower consisting of the calyx and corolla and enclosing the stamen and carpel</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108004037">
	<b:glossaryEntry>calyx composed of scales or bristles or featherlike hairs in plants of the Compositae such as thistles and dandelions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108004225">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pappus of a thistle consisting of silky featherlike hairs attached to the seedlike fruit of a thistle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108004384">
	<b:glossaryEntry>herbs, shrubs and trees: includes families Ranunculaceae; Annonaceae; Berberidaceae; Magnoliaceae; Menispermaceae; Myristicaceae; Nymphaeaceae; Lardizabalaceae; Lauraceae; Calycanthaceae; Ceratophyllaceae; Cercidiphyllaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108004998"
	 b:glossaryEntry="chiefly tropical trees or shrubs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108005244">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Annonaceae; tropical American trees or shrubs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108005413">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several tropical American trees bearing fruit with soft edible pulp</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108005732">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small tropical American tree bearing round or oblong soft-fleshed fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108005914">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tropical American tree grown in southern United States having a whitish pink-tinged fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108006109">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small tropical American tree bearing large succulent slightly acid fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108006311">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small tropical American tree bearing a bristly heart-shaped acid tropical fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108006530">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tropical American tree bearing sweet pulpy fruit with thick scaly rind and shiny black seeds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108006730">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small evergreen tree of tropical America with edible fruit; used chiefly as grafting stock</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108006930"
	 b:glossaryEntry="pawpaw"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108007044">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small tree native to the eastern United States having oblong leaves and fleshy fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108007258"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of Malayan tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108007419">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen Asian tree with aromatic greenish-yellow flowers yielding a volatile oil; widely grown in the tropics as an ornamental</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108007656">
	<b:glossaryEntry>oil distilled from flowers of the ilang-ilang tree; used in perfumery</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108007783"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of tropical trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108007895"
	 b:glossaryEntry="source of most of the lancewood of commerce"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108008051">
	<b:glossaryEntry>durable straight-grained wood of the lacewood tree; used for building and cabinetwork and tools</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108008221"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tropical evergreen trees or shrubs; chiefly African"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108008380">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tropical west African evergreen tree bearing pungent aromatic seeds used as a condiment and in folk medicine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108008603"
	 b:glossaryEntry="shrubs or herbs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108008833">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large genus of shrubs of temperate zones of New and Old Worlds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108009005">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous plants of the genus Berberis having prickly stems and yellow flowers followed by small red berries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108009247">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deciduous shrub of eastern North America whose leaves turn scarlet in autumn and having racemes of yellow flowers followed by ellipsoid glossy red berries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108009487">
	<b:glossaryEntry>upright deciduous European shrub widely naturalized in United States having clusters of juicy berries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108009690">
	<b:glossaryEntry>compact deciduous shrub having persistent red berries; widespread in cultivation especially for hedges</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108009878"
	 b:glossaryEntry="blue cohosh"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108010007">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall herb of eastern North America and Asia having blue berrylike fruit and a thick knotty rootstock formerly used medicinally</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108010340">
	<b:glossaryEntry>herbaceous perennials of Mediterranean to India and eastern Asia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108010516">
	<b:glossaryEntry>slow-growing creeping plant with semi-evergreen leaves on erect wiry stems; used as ground cover</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108010728">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen shrubs and small trees of North and Central America and Asia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108010887">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ornamental evergreen shrub of Pacific coast of North America having dark green pinnate leaves and racemes of yellow flowers followed by blue-black berries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108011196">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small shrub with gray-green leaves and yellow flowers followed by glaucous blue berries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108011360"
