<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108405743"
	 b:glossaryEntry="plant with lead-blue flowers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108405865"
	 b:glossaryEntry="shrubby or herbaceous low-growing evergreen perennials"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108406027">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous sun-loving low-growing evergreens of the genus Armeria having round heads of pink or white flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108406231">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tufted thrift of seacoasts and mountains of north temperate zone; occasionally grown as a ground cover</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108406420"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sea lavender"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108406542">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various plants of the genus Limonium of temperate salt marshes having spikes of whit or mauve flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108406755">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family of mainly tropical American trees and shrubs similar to those of the Myrsinaceae; often included in the Myrsinaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108407002"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sometimes placed in family Myrsinaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108407171">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small West Indian shrub or tree with hard glossy seeds patterned yellow and brown that are used to make bracelets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108407386">
	<b:glossaryEntry>West Indian shrub or small tree having leathery saponaceous leaves and extremely hard wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108407581"
	 b:glossaryEntry="grasses; sedges; rushes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108407737">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the grasses: chiefly herbaceous but some woody plants including cereals; bamboo; reeds; sugar cane</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108409068">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cosmopolitan herbaceous or woody plants with hollow jointed stems and narrow long-bladed leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108409338">
	<b:glossaryEntry>narrow-leaved green herbage: grown as lawns; used as pasture for grazing animals; cut and dried as hay</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108410428">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various grasses of many genera that grow in tufts or clumps rather than forming a sod or mat; chiefly of western United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108410632">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various grasses of moderate height which covered the undisturbed prairie in the United States; includes most of the forage grasses of the temperate zone</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108410846">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various grasses that are short and can tolerate drought conditions; common on the dry upland plains just east of the Rocky Mountains</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108411042">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various grasses or sedges having sword-shaped leaves with sharp edges</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108411176">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various grasses that are tall and that flourish with abundant moisture</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108411309"
	 b:glossaryEntry="succulent herbaceous vegetation of pasture land"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108411422"
	 b:glossaryEntry="goat grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108411542">
	<b:glossaryEntry>European grass naturalized as a weed in North America; sharp-pointed seeds cause injury when eaten by livestock</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108411751">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial grasses of temperate and cool regions: wheatgrass; dog grass</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108411933"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a grass of the genus Agropyron"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108412147">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Eurasian grass grown in United States great plains area for forage and erosion control</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108412372">
	<b:glossaryEntry>European grass spreading rapidly by creeping rhizomes; naturalized in North America as a weed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108412622">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a wheatgrass with straight terminal awns on the flowering glumes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108412775"
	 b:glossaryEntry="valuable forage grass of western United States"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108412928">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Asiatic grass introduced into United States rangelands for pasture and fodder</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108413117">
	<b:glossaryEntry>North American grass cultivated in western United States as excellent forage crop</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108413334">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual or perennial grasses cosmopolitan in northern hemisphere: bent grass (so named from `bent'' meaning an area of unfenced grassland)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108413580">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grass for pastures and lawns especially bowling and putting greens</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108413795"
	 b:glossaryEntry="common grass with slender stems and narrow leaves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108413984">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Spanish grass with light feathery panicles grown for dried bouquets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108414129"
	 b:glossaryEntry="common pasture or lawn grass spread by long runners"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108414282">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual or perennial grasses including decorative and meadow species as well as notorious agricultural weeds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108414503">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stout erect perennial grass of northern parts of Old World having silky flowering spikes; widely cultivated for pasture and hay; naturalized in North America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108414763">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grasses of the genera Alopecurus and Setaria having dense silky or bristly brushlike flowering spikes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108415042">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall annual or perennial grasses with spikelike racemes; warm regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108415225">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several grasses of the genus Andropogon; used in broom making</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108415406"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tall tufted grass of southeastern United States"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108415535"
	 b:glossaryEntry="oat grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108415664">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coarse perennial Eurasian oatlike grass found on roadside verges and rough grassland and in hay meadows; introduced in North America for forage</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108415974">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several coarse tall perennial grasses of most warm areas: reeds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108416150"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used by Maoris for thatching"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108416283">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large rhizomatous perennial grasses found by riversides and in ditches having jointed stems and large gray-white feathery panicles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108416504"
	 b:glossaryEntry="oats"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108416612">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual grass of Europe and North Africa; grains used as food and fodder (referred to primarily in the plural: `oats'')</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108416870"
	 b:glossaryEntry="widely cultivated in temperate regions for its edible grains"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108417021"
	 b:glossaryEntry="common in meadows and pastures"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108417137"
	 b:glossaryEntry="oat of southern Europe and southwestern Asia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108417260">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Mediterranean oat held to be progenitor of modern cultivated oat</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108417415"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of grasses of the family Gramineae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108417563">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various woodland and meadow grasses of the genus Bromus; native to temperate regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108417828">
	<b:glossaryEntry>drought-resistant perennial with awns lacking or very short and long creeping rhizomes; Europe and temperate Asia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108418023">
	<b:glossaryEntry>weedy annual native to Europe but widely distributed as a weed especially in wheat</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108418184">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual or winter annual grass with softly hairy leaves of the Mediterranean</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108418412"
	 b:glossaryEntry="annual grass of Europe and temperate Asia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108418529"
	 b:glossaryEntry="grass of Mediterranean and temperate Asia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108418667"
	 b:glossaryEntry="forage grasses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108418793">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pasture grass of plains of South America and western North America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108419004"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of western North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108419106"
	 b:glossaryEntry="especially of western coastal regions of North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108419239"
	 b:glossaryEntry="buffalo grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108419360">
	<b:glossaryEntry>short grass growing on dry plains of central United States (where buffalo roam)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108419540"
	 b:glossaryEntry="reed grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108419669">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various tall perennial grasses of the genus Calamagrostis having feathery plumes; natives of marshland fens and wet woodlands of temperate northern hemisphere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108419946"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a variety of reed grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108420074"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tall Australian reedlike grass sometimes used for hay"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108420222"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of grasses of the family Gramineae that have burs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108420408"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a grass of the genus Cenchrus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108420541">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grass of the eastern United States and tropical America having spikelets enclosed in prickly burs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108420761">
	<b:glossaryEntry>erect tussock-forming perennial bur grass used in especially in South Africa and Australia for pasture and forage</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108420978">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tufted or perennial or annual grasses having runners: finger grass; windmill grass</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108421149">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any grass of the genus Chloris; occurs in short grassland especially on waste ground or poor soils</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108421342">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial grass of South Africa introduced into United States; cultivated as forage grass in dry regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108421522">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial Australian grass having numerous long spikes arranged like the vanes of a windmill</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108421733">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall ornamental grasses of South America and New Zealand and New Guinea: pampas grass</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108421932">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall perennial grass of pampas of South America having silvery plumes and growing in large dense clumps</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108422135"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tall grass of New Zealand grown for plumelike flower heads"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108422315"
	 b:glossaryEntry="creeping perennial grasses of tropical and southern Africa"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108422500">
	<b:glossaryEntry>trailing grass native to Europe now cosmopolitan in warm regions; used for lawns and pastures especially in southern United States and India</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108422810">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial grass having stems 3 to 4 feet high; used especially in Africa and India for pasture and hay</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108423019">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a monocotyledonous grass of the family Gramineae (has only one species)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108423181"
	 b:glossaryEntry="widely grown stout Old World hay and pasture grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108423335"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a monocotyledonous genus of the family Gramineae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108423486"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a creeping grass with spikes like fingers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108423633"
	 b:glossaryEntry="crab grass; finger grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108423769">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grasses with creeping stems that root freely; a pest in lawns</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108423987"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a weed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108424078">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a European forage grass grown for hay; a naturalized weed in United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108424259"
	 b:glossaryEntry="annual or perennial succulent grasses of warm regions"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108424447">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a coarse annual panic grass; a cosmopolitan weed; occasionally used for hay or grazing</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108424664">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coarse annual grass cultivated in Japan and southeastern Asia for its edible seeds and for forage; important wildlife food in United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108424975">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual and perennial grasses of savannas and upland grasslands</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108425166">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coarse annual grass having fingerlike spikes of flowers; native to Old World tropics; a naturalized weed elsewhere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108425413">
	<b:glossaryEntry>East Indian cereal grass whose seed yield a somewhat bitter flour, a staple in the Orient</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108425664">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall tufted perennial grasses (such as lyme grass or wild rye)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108425851"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a grass of the genus Elymus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108425990"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of several grasses of the genus Elymus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108426142"
	 b:glossaryEntry="stout perennial grass of western North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108426291"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a dune grass of the Pacific seacoast used as a sand binder"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108426469"
	 b:glossaryEntry="North American wild rye"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108426574"
	 b:glossaryEntry="weedy rye grass having long bristling awns"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108426699"
	 b:glossaryEntry="annual or perennial grasses of tropics and subtropics"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108426866">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various grasses of the genus_Eragrostis; useful especially for forage and prevention of erosion</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108427092">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an African grass economically important as a cereal grass (yielding white flower of good quality) as well as for forage and hay</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108427324">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial South African grass having densely clumped flimsy stems; introduced into United States especially for erosion control</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108427558">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of reedlike grasses having spikes crowded in a panicle covered with long silky hairs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108427779"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a reedlike grass of the genus Erianthus having large plumes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108427933">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grass often cultivated for its long white-ribbed leaves and large plumes resembling those of pampas grass</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108428151"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of tufted perennial grasses of the family Gramineae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108428356">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grass with wide flat leaves cultivated in Europe and America for permanent pasture and hay and for lawns</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108428581">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cultivated for sheep pasturage in upland regions or used as a lawn grass</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108428764"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of Australia and New Zealand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108428870"
	 b:glossaryEntry="manna grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108428991">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several moisture-loving grasses of the genus Glyceria having sweet flavor or odor</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108429188"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a pasture grass of moist places throughout North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108429327"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of Old World grasses widely cultivated in America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108429471">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall European perennial grass having a velvety stem; naturalized in United States and used for forage</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108429664"
	 b:glossaryEntry="European perennial grass with soft velvety foliage"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108429796">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual to perennial grasses of temperate northern hemisphere and South America: barley</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108429990">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cultivated since prehistoric times; grown for forage and grain</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108430196">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grass yielding grain used for for breakfast food, animal feed and in malt beverages</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108430395"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a grain of barley"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108430488">
	<b:glossaryEntry>European annual grass often found as a weed in waste ground especially along roadsides and hedgerows</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108430679">
	<b:glossaryEntry>barley grown for its highly ornamental flower heads with delicate long silky awns; North America and northeastern Asia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108430919">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual barley native to western North America and widespread in southern United States and tropical America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108431105"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus that in some classifications overlaps the genus Elymus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108431252"
	 b:glossaryEntry="darnel; ryegrass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108431374"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of several annual or perennial Eurasian grasses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108431565">
	<b:glossaryEntry>European perennial grass widely cultivated for pasture and hay and as a lawn grass</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108431748">
	<b:glossaryEntry>European grass much used for hay and in United States also for turf and green manure</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108431930">
	<b:glossaryEntry>weedy annual grass often occurs in grainfields and other cultivated land; seeds sometimes considered poisonous</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108432145">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a genus of grasses of the family Gramineae grown in America and Asia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108432331">
	<b:glossaryEntry>slender branching American grass of some value for grazing in central United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108432529"
	 b:glossaryEntry="rice"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108432637">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual or perennial rhizomatous marsh grasses; seed used for food; straw used for paper</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108432831"
	 b:glossaryEntry="yields the staple food of 50 percent of world''s population"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108432985"
	 b:glossaryEntry="rice grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108433107"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any grass of the genus Oryzopsis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108433263">
	<b:glossaryEntry>valuable forage grass of dry upland areas and plains of western North America to northern Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108433483">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial mountain rice native to Mediterranean region and introduced into North America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108433658"
	 b:glossaryEntry="panic grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108433777"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any grass of the genus Panicum; grown for grain and fodder"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108433984">
	<b:glossaryEntry>North American grass with slender brushy panicles; often a weed on cultivated land</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108434207"
	 b:glossaryEntry="grass of western America used for hay"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108434322">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extensively cultivated in Europe and Asia for its grain and in United States sometimes for forage</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108434524"
	 b:glossaryEntry="annual weedy grass used for hay"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108434646"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of perennial grasses of warm regions"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108434827">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall tufted perennial tropical American grass naturalized as pasture and forage grass in southern United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108435062">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial tropical American grass used as pasture grass in arid areas of Gulf states</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108435242"
	 b:glossaryEntry="low-growing weedy grass with spikelets along the leaf stems"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108435397"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of Old World grasses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108435577">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall grass having cattail like spikes; grown in Africa and Asia for its grain and in the United States chiefly for forage; sometimes used in making beer</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108435886">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall perennial ornamental grass with long nodding flower plumes of tropical Africa and Asia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108436101"
	 b:glossaryEntry="northeastern tropical African plant having feathery panicles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108436277">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a genus of grasses with broad leaves and a dense spike of flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108436490">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial grass of marshy meadows and ditches having broad leaves; Europe and North America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108436738"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Canary Islands grass; seeds used as feed for caged birds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108436912">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial grass of Australia and South Africa; introduced in North America as forage grass</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108437160"
	 b:glossaryEntry="grasses native to temperate regions"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108437301">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grass with long cylindrical spikes frown in northern United States and Europe for hay</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108437511">
	<b:glossaryEntry>reeds of marshes and riversides in tropical or temperate regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108437688">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall North American reed having relative wide leaves and large plumelike panicles; widely distributed in moist areas; used for mats, screens and arrow shafts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108437966"
	 b:glossaryEntry="chiefly perennial grasses of cool temperate regions"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108438136">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various grasses that thrive in the presence of abundant moisture</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108438371">
	<b:glossaryEntry>valuable meadow and pasture grass in Europe and especially central United States having tall stalks and slender bright green leaves; a chief constituent in lawn grass mixtures</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108438696">
	<b:glossaryEntry>slender European grass of shady places; grown also in northeastern America and temperate Asia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108438903">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall perennial reedlike grass originally of southeastern Asia: sugarcane</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108439106">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall tropical southeast Asian grass having stout fibrous jointed stalks; sap is a chief source of sugar</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108439358">
	<b:glossaryEntry>juicy canes whose sap is a source of molasses and commercial sugar; fresh canes are sometimes chewed for the juice</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108439560">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sugarcanes representing the highest development of the species; characterized by large soft-rinded juicy stalks and high sugar content</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108439751">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tough Asiatic grass whose culms are used for ropes and baskets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108439932"
	 b:glossaryEntry="overlaps the genus Andropogon"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108440062">
	<b:glossaryEntry>handsome hardy North American grass with foliage turning pale bronze in autumn</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108440282">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall grass with smooth bluish leaf sheaths grown for hay in the United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108440470"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cereal grass widely cultivated for its grain: rye"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108440625">
	<b:glossaryEntry>hardy annual cereal grass widely cultivated in northern Europe where its grain is the chief ingredient of black bread and in North America for forage and soil improvement</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108440900"
	 b:glossaryEntry="annual or perennial grasses of warm regions: bristlegrasses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108441086">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grasses of grasslands and woodlands having large gracefully arching spikes with long bristles beneath each spikelet</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108441295">
	<b:glossaryEntry>two species of coarse annual foxtails that are naturalized weeds in United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108441436">
	<b:glossaryEntry>common weedy and bristly grass found in nearly all temperate areas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108441649">
	<b:glossaryEntry>European foxtail naturalized in North America; often a troublesome weed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108441869">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coarse drought-resistant annual grass grown for grain, hay and forage in Europe and Asia and chiefly for forage and hay in United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108442174">
	<b:glossaryEntry>millet having orange to reddish grains in long bristly spikes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108442327"
	 b:glossaryEntry="millet having yellow grains in large drooping spikes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108442496">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various small-grained annual cereal and forage grasses of the genera Panicum, Echinochloa, Setaria, Sorghum, and Eleusine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108442803">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a strong slender often flexible stem as of bamboos, reeds, rattans, or sugar cane</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108442989">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the stem of various climbing palms of the genus Calamus and related genera used to make wickerwork and furniture and canes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108443178">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stem of the rattan palm used for making canes and umbrella handles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108443317">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall woody perennial grasses with hollow slender stems especially of the genera Arundo and Phragmites</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108443523">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual or perennial tropical and subtropical cereal grasses: sorghum</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108443689"
	 b:glossaryEntry="economically important Old World tropical cereal grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108443906">
	<b:glossaryEntry>important for human and animal food; growth habit and stem form similar to Indian corn but having sawtooth-edged leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108444138"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of several sorghums cultivated primarily for grain"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108444360"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sorghums of dry regions of Asia and North Africa"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108444523">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Sudanese sorghum having exceptionally large soft white grains</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108444679">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Sudanese sorghums having white seeds; one variety grown in southwestern United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108444817">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sorghums of China and Manchuria having small white or brown grains (used for food) and dry pithy stalks (used for fodder, fuel and thatching)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108445013">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small drought-resistant sorghums having large yellow or whitish grains</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108445147">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sorghum having slender dry stalks and small hard grains; introduced into United States from India</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108445326"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of several sorghums cultivated as a source of syrup"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108445474">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall perennial grass that spreads by creeping rhizomes and is grown for fodder; naturalized in southern United States where it is a serious pest on cultivated land</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108445785">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall grasses grown for the elongated stiff-branched panicle used for brooms and brushes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108445956">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grass of freshwater swamps and salt marshes of Europe, Africa, America, and South Atlantic islands</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108446164">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several perennial grasses of the genus Spartina; some important as coastal soil binders</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108446382"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tall reedlike grass common in salt meadows"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108446510">
	<b:glossaryEntry>North American cordgrass having leaves with dry membranous margins and glumes with long awns</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108446726">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cosmopolitan annual and perennial grasses (as dropseed or rushgrass)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108446946"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a grass of the genus Sporobolus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108447087">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grass native to West Indies but common in southern United States having tufted wiry stems often infested with a dark fungus (Helminthosporum_ravenelii)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108447363"
	 b:glossaryEntry="erect smooth grass of sandy places in eastern North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108447507"
	 b:glossaryEntry="grass having wiry stems and sheathed panicles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108447627"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lawn grasses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108447757">
	<b:glossaryEntry>low mat-forming grass of southern United States and tropical America; grown as a lawn grass</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108447975">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grass whose starchy grains are used as food: wheat; rice; rye; oats; maize; buckwheat; millet</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108448316">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual cereal grasses from Mediterranean area; widely cultivated in temperate regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108448511">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual or biennial grass having erect flower spikes and light brown grains</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108448790"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a grain of wheat"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108448883">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wheat with hard dark-colored kernels high in gluten and used for bread and pasta; grown especially in southern Russia, North Africa, and northern central North America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108449184">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wheat with soft starch kernels used in pastry and breakfast cereals</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108449308">
	<b:glossaryEntry>widely cultivated in temperate regions in many varieties for its commercially important grain</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108449499"
	 b:glossaryEntry="hardy wheat grown mostly in Europe for livestock feed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108449649">
	<b:glossaryEntry>hard red wheat grown especially in Russia and Germany; in United States as stock feed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108449839">
	<b:glossaryEntry>found wild in Palestine; held to be prototype of cultivated wheat</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108450006"
	 b:glossaryEntry="corn"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108450110">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall annual cereal grass bearing kernels on large ears: widely cultivated in America in many varieties; the principal cereal in Mexico and Central and South America since pre-Columbian times</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108450514"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(South African) an ear of corn"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108450597"
	 b:glossaryEntry="corn grown primarily for animal feed or market grain"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108450760">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the dried grains or kernels or corn used as animal feed or ground for meal</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108450904">
	<b:glossaryEntry>corn whose young ears are sweet and suitable for eating as a vegetable</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108451111">
	<b:glossaryEntry>corn whose kernels contain both hard and soft starch and become indented at maturity</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108451272">
	<b:glossaryEntry>corn having kernels with a hard outer layer enclosing the soft endosperm</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108451449"
	 b:glossaryEntry="corn having kernels almost entirely of soft starch"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108451601">
	<b:glossaryEntry>corn having small ears and kernels that burst when exposed to dry heat</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108451762"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wild rice"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108451879">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial aquatic grass of North America bearing grain used for food</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108452060">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lawn grasses native to southeastern Asia and New Zealand; grown especially in warm regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108452271"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of several creeping grasses of the genus Zoysia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108452448">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lawn grass common in the Philippines; grown also in United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108452614">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lawn grass common in China and Japan; grown also in United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108452783">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Asiatic creeping perennial grass; introduced in southern United States as a dought-resistant lawn grass</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108452990"
	 b:glossaryEntry="bamboos"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108453185">
	<b:glossaryEntry>woody tropical grass having hollow woody stems; mature canes used for construction and furniture</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108453498">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the hard woody stems of bamboo plants; used in construction and crafts and fishing poles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108453658"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tall tender clumping bamboos"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108453794">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extremely vigorous bamboo having thin-walled culms striped green and yellow; so widely cultivated that native area is uncertain</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108454038"
	 b:glossaryEntry="North American bamboo"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108454194"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tall grass of southern United States growing in thickets"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108454380">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small cane of watery or moist areas in southern United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108454551"
	 b:glossaryEntry="giant clumb-forming bamboos"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108454698">
	<b:glossaryEntry>immense tropical southeast Asian bamboo with tough hollow culms that resemble tree trunks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108454903"
	 b:glossaryEntry="medium and large bamboos"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108455085">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small bamboo of southeastern China having slender culms flexuous when young</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108455291"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small bamboo having thin green culms turning shining black"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108455462">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large bamboo having thick-walled culms; native of China and perhaps Japan; widely brown elsewhere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108455692"
	 b:glossaryEntry="bulrush; chufa; cotton grass; papyrus; umbrella plant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108455969">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grasslike or rushlike plant growing in wet places having solid stems, narrow grasslike leaves and spikelets of inconspicuous flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108456370">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of Cyperaceae; grasslike rhizomatous herbs; cosmopolitan except very cold regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108456645">
	<b:glossaryEntry>African sedge widely cultivated as an ornamental water plant for its terminal umbrellalike cluster of slender grasslike leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108456892"
	 b:glossaryEntry="European sedge having small edible nutlike tubers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108457093">
	<b:glossaryEntry>European sedge having rough-edged leaves and spikelets of reddish flowers and aromatic roots</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108457288">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall sedge of the Nile valley yielding fiber that served many purposes in historic times</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108457502">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a widely distributed perennial sedge having small edible nutlike tubers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108457701">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large genus of plants found in damp woodlands and bogs and ditches or at water margins: sedges</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108457918">
	<b:glossaryEntry>European maritime sedge naturalized along Atlantic coast of United States; rootstock has properties of sarsaparilla</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108458138"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tufted sedge of temperate regions; nearly cosmopolitan"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108458293"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cotton grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108458419">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any sedge of the genus Eriophorum; north temperate bog plants with tufted spikes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108458609"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having densely tufted white cottony or downlike glumes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108458756"
	 b:glossaryEntry="rhizomatous perennial grasslike herbs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108458920">
	<b:glossaryEntry>widely distributed North American sedge having rigid olive green stems</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108459111">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sedge of eastern North America having numerous clustered woolly spikelets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108459280">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sedges having dense spikes of flowers and leaves reduced to basal sheaths</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108459467"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a sedge of the genus Eleocharis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108459628"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Chinese sedge yielding edible bulb-shaped tubers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108459801">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fine-leaved aquatic spike rush; popular as aerator for aquariums</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108460001"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cylendrical-stemmed sedge"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108460115"
	 b:glossaryEntry="families Typhaceae; Sparganiaceae; Pandanaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108460313">
	<b:glossaryEntry>family of woody plants of the order Pandanales including: pandanus; freycinetia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108460529"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Pandanaceae (as screw pines)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108460674">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various Old World tropical palmlike trees having huge prop roots and pineapplelike leaves and edible conelike fruits</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108460902"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Polynesian screw pine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108461019">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fiber from leaves of the pandanus tree; used for woven articles (such as mats)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108461152">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial marsh plants with creeping rootstocks and long linear leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108461352"
	 b:glossaryEntry="reed maces; cattails"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108461476">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall erect herbs with sword-shaped leaves; cosmopolitan in fresh and salt marshes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108461666">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall marsh plant with pokerlike seed heads that explode when mature shedding large quantities of down; its long flat leaves are used for making mats and chair seats; of North America, Europe, Asia and North Africa</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108462008"
	 b:glossaryEntry="reed maces of America, Europe, North Africa, Asia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108462199"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coextensive with the genus Sparganium"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108462375">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type and sole genus of Sparganiaceae; marsh or aquatic herbs of temperate regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108462570">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marsh plant having elongated linear leaves and round prickly fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108462710">
	<b:glossaryEntry>dry seedlike fruit produced by the cereal grasses: e.g. wheat, barley, Indian corn</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108462946"
	 b:glossaryEntry='a single whole grain of a cereal: "a kernel of corn"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108463069"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the seed of the cereal grass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108463166">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a family of herbaceous vines (such cucumber or melon or squash or pumpkin)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108463546"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any plant of the family Cucurbitaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108463638">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any vine of the family Cucurbitaceae that bears hard-rinded fruits</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108463896"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of numerous hard-rinded inedible fruits"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108464009"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Cucurbitaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108464304">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a coarse vine widely cultivated for its non-keeping large pulpy round orange fruit with firm orange skin and numerous seeds; subspecies of Cucurbita pepo include the summer squashes and a few autumn squashes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108464671">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous annual tendril-bearing trailing plants of the genus Cucurbita grown for their fleshy edible fruits</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108464944">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various usually bushy plants producing fruit that is eaten while immature and before the rind or seeds harden</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108465277">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various squash plants grown for their yellow fruits with somewhat elongated necks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108465444">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various squash plants grown for their elongated fruit with smooth dark green skin and whitish flesh</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108465693"
	 b:glossaryEntry="marrow squash plant whose fruit are eaten when small"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108465831">
	<b:glossaryEntry>squash plant having dark green fruit with skin mottled with light green or yellow</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108466014">
	<b:glossaryEntry>squash plant having flattened round fruit with a scalloped edge; usually greenish white</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108466192">
	<b:glossaryEntry>squash plant bearing oval fruit with smooth yellowish skin and tender stranded flesh resembling spaghetti</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108466379">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various plants of the species Cucurbita maxima and Cucurbita moschata producing hard-rinded squashes that mature in the fall</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108466738">
	<b:glossaryEntry>squash plant bearing small yellow-fleshed acorn-shaped fruits having dark green or yellow rind with longitudinal ridges</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108466935">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several winter squash plants producing large grayish-green football-shaped fruit with a rough warty rind</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108467164">
	<b:glossaryEntry>squash plants bearing hard-shelled fruit shaped somewhat like a turban with a rounded central portion protruding from the end opposite the stem</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108467456">
	<b:glossaryEntry>plant bearing somewhat drum-shaped fruit having dark green rind with grayish markings</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108467623">
	<b:glossaryEntry>plant bearing buff-colored squash having somewhat bottle-shaped fruit with fine-textured edible flesh and smooth thin rind</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108467846">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various plants bearing hard-rinded squash having elongated recurved necks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108468055">
	<b:glossaryEntry>plant bearing squash having globose to ovoid fruit with variously striped gray and green and white warty rinds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108468299">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial vine of dry parts of central and southwestern United States and Mexico having small hard mottled green inedible fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108468632">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small hard green-and-white inedible fruit of the prairie gourd plant</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108468779"
	 b:glossaryEntry="climbing perennial herbs: bryony"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108468909">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tendril-bearing vine of the genus Bryonia having large leaves and small flowers and yielding acrid juice with emetic and purgative properties</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108469169">
	<b:glossaryEntry>white-flowered vine having thick roots and bearing small black berries; Europe to Iran</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108469346">
	<b:glossaryEntry>bryony having fleshy roots pale green flowers and very small red berries; Europe; North Africa; western Asia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108469539">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a dicot genus of the family cucurbitaceae including watermelons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108469714">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various fruit of cucurbitaceous vines including: muskmelons; watermelons; cantaloupes; cucumbers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108469955"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an African melon"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108470105"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cucumbers; muskmelons"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108470253">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several varieties of vine whose fruit has a netted rind and edible flesh and a musky smell</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108470546">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a variety of muskmelon vine having fruit with a tan rind and orange flesh</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108470754">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of a variety of muskmelon vines having fruit with a smooth white rind and white or greenish flesh that does not have a musky smell</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108471043">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a muskmelon vine with fruit that has a thin reticulated rind and sweet green flesh</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108471257">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a melon vine of the genus Cucumis; cultivated from earliest times for its cylindrical green fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108471481"
	 b:glossaryEntry="exploding cucumber; squirting cucumber"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108471631">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Mediterranean vine having oblong fruit that when ripe expels its seeds and juice violently when touched</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108471876"
	 b:glossaryEntry="bottle gourds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108472001">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Old World climbing plant with hard-shelled bottle-shaped gourds as fruits</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108472185"
	 b:glossaryEntry="dishcloth gourds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108472297">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several tropical annual climbers having large yellow flowers and edible young fruits; grown commercially for the mature fruit''s dried fibrous interior that is used as a sponge</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108472666"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the bathroom loofah"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108472776"
	 b:glossaryEntry="loofah of Pakistan; widely cultivated throughout tropics"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108472922">
	<b:glossaryEntry>dried fibrous part of the gourd-like fruit of a plant of the genus Luffa; used as a washing sponge or strainer</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108473136"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Old World tropical vine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108473290">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tropical Old World flowering vine with red or orange warty fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108473456"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tropical Old World vine with yellow-orange fruit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108473603">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not recognized in all classification systems; in some classifications lobeliaceous plants are included in family Campanulaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108473844">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in some classifications considered the type genus of a separate family Lobeliaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108474024"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any plant or flower of the genus Lobelia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108474209"
	 b:glossaryEntry="North American lobelia having brilliant red flowers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108474357">
	<b:glossaryEntry>North American wild lobelia having small blue flowers and inflated capsules formerly used as an antispasmodic</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108474558">
	<b:glossaryEntry>erect perennial aquatic herb of Europe and North America having submerged spongy leaves and pendulous racemes of blue flowers above the water</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108474779">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall erect and very leafy perennial herb of eastern North America having dense spikes of blue flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108474985">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Malvaceae; Bombacaceae; Elaeocarpaceae; Sterculiaceae; Tiliaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108475235"
	 b:glossaryEntry="herbs and shrubs and some trees: mallows; cotton; okra"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108475874"
	 b:glossaryEntry="herbs and subshrubs: mallows"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108476044"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of various plants of the family_Malvaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108476557">
	<b:glossaryEntry>erect Old World perennial with faintly musk-scented foliage and white or pink flowers; adventive in United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108476775">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual Old World plant with clusters of pink or white flowers; naturalized in United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108476962">
	<b:glossaryEntry>erect or decumbent Old World perennial with axillary clusters of rosy-purple flowers; introduced in United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108477209">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of tropical coarse herbs having large lobed leaves and often yellow flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108477425">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall coarse annual of Old World tropics widely cultivated in southern United States and West Indies for its long mucilaginous green pods used as basis for soups and stews; sometimes placed in genus Hibiscus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108477823"
	 b:glossaryEntry="long green edible beaked pods of the okra plant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108477940">
	<b:glossaryEntry>bushy herb of tropical Asia grown for its yellow or pink to scarlet hibiscuslike blooms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108478160">
	<b:glossaryEntry>herbs or shrubs or small trees: flowering maple; parlor maple; indian mallow</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108478327">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall annual herb or subshrub of tropical Asia having velvety leaves and yellow flowers and yielding a strong fiber; naturalized in southeastern Europe and United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108478632">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of erect herbs of the Middle East having showy flowers: hollyhocks; in some classification systems synonymous with Althea</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108478882">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various tall plants of the genus Alcea; native to the Middle East but widely naturalized and cultivated for its very large variously colored flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108479130">
	<b:glossaryEntry>plant with terminal racemes of showy white to pink or purple flowers; the English cottage garden hollyhock</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108479342">
	<b:glossaryEntry>hollyhocks; in some classification systems synonymous with Alcea</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108479523">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various plants of the genus Althaea; similar to but having smaller flowers than genus Alcea</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108479733">
	<b:glossaryEntry>European perennial plant naturalized in United States having triangular ovate leaves and lilac-pink flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108479954">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of North American herbs having usually red or purple flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108480138">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a plant of the genus Callirhoe having palmately cleft leaves and white to red or purple flowers borne throughout the summer</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108480393">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial poppy mallow of United States southern plains states having rose-red or rose-purple flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108480582">
	<b:glossaryEntry>hairy perennial of central United States having round deeply-lobed leaves and loose panicles of large crimson-purple or cherry-red flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108480810">
	<b:glossaryEntry>densely hairy perennial having mostly triangular basal leaves and rose-purple flowers in panicled clusters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108481009"
	 b:glossaryEntry="herbs and shrubs and small trees: cotton"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108481161">
	<b:glossaryEntry>erect bushy mallow plant or small tree bearing bolls containing seeds with many long hairy fibers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108481490">
	<b:glossaryEntry>East Indian shrub cultivated especially for ornament for its pale yellow to deep purple blossoms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108481665">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small bushy tree grown on islands of the Caribbean and off the southern United States Atlantic coast and yielding cotton with unusually long silky fibers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108481919">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Old World annual having heart-shaped leaves and large seeds with short grayish lint removed with difficulty; considered an ancestor of modern short-staple cottons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108482163">
	<b:glossaryEntry>native tropical American plant now cultivated in the United States yielding short-staple cotton</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108482339"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cotton with long rough hairy fibers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108482459">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fine long-stable somewhat brownish cotton grown in Egypt; believed to be derived from sea island cotton or by hybridization with Peruvian cotton</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108482665"
	 b:glossaryEntry="shrub of southern Arizona and Mexico"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108482802">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large genus of tropical and subtropical herbs and shrubs and trees often grown as ornamentals for their profusion of large flowers in a variety of colors</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108483054"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any plant of the genus Hibiscus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108483339">
	<b:glossaryEntry>valuable fiber plant of East Indies now widespread in cultivation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108483568"
	 b:glossaryEntry="fiber from an East Indian plant Hibiscus cannabinus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108483704">
	<b:glossaryEntry>erect forest tree of Cuba and Jamaica having variably hairy leaves and orange-yellow or orange-red flowers; yields a moderately dense timber for cabinetwork and gunstocks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108483990"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Australian tree with acid foliage"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108484106">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showy shrub of salt marshes of the eastern United States having large rose-colored flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108484332">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Chinese shrub or small tree having white or pink flowers becoming deep red at night; widely cultivated; naturalized in southeastern United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108484601">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large showy Asiatic shrub or small tree having large single or double red to deep-red flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108484842">
	<b:glossaryEntry>East Indian sparsely prickly annual herb or perennial subshrub widely cultivated for its fleshy calyxes used in tarts and jelly and for its bast fiber</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108485117">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Asiatic shrub or small shrubby tree having showy bell-shaped rose or purple or white flowers and usually 3-lobed leaves; widely cultivated in temperate North America and Europe</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108485374">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shrubby tree widely distributed along tropical shores; yields a light tough wood used for canoe outriggers and a fiber used for cordage and caulk; often cultivated for ornament</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108485662">
	<b:glossaryEntry>annual weedy herb with ephemeral yellow purple-eyed flowers; Old World tropics; naturalized as a weed in North America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108485920">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of shrubs and small trees of New Zealand: lacebarks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108486091">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small tree or shrub of New Zealand having a profusion of axillary clusters of honey-scented paper-white flowers and whose bark is used for cordage</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108486353">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of perennial herbs or subshrubs; some often placed in other genera</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108486558">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a rare mallow found only in Illinois resembling the common hollyhock and having pale rose-mauve flowers; sometimes placed in genus Sphaeralcea</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108486818">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial of northwestern United States and western Canada resembling a hollyhock and having white or pink flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108487057">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of herbs of southeastern United States and tropical America and Africa</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108487255">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various plants of the genus Kosteletzya predominantly of coastal habitats; grown for their hibuscuslike flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108487472"
	 b:glossaryEntry="subshrub of southeastern United States to New York"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108487610">
	<b:glossaryEntry>widespread genus or herbs or soft-wooded arborescent shrubs cultivated for their showy flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108487814">
	<b:glossaryEntry>arborescent perennial shrub having palmately lobed furry leaves and showy red-purple flowers; southwestern United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108488043"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of shrubs or small trees: chaparral mallow"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108488211">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shrub of coastal ranges of California and Baja California having hairy branches and spikes of numerous mauve flowers; sometimes placed in genus_Sphaeralcea</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108488503"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of chiefly European herbs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108488637">
	<b:glossaryEntry>western Mediterranean annual having deep purple-red flowers subtended by 3 large cordate bracts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108488821">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of mallows characterized by red and yellow flowers often placed in other genera</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108489001"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an American plant of the genus Malvastrum"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108489101">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of shrubs of Central and South America: wax mallows</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108489280">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various plants of the genus Malvaviscus having brilliant bell-shaped drooping flowers like incompletely opened hibiscus flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108489522"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1 species: glade mallow"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108489651">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall coarse American herb having palmate leaves and numerous small white dioecious flowers; found wild in most alluvial soils of eastern and central United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108489907">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of tropical hairy shrubs or herbs of tropics and subtropics especially South America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108490095">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various evergreen plants of the genus Pavonia having white or yellow or purple flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108490261"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of shrubs and trees of Australia and New Zealand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108490437">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deciduous New Zealand tree whose inner bark yields a strong flax-like fiber called New Zealand cotton</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108490692">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fiber from the bast of New Zealand ribbon trees that resembles cotton fiber</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108490853">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very small species of shrubs of southern hemisphere: bush_hibiscus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108491027">
	<b:glossaryEntry>southern and western Australian shrub with unlobed or shallowly lobed toothed leaves and purple flowers; sometimes placed in genus Hibiscus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108491285">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large genus of tropical subshrubs or herbs some of which yield fibers of musilaginous substances</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108491521">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall handsome perennial herb of southeastern United States having maplelike leaves and white flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108491723">
	<b:glossaryEntry>herb widely distributed in tropics and subtropics used for forage and medicinally as a demulcent and having a fine soft bast stronger than jute; sometimes an aggressive weed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108492008">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tropical American weed having pale yellow or orange flowers naturalized in southern United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108492199">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of showy plants of western North America having palmate leaves and variously colored racemose flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108492416">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial purple-flowered wild mallow of western North America that is also cultivated</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108492619">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large genus of chiefly tropical herbs with showy flowers and mostly globose fruits: globe mallows</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108492832">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of coarse herbs and subshrubs of arid North and South America having pink or scarlet flowers and globose fruits</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108493060">
	<b:glossaryEntry>false mallow of western United States having racemose red flowers; sometimes placed in genus Malvastrum</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108493289"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of tropical trees: Portia tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108493443">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various trees yielding variously colored woods similar to true tulipwood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108493638">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the variegated or showily striped ornamental wood of various tulipwood trees</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108493789">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pantropical tree of usually seacoasts sometimes cultivated as an ornamental for its rounded heart-shaped leaves and showy yellow and purple flowers; yields valuable pink to dark red close-grained wood and oil from its seeds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108494139">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tropical trees with large dry or fleshy fruit containing usually woolly seeds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108494447"
	 b:glossaryEntry="trees of chiefly South America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108494583">
	<b:glossaryEntry>East Indian silk cotton tree yielding fibers inferior to kapok</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108494774"
	 b:glossaryEntry="baobab; cream-of-tartar tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108494933"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Australian tree having an agreeably acid gourd-like fruit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108495129">
	<b:glossaryEntry>African tree having an exceedingly thick trunk and gourd-like fruit with edible pulp called monkey bread</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108495365">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tropical American trees with palmately compound leaves and showy bell-like flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108495532">
	<b:glossaryEntry>massive tropical tree with deep ridges on its massive trunk and bearing large pods of seeds covered with silky floss; source of the silky kapok fiber</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108495834"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of tall Asian trees of the family Bombacaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108495991">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tree of southeastern Asia having edible oval fruit with a hard spiny rind</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108496216">
	<b:glossaryEntry>1 species: medium-sized evergreen tree of Puerto Rico or Mexico</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108496379">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen tree with large leathery leaves and large pink to orange flowers; considered a link plant between families Bombacaceae and Sterculiaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108496600"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1 species: balsa"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108496724">
	<b:glossaryEntry>forest tree of lowland Central America having a strong very light wood; used for making floats and rafts and in crafts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108496950">
	<b:glossaryEntry>strong lightweight wood of the balsa tree used especially for floats</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108497102"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tropical American deciduous shrubs or small trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108497269">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tree of Mexico to Guatemala having densely hairy flowers with long narrow petals clustered at ends of branches before leaves appear</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108497510">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of trees and shrubs widely distributed in warm regions some yielding useful timber; in some classifications included in the family Santalaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108497859"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the family Elaeocarpaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108498014">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Australian tree having hard white timber and glossy green leaves with white flowers followed by one-seeded glossy blue fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108498342"
	 b:glossaryEntry="pale easily worked timber from the quandong tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108498471"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the fruit of the Brisbane quandong tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108498595">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of shrubs or small trees of Australia and New Zealand and western South America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108498802">
	<b:glossaryEntry>graceful deciduous shrub or small tree having attractive foliage and small red berries that turn black at maturity and are used for making wine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108499101">
	<b:glossaryEntry>1 species: Jamaican cherry; sometimes placed in family Flacourtiaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108499282">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fast-growing tropical American evergreen having white flowers and white fleshy edible fruit; bark yields a silky fiber used in cordage and wood is valuable for staves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108499589"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of tropical hardwood timber trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108499736"
	 b:glossaryEntry="West Indian timber tree having very hard wood"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108499887"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a large family of plants of order Malvales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108500297">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Sterculiaceae: deciduous or evergreen trees of Old and New World tropics and subtropics</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108500502"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any tree of the genus Sterculia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108500644">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large deciduous tree native to Panama and from which the country takes its name having densely leafy umbrellalike crown and naked trunk</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108500857">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large tree of Old World tropics having foul-smelling orange-red blossoms followed by red pods enclosing oil-rich seeds sometimes used as food</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108501088"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Australian trees (usually with swollen trunks)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108501252"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an Australian tree of the genus Brachychiton"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108501444">
	<b:glossaryEntry>south Australian tree having panicles of brilliant scarlet flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108501635"
	 b:glossaryEntry="north Australian tree having white flowers and broad leaves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108501802">
	<b:glossaryEntry>widely distributed tree of eastern Australia yielding a tough durable fiber and soft light attractively grained wood; foliage is an important emergency food for cattle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108502062">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large tree of Queensland Australia having cream-colored flowers blotched with red inside; sometimes placed in genus Sterculia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108502329"
	 b:glossaryEntry="large genus of African trees bearing kola nuts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108502477">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tree bearing large brown nuts containing e.g. caffeine; source of cola extract</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108502719"
	 b:glossaryEntry="bitter brown seed containing caffein; source of cola extract"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108502883"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of African shrubs or small trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108503019">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various shrubs or small trees of the genus Dombeya grown for their rounded clusters of exquisite often sweet-scented flowers usually hanging beneath the leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108503258"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of Asian trees or shrubs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108503404">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deciduous tree widely grown in southern United States as an ornamental for its handsome maplelike foliage and long racemes of yellow-green flowers followed by curious leaflike pods</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108503746"
	 b:glossaryEntry="flannelbush"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108503911">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several handsome evergreen shrubs of California and northern Mexico having downy lobed leaves and showy yellow flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108504148">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of shrubs and small trees of tropical America and Asia having cylindrical fruits spirally twisted around one another</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108504384"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a tree or shrub of the genus Helicteres"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108504517">
	<b:glossaryEntry>East Indian shrub often cultivated for its hairy leaves and orange-red flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108504683">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of timber trees of eastern Asia, Australasia and tropical Africa that form large buttresses</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108504909"
	 b:glossaryEntry="large tree of Australasia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108505101">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large evergreen tree of India and Burma whose leaves are silvery beneath</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108505262">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small tree of coastal regions of Old World tropics whose leaves are silvery beneath</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108505434">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of African herbs and subshrubs having honey-scented bell-shaped flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108505623">
	<b:glossaryEntry>African shrub having decumbent stems and slender yellow honey-scented flowers either solitary or in pairs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108505866"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of tropical Asian trees and shrubs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108506024">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Indian tree having fragrant nocturnal white flowers and yielding a reddish wood used for planking; often grown as an ornamental or shade tree</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108506286"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of east Asian and Australian timber trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108506451"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Australian timber tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108506576"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cacao plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108506700"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tropical American tree producing cacao beans"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108506881"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of tropical African trees with maplelike leaves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108507058">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large west African tree having large palmately lobed leaves and axillary cymose panicles of small white flowers and one-winged seeds; yields soft white to pale yellow wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108507354">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the wood of an African obeche tree; used especially for veneering</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108507472">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chiefly trees and shrubs of tropical and temperate regions of especially southeastern Asia and Brazil; genera Tilia; Corchorus; Entelia; Grewia; Sparmannia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108507813">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deciduous trees with smooth usually silver-gray bark of North America and Europe and Asia: lime trees; lindens; basswood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108508037">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various deciduous trees of the genus Tilia heart-shaped leaves and drooping cymose clusters of yellowish often fragrant flowers; several yield valuable timber</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108508427">
	<b:glossaryEntry>soft light-colored wood of any of various linden trees; used in making crates and boxes and in carving and millwork</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108508625">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large American shade tree with large dark green leaves and rounded crown</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108508795">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large spreading European linden with small dark green leaves; often cultivated as an ornamental</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108508992"
	 b:glossaryEntry="American basswood of the Allegheny region"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108509128"
	 b:glossaryEntry="medium-sized tree of Japan used as an ornamental"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108509271">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large tree native to eastern Europe and Asia Minor having leaves with white tomentum on the under side; widely cultivated as an ornamental</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108509501">
	<b:glossaryEntry>widely distributed genus of tropical herbs or subshrubs; especially Asia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108509673">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various plants of the genus Corchorus having large leaves and cymose clusters of yellow flowers; a source of jute</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108509868">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a genus of tropical and subtropical Old World climbers or shrubs or trees</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108510047">
	<b:glossaryEntry>drought-resistant Asiatic treelike shrub bearing pleasantly acid small red edible fruits commonly used in sherbets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108510251"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of tropical African shrubs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108510403">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large shrub of South Africa having many conspicuously hairy branches with large hairy leaves and clusters of conspicuous white flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108510637">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a plant lacking a permanent woody stem; many are flowering garden plants or potherbs; some having medicinal properties; some are pests</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108517232">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various herbaceous plants cultivated for an edible part such as the fruit or the root of the beet or the leaf of spinach or the seeds of bean plants or the flower buds of broccoli or cauliflower</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108517561"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(archaic) any herbaceous plant having medicinal properties"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108517672">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a group of trees and shrubs and herbs mostly with polypetalous flowers; contains 108 families including Rosaceae; Crassulaceae; Myrtaceae; Melastomaceae; Euphorbiaceae; Umbelliferae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108518059">
	<b:glossaryEntry>plants having umbels or corymbs of uniovulate flowers; includes the Umbelliferae (chiefly herbs) and Cornaceae (chiefly trees or shrubs)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108518347"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coextensive with the family Proteaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108518497">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large family of Australian and South African shrubs and trees with leathery leaves and clustered mostly tetramerous flowers; constitutes the order Proteales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108519107">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a living fossil or so-called "green dinosaur": genus or subfamily of primitive nut-bearing trees thought to have died out 50 million years ago; a single specimen found in 1994 on Mount Bartle Frere in eastern Australia; not yet officially named</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108519467"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of Proteaceae; tropical African shrubs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108519651">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any tropical African shrub of the genus Protea having alternate rigid leaves and dense colorful flower heads resembling cones</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108519884">
	<b:glossaryEntry>South African shrub whose flowers when open are cup- or goblet-shaped resembling globe artichokes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108520089"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Australian shrub whose flowers yield honey copiously"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108520237">
	<b:glossaryEntry>important genus of Australian evergreen shrubs or trees with alternate leathery leaves and yellowish flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108520443">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any shrub or tree of the genus Banksia having alternate leathery leaves apetalous yellow flowers often in showy heads and conelike fruit with winged seeds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108520688">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shrubby tree with silky foliage and spikes of cylindrical yellow nectarous flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108520891">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Australian shrubs (some trees) with flowers in dense spikes: smoke bush</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108521078">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various shrubs of the genus Conospermum with panicles of mostly white woolly flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108521245">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of South American evergreen shrubs or small trees with long willowy branches and flowers in flamboyant terminal clusters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108521491">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grown for outstanding display of brilliant usually scarlet-crimson flowers; Andes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108521699"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1 species: Chilean nut"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108521829">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Chilean shrub bearing coral-red fruit with an edible seed resembling a hazelnut</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108522072">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large genus of Australian shrubs and trees having usually showy orange or red flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108522314"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any shrub or tree of the genus Grevillea"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108522447">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several Australian timber trees having usually fernlike foliage and mottled wood used in cabinetry and veneering</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108522712">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall shrub with cylindrical racemes of red flowers and pinnatifid leaves silky-gray beneath; eastern Australia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108522911">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small slender tree with usually entire gray-green pendulous leaves and white or cream flowers; northern Australia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108523123">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium to tall fast-growing tree with orange flowers and feathery bipinnate leaves silky-hairy beneath; eastern Australia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108523339"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tree yielding hard heavy reddish wood"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108523470">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Australian shrubs and small trees with evergreen usually spiny leaves and dense clusters of showy flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108523717">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall straggling shrub with large globose crimson-yellow flowers; western Australia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108523914">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large bushy shrub with pungent pointed leaves and creamy white flowers; central and eastern Australia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108524124">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shrub with pungent rigid needle-shaped leaves and white flowers; eastern Australia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108524316">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of trees or shrubs of New Zealand and New Caledonia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108524489">
	<b:glossaryEntry>slender elegant tree of New Zealand having racemes of red flowers and yielding valuable mottled red timber</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108524696"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of Australian shrubs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;108524840">
	<b:glossaryEntry>erect bushy shrub of eastern Australia having terminal clusters of red flowers yielding much nectar</b:glossaryEntry>
