</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109030581"
	 b:glossaryEntry="willow with long flexible twigs used in basketry"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109030754">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a genus of trees of the family Salicaceae that is found in the northern hemisphere; poplars</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109030953">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous trees of north temperate regions having light soft wood and flowers borne in catkins</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109031266"
	 b:glossaryEntry="soft light-colored nondurable wood of the poplar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109031386">
	<b:glossaryEntry>poplar of northeastern North America with broad heart-shaped leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109031560">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a poplar that is widely cultivated in the United States; has white bark and leaves with whitish undersurfaces</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109031803">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large rapidly growing poplar with faintly lobed dentate leaves gray on the lower surface; native to Europe but introduced and naturalized elsewhere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109032028"
	 b:glossaryEntry="large European poplar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109032142">
	<b:glossaryEntry>distinguished by its columnar fastigiate shape and erect branches</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109032293">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several North American trees of the genus Populus having a tuft of cottony hairs on the seed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109032503">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a common poplar of eastern and central United States; cultivated in United States for its rapid growth and luxuriant foliage and in Europe for timber</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109032755">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cottonwood of western North America with dark green leaves shining above and rusty or silvery beneath</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109032965">
	<b:glossaryEntry>North American poplar with large rounded scalloped leaves and brownish bark and wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109033184">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several trees of the genus Populus having leaves on flattened stalks so that they flutter in the lightest wind</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109033407">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Old World aspen with a broad much-branched crown; northwestern Europe and Siberia to North Africa</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109033607"
	 b:glossaryEntry="slender aspen native to North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109033752"
	 b:glossaryEntry="aspen with a narrow crown; eastern North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109033983">
	<b:glossaryEntry>order of plants distinguished by having a one-celled inferior ovary; many are parasitic or partly parasitic usually on roots</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109034259">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chiefly tropical herbs or shrubs or trees bearing nuts or one-seeded fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109034546"
	 b:glossaryEntry="parasitic Indo-Malayan trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109034684">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Indo-Malayan parasitic tree having fragrant close-grained yellowish heartwood with insect-repelling properties and used for e.g. chests</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109034954">
	<b:glossaryEntry>close-grained fragrant yellowish heartwood of the true sandalwood; has insect-repelling properties and is used for carving and cabinetwork</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109035168"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of Asiatic and American parasitic shrubs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109035319">
	<b:glossaryEntry>parasitic shrub of the eastern United States having opposite leaves and insignificant greenish flowers followed by oily dull-green olivelike fruits</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109035566"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of chiefly North American parasitic plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109035730">
	<b:glossaryEntry>woody creeping parasite of western North America having numerous thick powderly-looking leaves and panicles of small dull-white flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109035966"
	 b:glossaryEntry="quandong trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109036114"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Australian tree with edible flesh and edible nutlike seed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109036351"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of chiefly Asiatic parasitic shrubs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109036510">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shrub of southeasternUni ted States parasitic on roots of hemlocks having sparse spikes of greenish flowers and pulpy drupes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109036762"
	 b:glossaryEntry="oily drupaceous fruit of rabbitwood"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109036894">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in some classification includes Viscaceae: parasitic or hemiparasitic shrublets or shrubs or small trees of tropical and temperate regions; attach to hosts by haustoria</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109037256"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Loranthaceae: 1 species"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109037409">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shrub of central and southeastern Europe; partially parasitic on beeches, chestnuts and oaks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109037598"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of chiefly American plants parasitic on conifers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109037770">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small herb with scalelike leaves on reddish-brown stems and berrylike fruits; parasitic on spruce and larch trees</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109037991"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one species"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109038110">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a terrestrial evergreen shrub or small tree of western Australia having brilliant yellow-orange flowers; parasitic on roots of grasses</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109038370">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in some classifications considered a subfamily of Loranthaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109038583">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Viscaceae: Old World evergreen shrubs parasitic on many trees including oaks but especially apple trees, poplars, aspens and cottonwoods</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109038843">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Old World parasitic shrub having branching greenish stems with leathery leaves and waxy white glutinous berries; the traditional mistletoe of Christmas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109039106">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various American parasitic plants similar to Old World mistletoe: false mistletoe</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109039312"
	 b:glossaryEntry="American plants closely resembling Old World mistletoe"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109039477">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the traditional mistletoe of Christmas in America: grows on deciduous trees and can severely weaken the host plant</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109039707"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an order od dicotyledonous plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109040025">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chiefly tropical New and Old World deciduous and evergreen trees and shrubs bearing leathery drupes with yellow translucent flesh; most plants produce toxic saponins</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109040459"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a tree of the genus Sapindus whose fruit is rich in saponin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109040646"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Sapindaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109040823">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deciduous tree of southwestern United States having pulpy fruit containing saponin</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109041029">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen of tropical America having pulpy fruit containing saponin which was used as soap by native Americans</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109041278">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of western African evergreen trees and shrubs bearing fleshy capsular three-seeded fruits edible when neither unripe nor overripe</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109041527">
	<b:glossaryEntry>widely cultivated in tropical and subtropical regions for its fragrant flowers and colorful fruits; introduced in Jamaica by Captain Bligh of HMS Bounty</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109041797">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tendril-climbing herbs or shrubs whose seeds have a white heart-shaped spot</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109041992"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tendril-climbing vine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109042129"
	 b:glossaryEntry="herbaceous vine of tropical America and Africa"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109042260">
	<b:glossaryEntry>woody perennial climbing plant with large ornamental seed pods that resemble balloons; tropical India and Africa and America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109042483"
	 b:glossaryEntry="longan"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109042603">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tree of southeastern Asia to Australia grown primarily for its sweet translucent-fleshed edible fruit resembling litchi nuts; sometimes placed in genera Euphoria or Nephelium</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109042949"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of tropical Asiatic and African trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109043092"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of various tree of the genus Harpullia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109043245">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fast-growing tree of India and East Indies yielding a wood used especially for building</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109043411"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Australian tree yielding a variegated tulipwood"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109043546"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Chinese trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109043656">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Chinese tree cultivated especially in Philippines and India for its edible fruit; sometimes placed in genus Nephelium</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109043925"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tropical American trees and shrubs bearing berries"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109044119">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tropical American tree bearing a small edible fruit with green leathery skin and sweet juicy translucent pulp</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109044429">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a genus of dicotyledonous trees of the family Sapindaceae that are native to Asia and Australia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109044655"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Malayan tree bearing spiny red fruit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109044833">
	<b:glossaryEntry>East Indian fruit tree bearing fruit similar to but sweeter than that of the rambutan</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109045057"
	 b:glossaryEntry="widely distributed evergreen shrubs and trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109045245"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Buxaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109045375"
	 b:glossaryEntry="evergreen shrubs or small trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109045522">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large slow-growing multistemmed evergreen shrub or small tree; extensively used for hedges or borders and topiary figures</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109045736">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very hard tough close-grained light yellow wood of the box especially the common box; used in delicate woodwork: musical instruments and inlays and engraving blocks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109045991"
	 b:glossaryEntry="evergreen perennial procumbent subshrubs or herbs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109046142">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any plant of the genus Pachysandra; low-growing evergreen herbs or subshrubs having dentate leaves and used as ground cover</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109046378">
	<b:glossaryEntry>low semi-evergreen perennial herb having small spikes of white or pinkish flowers; native to southern United States but grown elsewhere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109046629">
	<b:glossaryEntry>slow-growing Japanese evergreen subshrub having terminal spikes of white flowers; grown as a ground cover</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109046821">
	<b:glossaryEntry>trees and shrubs and woody vines usually having bright-colored fruits</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109047070">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Celastraceae: bittersweet and shrubby bittersweet; woody vines and shrubs native chiefly to Asia and Australia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109047348"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any small tree or twining shrub of the genus Celastrus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109047478">
	<b:glossaryEntry>twining shrub of North America having three-valved yellow capsules enclosing scarlet seeds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109047755">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ornamental Asiatic vine with showy orange-yellow fruit with a scarlet aril; naturalized in North America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109048036">
	<b:glossaryEntry>widely distributed chiefly evergreen shrubs or small trees or vines</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109048270">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any shrubby trees or woody vines of the genus Euonymus having showy usually reddish berries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109048510">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small erect deciduous shrub having tough white wood and cathartic bark and fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109048677">
	<b:glossaryEntry>bushy deciduous shrub with branches having thin wide corky longitudinal wings; brilliant red in autumn; northeastern Asia to central China</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109048900">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deciduous shrub having purple capsules enclosing scarlet seeds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109049073">
	<b:glossaryEntry>upright deciduous plant with crimson pods and seeds; the eastern United States from New York to Florida and Texas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109049297">
	<b:glossaryEntry>broad and bushy Asiatic twining shrub with pinkish fruit; many subspecies or varieties</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109049527">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shrubs and trees with leathery leaves and small white flowers in racemes: genera Cyrilla and Cliftonia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109049798">
	<b:glossaryEntry>1 species: trees and shrubs having flowers with acute or twisted petals and wingless fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109049986">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shrub or small tree of southeastern United States to West Indies and Brazil; grown for the slender racemes of white flowers and orange-crimson foliage</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109050258"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1 species: titi"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109050385">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tree of low-lying coastal areas of southeastern United States having glossy leaves and racemes of fragrant white flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109050615"
	 b:glossaryEntry="heathlike shrubs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109050767"
	 b:glossaryEntry="crowberries"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109050888">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a low evergreen shrub with small purple flowers and black berrylike fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109051036">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a family of trees and shrubs of order Sapindales including the maples</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109051252">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Aceraceae; trees or shrubs having winged fruit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109051417">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous trees or shrubs of the genus Acer bearing winged seeds in pairs; north temperate zone</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109051877">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wood of any of various maple trees; especially the hard close-grained wood of the sugar maple; used esp for furniture and flooring</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109052096"
	 b:glossaryEntry="maple wood having a wavy grain with eyelike markings"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109052211">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a common North American maple tree; 5-lobed leaves are light green above and silvery white beneath; source of hard close-grained but brittle light-brown wood</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109052446">
	<b:glossaryEntry>maple of eastern and central North America having 3- to 5-lobed leaves and hard close-grained wood much used for cabinet work especially the curly-grained form; sap is chief source of maple syrup and maple sugar; many subspecies</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109052763">
	<b:glossaryEntry>maple of eastern and central America; 5-lobed leaves scarlet and yellow in autumn</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109052944">
	<b:glossaryEntry>maple of eastern North America with striped bard and large 2-lobed leaves clear yellow in autumn</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109053170">
	<b:glossaryEntry>maple of western North America having large 5-lobed leaves orange in autumn</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109053341"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small maple of northwestern North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109053478">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small shrubby maple of eastern North America; scarlet in autumn</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109053635">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small maple of northwestern North America having prostrate stems that root freely and form dense thickets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109053815"
	 b:glossaryEntry="shrubby Eurasian maple often used as a hedge"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109053948">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a large Eurasian maple tree naturalized in North America; 5-lobed leaves yellow in autumn; cultivated in many varieties</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109054145">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Eurasian maple tree with pale gray bark that peels in flakes like that of a sycamore tree; leaves with five ovate lobes yellow in autumn</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109054389"
	 b:glossaryEntry="common shade tree of eastern and central United States"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109054551">
	<b:glossaryEntry>maple of the United States Pacific coast; fruits are white when mature</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109054716">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small shrubby Japanese plant with leaves having 5 to 7 acuminate lobes; yellow in autumn</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109054884"
	 b:glossaryEntry="leaves deeply incised and bright red in autumn; Japan"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109055033">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ornamental shrub or small tree of Japan and Korea with deeply incised leaves; cultivated in many varieties</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109055216">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of large deciduous shrubs having large clusters of winged seeds that turn red as they mature; central and southern China</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109055443"
	 b:glossaryEntry="widely distributed shrubs and trees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109055614"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any tree or shrub of the genus Ilex"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109055954">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a large genus of dicotyledonous trees and shrubs of the family Aquifoliaceae that have small flowers and berries (including hollies)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109056207">
	<b:glossaryEntry>dense rounded evergreen shrub of China having spiny leaves; widely cultivated as an ornamental</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109056395"
	 b:glossaryEntry="deciduous shrub of southeastern and central United States"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109056550">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen holly of eastern North America with oblong leathery leaves and small black berries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109056747"
	 b:glossaryEntry="South American holly; leaves used in making a tealike drink"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109056894"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an evergreen tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109057008"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an evergreen shrub"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109057092"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an evergreen shrub"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109057177"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an evergreen shrub"/>
