</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109490432">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of a number of families into which the family Polypodiaceae has been subdivided in some classification systems; Pteridaceae is itself in turn sometimes subdivided into the families Actiniopteridaceae; Adiantaceae; Cryptogrammataceae; includes genera Acrostichum; Adiantopsis; Adiantum; Anogramma; Bommeria; Cheilanthes; Coniogramme; Doryopteris; Gymnopteris; Hemionitis; Jamesonia; Paraceterach; Pellaea; Pityrogramma; Pteris; Ptilopteris</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109491219">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in some classification systems placed in family Polypodiaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109491396">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stout tropical swamp fern (especially tropical America) having large fronds with golden yellow sporangia covering the undersides</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109491637"
	 b:glossaryEntry="terrestrial ferns of tropical Asia and Africa"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109491783">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cosmopolitan genus of ferns: maidenhair ferns; in some classification systems placed in family Polypodiaceae or Adiantaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109492016">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various small to large terrestrial ferns of the genus Adiantum having delicate palmately branched fronds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109492293">
	<b:glossaryEntry>delicate maidenhair fern with slender shining black leaf stalks; cosmopolitan</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109492534">
	<b:glossaryEntry>hardy palmately branched North American fern with divergent recurved branches borne on lustrous dark reddish stipes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109492771"
	 b:glossaryEntry="delicate endemic Bermudian fern with creeping rootstock"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109492935"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tropical American fern with broad pinnae; widely cultivated"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109493122">
	<b:glossaryEntry>named for a country house in Barbados where it was discovered</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109493337"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a genus of ferns belonging to the family Pteridaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109493501"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small short-lived fern of Central and South America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109493667">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small evergreen ferns: lipferns; in some classifications placed in family Polypodiaceae or Adiantaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109493904">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various terrestrial ferns of the genus Cheilanthes; cosmopolitan in arid and semi-arid temperate or tropical regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109494165"
	 b:glossaryEntry="southeastern United States to northern Mexico and Jamaica"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109494329"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small tufted fern of northwestern America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109494470">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small North American evergreen fern whose stipes and lower frond surfaces are densely wooly</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109494660">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lip fern of Texas to Oklahoma and Colorado and Arizona and Mexico having tall erect tufted fronds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109494847">
	<b:glossaryEntry>terrestrial ferns of Pacific islands and Asia; sometimes placed in family Polypodaceae or Adiantaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109495064">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fast-growing sturdy Japanese fern; cultivated for their attractive broad dark-green pinnate fronds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109495262">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sometimes placed in family Polypodiaceae or Cryptogrammataceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109495461"
	 b:glossaryEntry="dwarf deciduous lithophytic ferns"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109495606">
	<b:glossaryEntry>rock-inhabiting fern of northern North America growing in massive tufts and having parsleylike fronds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109495846">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fern of Europe and Asia Minor having short slender rhizome and densely tufted bright green parsleylike fronds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109496069">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small to medium tropical tufted ferns; sometimes placed in family Adiantaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109496262">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tropical American fern with coarsely lobed to palmatifid fronds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109496421"
	 b:glossaryEntry="xerophytic ferns of South America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109496547">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small terrestrial ferns of Old World tropics and subtropics: claw ferns; sometimes placed in family Cryptogrammataceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109496756">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of chiefly small rock-loving ferns; in some classifican systems placed in family Polypodiaceae or Adiantaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109497017">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several small lithophytic ferns of tropical and warm temperate regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109497251"
	 b:glossaryEntry="evergreen fern of California and Baja California"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109497401">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very short shallowly creeping North American fern usually growing on cliffs or walls and having dark glossy leaf axes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109497605">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cliff brake of California and Baja California having purple-brown leafstalks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109497785">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fern of New Zealand and Australia having trailing fronds with dark green buttonlike leaflets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109497977">
	<b:glossaryEntry>terrestrial tropical ferns having fronds with powdery yellowish or white undersides; sometimes placed in family Polypodiaceae or Adiantaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109498292">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fern of southern tropical Africa having fronds with white undersides</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109498461"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tropical American fern having fronds with white undersides"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109498623">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tropical American fern having fronds with light golden undersides</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109498803">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fern of West Indies and South America having fronds with bright golden-yellow undersides</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109498994">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large genus of terrestrial ferns of tropics and subtropics; sometimes placed in family Polypodiaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109499257">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various ferns of the genus Pteris having pinnately compound leaves and including several popular houseplants</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109499443"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cultivated in many varieties as houseplants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109499566"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Asiatic fern introduced in America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109499711"
	 b:glossaryEntry="fern of North Africa and Azores and Canary Islands"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109499871"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lower ferns coextensive with the family Marattiaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109500039">
	<b:glossaryEntry>constituting the order Marattiales: chiefly tropical eusporangiate ferns with gigantic fronds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109500289">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Marattiaceae: ferns having the sporangia fused together in two rows</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109500484">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large Australasian evergreen fern with an edible rhizome sometimes used as a vegetable by indigenous people</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109500688"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1 species: tree fern"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109500810">
	<b:glossaryEntry>highly variable species of very large primitive ferns of the Pacific tropical areas with high rainfall</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109501023"
	 b:glossaryEntry="fairly small terrestrial ferns of tropical America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109501160">
	<b:glossaryEntry>whisk ferns; comprising the family Psilotaceae or Psilotatae: vascular plants with no roots, partial if any leaf differentiation, and rudimentary spore sacs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109501462">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lower vascular plants having dichotomously branched sporophyte divided into aerial shoot and rhizome and lacking true roots</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109501699">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small family of lower ferns having nearly naked stems and minute scalelike leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109501897"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Psilotaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109502036">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chiefly tropical clump-forming plants of skeletal broom-like appearance lacking roots</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109502215">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pantropical epiphytic or terrestrial whisk fern with usually dull yellow branches and minute leaves; America; Japan; Australia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109502423">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Paleozoic simple dichotomously branched plants of Europe and eastern Canada including the oldest known vascular land plants</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109502704"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any plant of the order Psilophytales: a savannah plant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109502834"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Paleozoic plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109502973">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Psilophytaceae: genus of small wiry herbaceous Paleozoic plants with underground rhizomes and apical sporangia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109503203"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any plant or fossil of the genus Psilophyton"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109503324"
	 b:glossaryEntry="primitive plants of the Paleozoic"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109503491">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type genus of the Rhyniaceae; small leafless dichotomously branching fossil plants with terminal sporangia and smooth branching rhizomes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109503714">
	<b:glossaryEntry>Devonian fossil plant considered one of the earliest forms of vascular land plants; similar to genus Rhynia but smaller</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109503932"
	 b:glossaryEntry="horsetails and related forms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109504110"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lower tracheophytes in existence since the Devonian"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109504277"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sole surviving family of the Equisetales: fern allies"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109504468"
	 b:glossaryEntry="horsetails; coextensive with the family Equisetaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109504632">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial rushlike flowerless herbs with jointed hollow stems and narrow toothlike leaves that spread by creeping rhizomes; tend to become weedy; common in northern hemisphere; some in Africa and South America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109505043"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of Eurasia and Greenland and North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109505186"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Eurasia; northern North America to Virginia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109505332">
	<b:glossaryEntry>evergreen erect horsetail with rough-edged stems; formerly used for scouring utensils</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109505565">
	<b:glossaryEntry>scouring-rush horsetail widely distributed in wet or boggy areas of northern hemisphere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109505735"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Eurasia except southern Russia; northern North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109505873">
	<b:glossaryEntry>northern North America; Greenland; northern and central Europe</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109506052">
	<b:glossaryEntry>club mosses and related forms: includes Lycopodiales; Isoetales; Selaginellales; and extinct Lepidodendrales; sometimes considered a subdivision of Tracheophyta</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109506453">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used in some classifications for the class Lycopsida: club mosses</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109506593"
	 b:glossaryEntry="alternative designation for the class Lycopsida"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109506739">
	<b:glossaryEntry>primitive evergreen mosslike plant with spores in club-shaped strobiles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109507015">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fossil arborescent plants arising during the Lower Devonian and conspicuous throughout the Carboniferous</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109507243">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fossil plants characterized by conspicuous spirally arranged leaf scars on the trunk</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109507435">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lower vascular plants coextensive with the family Lycopodiaceae; in some classifications includes the Selaginellaceae and Isoetaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109507685"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a family of ferns belonging to the order Lycopodiales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109507877">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type and sole genus of the Lycopodiaceae; erect or creeping evergreen plants often used for Christmas decorations</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109508085"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a variety of clubmoss"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109508192"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a variety of clubmoss"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109508296"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of northern Europe and America; resembling a miniature fir"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109508471">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several club mosses having long creeping stems and erect branches</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109508691"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a variety of clubmoss"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109508793"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a variety of clubmoss"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109508914"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a variety of clubmoss"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109509046">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ground pine thickly covered with bristly leaves; widely distributed in barren sandy or peaty moist coastal regions of eastern and southeastern United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109509289"
	 b:glossaryEntry="in some classifications included in Lycopodiales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109509459">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lesser clubmosses: terrestrial chiefly tropical plants resembling mosses</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109509656">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type and sole genus of the Selaginellaceae; evergreen mosslike plants: spike moss and little club moss</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109509873"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of numerous fern allies of the genus Selaginella"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109510121"
	 b:glossaryEntry="spikemoss forming dense mats; eastern North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109510274">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tufted spikemoss forming loose spreading mats; eastern North America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109510443"
	 b:glossaryEntry="prostrate spikemoss; California"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109510564">
	<b:glossaryEntry>densely tufted fern ally of southwestern United States to Peru; curls up in a tight ball when dry and expands and grows under moist conditions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109510816"
	 b:glossaryEntry="occurs widely in Florida"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109510928">
	<b:glossaryEntry>aquatic or marsh-growing fern allies; known to have existed since the Cenozoic; sometimes included in Lycopodiales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109511154"
	 b:glossaryEntry="quillworts; coextensive with the genus Isoetes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109511334">
	<b:glossaryEntry>type and genus of the Isoetaceae and sole extant genus of the order Isoetales</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109511519">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several spore-bearing aquatic or marsh plants having short rhizomes and quill-like leaves; worldwide except Polynesia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109511718"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a family of fungi belonging to the order Helotiales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109511890"
	 b:glossaryEntry="type genus of the Geoglossaceae comprising the earthtongues"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109512063"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any club-shaped fungus of the genus Geoglossum"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109512186">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of a number of families into which the family Polypodiaceae has been subdivided in some classification systems</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109512412"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genera Thelypteris; Phegopteris; others"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109512730">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marsh ferns: in some classification systems considered part of genus Dryopteris in family Dryopteridaceae</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109512951">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fern having pinnatifid fronds and growing in wet places; cosmopolitan in north temperate regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109513190"
	 b:glossaryEntry="fern of northeastern North America"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109513367">
	<b:glossaryEntry>epiphytic or terrestrial ferns of America and Africa and Polynesia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109513530">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium to large terrestrial ferns of tropical forests of Old World to Americas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109513709">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several tropical ferns of the genus Christella having thin brittle fronds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109513865">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small genus of terrestrial ferns of tropical and subtropical southern hemisphere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109514040">
	<b:glossaryEntry>terrestrial ferns of Florida and West Indies to Central and South America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109514191">
	<b:glossaryEntry>medium to large terrestrial ferns of tropical Asia to Polynesia and Australia; naturalized in Americas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109514400"
	 b:glossaryEntry="terrestrial ferns of tropical Americas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109514531">
	<b:glossaryEntry>3 species of ferns formerly included in genus Dryopteris or Thelypteris</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109514716">
	<b:glossaryEntry>common European mountain fern having fragrant lemon or balsam scented fronds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109514920">
	<b:glossaryEntry>terrestrial ferns of warm and tropical Asia and North America</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109515124">
	<b:glossaryEntry>slender shield fern of moist woods of eastern North America; sometimes placed in genus Dryopteris</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109515363">
	<b:glossaryEntry>delicate feathery shield fern of the eastern United States; sometimes placed in genus Thelypteris</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109515594">
	<b:glossaryEntry>beech ferns: genus is variously classified: considered alternative name for genus Drypopteris or included in genus Thelypteris</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109515836">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any fern of the genus Phegopteris having deeply cut triangular fronds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109516017">
	<b:glossaryEntry>beech fern of North American woodlands having straw-colored stripes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109516252"
	 b:glossaryEntry="beech fern of North America and Eurasia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109516472">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a dense mass of hyphae forming a rootlike structure characteristic of many fungi</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109516609">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of edible mushrooms having white spores an annulus and blue juice; some are edible; some cause root rot</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109516851">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several fungi of the genus Armillaria that form brown stringy rhizomorphs and cause destructive rot of the roots of some trees such as apples or maples</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109517092">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fungus with a brown cap and white gills and a membranous ring halfway up the stalk</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109517279">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a large white mushroom that develops brown stains as it ages; gills are white; odor is spicy and aromatic; collected commercially for oriental cooking the Pacific Northwest</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109517536">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a large fungus with viscid cap that dries and turns brown with age; gills are off-white</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109517688">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a honey-colored diminutive form of genus Armillaria; grows in clusters; edible (when cooked) but most attention has been on how to get rid of it</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109517952">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a honey-colored edible mushroom commonly associated with the roots of trees in late summer and fall; do not eat raw</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109518185">
	<b:glossaryEntry>widely distributed family of herbs and shrubs of the order Gentianales; most with milky juice</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109518590"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any plant of the family Asclepiadaceae"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109518703">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of chiefly North American perennial herbs: silkweed; milkweed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109518882">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of numerous plants of the genus Asclepias having milky juice and pods that split open releasing seeds with downy tufts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109519269">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall herb with leafless waxy-white stems and whitish starlike flowers; southwestern United States</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109519448">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tropical herb having orange-red flowers followed by pods suggesting a swallow with outspread wings; a weed throughout the tropics</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109519674">
	<b:glossaryEntry>milkweed of the eastern United States with leaves resembling those of pokeweed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109519833">
	<b:glossaryEntry>densely branching perennial of the eastern United States with white to crimson or purple flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109520012"
	 b:glossaryEntry="milkweed of central North America; a threatened species"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109520166">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perennial of eastern North America having pink-purple flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109520314">
	<b:glossaryEntry>milkweed of southern North America having large starry purple-pink flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109520470">
	<b:glossaryEntry>milkweed of southwestern United States and Mexico; poisonous to livestock</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109520654">
	<b:glossaryEntry>erect perennial of eastern and southern United States having showy orange flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109520900">
	<b:glossaryEntry>milkweed of the eastern United States with narrow leaves in whorls and greenish-white flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109521081"
	 b:glossaryEntry="small genus of South American evergreen vines"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109521234">
	<b:glossaryEntry>robust twining shrub having racemes of fragrant white or pink flowers with flat spreading terminal petals that trap nocturnal moths and hold them until dawn</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109521488"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of perennial tropical African lianas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109521629">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various mostly giant tropical lianas of Africa and Madagascar having greenish or purple flowers and long smooth pods; roots formerly used as an emetic</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109521860">
	<b:glossaryEntry>large genus of climbing shrubs of Australia and Asia and Polynesia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109522021">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any plant of the genus Hoya having fleshy leaves and usually nectariferous flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109522209"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a plant that furnishes nectar suitable for making honey"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109522322">
	<b:glossaryEntry>succulent climber of southern Asia with umbels of pink and white star-shaped flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109522477"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of woody vines of warm regions of the Old World"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109522642">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deciduous climber for arches and fences having ill-scented but interesting flowers and poisonous yellow fruits; cultivated for its dark shining foliage; southeastern Europe to Asia Minor</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109522922">
	<b:glossaryEntry>succulent subshrubs or vines; tropical and subtropical India and Africa and Malaysia</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109523122">
	<b:glossaryEntry>leafless East Indian vine; its sour milky juice formerly used to make an intoxicating drink</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109523312">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of evil-smelling cactus-like plants; Africa to East India</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109523493">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various plants of the genus Stapelia having succulent leafless toothed cactus-like stems and large evil-smelling flowers often star-shaped</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109523765">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stapelia of Cape Province Africa having mostly dark red-brown flowers with flat starlike corollas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109523946"
	 b:glossaryEntry="genus of Old World tropical woody vines"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109524106">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various evergreen climbing shrubs of the genus Stephanotis having fragrant waxy flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109524295">
	<b:glossaryEntry>twining woody vine of Madagascar having thick dark waxy evergreen leaves and clusters of large fragrant waxy white flowers along the stems; widely cultivated in warm regions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109524589">
	<b:glossaryEntry>genus of chiefly tropical American vines having cordate leaves and large purple or greenish cymose flowers; supposedly having powers as an antidote</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109524854">
	<b:glossaryEntry>twining vine with hairy foliage and dark purplish-brown flowers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109525039">
	<b:glossaryEntry>forest or woodland having a mature or overmature ecosystem more or less uninfluenced by human activity</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109525214">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a second growth of trees covering an area where the original stand was destroyed by fire or cutting</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109525373">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the biblical tree in the Garden of Eden whose forbidden fruit was tasted by Adam and Eve</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109525525"
	 b:glossaryEntry="possession with the right to transfer possession to others"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109525765">
	<b:glossaryEntry>by title vested in oneself or by virtue of qualifications one has achieved; "a peer of the realm in his own right"; "a leading sports figure in his own right"; "a fine opera in its own right"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109526012"
	 b:glossaryEntry='common ownership; "they shared a community of possessions"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109526126"
	 b:glossaryEntry="exclusive individual ownership"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109526213"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the right of ownership"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109526314">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the right granting to shareholders the first opportunity to buy a new issue of stock; provides protection against dilution of the shareholder''s ownership interest</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109526539">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the right of a shareholder in a company to subscribe to shares of a new issue of common stock before it is offered to the public</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109526732">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the right to buy or sell property at an agreed price; the right is purchased and if it is not exercised by a stated date the money is forfeited</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109527072">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the right to buy or sell a stock at a specified price within a stated period</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109527243"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an option to buy"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109527317"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an option to sell"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109527391">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an option to buy the crown jewels offered to a white knight in order to forestall a hostile takeover</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109527552">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the right to hold property; part of an ancient hierarchical system of holding lands</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109527756">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a medieval form of land tenure in England; a copyhold was a parcel of land granted to a peasant by the lord of the manor in return for agricultural services</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109527967"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tenure by which land is held in fee simple or for life"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109528076"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an estate held in fee simple or for life"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109528171"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tenure by which a villein held land"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109528263">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a corporation''s purchase of its own outstanding stock; increases earnings/share so stock price rises (which can discourage a takeover attempt)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109528465"
	 b:glossaryEntry="property rights that are held by the public at large"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109528577">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an unincorporated business owned by a single person who is responsible for its liabilities and entitled to its profits</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109528770"
	 b:glossaryEntry="ownership of a business by the people who work for it"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109528888">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any tangible possession that is owned by someone; "that hat is my property"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109529387">
	<b:glossaryEntry>all the property that you possess; "he left all his worldly possessions to his daughter"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109529578">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) property that provides tax income for local governments</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109529710">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any property (real or personal or mixed) that can be inherited</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109529831"
	 b:glossaryEntry="property and income belonging jointly to a married couple"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109529953"
	 b:glossaryEntry="movable property (as distinguished from real estate)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109530191">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any tangible movable property (furniture or domestic animals or a car etc)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109530338">
	<b:glossaryEntry>property of a personal character that is portable but not used in business; "she left some of her personal effects in the house"; "I watched over their effects until they returned"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109530591">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any movable possession (especially articles of clothing); "she packed her things and left"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109530734"
	 b:glossaryEntry="property consisting of houses and land"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109530909">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extensive landed property (especially in the country) retained by the owner for his own use; "the family owned a large estate on Long Island"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109531396"
	 b:glossaryEntry="property held in common"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109531475">
	<b:glossaryEntry>plot of land belonging to an English parish church or an ecclesiastical office</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109531605"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a holding in the form of land"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109531692"
	 b:glossaryEntry="ownership of land; the state or fact of owning land"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109531801"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a holding in the form of shares of corporations"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109531907"
	 b:glossaryEntry="property or income owned by a church"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109532070"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the worldly possessions of a church"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109532176"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an endowed church office giving income to its holder"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109532327">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a benefice to which no spiritual or pastoral duties are attached</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109532446"
	 b:glossaryEntry="property that is leased or rented out or let"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109532579">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an item of property that is given in part payment for a new one</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109532697"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a lease from one one lessee to another"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109532799"
	 b:glossaryEntry="property owned by a government"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109532891"
	 b:glossaryEntry="land or property held under a lease"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109532982">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) a piece of land under 50 acres that is sold or let to someone for cultivation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109533128">
	<b:glossaryEntry>land acquired from the US public lands by filing a record and living on and cultivating it under the homestead law</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109533298"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the home and adjacent grounds occupied by a family"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109533404"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the buildings and adjacent grounds of a farm"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109533504"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a piece of land held under the feudal system"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109533607">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the land on which real estate is located; "he built the house on land leased from the city"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109533785">
	<b:glossaryEntry>real property held inalienably (as by an ecclesiastical corporation)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109533920">
	<b:glossaryEntry>property that has economic utility: a monetary value or an exchange value</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109534064">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wealth reckoned in terms of money: "all his money is in real estate"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109534202"
	 b:glossaryEntry="informal: a large sum of money"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109534283">
	<b:glossaryEntry>all of your assets (whether real or personal property) and liabilities</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109534478">
	<b:glossaryEntry>informal terms for personal possessions; "did you take all your clobber?"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109534613">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the total valuation of the estate''s assets at the time of the person''s death</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109534748">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the estate remaining after debts and funeral expenses and administrative expenses have been deducted from the gross estate; the estate then left to be distributed (and subject to federal and state inheritance taxes)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109535020">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an estate whose duration is limited to the life of the person holding it</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109535186"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the estate of a baron"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109535260"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an estate in the country"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109535342"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(Great Britain) land that belongs to the Crown"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109535445"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the landed estate of a lord (including the house on it)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109535552"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the estate of a seigneur"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109535653"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a large estate in Spanish-speaking countries"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109535771">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an estate where cash crops are grown on a large scale (especially in tropical areas)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109535912"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a territorial possession controlled by a ruling state"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109536193">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a dependent country; administered by a country under the supervision of the United Nations</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109536359"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a valuable possession whole upkeep is expensive"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109536467"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a possession whose ownership changes or lapses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109536731"
	 b:glossaryEntry='something acquired; "a recent acquisition by the museum"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109536899"
	 b:glossaryEntry="something acquired by purchase"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109537020">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an advantageous purchase; "she got a bargain at the auction"; "the stock was a real buy at that price"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109537226"
	 b:glossaryEntry='a very small sum; "he bought it for a song"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109537320">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bargain rate for travellers on commercial routes (usually air routes)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109537452"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(law) a transfer of property by deed of conveyance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109537603">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a grant (by a sovereign or a legislative body) of resources to maintain a dependent member of a ruling family</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109537777"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a grant of public land (as to a railway or college)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109537885">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the amount by which the revenue of a business exceeds its cost of operating</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109538047"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the amount of monetary gain"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109538207">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the financial gain (earned or unearned) accruing over a given period of time</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109538570">
	<b:glossaryEntry>income (after taxes) that is available to you for saving or spending</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109538702"
	 b:glossaryEntry="income obtained with a minimum of effort"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109538813"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the economic condition in which credit is easy to secure"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109538944">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the economic condition in which credit is difficult to secure</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109539081"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the property that reverts to the state"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109539173"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the entire amount of income before any deductions are made"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109539340">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the total value of all income in a nation (wages and profits and interest and rents and pension payments) during a given period (usually 1 yr)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109539544">
	<b:glossaryEntry>former measure of the US economy; the total market value of goods and services produced by all citizens and capital during a given period (usually 1 yr)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109539789">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a version of the GNP that has been adjusted for the effects of inflation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109539946">
	<b:glossaryEntry>measure of the US economy adopted in 1991; the total market values of goods and services by produced by workers and capital within the US borders during a given period (usually 1 yr)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109540203">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a statistical factor designed to remove the effect of inflation; inflation adjusted variables are in constant dollars</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109540375">
	<b:glossaryEntry>payment to the holder of a patent or copyright or resource for the right to use their property</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109540523"
	 b:glossaryEntry="total admission receipts for an entertainment"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109540625"
	 b:glossaryEntry="total admission receipts at a sports event"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109540718">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the excess of revenues over outlays in a given period of time</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109541038">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the portion of a company''s profit allocated to each outstanding share of common stock</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109541187">
	<b:glossaryEntry>profit that occurs unexpectedly as a consequence of some event not controlled by those who profit from it</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109541354"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(informal) a very large profit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109541448"
	 b:glossaryEntry="something won (especially money)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109541569">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the income arising from land or other property; "the average return was about 5%"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109541774">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(corporate finance) net sales minus the cost of goods and services sold</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109541935">
	<b:glossaryEntry>income (at invoice values) received for goods and services over some given period of time</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109542106">
	<b:glossaryEntry>gross sales reduced by customer discounts, returns, freight out, and allowances</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109542241"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ratio gross profits divided by net sales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109542379">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(accounting) income received but not yet earned (usually considered a current liability on a company''s balance sheet)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109542577">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(taxes) personal income that you did not earn (e.g., dividends or interest or rent income)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109542748"
	 b:glossaryEntry="income available to the government"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109542885"
	 b:glossaryEntry="government income due to taxation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109543028"
	 b:glossaryEntry="government revenue from domestic sources (excluding customs)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109543151">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the total national income divided by the number of people in the nation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109543286"
	 b:glossaryEntry="property that has been stolen"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109543413">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(usually plural) valuables taken by violence (especially in war); "to the victor belong the spoils of the enemy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109543577"
	 b:glossaryEntry="goods or money obtained illegally"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109543723"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a title or property that is inherited"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109543974"
	 b:glossaryEntry="right of inheritance belongs exclusively to the eldest son"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109544092">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a former English custom by which the youngest son inherited land to the exclusion of his older brothers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109544257">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(law) an increase in a beneficiary''s share in an estate (as when a co-beneficiary dies or fails to meet some condition or rejects the inheritance)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109544459"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(law) a gift of personal property by will"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109544581">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an inheritance coming by right of birth (especially by primogeniture)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109544719"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(law) a gift of real estate by will"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109544825">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a life estate to which a wife is entitled on the death of her husband</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109544946">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an estate secured to a prospective wife as a marriage settlement in lieu of a dower</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109545084"
	 b:glossaryEntry="something that has been in a family for generations"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109545190">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(law) any property that is considered by law or custom as inseparable from an inheritance is inherited with that inheritance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109545388"
	 b:glossaryEntry="something acquired without compensation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109545730">
	<b:glossaryEntry>money or property brought by a woman to her husband at marriage</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109545862">
	<b:glossaryEntry>money or property given (in some societies) by the bridegroom to the family of his bride</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109546008">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a gift or money given (as for service or out of benevolence); usually given ostentatiously</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109546163"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a gift of money to support a worthy person or cause"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109546354">
	<b:glossaryEntry>financial aid provided to a student on the basis of academic merit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109546496">
	<b:glossaryEntry>money granted (by a university or foundation or other agency) for advanced study or research</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109546663">
	<b:glossaryEntry>aid (such as economic or military assistance) provided to one nation by another</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109546801"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any monetary aid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109546905">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a grant paid by a government to an enterprise that benefits the public: "a subsidy for research in artificial intelligence"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109547118">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grant of financial aid as from a government to an educational institution</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109547248">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a government subsidy used to maintain prices at a certain level</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109547371"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a grant to a person or school for some educational project"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109547488">
	<b:glossaryEntry>financial aid that enables you to get trained for a specified job; "the bill provided traineeships in vocational rehabilitation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109547674">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an award (as for meritorious service) given without claim or obligation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109547800">
	<b:glossaryEntry>something given for victory or superiority in a contest or competition; "the prize was a free trip to Europe"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109548059"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any outstanding award"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109548134"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any money given as a prize"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109548218">
	<b:glossaryEntry>something presented as a gift; "his tie was a present from his wife"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109548394"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a present given in celebration of a person''s birthday"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109548526"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a present given at Christmas time"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109548640"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a present given to someone getting married"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109548777"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a wedding present to the bride"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109548864">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an amount of money contributed; "he expected his contribution to be repaid with interest"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109549012">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a voluntary gift (as of money or service or ideas) made to some worthwhile cause</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109549198"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a contribution of money to a religious organization"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109549376"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an offering of a tenth part of some personal income"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109549479"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the offerings of the congregation at a religious service"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109549591">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an annual contribution made by Roman Catholics to support the Papal see</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109549737"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a contribution of money or assistance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109549832"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the capital that provides income for an institution"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109549992"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a gift that significantly increases the recipient''s wealth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109550107"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a church endowment"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109550181"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an endowment for the singing of Masses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109550273">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a small gift (especially one given by a merchant to a customer who makes a purchase)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109550413"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a gift that is bestowed or conferred"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109550517">
	<b:glossaryEntry>payment or reward (esp from a government) for acts such as catching criminals or killing predatory animals or enlisting in the military</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109550715">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(informal) a gift of public land or resources for the private gain of a limited group</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109550855">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a gift (usually of inferior quality) that should be accepted uncritically; "it wasn''t much, but don''t look a gift horse in the mouth"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109551045"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an offering made as an expression of thanks"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109551149">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an additional payment to employees as a means of increasing output</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109551334">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a formal scheme for inducing someone (as employees) to do something</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109551503"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a sudden unexpected piece of good fortune"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109551625">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an amount that can be deducted (especially for the purposes of calculating income tax)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109551768"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a bonus; something extra (especially a share of a surplus)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109551881">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an amount added or deducted on the basis of qualifying circumstances; "an allowance for profit"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109552117"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an allowance for changes in the consumer price index"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109552240"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an allowance for loss due to depreciation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109552350"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an amount or percentage deducted"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109552467">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a discount from the list price of a commodity allowed by a manufacturer or wholesaler to a merchant</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109552627">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a statistical adjustment made to accommodate predictable fluctuations as a function of the season of the year; "seasonal adjustments for housing starts must be made in mid-winter"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109552872"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money paid out"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109553033">
	<b:glossaryEntry>amounts paid for goods and services that may be currently tax deductible (as opposed to capital expenditures)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109553349">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the total spent for goods or services including money and time and labor</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109553814">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ordinary and necessary expenses incurred in a taxpayer''s business or trade</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109553967">
	<b:glossaryEntry>expenses incurred in promoting or evaluating legislation; "many lobbying expenses are deductible by a taxpayer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109554141">
	<b:glossaryEntry>amount spent for diagnosis or treatment or prevention of medical problems</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109554276">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the cost of moving your residence from one location to another</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109554399">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the expense of maintaining property (e.g., paying property taxes and utilities and insurance); it does not include depreciation or the cost of financing or income taxes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109554694">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the cost (over a period of five years) of organizing a new corporation or partnership</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109554846">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the cost of personal or family living; "some personal expenses are tax deductible"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109554991">
	<b:glossaryEntry>money spent to perform work and usually reimbursed by an employer; "he kept a careful record of his expenses at the meeting"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109555187">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a public expenditure (as for unemployment compensation or veteran''s benefits) that is not for goods and services</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109555362"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the cost of long-term improvements"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109555462"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a sum of money paid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109556039"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a payment larger than needed or expected"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109556137"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a payment smaller than needed or expected"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109556237">
	<b:glossaryEntry>something that remunerates; "wages were paid by check"; "he wasted his pay on drink"; "they saved a quarter of all their earnings"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109556658"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money paid to strikers from union funds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109556754"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a doubled wage (for working overtime)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109556849">
	<b:glossaryEntry>food and lodging provided in addition to money; "they worked for $30 and found"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109556980"
	 b:glossaryEntry="reduced wage paid to someone who is not working full time"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109557092">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a wage sufficient for a worker and family to subsist comfortably</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109557214">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extra pay awarded to an employee on the basis of merit (especially to school teachers)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109557356">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the lowest wage that an employer is allowed to pay; determined by contract or by law</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109557499">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wages enclosed in an envelope for distribution to the wage earner</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109557636"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wages paid to an employee who is on sick leave"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109557737">
	<b:glossaryEntry>what is left of your pay after deductions for taxes and dues and insurance etc</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109557875">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a payment for consecutive issues of a newspaper or magazine for a given period of time</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109558020"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a payment made at regular times"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109558221">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[amount of money received per unit time; "women''s pay rate is lower than men''s"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109558386">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a rate of pay that is 1.5 times the regular rate; for overtime work</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109558515"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the amount of money paid out per unit time"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109558670"
	 b:glossaryEntry="paid to a hired murderer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109558752"
	 b:glossaryEntry="payment or reward (as for service rendered)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109558888"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money returned to a payer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109558984"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a refund of some fraction of the amount paid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109559110">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) a rebate on rent given by a local government authority</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109559232"
	 b:glossaryEntry="something given to recompense for loss or injury"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109559483"
	 b:glossaryEntry="excessive compensation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109559568">
	<b:glossaryEntry>compensation for death or injury suffered by a worker in the course of his employment</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109559722">
	<b:glossaryEntry>payment made voluntarily to reduce guilt over dishonest dealings</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109559849"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a payment made by one person for the support of another"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109560020">
	<b:glossaryEntry>support paid by one half of an unmarried partnership after the relationship ends</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109560155">
	<b:glossaryEntry>court-ordered support paid by one spouse to another after they are separated</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109560299"
	 b:glossaryEntry="payment made in return for a service rendered"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109560487"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fee paid for a nominally free service"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109560583"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money demanded for the return of a captured person"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109560701"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a reward for information about a murderer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109560800"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a reward or payment"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109560873"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(archaic) a fitting reward"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109560950">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a payment made to someone to insure that something is kept secret</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109561072">
	<b:glossaryEntry>payment made to a person in a position of trust to corrupt his judgment</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109561258">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a commercial bribe paid by a seller to a purchasing agent in order to induce the agent to enter into the transaction</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109561429"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money offered as a bribe"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109561504">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bribe given to a disc jockey to induce him to promote a particular record</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109561632">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an asset belonging to or due to or contributed by an individual person or group; "he wanted his share in cash"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109562041"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a share that has been dispensed or distributed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109562146">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a share of money or food or clothing that has been charitably given</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109562264">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a portion of something divided into shares: "the split the loot three ways"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109562389"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fixed portion alloted (especially in times of scarcity)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109562499">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[an amount allowed or granted (as during a given period): "travel allowance"; "my weekly allowance of two eggs"; "a child''s allowance should not be too generous"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109562733">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a share of something; "a slice of the company''s revenue"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109562849">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a promised or claimed share of loot or money; "he demanded his split before they disbanded"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109562992">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a right or legal share of something; a financial involvement with something; "they have interests all over the world"; "a stake in the company''s future"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109563387">
	<b:glossaryEntry>funds advanced to a prospector or to someone starting a business in return for a share of the profits</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109563544"
	 b:glossaryEntry="ownership of more than 50% of a corporation''s voting shares"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109563670">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an interest in which there is a fixed right to present or future enjoyment</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109563806">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any interest in a property that secures the payment of an obligation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109564010">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an interest in property that terminates under specific conditions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109564141">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the interest in property owned by tenants whereby each tenant has an equal right to enjoy the entire property</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109564333"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an interest in land capable of being inherited"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109564465">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fee without limitation to any class of heirs; the can sell it or give it away</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109564601">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fee limited to a particular line of heirs; they are not free to sell it or give it away</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109564745"
	 b:glossaryEntry="land received by fee tail"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109564823">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a system in which employees receive a share of the net profits of the business</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109564962"
	 b:glossaryEntry='a share of the profits; "everyone got a cut of the earnings"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109565109">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a percentage (of winnings or loot or profit) taken by an operator or gangster</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109565241"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a share set aside for a specific purpose"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109565386"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a share that has been allocated again"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109565482"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a proportional share assigned to each participant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109565583"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money set aside (as by a legislature) for a specific purpose"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109565721">
	<b:glossaryEntry>compensation paid (to someone) for damages or losses or money already spent etc.; "he received reimbursement for his travel expenses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109565950">
	<b:glossaryEntry>compensation received by virtue of holding an office or having employment (usually in the form of wages or fees); "a clause in the U.S. constitution prevents sitting legislators from receiving emoluments from their own votes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109566231"
	 b:glossaryEntry="compensation paid to the family of a murdered person"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109566341"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a sum of money paid in compensation for loss or injury"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109566602">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(law) redress awarded by a court; "was the relief supposed to be protection from future harm or compensation for past injury?"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109566781"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a compensating equivalent"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109566876">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(law) compensation for losses that can readily be proven to have occurred and for which the injured party has the right to be compensated</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109567115">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(law) a trivial sum (usually $1.00) awarded as recognition that a legal injury was sustained (as for technical violations of a contract)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109567313">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(law) compensation in excess of actual damages (a form of punishment awarded in cases of malicious or willful misconduct)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109567567">
	<b:glossaryEntry>twice the amount that a court would normally find the injured party entitled to</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109567707">
	<b:glossaryEntry>three times the amount that a court would normally find the injured party entitled to</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109567853"
	 b:glossaryEntry="compensation exacted from a defeated nation by the victors"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109567968">
	<b:glossaryEntry>compensation for a wrong; "we were unable to get satisfaction from the local store"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109568134">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(often plural) a payment that is made to a performer or writer or director of a television show or commercial that is paid for every repeat showing; "he could retire on his residuals"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109568372"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the income received by a single individual"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109568494"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a level of personal income defining the state of poverty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109568609"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a sum granted as reimbursement for expenses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109568726">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pecuniary reimbursement to the winning party for the expenses of litigation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109568853"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a sum allowed for travel"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109568958"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a travel allowance at a given rate per mile traveled"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109569064">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a sum of money allotted on a regular basis; usually for some specific purpose</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109569213"
	 b:glossaryEntry="allowance for a monarch''s personal expenses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109569314"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the stipend assigned by a cathedral to a canon"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109569414">
	<b:glossaryEntry>income from capital investment paid in a series of regular payments; "his retirement fund was set up to be paid as an annuity"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109569674">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an annuity paid in a series of more or less equal payments at the beginning of equally spaced periods; "rent payable in advance constitutes an annuity in advance for the landlord"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109569936">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an annuity paid in a series of more or less equal payments at the end of equally spaced periods</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109570094">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an annuity payable to one person in the event that someone else is unable to receive it</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109570271">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an annuity scheme wherein participants share certain benefits and on the death of any participant his benefits are redistributed among the remaining participants; can run for a fixed period of time or until the death of all but one participant</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109570568">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a regular payment by a tenant to a landlord for use of some property</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109570723"
	 b:glossaryEntry="payment for the right to occupy and improve a piece of land"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109570840"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an extortionate rent"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109570916">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the return derived from cultivated land in excess of that derived from the poorest land cultivated under similar conditions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109571106"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a system for paying for goods by installments"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109571263"
	 b:glossaryEntry="financial assistance in time of need"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109571443">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a benefit that goes to anyone whose money receipts increase automatically as prices rise</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109571600">
	<b:glossaryEntry>insurance or pension money payable to a beneficiary of a deceased</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109571743">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a percentage of death benefits paid directly to policy holders having a short life expectancy (usually 6 months)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109571941">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cash derived from sale of an insurance policy by a terminally ill policy holder</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109572086"
	 b:glossaryEntry="insurance benefits paid in case of disability"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109572196">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) money paid (by the government) to someone who is too ill to work</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109572348">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an incidental benefit awarded for certain types of employment (especially if it is regarded as a right); "a limousine is one of the fringe benefits of the job"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109572624">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any customary and rightful perquisite appropriate to your station in life</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109572762">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax deduction that is granted in order to encourage a particular type of commercial activity</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109572926">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a relatively small amount of money given for services rendered (as by a waiter)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109573130">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) a present given at Christmas for services during the year</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109573257">
	<b:glossaryEntry>court-ordered support paid by one spouse to the other who has custody of the children after the parents are separated</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109573434"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the final payment of a debt"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109573530"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a payment of money sent to a person in another place"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109573662"
	 b:glossaryEntry="payment of a debt or obligation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109573761"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a small payment made in acknowledgement of an obligation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109573877"
	 b:glossaryEntry="loss resulting from failure of a debt to be paid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109574042"
	 b:glossaryEntry="nonpayment of a debt when due"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109574129"
	 b:glossaryEntry="act of failing to meet a financial obligation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109574255"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a payment required for not fulfilling a contract"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109574411">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an inadequate payment; "they work all day for a mere pittance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109574528"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a justly deserved penalty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109574623">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a penalty for a fault or mistake that involves losing or giving up something; "the contract specified forfeits if the work was not completed on time"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109574839"
	 b:glossaryEntry="something that is lost or surrendered as a penalty;"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109574958"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money extracted as a penalty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109575058"
	 b:glossaryEntry="payment for insurance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109575153">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a payment of part of a debt; usually paid at regular intervals</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109575273">
	<b:glossaryEntry>excess of cost over budget; "the cost overrun necessitated an additional allocation of funds in the budget"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109575439">
	<b:glossaryEntry>average cost of basic necessities of life (as food and shelter and clothing); "a rise in the cost of living reflects the rate of inflation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109575639"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the cost of borrowing something"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109575731">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any cost incurred by a producer or wholesaler or retailer or distributor (as for advertising and shipping etc)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109575905">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the cost of handling (especially the cost of packaging and mailing an order)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109576059">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the cost of marketing (e.g., the cost of transferring title and moving goods to the customer)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109576213">
	<b:glossaryEntry>combined costs of raw material and labor incurred in producing goods</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109576343">
	<b:glossaryEntry>current cost of replacing a fixed asset with a new one of equal effectiveness</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109576483">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cost of reproducing physical property minus various allowances (especially depreciation)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109576652"
	 b:glossaryEntry="calculated cost for a given unit of a product"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109576753">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the amount of money needed to purchase something; "the price of gasoline"; "he got his new car on excellent terms"; "how much is the damage?"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109577053">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cost of bribing someone: "they say that every politician has a price"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109577174"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the amount added to the cost to determine the asking price"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109577285">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(on the stock exchange) the price at which a broker is willing to buy a certain security</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109577429">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(on the stock exchange) the price of the last transaction completed during a day''s trading session</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109577587"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the price at which something is offered for sale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109577764">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(on the stock exchange) the price at which a broker is willing to sell a certain security</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109577911">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(in an auction) the minimum price at which a seller of property will entertain bids</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109578052"
	 b:glossaryEntry="price charged for goods picked up at the factory"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109578160">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the selling price of something as stated in a catalogue or price list</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109578286"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the price at which something is actually purchased"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109578397"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cost in terms of foregone alternatives"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109578516">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the opportunity cost of unproductive assets; the expense incurred by ownership</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109578672"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the cost of carrying or transporting"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109578762">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(frequently plural) an expense not budgeted or not specified; "he requested reimbursement of $7 for incidental expenses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109578976">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the price charged for some article or service; "the admission charge"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109579386">
	<b:glossaryEntry>charge made for carrying an account or for merchandise sold on an installment plan</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109579530">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an amount periodically charged to expense or against revenue in compensation for depreciation of property</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109579701"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a price that is too low"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109579782"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a price that is too high"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109579881"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an exorbitant charge"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109579957">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a charge added at a restaurant for every bottle of wine served that was not bought on the premises</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109580109"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the sum charged for riding in a public conveyance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109580263"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the fare charged for riding a bus or streetcar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109580374"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the fare charged for riding in a taxicab"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109580481"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the fare charged for riding a subway train"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109580581"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the fare charged for riding a train"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109580691"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a charge imposed and collected"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109580808">
	<b:glossaryEntry>charge against a citizen''s person or property or activity for the support of government</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109581284"
	 b:glossaryEntry="collective value of taxable assets"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109581373"
	 b:glossaryEntry="rate used to calculate tax liability"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109581464">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the amount of tax owed; calculated by applying the tax rate to the tax base</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109581599">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a system of taxation in which a tax is levied on a single commodity (usually land)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109581738"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a personal tax levied on annual income"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109581905">
	<b:glossaryEntry>income tax paid periodically on income that is not subject to withholding taxes; based on the taxpayer''s predicted tax liability</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109582093">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax on employees and employers that is used to fund the Social Security system</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109582224">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a reduction in the gross amount on which a tax is calculated; reduces taxes by the percentage fixed for the taxpayer''s income bracket</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109582465"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tax deduction for expenses of doing business"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109582592">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deduction allowed for some (limited) kinds of entertainment for business purposes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109582743">
	<b:glossaryEntry>income tax withheld from employees'' wages and paid directly to the government by the employer</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109582930"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the British system of withholding tax"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109583036">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an unearned rise in the market value of property resulting from general market factors</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109583187">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the amount by which the selling price of an asset exceeds the purchase price</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109583322">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the amount by which the purchase price of an asset exceeds the selling price</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109583457">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax on capital gains; "he avoided the capital gains tax by short selling"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109583596"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a tax on capital or property"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109583701"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a capital tax on property imposed by municipalities"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109583822">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax that is imposed by states on corporations; it depends both on the net worth of the corporation and on its net income attributable to activities within the state</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109584048">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax imposed on transfers of property by gift during the lifetime of the giver</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109584182"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a tax on the estate of the deceased person"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109584324">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax paid directly by the person or organization on whom it is levied</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109584451">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an advantage bestowed by legislation that reduces a tax on some preferred activity</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109584593"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a way of organizing business to reduce the taxes it must pay"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109584721">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax levied on goods or services rather than on persons or organizations</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109584871">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax paid unwittingly by the consumer (such as ad valorem taxes)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109584993"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a tax levied on the basis of a fixed amount per person"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109585122">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax of a fixed amount per person and payable as a requirement for the right to vote</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109585262">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any tax in which the rate increases as the amount subject to taxation increases</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109585403">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) a local tax on property (usually used in the plural)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109585534"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(British) a local tax for the relief of the poor"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109585638">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax collected by requiring a stamp to be purchased and attached (usually on documents or publications)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109585811">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an additional tax on certain kinds of income that has already been taxed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109585947"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a tax toward paving streets"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109586027"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any tax levied on the passing of title to property"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109586154"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a levy of one tenth of something"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109586238">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an additional tax levied on private property for public improvements that enhance the value of the property</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109586410">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a government tax on imports or exports; "they signed a treaty to lower duties on trade between their countries"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109586725">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax that is measured by the amount of business done (not on property or income from real estate)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109586943"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a tax based on the cost of the item purchased"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109587044">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax levied on the difference between a commodity''s price before taxes and its cost of production</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109587227"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a tax on every gallon of gasoline sold"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109587324"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money collected under a tariff"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109587459">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an impost levied in England to provide money for ships for national defense</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109587591">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tax imposed on ships that enter the US; based on the tonnage of the ship</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109587744"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a tax on various goods brought into a town"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109587839"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a tariff imposed to raise revenue"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109587933">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a tariff imposed to protect domestic firms from import competition</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109588081"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a tariff imposed to prevent dumping"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109588180"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a duty imposed on imports"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109588263"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a duty imposed on exports"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109588346"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a duty imposed to offset subsidies by foreign governments"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109588469">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a periodic charge that does not vary with business volume (as insurance or rent or mortgage payments etc.)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109588706">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fixed charge by a restaurant or night club over and above the charge for food and drink</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109588854">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fixed charge for borrowing money; usually a percentage of the amount borrowed; "how much interest do you pay on your mortgage?"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109589074"
	 b:glossaryEntry="interest paid on the principle alone"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109589172">
	<b:glossaryEntry>interest calculated on both the principal and the accrued interest</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109589302"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the percentage of a sum of money charged for its use"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109589505"
	 b:glossaryEntry="interest on an annual basis deducted in advance on a loan"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109589667"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the discount rate fixed by a central bank"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109589764">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(US) the rate of interest set by the Federal Reserve that member banks are charged when they borrow money through the Federal Reserve System</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109589964">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the interest rate on short-term loans that banks charge their commercial customers with high credit ratings</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109590137"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an exorbitant or unlawful rate of interest"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109590231"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fixed charge for professional services"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109590573"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a charge for storing goods in a cellar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109590667">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fee for services rendered based on a percentage of an amount received or collected or agreed to be paid (as distinguished from a salary); "he works on commission"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109590888"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fee charged for a vessel to use a dock"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109590996"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the fee charged for admission"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109591187"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fee paid for legal service"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109591289">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fee paid to the government for the privilege of being licensed to do something (as selling liquor or practicing medicine)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109591486">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) a fee (in addition to that marked on the brief) paid to counsel in a case that lasts more than one day</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109591654"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the charge for carrying goods in lighters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109591752">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a charge to a borrower (especially for a mortgage loan) to cover the costs of initiating the loan</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109591911"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fee charged for the use of pipes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109591998"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fee charged for work done"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109592096"
	 b:glossaryEntry="charged by a government for coining bullion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109592197">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fee levied for the use of roads or bridges (used for maintenance)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109592315">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fee paid for instruction (especially for higher education); "tuition and room and board were more than $25,000"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109592482"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fee charged for the use of a wharf"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109592573"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fee charged for exchanging currencies"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109592703">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a charge required as compensation for the delay of a ship or freight car or other cargo beyond its scheduled time of departure</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109592885"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the charge for installing something"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109592986"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the charge for carrying burdens by porters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109593084">
	<b:glossaryEntry>amount of a charge or payment relative to some basis; "a 10-minute phone call at that rate would cost $5"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109593438"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(Brit) rate per quarter for water from a public supply"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109593549">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an additional charge (as for items previously omitted or as a penalty for failure to exercise common caution or common skill)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109593730">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a percentage of a bill (as at a hotel or restaurant) added in payment for service</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109593890"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the charge for stowing goods"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109593972"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the charge for storing something in tanks"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109594067">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the charge for transporting something by common carrier; "we pay the freight," or "the freight rate is usually cheaper"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109594268">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the rate at which the value of property is reduced; used to calculate tax deduction</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109594438">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the charge for exchanging currency of one country for currency of another</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109594590"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a reduced rate for a round-trip ticket"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109594689"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a rate of charging by the linear foot of work done"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109594793">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a rate of payment for written material that is measured according to the number of lines submitted</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109594954">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the amount by which the cost of a business exceeds its revenue; "the company operated at a loss last year"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109595129">
	<b:glossaryEntry>something that is lost; "the car was a total loss"; "loss of livestock left the rancher bankrupt"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109595349"
	 b:glossaryEntry="loss of money or decrease in financial value"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109595562">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a loss entailed by giving up or selling something at less than its value; "he had to sell his car at a considerable sacrifice"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109595744"
	 b:glossaryEntry="anything lost by wear or waste"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109595828">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the loss to an insurance company resulting from possible lack of prudence or honesty on the part of policyholders</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109596000"
	 b:glossaryEntry="decrease in value of an asset due to obsolescence or use"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109596149"
	 b:glossaryEntry="reduction in the book value of an asset"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109596257"
	 b:glossaryEntry="something lost (especially money lost at gambling)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109596388"
	 b:glossaryEntry="anything of material value or usefulness"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109596858"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money that you currently expect to pay on notes and accounts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109596973">
	<b:glossaryEntry>money that you currently expect to receive from notes or accounts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109597096">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the most desirable assets of a corporation; "their crown jewels figured prominently in the takeover attempt"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109597262">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capital held back from investment in order to meet probable or possible demands</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109597419">
	<b:glossaryEntry>reserve assets in the International Money Fund; designed to supplement reserves of gold and convertible currencies used to maintain stability in the foreign exchange market</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109597673">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a quantity of money; "he borrowed a large sum"; "the amount he had in cash was insufficient"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109598019">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an amount of money expressed numerically; "a figure of $17 was suggested"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109598145">
	<b:glossaryEntry>available source of wealth; a new or reserve supply that can be drawn upon when needed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109598358"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resources (actual and potential) supplied by nature"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109598528"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resources of available manpower"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109598621"
	 b:glossaryEntry="natural resources in the form or arable land"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109598726"
	 b:glossaryEntry="natural resources in the form of minerals"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109598831"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an asset that is saleable though not material or physical"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109598982">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(accounting) an intangible asset valued according to the advantage or reputation a business has acquired (over and above its tangible assets)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109599190">
	<b:glossaryEntry>assets in the form of money (or easily convertible into money)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109599330"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money that is invested with an expectation of profit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109599476"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ownership interest of shareholders in a corporation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109599584">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the difference between the market value of a property and the claims held against it</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109599721">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[the capital raised by a corporation through the issue of shares entitling holders to partial ownership; "he owns a controlling share of the company''s stock"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109600182">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ownership of stocks; the state or fact of holding stock: "prohibition of unrestricted intercorporate stockholding"- W.Z.Ripley</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109600367">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a specific number of stocks or shares owned: "sell holdings he has in corporations"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109600525"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the book value of the outstanding shares of a corporation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109600642">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a common stock of a nationally known company whose value and dividends are reliable; typically have high price and low yield</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109600840">
	<b:glossaryEntry>common stock classified as A or B where A has certain advantages (e.g., voting power) that B does not</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109601004"
	 b:glossaryEntry="stock other than preferred stock"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109601183"
	 b:glossaryEntry="stock held by stockholders of record on a given date"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109601297">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the value of a security that is set by the company issuing it; unrelated to market value</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109601470">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stock with no par value specified kin the corporate charter or on the stock certificate</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109601637">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stock whose holders are guaranteed priority in the payment of dividends</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109601827">
	<b:glossaryEntry>preferred stock whose dividends if omitted accumulate until paid out</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109601993">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the number of shares outstanding and available for trading by the public</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109602117">
	<b:glossaryEntry>preferred stock or convertible bonds or stock warrants that can be converted into common stock</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109602281"
	 b:glossaryEntry="shares owned by shareholders who have a controlling interest"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109602401">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stock of a corporation that has had faster than average gains in earnings and is expected to continue to</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109602564"
	 b:glossaryEntry="newly issued stock that is in great public demand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109602681"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a stock selling for less that $1/share"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109602777">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the value at which an asset is carried on a balance sheet; equals cost minus accumulated depreciation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109602953">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the price at which buyers and sellers trade the item in an open marketplace</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109603102"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a bond that is back by collateral"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109603212">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond that is backed by the credit of the issuer but not by any specific collateral</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109603404">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond issued with detachable coupons that must be presented to the issuer for interest payments</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109603572">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a security issued by US government agencies or the Federal Land Bank</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109603724">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a security created when a group of mortgages are gathered together and bonds are sold to other institutions or the public; investors receive a portion of the interest payments on the mortgages as well as the principal payments; usually guaranteed by the government</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109604059">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a security whose owner''s name is recorded on the books of the issuer (or issuer''s agent)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109604231">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond whose owner is recorded on the books of the issuer; can be transferred to another owner only when endorsed by the registered owner</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109604448">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond that can be converted to other securities under certain conditions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109604584">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond issued by a corporation; "a corporate bond is a safer investment than common stock in the same company"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109604755">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond that is an IOU on the US Treasury; considered the safest security in the investment world</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109604949">
	<b:glossaryEntry>non-negotiable government bond; cannot be bought and sold once the original purchase is made</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109605100">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond given to protect the recipient against loss in case the terms of a contract are not filled; a surety company assumes liability for nonperformance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109605329">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond issued by an agency that is commissioned to finance public works; revenue from the public property is used to pay off the bond</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109605521">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a (speculative) bond with a credit rating of BB or lower; issued for leveraged buyouts and other takeovers by companies with questionable credit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109605721"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a bond issued by a state or local government"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109605844">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond issued to finance the construction of public utility services</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109605994">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond made by a reversioner to secure a loan; payable out of his reversion</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109606130">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond (corporate or government) that is issued at a deep discount from its value at maturity and pays no interest during the life of the bond</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109606335">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(law) an interest in an estate that reverts to the grantor (or his heirs) at the end of some period (e.g., the death of the grantee)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109606541">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a reversion to the state (as the ultimate owner of property) in the absence of legal heirs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109606685">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(frequently plural) the interest possessed by law or custom in some intangible thing: "mineral rights"; "film rights"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109606854">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of the equal portions into which the capital stock of a corporation is divided and ownership of which is evidenced by a stock certificate; "he bought 100 shares of IBM at the market price"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109607117">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the maximum number of shares authorized under the the terms of a corporations articles of incorporation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109607319"
	 b:glossaryEntry="stock with a par value of $25/share"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109607414">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an investment that is very risky but could yield great profits; "he knew the stock was a speculation when he bought it"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109607637">
	<b:glossaryEntry>money bet or invested by experienced gamblers or investors (especially if they have inside information)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109607798"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money that is risked for possible monetary gain"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109607916"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the money risked on a gamble"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109608092"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the cumulative stake in a game (such as poker)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109608206">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(in poker) the initial contribution that each player makes to the pot</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109608326">
	<b:glossaryEntry>defense against financial failure; "his pension gave him security in his old age"; "insurance provided protection against loss of wages due to illness"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109608581">
	<b:glossaryEntry>taking two positions that will offset each other if prices change and so limiting financial risk</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109608739">
	<b:glossaryEntry>promise of reimbursement in the case of loss; paid to people or companies so concerned about hazards that they have made prepayments to an insurance company</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109609106"
	 b:glossaryEntry="insurance issued jointly by two or more underwriters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109609216">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[sharing the risk by insurance companies; part or all of the insurer''s risk is assumed by other companies in return for part of the premium paid by the insured; "reinsurance enables a client to get coverage that would be too great for any one company to assume"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109609534">
	<b:glossaryEntry>insuring oneself by setting aside money to cover possible losses rather than by purchasing an insurance policy</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109609705"
	 b:glossaryEntry="insurance against loss due to ill health"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109609826">
	<b:glossaryEntry>group insurance that entitles members to services of participating hospitals and clinics and physicians</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109610013"
	 b:glossaryEntry="insurance against loss due to fire"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109610108">
	<b:glossaryEntry>insurance paid to named beneficiaries when the insured person dies; "in England they call life insurance life assurance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109610342"
	 b:glossaryEntry="insurance against loss due to traffic accidents"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109610472"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a British term for some kinds of insurance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109610570">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a form of life insurance whereby on the death or default of a participant his share is distributed to the remaining members</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109610767">
	<b:glossaryEntry>low-cost life insurance; valid only for a stated period of time</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109610891">
	<b:glossaryEntry>property that your creditor can claim in case you default on your obligation; "bankers are reluctant to lend without good security"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109611158">
	<b:glossaryEntry>money given as security for an article acquired for temporary use; "his deposit was refunded when he returned the car"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109611330">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a partial payment made at the time of purchase; the balance to be paid later</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109611493">
	<b:glossaryEntry>something of value given by one person to another to bind a contract</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109611633"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money given by a buyer to a seller to bind a contract"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109611746">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(law) a security entered into before a court with a condition to perform some act required by law; on failure to perform that act a sum is forfeited</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109611986">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a deposit of personal property as security for a debt; "his saxophone was in pledge"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109612141"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an article deposited as security"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109612224">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(criminal law) money that must be forfeited by the bondsman if an accused person fails to appear in court for trial; "the judge set bail at $10,000"; "a $10,000 bond was furnished by an alderman"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109612489">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the amount of collateral a customer deposits with a broker when borrowing from the broker to buy securities</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109612668">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a fund that a customer has entrusted to a securities brokerage; "you can''t get a brokerage account unless you have $20,000"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109612891">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an account with a securities brokerage whose transactions are settled on a cash basis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109613071">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a brokerage firm account that parents have created for a minor</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109613197">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an account with a securities brokerage in which the broker extends credit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109613349">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a conditional conveyance of property as security for the repayment of a loan</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109613516"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a security interest taken by the seller in return for credit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109613639">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a mortgage that has priority over all mortgages and liens except those imposed by law</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109613785"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a mortgage that is subordinate to a first mortgage"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109613897"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a security pledged for the repayment of a loan"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109614018">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a collateral agreement to answer for the debt of another in case that person defaults</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109614170"
	 b:glossaryEntry="assets in the form of material possessions"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109614312"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an abundance of material possessions and resources"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109614460">
	<b:glossaryEntry>great wealth; "Whilst that for which all virtue now is sold, and almost every vice--almighty gold"--Ben Jonson</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109614621"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the material wealth owned by a person or a business"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109614780">
	<b:glossaryEntry>considerable capital (wealth or income); "he is a man of means"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109614925"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the necessary means (especially financial means)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109615031">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wealth available for investment in new or speculative enterprises</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109615173"
	 b:glossaryEntry="assets available for use in the production of further assets"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109615378">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capital available for the operations of a firm (e.g. manufacturing or transportation) as distinct from financial transactions and long-term improvements</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109615594">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a statement of recent transactions and the resulting balance; "they send me an accounting every month"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109615892">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a financial statement that gives operating results for a specific period</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109616095">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(finance) an account of the net value of a business at a specified date</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109616228">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(economics) that part of the balance of payments recording a nation''s outflow and inflow of financial securities</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109616421"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capital as contrasted with the income derived from it"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109616555"
	 b:glossaryEntry="assets in the form of money"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109616845">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the funds held by a gambling house or the dealer in some gambling games; "he tried to break the bank at Monte Carlo"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109617012">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a roll of currency notes (often taken as the resources of a person or business etc.); "he shot his roll on a bob-tailed nag"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109617198">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a supply of money; "they dipped into the taxpayers'' pockets"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109617311"
	 b:glossaryEntry="public funds used to pay for medicaid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109617409"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the funds of a government or institution or individual"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109617602">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the total stock of money in the economy; currency held by the public plus money in accounts in banks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109617834">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the money supply; includes currency in circulation plus demand deposits or checking account balances</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109617996">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the money supply; M1 plus net time deposits (other than large certificates of deposit)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109618144">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the money supply; M2 plus deposits at institutions that are not banks (such as savings and loan associations)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109618315"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a treasury for government funds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109618424">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a legislative appropriation designed to ingratiate legislators with their constituents</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109618575"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the treasury of a public institution or religious order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109618684"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a subordinate treasury or place of deposit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109618784"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a royal treasury"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109618851"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a reserve of money set aside for some purpose"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109619143">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fund which, if borrowed or used, is intended to be replenished so it may be loaned or spent repeatedly</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109619308">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fund accumulated regularly in a separate account and used to redeem debt securities</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109619452"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fund of money put by as a reserve"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109619552">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fund that a customer has entrusted to a bank and from which they can make withdrawals; "he moved his bank account to a new bank"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109619795">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) an account at a post office that can be used in similar ways to an account at a bank</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109619948"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fund reserved to pay workers'' pensions"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109620047">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a fund accumulated to finance a war (or a political campaign)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109620164"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fund for buying votes or bribing public officials"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109620272">
	<b:glossaryEntry>something (as property) held by one party (the trustee) for the benefit of another (the beneficiary); "he is the beneficiary of a generous trust set up by his father"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109620706"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a trust in which the trustee must perform certain duties"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109620839"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a trust in which the trustee performs no active duties"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109620971">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a trust created for charitable or religious or educational or scientific purposes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109621130">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a trust established to shift the income to someone who is taxed at a lower rate than the grantor for a period of 10 years or more</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109621336"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a trust inferred by operation of law"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109621468">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a trust created by a court (regardless of the intent of the parties) to benefit a party that has been wrongfully deprived of its rights</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109621688">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a trust created by a court when it is judged that it was the intention of the parties to create a trust</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109621853">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a trust created by the free and deliberate act of the parties involved (usually on the basis of written documentation)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109622046">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a trust that gives the trustee discretion to pay the beneficiary as much of the trust income as the trustee believes appropriate</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109622240">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an investment trust that can buy only those securities listed when the trust was organized</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109622424"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a trust created and operating during the grantor''s lifetime"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109622562">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a trust created to maintain a beneficiary but to be secure against the beneficiary''s improvidence</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109622723">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a trust that is created under a will and that becomes active after the grantor dies</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109622871">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a savings account deposited by someone who makes themselves the trustee for a beneficiary and who controls it during their lifetime; afterward the balance is payable to the previously named beneficiary</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109623210">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an agreement whereby persons owning stock with voting powers retain ownership while transferring the voting rights to the trustees</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109623399"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a fund held in trust"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109623476">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bank account against which the depositor can drawn checks payable on demand</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109623616"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a bank account that accumulates interest"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109623772">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a savings account in which the deposit is held for a fixed term or in which withdrawals can be made only after giving notice</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109623963">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a savings account showing no activity (other than posting interest) for some specified period; "the dormant account reverted to the state under escheat laws"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109624182">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a savings account in which deposits and withdrawals are recorded in the depositor''s passbook</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109624345"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a debt instrument issued by a bank; usually pays interest"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109624494">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the financial means whereby one lives; "each child was expected to pay for their keep"; "he applied to the state for support"; "he could no longer earn his own livelihood"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109624834">
	<b:glossaryEntry>financial resources provided to make some project possible; "the foundation provided support for the experiment"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109625060">
	<b:glossaryEntry>minimal (or marginal) resources for subsisting; "social security provided only a bare subsistence"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109625216"
	 b:glossaryEntry="means of maintenance of a family or group"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109625315">
	<b:glossaryEntry>things that make you comfortable and at ease; "all the comforts of home"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109625489">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(finance) profits that are not paid out as dividends but are added to the capital base of the corporation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109625689"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a secret store of valuables or money"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109625793">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a supply of something available for future use; "he brought back a large store of Cuban cigars"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109626009"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a store or supply (especially of food or clothing or arms)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109626162">
	<b:glossaryEntry>supplies (as food or clothing or ammunition) issued by the government</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109626319"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a supply of seeds (or tubers) reserved for planting"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109626445">
	<b:glossaryEntry>good quality seeds (as kernels of corn) that are reserved for planting</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109626584">
	<b:glossaryEntry>something kept back or saved for future use or a special purpose</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109626760">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a supply or stock held in reserve especially for future use (especially in emergencies)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109626952">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a place for storing whole blood or blood plasma; "the Red Cross created a blood bank for emergencies"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109627110">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a place where food is contributed and made available to those in need; "they set up a food bank for the flood victims"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109627284">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any asset that is concealed until it can be used advantageously</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109627403">
	<b:glossaryEntry>land retired from crop cultivation and planted with soil-building crops; government subsidies are paid to farmers for their retired land</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109627595"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the combined stakes of the betters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109627688">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any communal combination of funds; "everyone contributed to the pool"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109627827">
	<b:glossaryEntry>funds taken out of earnings to provide for anticipated future payments</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109628010">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a reserve fund created by a charge against profits in order to provide for changes in the value of a company''s assets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109628243">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any possession that is highly valued by its owner; "the children returned from the seashore with their shells and other treasures"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109628428">
	<b:glossaryEntry>accumulated wealth in the form of money or jewels etc.; "the pirates hid their treasure on a small island in the West Indies"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109628697"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a large amount of wealth or prosperity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109628789"
	 b:glossaryEntry='something of value; "all our valuables were stolen"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109628931"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(informal) valuable goods"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109629007"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a very large treasure"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109629088">
	<b:glossaryEntry>treasure of unknown ownership found hidden (usually in the earth)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109629222">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of the less common and valuable metals often used to make coins or jewelry</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109629415">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a mass of precious metal (usually in the form of bars or ingots)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109629533"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coins made of gold"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109629621"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coins made of silver"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109629713">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a transparent piece of diamond that has been cut and polished and is valued as a precious gem</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109629878"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(informal) diamonds; "look at the ice on that dame!"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109629980">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a transparent piece of ruby that has been cut and polished and is valued as a precious gem</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109630121">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a smooth lustrous round structure inside the shell of a clam or oyster; much valued as a jewel</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109630285"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a small imperfect pearl"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109630365">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a transparent piece of emerald that has been cut and polished and is valued as a precious gem</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109630512">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a transparent piece of sapphire that has been cut and polished and is valued as a precious gem</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109630661">
	<b:glossaryEntry>anything that is generally accepted as a standard of value and a measure of wealth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109630898"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the value behind the money in a monetary system"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109631074">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a monetary standard under which the basic unit of currency is defined by a stated quantity of gold</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109631232">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a monetary standard under which the basic unit of currency is defined by a stated quantity of silver</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109631394">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a monetary standard under which the basic unit of currency is defined by stated amounts of two metals (usually gold and silver) with values set at a predetermined ratio</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109631620"
	 b:glossaryEntry="something used as an official medium of payment"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109631753"
	 b:glossaryEntry="government-issued stamps used in exchange for food"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109631860"
	 b:glossaryEntry="arrangement for deferred payment for goods and services"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109632005"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a line of credit extended for personal or household use"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109632176"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a loan secured by equity value in the borrower''s home"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109632333"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a loan repaid with interest in equal periodic payments"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109632470">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a consumer credit line that can be used up to a certain limit or paid down at any time</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109632715"
	 b:glossaryEntry="credit extended by a business to a customer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109632869">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a charge account that does not have to be paid to zero balance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109633002"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an amount paid before it is earned"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109633105">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a card (usually plastic) that assures a seller that the person using it has a satisfactory credit rating and that the issuer will see to it that the seller receives payment for the merchandise delivered</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109633406">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a document ordering the payment of money; drawn by one person or bank on another</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109633792"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a draft in excess of the credit balance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109633887">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bill of exchange that is drawn in one country and made payable in another</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109634037">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bill of exchange that is both drawn and made payable in the same country</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109634169">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a draft for the amount of a dishonored draft plus the costs and charges of drafting again</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109634312">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bill of exchange for a specific purchase; drawn on the buyer by the seller and bearing the buyer''s acceptance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109634486">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the system by which one currency is exchanged for another; enables international transactions to take place</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109634656"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money available for a client to borrow"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109634855"
	 b:glossaryEntry="credit available at low rates of interest"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109634954">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a direct reduction in tax liability (not dependent on the taxpayer''s tax bracket)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109635092">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a credit opened by an importer with a bank in an exporter''s country to finance an export operation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109635250">
	<b:glossaryEntry>credit opened by an importer at a bank in his own country upon which an exporter may draw</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109635399"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the maximum credit that a customer is allowed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109635606">
	<b:glossaryEntry>credit granted by a bank to a business concern for commercial purposes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109635740">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a document issued by a bank that guarantees the payment of a customer''s draft; substitutes the bank''s credit for the customer''s credit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109635991">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a letter of credit given to a business concern for commercial purposes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109636135"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a letter of credit given to a traveler"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109636296">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a letter of credit issued by a bank or express company that is payable on presentation to any correspondent of the issuer</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109636517">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a draft drawn by a bank against funds deposited in another bank</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109636654">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an order of payment (such as a check payable to a shareholder) in which a dividend is paid</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109636807">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a written order for the payment of a sum to a named individual; obtainable and payable at a post office</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109636983">
	<b:glossaryEntry>credit provided by a bank in honoring a customer''s overdrafts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109637132"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money deposited in a bank"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109637244">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bank deposit from which withdrawals can be made without notice</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109637369">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a certificate of deposit from which withdrawals can be made only after advance notice or at a specified future date</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109637543"
	 b:glossaryEntry="banking: a time draft drawn on and accepted by a bank"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109637675">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a written order directing a bank to pay money; "he paid all his bills by check"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109637972">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a check that is dishonored on presentation because of insufficient funds; "issuing a bad check is a form of larceny"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109638157">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) a check given by the British government to someone who is unemployed; it can be cashed either at a bank or at the post office</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109638357"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a check issued in payment of wages or salary"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109638472">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a check containing certification that the person who issued the check has sufficient funds on deposit to cover payment</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109638671">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a check drawn against funds deposited in your personal checking account</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109638821">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a check issued by the officer of a bank on the banks own account (not that of a private person); "cashier''s checks are as good as cash"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109639078">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a check that has been signed but with the amount payable left blank</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109639237">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a monthly payment made to someone who has become disabled and is unable to work</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109639400">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a check reimbursing an aged person for the expenses of health care</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109639546"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a monthly payment to someone who is retired from work"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109639711">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the most common medium of exchange; functions as legal tender; "we tried to collect the money he owed us"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109639977">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the official currency issued by a government or national bank; "he changed his money into francs"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109640144">
	<b:glossaryEntry>British money; especially the pound sterling as the basic monetary unit of the UK</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109640280"
	 b:glossaryEntry="informal terms for money"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109640438"
	 b:glossaryEntry="paper money of little value issued on insufficient security"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109640554">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coins of regular issue whose face value is greater than their intrinsic value</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109640689"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the metal or paper medium of exchange that is presently used"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109640876">
	<b:glossaryEntry>paper currency in denominations less than the basic monetary unit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109641007">
	<b:glossaryEntry>small beads made from polished shells and formerly used as money by native Americans</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109641169"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used as currency in Massachusetts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109641259"
	 b:glossaryEntry="prompt payment for goods or services in currency or by check"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109641408"
	 b:glossaryEntry="money in the form of bills or coins"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109641566">
	<b:glossaryEntry>currency issued by a government or central bank and consisting of printed paper that can circulate as a substitute for specie</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109641836">
	<b:glossaryEntry>money received in return for its equivalent in a larger denomination or a different currency; "he got change for a twenty and used it to pay the taxi driver"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109642046">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the balance of money received when the amount you tender is greater than the amount due; "I paid with a twenty and pocketed the change"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109642234"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coins collectively"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109642357"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a trifling sum of money"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109642453">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coins of small denomination regarded collectively; "he had a pocketful of change"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109642587"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metal piece (usually a disc) used as money"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109643278"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an old Scottish coin of little value"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109643367">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a gold coin of the Byzantine Empire; widely circulated in Europe in the Middle Ages</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109643522"
	 b:glossaryEntry="formerly a gold coin of various European countries"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109643624"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an old small silver Spanish coin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109643707"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an old silver Spanish coin; worth 8 reales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109643810"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an English coin worth one twentieth of a pound"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109643911"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an English coin worth 5 shillings"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109643996"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an English coin worth half a crown"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109644087"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a US coin worth one tenth of a dollar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109644175"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a US coin worth one twentieth of a dollar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109644269">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a US coin worth one fourth of a dollar; "he fed four quarters into the slot machine"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109644407"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a US coin worth half of a dollar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109644516"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an English coin worth half a penny"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109644617"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a coin worth one-hundredth of the value of the basic unit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109644779"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(Brit) UK decimal coin worth ten pennies"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109644874">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a former UK silver coin; UK bronze decimal coin worth two pennies</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109645005"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(Brit) former cupronickel coin of UK equal to three pennies"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109645121"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a former English silver coin worth four pennies"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109645232"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a five-cent piece"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109645305">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a small British coin worth six pennies; not minted since 1970</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109645430"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a coin worth eight pennies"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109645513"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a coin worth nine pennies"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109645594"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a copper penny"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109645661">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a coin used in Great Britain since 1971 worth one hundredth of a pound</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109645787">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a US coin worth one dollar; "the dollar coin has never been popular in the United States"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109645965"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a US coin worth one dollar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109646060"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a dollar made of silver"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109646155"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a former gold coin in US worth 10 dollars"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109646248"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a former gold coin in US worth 5 dollars"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109646345"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a former British gold coin worth 21 chillings"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109646443"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a former British bronze coin worth a quarter of a penny"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109646553"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a former Spanish gold coin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109646634"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a former French gold coin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109646716">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a former French coin of low denomination; often used of any small amount of money; "he hasn''t a sou to his name"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109646878">
	<b:glossaryEntry>money that the government declares to be legal tender although it cannot be converted into standard specie</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109647041">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a piece of paper money (especially one issued by a central bank); "he peeled off five one-thousand-zloty notes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109647422">
	<b:glossaryEntry>formerly a bank note issued by the US Treasury and redeemable in silver</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109647558">
	<b:glossaryEntry>negotiable debt obligations of the US government secured by its full faith and credit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109647777">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a short-term noninterest-bearing obligation that can be traded on a discount basis for 91 days</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109647940"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a debt instrument with maturities of 10 years or longer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109648055">
	<b:glossaryEntry>securities with maturities of 1 to 10 years; sold for cash or in exchange for maturing issues or at auction</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109648222"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a US bill worth 100 dollars"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109648324"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a US bill worth 5 dollars"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109648432"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a US bill worth 2 dollars"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109648519"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a US bill worth 20 dollars"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109648610"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(United States) a piece of paper money worth one dollar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109648764"
	 b:glossaryEntry="possession that is owed to someone else"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109649064">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an excess of liabilities over assets (usually over a certain period)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109649222"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an excess of expenditures over revenues"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109649340">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[an excess of the federal government''s spending over its revenue; "federal deficits can cause inflation"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109649505"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an excess of imports over exports"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109649598">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a payment that is due (e.g., as the price of membership); "the society dropped him for non-payment of dues"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109649755">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the liability of a firm''s owners for no more than the capital they have invested in the firm</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109649911"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the amount that is owed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109650129">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the debt of the national government (as distinguished from the debts of individuals and businesses and political subdivisions)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109650315">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the total of the nation''s debts: debts of local and state and national governments; an indicator of how much public spending is financed by borrowing instead of taxation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109650542"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the maximum borrowing power of a governmental entity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109650684">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a limit set by Congress beyond which the national debt cannot rise; periodically raised by Congress</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109650851"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a written promise to repay a debt"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109651066">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a promise to pay a specified amount on demand or at a certain time; "I had to co-sign his note at the bank"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109651365"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a debt that is unlikely to be repaid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109651456"
	 b:glossaryEntry="debt to be paid by installments"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109651550"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the provision of money temporarily (usually at interest)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109651802"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a loan that is repayable on demand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109651906">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a loan by a lender to a customer without the use of a third party; direct lending gives the lender greater discretion in making loans</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109652097">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a loan that is shared by a group of banks that join to make a loan too big for any one of them alone</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109652309">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a loan granted for personal use; usually unsecured and based on the borrower''s integrity and ability to pay</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109652492"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a loan that is payable on or before a specified date"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109652600"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a note payable on demand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109652682">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the original amount of a debt on which interest is calculated</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109652818">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a financial liability (such as a tax); "the charges against the estate"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109652996">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the right to take another''s property if an obligation is not discharged</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109653118"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a charge against property (as a lien or mortgage)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109653239">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[an amount determined as payable; "the assessment for repairs outraged the club''s membership"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109653388"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a written account of ownership or obligation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109653559">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a document or receipt certifying release from an obligation or debt</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109653682">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a document that can serve as legal evidence of a transaction; "they could find no record of the purchase"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109653966">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a record of the financial situation of an institution on a particular date by listing its assets and the claims against those assets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109654158"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a written record of money spent"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109654250">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a record in which commercial accounts are recorded; "they got a subpoena to examine our books"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109654518">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the ledger that contains all of the financial accounts of a business; contains offsetting debit and credit accounts (including control accounts)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109654742">
	<b:glossaryEntry>details of an account supporting the amount stated in the general ledger</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109654878">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an account that shows totals of amounts entered in a subsidiary ledger</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109655029">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a ledger in which transactions have been recorded as they occurred</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109655159"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a written record of a commercial transaction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109655306"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an accounting entry acknowledging sums that are owing"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109655443"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an accounting entry acknowledging income or capital items"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109655604">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bookkeeper''s chronological list of related debits and credits of a business; forms part of a ledger of accounts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109655998">
	<b:glossaryEntry>account of payments received; usually the right side of a financial statement</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109656133">
	<b:glossaryEntry>account of payments owed; usually the left side of a financial statement</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109656262">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a method of accounting in which each item is entered as it is earned or incurred regardless of when actual payments are received or made</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109656476">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a method of accounting in which each item is entered as payments are received or made</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109656636">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an accounting method used in the merging of companies; the balance sheets are added together item by item; this method is tax-free</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109656832">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a creditor''s accounts of money owed to him; normally arise from the sale of products or services</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109656994">
	<b:glossaryEntry>your right in a promissory note in which the maker promises to pay a certain amount of money at a certain time</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109657166">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a debtor''s accounts of money he owes; normally arise from the purchase of products or services</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109657323">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a note promising to pay a certain amount of money at a certain time</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109657449">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an account compiled at the end of an accounting period to show gross and net profit or loss</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109657628">
	<b:glossaryEntry>that part of the earnings of a corporation that is distributed to its shareholders; usually paid quarterly</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109657861"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a dividend paid in stock rather than in cash"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109657966"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a dividend paid in addition to the regular dividend"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109658078">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a dividend paid to compensate shareholders for losses resulting from a change in the dividend schedule</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109658246">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) short for dividend; especially one paid by a cooperative society</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109658372">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an account used temporarily to carry doubtful receipts and disbursements or discrepancies pending their analysis and permanent classification</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109658576"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an amount on the credit side of an account"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109658763">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[the difference in value over a period of time of a country''s imports and exports of merchandise; "a nation''s balance of trade is favorable when its exports exceed its imports"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109659047">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the difference in value over a period of time of a country''s imports and exports of services and payments of property incomes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109659236">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a system of recording all of a country''s economic transactions with the rest of the world over a period of one year; "a favorable balance of payments exists when more payments are coming in than going out"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109659581">
	<b:glossaryEntry>that part of the balance of payments recording a nation''s exports and imports of goods and services and transfer payments</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109659783">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a balance of debits and credits in double-entry bookkeeping; drawn up to test their equality</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109659935">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) the accumulated and undivided profits of a corporation after provision has been made for dividends and reserves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109660129">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an inspection of the accounting procedures and records by a trained accountant</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109660314">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an audit of limited scope (limited in time span or confined to particular accounts etc.)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109660462">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(accounting) an accounting service (less exhaustive than an audit) that provides some assurance to interested parties as to the reliability of financial data</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109660707">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an auditing procedure based on ratios among accounts and tries to identify significant changes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109660865">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an account to which salespersons or executives can charge travel and entertainment expenses</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109661053">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a slip of paper included with your pay that records how much money you have earned and how much tax or insurance etc. has been taken out</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109661243"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a book in which names and transactions are listed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109661401">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(accounting) the value of a firm''s current assets including raw materials and work in progress and finished goods</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109661570">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a list of employees and their salaries; "the company had a long payroll"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109661726">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the total amount of money paid in wages; "the company had a large payroll"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109661865">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a sum of money offered as a prize; "the purse barely covered the winner''s expenses"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109662000">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a sum of money spoken of as the contents of a money purse; "he made the contribution out of his own purse"; "he and his wife shared a common purse"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109662199">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the amount (of money or goods or services) that is considered to be a fair equivalent for something else; "he tried to estimate the value of the produce at normal prices"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109662438">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a record of deposits and withdrawals and interest held by depositors at certain banks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109662589"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a book issued to holders of checking accounts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109662722"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a pawnbroker''s receipt for articles taken as security"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109662833">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a written agreement (or property or money) delivered to a third party or put in trust by one party to a contract to be returned after fulfillment of some condition</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109663049"
	 b:glossaryEntry="funds held in escrow"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109663128">
	<b:glossaryEntry>short-term obligations issued by institutional borrowers to investors with temporarily idle cash</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109663287">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a municipal debt instrument with a maturity of less than 2 years</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109663412"
	 b:glossaryEntry='an informal debt instrument; representing "I owe you"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109663515"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a note that specifies the time (or times) of repayment"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109663625">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a debt instrument with a variable interest rate tied to some other interest rate (e.g. the rate paid by T-bills)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109663791"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a hotel plan that includes meals"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109663901">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a hotel plan that includes breakfast and dinner (but not lunch)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109664033"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a hotel plan that excludes meals"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109664125"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a will disposing of real property"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109664211">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a formal declaration that documents a fact of relevance to finance and investment; the holder has a right to receive interest or dividends; "he held several valuable securities"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109664691">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a certificate whose value is recognized by the payer and payee; scrip is not currency but may be convertible into currency</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109664865">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a certificate documenting the shareholder''s ownership in the corporation; "the value of his stocks doubled during the past year"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109665083">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an interest-bearing (or discounted) certificate of debt issued by a government or corporation in order to raise money</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109665540">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond containing a provision that the holder cannot redeem the security before a specific date (usually at maturity)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109665720">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a security that makes no interest payments but instead is sold at a deep discount from its face value</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109665906"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the commonest form of zero-coupon security"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109666011">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a certificate showing the interests of all parties in a business partnership</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109666157">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a certificate showing who is responsible in an individually owned business</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109666304">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a corporate security (usually bonds or preferred stock) that can be exchanged for another form of security (usually common stock)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109666514">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a stock or bond that is not registered with the Securities Exchange Commission and cannot be sold in the public market</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109666730">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a letter of intent saying that a letter security is being bought for investment and not for resale; avoids need for SEC registration</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109666926">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stock that has been bought back by the issuing corporation and is available for retirement or resale; it is issued but not outstanding; it cannot vote and pays no dividends</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109667196">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shares in a corporation that entitle the shareholder to voting and proxy rights</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109667334">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stock representing ownership of overvalued assets; stock of a corporation whose total worth is less than its invested capital</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109667519">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stock that has not been registered with the Securities and Exchange Commission and cannot be sold to the general public</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109667697">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a bond that has not been registered with the Securities and Exchange Commission and cannot be sold to the general public</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109667875">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a security that has been accepted for trading by one of the organized and registered securities exchanges in the US</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109668070"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a security traded in the over-the-counter market"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109668261"
	 b:glossaryEntry="stock that is not listed and traded on an organized exchange"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109668437">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a summary of intended expenditures along with proposals for how to meet them; "the president submitted the annual budget to Congress"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109668641">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a budget is balanced when current expenditures are equal to receipts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109668771">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a sum of money allocated for a particular purpose; "the laboratory runs on a budget of a million a year"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109668946">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a budget for current expenses as distinct from financial transactions or permanent improvements</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109669123">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a small fund of cash that a firm keeps for the payment of incidental expenses</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109669257"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cash for day-to-day spending on incidental expenses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109669396"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a draft payable on presentation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109669485"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a draft payable at a specified future date"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109669584">
	<b:glossaryEntry>funds that will be supplied in an amount matching the funds available from other sources</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109669733">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the last line in an audit; the line that shows profit or loss</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109669871">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a sustained phenomenon or one marked by gradual changes; "events now in process"; "the process of calcification begins later for boys than for girls"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109670326">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process existing in or produced by nature (rather than by the intent of human beings); "the action of natural forces"; "volcanic activity"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109671663"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of becoming acid or being converted into an acid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109671783">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of becoming softer; "refrigeration delayed the softening of the fruit" or "he observed the softening of iron by heat"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109671986">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of becoming hard or solid by cooling or drying or crystallization; "the hardening of concrete"; "he tested the set of the glue"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109672218"
	 b:glossaryEntry="softening due to soaking or steeping"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109672311"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of exposing to air (so as to purify)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109672414"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of bubbling as gas escapes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109672512">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any process in which an atomic or nuclear system acquires an additional particle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109672646">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process whereby a star or planet holds an object in its gravitational field</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109672777">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of obtaining something from a mixture or compound by chemical or physical or mechanical means</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109672975"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of filtering"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109673056">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of fossilizing a plant or animal that existed in some earlier age; the process of being turned to stone</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109673249"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of oscillating between states"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109673348">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of turning some plant material into stone by infiltration with water carrying mineral particles without changing the original shape</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109673566">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any process of combination (in solution) that depend on relatively weak chemical bonding</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109673748"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of combining with water; usually reversible"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109673859"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of forming a chemical precipitate"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109673964">
	<b:glossaryEntry>crushing and separating ore into valuable substances or waste by any of a variety of techniques</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109674191">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of separating substances by the use of a centrifuge</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109674333"
	 b:glossaryEntry="centrifugation at very high speeds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109674433">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the motion of charged particles in a colloid under the influence of an electric field; particles with a positive charge go to the cathode and negative to the anode</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109674709"
	 b:glossaryEntry="electrophoresis carried out on filter paper"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109674820"
	 b:glossaryEntry="electrophoresis to separate antigens and antibodies"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109674939">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of producing images of objects on photosensitive surfaces</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109675174">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of producing pictures in contrasting colors that appear three-dimensional when superimposed and viewed through spectacles with one red and one green lens</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109675395"
	 b:glossaryEntry="process for producing permanent prints in a carbon pigment"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109675519">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process for identifying minerals or crystals; a small rod is coated with a powdered form of the substance and subjected to suitably modified X-rays; the pattern of diffracted rings is used for identification</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109675828">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of making a radiograph; producing an image on a radiosensitive surface by radiation other than visible light</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109676037">
	<b:glossaryEntry>producing a radiograph by means of the radiation emitted from the specimen being photographed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109676192">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of translating photographs into a digital form that can be recognized by a computer</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109676342">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of capturing and converting and storing video images for use by a computer</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109676509">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of representing intermediate colors by patterns of tiny colored dots that simulate the desired color</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109676677">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process used for separating mixtures by virtue of differences in absorbency</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109676851">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chromatography that uses selective adsorption by a column of powders</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109676987">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chromatography that uses selective adsorption on a strip of paper</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109677119">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process in which ions are exchanged between a solution and an insoluble (usually resinous) solid; widely used in industrial processing</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109677314">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of ionizing; the formation of ions by separating atoms or molecules or radicals or by adding or subtracting electrons from atoms by strong electric fields in a gas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109677546">
	<b:glossaryEntry>washing of loaded ion-exchange resins to remove captured ions; used to obtain uranium ions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109677690">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process that makes a substance magnetic (temporarily or permanently)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109677859"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of removing magnetization"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109677976">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of making a (steel) ship''s hull nonmagnetic by producing an opposing magnetic field</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109678128">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process whereby changes in the current flow in a circuit produce magnetism or an EMF</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109678308">
	<b:glossaryEntry>induction in a circuit caused by changes in the circuit itself</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109678431">
	<b:glossaryEntry>induction in a second circuit caused by changes in a first circuit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109678560">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of changing from a gaseous to a liquid or solid state</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109678684">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process that uses heat to separate a substance into its components</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109678869"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of boiling a liquid and condensing its vapors"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109678999"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the destructive distillation of coal (as in coke ovens)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109679130"
	 b:glossaryEntry="fractionation of a liquid by distillation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109679241">
	<b:glossaryEntry>heating a solid substance in a closed container and collecting the volatile products</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109679414">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the gradual departure from an intended course due to external influences (as a ship or plane)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109679559">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process that liberates or discharges something; "there was a sudden release of oxygen"; "the release of iodine from the thyroid gland"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109679767"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the release of electrons from parent atoms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109679954"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the emission of electrons from very hot substances"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109680089">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the emission of electrons from a surface that is bombarded by higher energy primary electrons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109680247">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the release or absorption of quanta above a certain energy level</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109680380">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the emission of electrons that are stripped from parent atoms by a high electric field</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109680527">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the transformation of a gamma-ray photon into an electron and a positron when the photon passes close to an atomic nucleus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109680744">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any process determined by the atomic and molecular composition and structure of the substances involved</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109681504"
	 b:glossaryEntry="conversion of starch to sugar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109681590">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the coalescing of small particles that are suspended in solution; these larger masses are then (usually) precipitated</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109681792">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of forming a ring by forming one or more hydrogen bonds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109681915">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(med) the process of removing a heavy metal from the bloodstream by means of a chelate as in treating lead or mercury poisoning</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109682098">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process whereby heavy molecules of naphtha or petroleum are broken down into hydrocarbons of lower molecular weight (especially in the oil-refining process)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109682313">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the atomic process that occurs during a chemical reaction; "he determined unique mechanisms for the photochemical reactions"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109682532">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a chemical mechanism involved in vital processes occurring in living organisms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109682678">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(chemistry) a process in which one or more substances are changed into others; "there was a chemical reaction of the lime with the ground water"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109683095"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a chemical reaction produced by the action of light"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109683215"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a chemical reaction and its reverse proceed at equal rates"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109683372">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physiology) the normal equilibrium between acids and alkalis in the body; "with a normal acid-base balance in the body the blood is slightly alkaline"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109683611"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a process that makes something blue (or bluish)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109683721">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process that impregnates something with calcium (or calcium salts)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109683849">
	<b:glossaryEntry>acceleration of a chemical reaction induced the presence of material that is chemically unchanged at the end of the reaction</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109684047">
	<b:glossaryEntry>catalysis in which the catalyst is one of the products of the reaction</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109684177">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a series of chemical reactions in which the product of one is a reactant in the next</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109684322">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the addition or substitution of chlorine in organic compounds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109684460"
	 b:glossaryEntry="chlorination prior to another chemical process"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109684568">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of oxidizing; the addition of oxygen to a compound with a loss of electrons; always occurs accompanied by reduction</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109684851">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of providing or combining or treating with oxygen; "the oxygenation of the blood"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109685002">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the conversion of metals into their oxides as a result of heating to a high temperature</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109685147">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any process in which electrons are added to an atom or ion (as by removing oxygen or adding hydrogen); always occurs accompanied by oxidation of the reducing agent</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109685377">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a reversible chemical reaction in which one reaction is an oxidation and the reverse is a reduction</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109685550"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a chemical reaction accompanied by the absorption of heat"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109685674"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a nuclear reaction occurring with absorption of energy"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109685793"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a chemical reaction accompanied by the evolution of heat"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109685915"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a nuclear reaction accompanied by the evolution of energy"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109686036">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the formation of reddish-brown ferric oxides on iron by low-temperature oxidation in the presence of water</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109686240">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the oxidation of ammonium compounds into nitrates by soil bacteria</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109686366">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process in which a substance reacts with oxygen to give heat and light</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109686541">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[the process of combustion of inflammable materials producing heat and light and (often) smoke; "fire was one of our ancestors'' first discoveries"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109686791">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a strong flame that burns brightly; "the blaze spread rapidly"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109686915"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a sudden burst of flame"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109686990">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the combustion of fuel inside a cylinder (as in an internal-combustion engine)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109687134">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the formation of small pits in a surface as a consequence of corrosion</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109687284">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process of combining several molecules to form a polymer or polymeric compound</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109687442">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process for extracting gold and silver by treating ore with a sodium cyanide solution</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109687591">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a chemical reaction between two compounds in which parts of each are interchanged to form two new compounds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109687778">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a chemical process in which solvent molecules and molecules or ions of the solute combine to form a compound</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109687942">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of changing into gas; "coal gas is produced by the gasification of coal"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109688085"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of interacting with gas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109688174">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the swelling of certain substances when they are heated (often accompanied by release of water)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109688354">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the substitution or addition of iodine atoms in organic compounds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109688476"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the removal of iodine atoms from organic compounds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109688586"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the removal of salt (especially from sea water)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109688726">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of converting to a sol; bringing to a colloidal solution</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109688864">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the hydrolysis of proteins into peptides and amino acids by cleavage of their peptide bonds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109689013">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of decomposing organic matter (as in sewage) by bacteria</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109689137">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process for producing sodium carbonate from sodium chloride and ammonia and carbon dioxide</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109689290">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the separation of liquid from a gel that is caused by contraction (as in cheese making)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109689446">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of producing a chemical compound (usually by the union of simpler chemical compounds)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109689635"
	 b:glossaryEntry="production of a chemical compound by a living organism"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109689761">
	<b:glossaryEntry>synthesis of carbohydrate from carbon dioxide and water; limited to certain bacteria and fungi</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109689916">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of transfering an amino group from one compound to another</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109690047">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process in which an agent causes an organic substance to break down into simpler substances; especially, the anaerobic breakdown of sugar into alcohol</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109690303"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a natural process whereby geological features are modified"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109690534"
	 b:glossaryEntry="forming or depositing in layers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109690644"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the arrangement of leaflike layers in a rock"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109690744">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of deformation that produces continents and ocean basins in the earth''s crust</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109690892">
	<b:glossaryEntry>gradual formation of new land, by recession of the sea or deposit of sediment</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109691024">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the mechanical process of wearing or grinding something down (as by particles washing over it)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109691329"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the erosive process that reduces the size of glaciers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109691437"
	 b:glossaryEntry="erosion by friction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109691547"
	 b:glossaryEntry="erosion by chemical action"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109691687"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the erosion of beaches"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109691769"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of erosion whereby a level surface is produced"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109691883"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the washing away of soil"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109691966"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of covering with glaciers or masses of ice"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109692077">
	<b:glossaryEntry>change in the structure of rock by natural agencies such as pressure or heat of introduction of new chemical substances</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109692309">
	<b:glossaryEntry>occurs deep under the earth''s surface; changes simple minerals into complex minerals</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109692469">
	<b:glossaryEntry>occurs at or near the earth''s surface; breaks down complex minerals into simpler ones</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109692631">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a geological change in the mineral content of rock after the rock has formed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109692764"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a process occurring in living organisms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109693732">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the organic process of bearing flowers; "you will stop all bloom if you let the flowers go to seed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109693894">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process in reproduction and growth by which a cell divides to form daughter cells</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109694131">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cell division that produces reproductive cells in sexually reproducing organisms; the nucleus divides into four nuclei each containing half the chromosome number (leading to gametes in animals and spores in plants)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109694496"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a stage in meiosis or mitosis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109694720"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the first stage of meiosis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109694934"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the first stage of the prophase of meiosis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109695070">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the side by side pairing of homologous maternal and paternal chromosomes at the start of meiosis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109695240"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the second stage of the prophase of meiosis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109695357"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the third stage of the prophase of meiosis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109695474"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the fourth stage of the prophase of meiosis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109695611">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the interchange of sections between pairing homologous chromosomes during the prophase of meiosis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109695799">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of showing signs of life; "the quickening of seed that will become ripe grain"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109695946"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the final stage of the prophase of meiosis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109696064">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the contraction of chromatin towards one side of the nucleus during the prophase of meiosis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109696242"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the second stage of meiosis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109696344">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the stage of meiosis or mitosis when chromosomes move toward opposite ends of the nuclear spindle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109696515">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the final stage of meiosis when the chromosomes move toward opposite ends of the nuclear spindle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109696686">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cell division in which the nucleus divides into nuclei containing the same number of chromosomes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109696893">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the direct method of cell division characterized by simple division of the nucleus without formation of chromosomes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109697063"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the first stage of mitosis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109697163"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the second stage of mitosis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109697265"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the final stage of mitosis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109697366"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the suppression of a gene by the effect of an unrelated gene"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109697495">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the marked and rapid transformation of a larva into an adult that occurs in some animals</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109697656"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(biology) growth anew of lost or destroyed parts or organs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109697791">
	<b:glossaryEntry>regeneration on a reduced scale of a body part; observed especially in invertebrates such as certain lobsters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109697959">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(embryology) the organic process whereby a fertilized egg becomes implanted in the lining of the uterus of placental mammals</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109698142">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the expulsion of an ovum from the ovary (usually midway in the menstrual cycle)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109698295">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the absence of ovulation due to immaturity or post-maturity or pregnancy or oral contraceptive pills or dysfunction of the ovary</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109698499"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of generating offspring"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109698648">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process in which a gastrula develops from a blastula by the inward migration of cells</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109698815"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the production of eggs (especially in birds)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109698925">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the biological process whereby genetic factors are transmitted from one generation to the next</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109699074">
	<b:glossaryEntry>reproduction involving the union or fusion of a male and a female gamete</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109699280">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) reproduction by the union or fusion of two differing gametes (especially differing in size)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109699437">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) reproduction by the union or fusion of games of the same size and structure</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109699576">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the parturition process in human beings; having a baby; the process of giving birth to a child</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109699846">
	<b:glossaryEntry>childbirth during which the mother is encouraged to be an active participant</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109699981">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a method of childbirth that avoids intrusive high-tech medicine in favor of more natural and homely settings</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109700176">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a method that prepares a mother for natural childbirth by providing education and exercises and nutrition and techniques of breathing and relaxation (with the assistance of the father)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109700452">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a method that prepares a mother for natural childbirth; the pregnant woman (in classes and at home) practices (usually with the help of a coach) learns about the physiology of childbirth and techniques of relaxation and concentration and breathing</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109700808">
	<b:glossaryEntry>labor and childbirth without medical intervention; no drugs are given to relieve pain or aid the birth process; "natural childbirth is considered the safest for the baby"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109701097">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a method of childbirth that assumes it is a normal process and that the pain is largely psychological; involves education and breathing exercises to foster relaxation and other exercises</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109701369"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of giving birth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109701633">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sitting on eggs so as to hatch them by the warmth of the body</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109701762"
	 b:glossaryEntry="giving birth to a calf"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109701838"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the production of young from an egg"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109701936"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the production of a litter of pigs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109702035"
	 b:glossaryEntry="reproduction without the fusion of gametes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109702267"
	 b:glossaryEntry="human conception without fertilization by a man"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109702442">
	<b:glossaryEntry>male parthenogenesis in which the embryo contains only paternal chromosomes due to the failure of the egg nucleus to participate in fertilization</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109702658">
	<b:glossaryEntry>female parthenogenesis in which the embryo contains only maternal chromosomes due to the failure of the sperm to fuse with the egg nucleus</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109702854"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any of several kinds of reproduction without fertilization"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109703003">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) development of an embryo without fertilization; (especially in some ferns) development of a sporophyte from the gametophyte without fertilization</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109703211">
	<b:glossaryEntry>process in which an unfertilized egg develops into a new individual; common among insects and some other arthropods</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109703403">
	<b:glossaryEntry>reproduction of some unicellular organisms by division of the cell into two more or less equal parts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109703575">
	<b:glossaryEntry>asexual reproduction by multiple fission; characteristic of many sporozoan protozoans</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109703717"
	 b:glossaryEntry="asexual reproduction by budding"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109703808">
	<b:glossaryEntry>reproduction of some unicellular organisms by growth and specialization followed by the separation by constriction of a part of the parent</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109704000"
	 b:glossaryEntry="asexual reproduction by the production and release of spores"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109704168"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the development of both microspores and megaspores"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109704276"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the development of a single kind of asexual spores"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109704382">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the sequence of events involved in the evolutionary development of a species or taxonomic group of organisms</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109704645">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the evolution of one type of organism from another by a long series of gradual changes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109704790"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the evolution or genesis of the human race"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109704909">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the appearance of entirely new properties at certain critical stages in the course of evolution</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109705069"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the evolution of a biological species"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109705163">
	<b:glossaryEntry>synthesis of compounds with the aid of radiant energy (especially in plants)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109705300">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the organic circulation of carbon from the atmosphere into organisms and back again</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109705442">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the circulation of nitrogen; nitrates from the soil are absorbed by plants which are eaten by animals that die and decay returning the nitrogen back to the soil</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109705663">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the assimilation of atmospheric nitrogen by soil bacteria and its release for plant use on the death of the bacteria</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109705843">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the organic processes (in a cell or organism) that are necessary for life</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109706117"
	 b:glossaryEntry="synthesis of more complex substances from simpler ones"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109706290">
	<b:glossaryEntry>breakdown of more complex substances into simpler ones with release of energy</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109706515">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an enzymatic process in cell metabolism that synthesizes ATP from ADP</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109706675">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in all plants and animals: a series of enzymatic reactions in mitochondria involving oxidative metabolism of acetyl compounds to produce high-energy phosphate compounds that are the source of cellular energy</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109707025">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the amount of energy required to maintain the body of an individual in a resting state</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109707174">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the rate at which heat is produced by an individual in a resting state</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109707335">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the organic process by which an organism assimilates food and uses it for growth and maintenance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109707487"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the spreading of a disease to another part of the body"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109707598"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the origination and development of a disease"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109707719"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the confinement of an infection to a limited area"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109707842"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a growing together of parts or structures"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109707939">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a clumping of bacteria or red cells when held together by antibodies (agglutinins)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109708081"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming to full development; becoming mature"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109708216">
	<b:glossaryEntry>acquiring desirable qualities by being left undisturbed for some time</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109708369"
	 b:glossaryEntry="showing the effects of increasing age"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109708485"
	 b:glossaryEntry="normal aging of cells"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109708563">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of an individual organism growing organically; a purely biological unfolding of events involved in an organism changing gradually from a simple to a more complex level; "he proposed an indicator of osseous development in children"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109709245"
	 b:glossaryEntry="failure of normal development to occur"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109709380"
	 b:glossaryEntry="imperfect development; nondevelopment of a part"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109709493">
	<b:glossaryEntry>introduction during embryonic development of characters or structure not present in the earlier evolutionary history of the strain or species (as addition of the placenta in mammalian evolution)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109709822">
	<b:glossaryEntry>emergence during embryonic development of various characters or structures that appeared during the evolutionary history of the strain or species</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109710061"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the origin and development of cells"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109710154"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the developmental process of forming tooth enamel"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109710262"
	 b:glossaryEntry="inhibition of the growth of bacteria"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109710359"
	 b:glossaryEntry="growth from increase in cell size without cell division"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109710468"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of forming leaves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109710563"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process whereby seeds or spores sprout and begin to grow"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109710693"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of growing into a youth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109710787">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the course of developmental changes in an organism from fertilized zygote to maturity when another zygote can be produced</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109710965">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the abnormal development of male sexual characteristics in a female (usually as the result of hormone therapies or adrenal malfunction)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109711177"
	 b:glossaryEntry="development of ova"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109711251"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of bone formation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109711339"
	 b:glossaryEntry="growth by the rapid multiplication of parts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109711442"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of putting forth roots and beginning to grow"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109711552"
	 b:glossaryEntry="development of spermatozoa"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109711640"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the loss of foliage"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109711717"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of budding and unfolding of blossoms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109711877"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the bearing of fruit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109711958"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an exchange of chromosome parts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109712049"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the transport of dissolved material within a plant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109712159"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the emission of water vapor from the leaves of plants"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109712272">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the organic process by which food is converted into substances that can be absorbed into the body</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109712425">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of absorbing nutrients into the body after digestion</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109712579"
	 b:glossaryEntry="abnormal absorption of nutrients from the digestive tract"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109712696"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of organ formation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109712787">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the organic process of synthesizing and releasing some substance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109712943"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an organic process occurring as a consequence of disease"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109713159">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the pathological process that results in the formation and growth of a tumor</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109713291"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the development and spread of an infectious disease"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109713396">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an organic process that takes place in the body; "respiratory activity"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109713968"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process whereby something is shed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109714108">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shedding of flowers and leaves and fruit following formation of scar tissue in a plant</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109714251">
	<b:glossaryEntry>periodic shedding of the cuticle in arthropods or the outer skin in reptiles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109714417"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(embryology) the repeated division of a fertilised ovum"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109714561">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the breaking of a chemical bond in a molecule resulting in smaller molecules</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109714692"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the accumulation of blood in an organ"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109714786"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the production and secretion of milk by the mammary glands"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109714900"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the cessation of lactation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109714984"
	 b:glossaryEntry="shedding tears"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109715092">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the monthly discharge of blood from the uterus of nonpregnant women from puberty to menopause; "the women were sickly and subject to excessive menstruation"; "a woman does not take the gout unless her menses be stopped"--Hippocrates; "the semen begins to appear in males and to be emitted at the same time of life that the catamenia begin to flow in females"--Aristotle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109715593">
	<b:glossaryEntry>abnormally heavy or prolonged menstruation; can be a symptom of uterine tumors and can lead to anemia if prolonged</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109715800"
	 b:glossaryEntry="abnormally light or infrequent menstruation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109715904">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of several bodily processes by which substances go out of the body; "the discharge of pus"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109716131"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the discharge of semen"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109716211"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the bodily process of discharging waste matter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109716416"
	 b:glossaryEntry="involuntary urination or defecation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109716543">
	<b:glossaryEntry>inability to control the flow of urine and involuntary urination</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109716674"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the elimination of fecal waste through the anus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109716825"
	 b:glossaryEntry='a euphemism for defecation; "he had a bowel movement"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109716955"
	 b:glossaryEntry='a coarse term for defecation; "he took a shit"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109717059"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the discharge of urine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109717241"
	 b:glossaryEntry="involuntary urination (usually during sleep)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109717354"
	 b:glossaryEntry='a euphemism for urination; "he had to take a leak"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109717496"
	 b:glossaryEntry="passing small stones with the urine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109717588"
	 b:glossaryEntry="production of an abnormally small amount of urine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109717692"
	 b:glossaryEntry='informal terms for urination; "he took a pee"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109717813"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the formation and discharge of pus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109717917"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of coughing up and spitting out"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109718020">
	<b:glossaryEntry>excessive activity; "overactivity of the sebaceous glands causes the skin to become oily"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109718168"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the secretion of milk"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109718246"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the secretion of saliva"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109718344"
	 b:glossaryEntry="excessive flow of saliva"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109718423"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the formation of the placenta in the uterus"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109718525">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of wave-like muscle contractions of the alimentary tract that moves food along</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109718707">
	<b:glossaryEntry>muscular action of the alimentary tract in a direction opposite to peristalsis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109718860"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the natural process by which the body repairs itself"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109718984"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gradual healing (through rest) after sickness or injury"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109719143"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a marked recovery of strength or spirits during an illness"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109719253"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a process in which human beings are involved"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109719393"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a process involved in human language"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109719656">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the building of words from component morphemes that retain their form and meaning in the process of combining</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109719825">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(descriptive linguistics) the process whereby new words are formed from existing words or bases by affixation: `singer'' from `sing''; `undo'' from `do''</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109720031"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a process of linguistic change over a period of time"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109720135">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a linguistic process of transposition of sounds or syllables within a word or words within a sentence</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109720293"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the contraction of two vowels into a diphthong"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109720408">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a linguistic process by which one of two similar sounds in a word becomes less like the other; "the Old French MARBRE became the English MARBLE by dissimilation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109720629">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a linguistic process by which a sound becomes similar to an adjacent sound</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109720762"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the merging of adjacent sounds or syllables or words"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109720867">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any process whereby sounds are left out of spoken words or phrases</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109721071">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(linguistics) omission at the beginning of a word as in `coon'' for `raccoon'' or `till'' for `until''</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109721238">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the gradual disappearance of an initial (usually unstressed) vowel or syllable as in `squire'' for `esquire''</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109721399"
	 b:glossaryEntry="omission or suppression of parts of words or sentences"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109721519">
	<b:glossaryEntry>omission of a sound between two words (usually a vowel and the end of one word or the beginning of the next)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109721681"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a process that is assumed to occur in psychoanalytic theory"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109721827">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an unconscious process that tries to reduce the anxiety associated with instinctive desires</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109722204">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a defense mechanism that conceals your undesirable shortcomings by exaggerating desirable behaviors</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109722362">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a defense mechanism represses emotional conflicts which are then converted into physical symptoms that have no organic basis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109722543"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a defense mechanism that denies painful thoughts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109722644">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a defense mechanism that splits something you are ambivalent about into two representations--one good and one bad</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109722831">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a defense mechanism by which your own traits and emotions are attributed to someone else</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109722976">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a defense mechanism by which your true motivation is concealed by explaining your actions and feelings in a way that is not threatening</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109723191">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a defense mechanism in which you flee from reality by assuming a more infantile state</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109723333">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the classical defense mechanism that protects you from impulses or ideas that would cause anxiety by preventing them from becoming conscious</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109723530">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process that alters the energy or structure or composition of atomic nuclei</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109723778">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a self-sustaining nuclear reaction; a series of nuclear fissions in which neutrons released by splitting one atom leads to the splitting of others</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109723985">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a nuclear reaction in which a massive nucleus splits into smaller nuclei with the simultaneous release of energy</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109724169">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a nuclear reaction in which nuclei combine to form more massive nuclei with the simultaneous release of energy</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109724393">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a nuclear fusion reaction taking place at very high temperatures (as in the sun)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109724560"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a thermonuclear reaction in the interior of stars"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109724668">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the spontaneous disintegration of a radioactive substance along with the emission of ionizing radiation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109724860">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process in which something passes by degrees to a more advanced or mature stage; "the development of his ideas took many years"; "the evolution of Greek civilization"; "the slow development of her skill as a writer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109725201"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a process of becoming deeper and more profound"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109725303">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a developmental process; "the flowering of ante-bellum culture"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109725434">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a progression from simpler to more complex forms; "the growth of culture"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109725560">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any process affecting the production and development and management of material wealth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109725817">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the ability and desire to purchase goods and services; "the automobile reduced the demand for buggywhips"; "the demand exceeded the supply"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109726045">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(economics) the utilization of economic goods to satisfy needs or in manufacturing; "the consumption of energy has increased steadily"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109726321">
	<b:glossaryEntry>buying expensive services and products in order to flaunt your wealth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109726460">
	<b:glossaryEntry>steady growth in the productive capacity of the economy (and so a growth of national income)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109726614"
	 b:glossaryEntry="offering goods and services for sale"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109726721"
	 b:glossaryEntry="making steel from pig iron"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109726846"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a process for making steel using a Bessemer converter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109726962"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a process for making steel using an open-hearth furnace"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109727083">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a general and progressive increase in prices; "in inflation everything gets more valuable except money"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109727384">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an episode of inflation in which prices increase at an increasing rate and currency rapidly loses value</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109727553">
	<b:glossaryEntry>inflation caused by an increase in demand or in the supply of money</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109727688"
	 b:glossaryEntry="inflation caused by an increase in the costs of production"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109727812">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a period of slow economic growth and high unemployment (stagnation) while prices rise (inflation)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109727967">
	<b:glossaryEntry>inflation of currency after a period of deflation; restore the system to a previous state</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109728112">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a reduction of prices intended to improve the balance of payments</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109728272">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a contraction of economic activity resulting in a decline of prices</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109728431"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of forming semisolid lumps in a liquid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109728579">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process in which liquid blood is changed into a semisolid mass (a blood clot)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109728739"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of wasting"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109728815"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a process of becoming smaller"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109729055">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process of becoming larger; "the increase in unemployment"; "the growth of population"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109729327"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an increase by natural growth or addition"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109729457">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of multiplying; "this multiplication of cells is a natural correlate of growth"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109729681"
	 b:glossaryEntry="rapid increase"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109729767"
	 b:glossaryEntry="increase by a factor of two"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109729849"
	 b:glossaryEntry="increase by a factor of three"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109729933"
	 b:glossaryEntry="increase by a factor of four"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109730019"
	 b:glossaryEntry="increase by a factor of five"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109730105"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the natural process of laying down a deposit of something"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109730255">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the deposition of a substance on an electrode by the action of electricity (especially by electrolysis)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109730422"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an increase in width"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109730528"
	 b:glossaryEntry="deposition from one deposit to another"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109730625"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of preventing redeposition"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109730726"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a gradual decrease; as of stored charge or current"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109730856"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a decrease that follows an exponential function"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109731006">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the exponential return of a system to equilibrium after a disturbance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109731154">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a decrease of transmitted power in a mechanical system caused by slipping</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109731282"
	 b:glossaryEntry="change toward something smaller or lower"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109731479"
	 b:glossaryEntry="process of changing to an inferior state"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109731612"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a gradual decline (in size or strength or power or number)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109731736"
	 b:glossaryEntry='decline in activity: "a serious loss of business"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109731836"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a temporary decline in health or value"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109731934">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a noticeable decline in performance: "the team went into a slump"; "a sudden slack in output"; "a drop-off in attendance"; "a falloff in automobile sales"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109732183">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of reducing sensitivity; "the patient was desensitized to the allergen"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109732346"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a decrease in width"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109732439"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of gradually becoming inferior"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109732623"
	 b:glossaryEntry="separation into component parts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109732765">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a normal ongoing process that dissolves fibrin and results in the removal of small blood clots; "drugs causing fibrinolysis have been utilized therapeutically"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109732983">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biochemistry) dissolution or destruction of cells such as blood cells or bacteria</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109733225">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lysis (self-digestion) of tissues when they are detached from the organism</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109733355"
	 b:glossaryEntry="dissolution or destruction of bacteria"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109733453">
	<b:glossaryEntry>breakdown of cells by the destruction of their outer membrane</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109733570">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lysis of a bond produced by the passage of an electric current</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109733691">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a metabolic process that breaks down carbohydrates and sugars through a series of reactions to either pyruvic acid or lactic acid and release energy for the body in the form of ATP</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109733928"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lysis of erythrocytes with the release of hemoglobin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109734078">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a chemical reaction in which water reacts with a compound to produce other compounds; involves the splitting of a bond and the addition of the hydrogen cation and the hydroxide anion from the water</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109734332">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a chemical reaction in which an ester is heated with an alkali (especially the alkaline hydrolysis of a fat or oil to make soap)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109734521">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lysis of bone caused by disease or infection or inadequate blood supply</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109734649"
	 b:glossaryEntry="decaying caused by bacterial or fungal action"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109734785">
	<b:glossaryEntry>soft decayed area in a tooth; progressive decay can lead to the death of a tooth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109734957"
	 b:glossaryEntry="decay of matter (as by rot or oxidation)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109735054"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of becoming dilapidated"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109735155"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of becoming spoiled"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109735288"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of becoming sour"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109735370"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of becoming mildewed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109735462">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process in which one substance takes up or holds another (by either absorption or adsorption)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109735650">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process in which one substance permeates another; a fluid permeates or is dissolved by a liquid or solid</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109735844"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(chemistry) the absorption of a liquid by a solid or gel"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109735957">
	<b:glossaryEntry>molecules of a gas form a thin film on the surface of a solid</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109736116">
	<b:glossaryEntry>adsorption (especially when irreversible) by means of chemical instead of physical forces; "chemisorption of gaseous nitrogen on iron catalysts"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109736336">
	<b:glossaryEntry>changing from an adsorbed state on a surface to a gaseous or liquid state</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109736466">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of flocculating; forming woolly cloudlike aggregations</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109736591">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process of intermingling molecules as a result of random thermal agitation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109736777">
	<b:glossaryEntry>diffusion of molecules through a semipermeable membrane from a place of higher concentration to a place of lower concentration until the concentration on both sides is equal</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109737023"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of permeating or infusing"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109737205"
	 b:glossaryEntry="impregnation with ammonia or a compound of ammonia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109737316"
	 b:glossaryEntry="saturation with carbon dioxide (as soda water)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109737420">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an exchange of molecules (and their kinetic energy and momentum) across the boundary between adjacent layers of a fluid or across cell membranes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109737656">
	<b:glossaryEntry>transport of a substance (as a protein or drug) across a cell membrane against the concentration gradient; requires an expenditure of energy</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109737859">
	<b:glossaryEntry>transport of a substance across a cell membrane by diffusion; expenditure of energy is not required</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109738022">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of becoming softened and saturated as a consequence of being immersed in water (or other liquid); "a good soak put life back in the wagon"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109738243"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the natural process of washing"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109738356"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of leaching"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109738442"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a change from one state (solid or liquid or gas) to another"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109738666"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the conversion of a solid or a gas into a liquid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109738773">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the passage of gases through fine tubes because of differences in pressure or temperature</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109738922"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of becoming a vapor"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109739112"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of extracting moisture (as by heat)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109739257">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a hot vapor containing fine particles of carbon being produced by combustion; "the fire produced a tower of black smoke that could be seen for miles"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109739468">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process whereby water particles become visible in the sky</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109739598">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process whereby the degree of hotness of a body (or medium) changes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109739804">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the transfer of heat through a fluid (liquid or gas) caused by molecular motion</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109739940">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(meteorology) the vertical movement of heat or other properties by massive motion within the atmosphere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109740100">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(meteorology) the horizontal transfer of heat or other atmospheric properties</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109740233">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the application of heat to change something from a liquid to a gas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109740371"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of becoming warmer; a rising temperature"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109740577">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the heating of a conducting material caused by an electric current induced in it</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109740721"
	 b:glossaryEntry="excessive heating"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109740796">
	<b:glossaryEntry>heating a building by radiation from panels containing hot water or electrical heaters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109740944"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of cooling or freezing for preservative purposes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109741080"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of becoming cooler; a falling temperature"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109741258">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the withdrawal of hear to change something from a liquid to a solid</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109741425"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the formation of frost or ice on a surface"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109741527">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of heating to change something from a solid to a liquid; "they welcomed the spring thaw"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109741723"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any uninterrupted stream or discharge"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109741955"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gradual internal motion or deformation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109742047"
	 b:glossaryEntry="flow into something (as a container)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109742155"
	 b:glossaryEntry="filling again by supplying what has been used up"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109742299"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of flowing in"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109742440"
	 b:glossaryEntry='an inflow; "an inpouring of spiritual comfort"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109742560"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of flowing out"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109742697">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a natural process resulting in the evolution of organisms best adapted to the environment</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109742899">
	<b:glossaryEntry>adaptation to a new climate (a new temperature or altitude or environment)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109743067">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of adapting to something (such as environmental conditions)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109743280"
	 b:glossaryEntry="adaptation to intimate association with human beings"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109743392">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the structural adaptation of some body part for a particular function</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109743557"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the loss of specialization in form or function"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109743667"
	 b:glossaryEntry="raising silkworms in order to obtain raw silk"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109743770">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the working together of two things (muscles or drugs for example) to produce an effect greater than the sum of their individual effects</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109743971">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process in which part of the output of a system is returned to its input</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109744138">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process whereby a transducer accepts waves in one form and gives back related waves in a different form</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109744322"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the transduction of sound waves into electrical waves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109744434"
	 b:glossaryEntry="feedback in phase with (augmenting) the input"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109744561"
	 b:glossaryEntry="feedback in opposite phase with (decreasing) the input"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109744679"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one trouble leads to another that aggravates the first"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109744810">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a slow westward shift of the equinoxes along the plane of the ecliptic caused by precession of the Earth''s axis of rotation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109745007">
	<b:glossaryEntry>preparing or putting through a prescribed procedure: "the processing of newly arrived immigrants"; "the processing of ore to obtain minerals"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109745295"
	 b:glossaryEntry="processing that involves blowing a gas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109745405">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(medical) blowing air or medicated powder into the lungs (or into some other body cavity)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109745553">
	<b:glossaryEntry>processing a photosensitive material in order to make an image visible; "the development and printing of his pictures took only two hours"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109745780">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of film) inadequately processed and so lacking adequate contrast</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109745908">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of removing impurities from oil or metals or sugar</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109746038">
	<b:glossaryEntry>process of treating rubber or rubberlike materials with sulphur at great heat to improve elasticity and strength or to harden them</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109746244">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the temporary retention of data in a processor while calculations or logical operations are performed on it</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109746612"
	 b:glossaryEntry="data processing by a computer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109746755">
	<b:glossaryEntry>automatic data processing by electronic means without the use of tabulating cards or punched tapes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109746932">
	<b:glossaryEntry>automatic data processing in which data acquisition and other stages or processing are integrated into a coherent system</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109747131">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(computer science) data processing in which the result is completely specified by a rule (especially the processing that results from a single instruction); "it can perform millions of operations per second"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109747736">
	<b:glossaryEntry>operations that occur without a regular or predictable time relation to other events</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109747930">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a operation performed by off-line equipment not under the control of the central processing unit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109748113">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an operation that follows the rules of boolean algebra; each operand and the result take one of two values</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109748332">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an elementary operation that a computer is designed and built to perform</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109748525">
	<b:glossaryEntry>two or more operations performed at the same time (or within a give interval)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109748669">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an operation that controls the recording or processing or transmission of interpretation of data; "a control operation started the data processing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109748899"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an operation on exactly two operands"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109748998">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an operation that is completed in a specified number of regularly timed execution cycles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109749154"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an operation that follows the rules of symbolic logic"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109749289"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an operation with exactly one operand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109749408">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a threshold operation in which each operand is 0 or 1; output is 1 if and only if more than half the operands have the value 1</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109749599"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an operation in which two or more activities are interleaved"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109749725"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the simultaneous execution of two or more operations"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109749885">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an operation that controls the printing or display of information</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109750069"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the sequential execution of operations one after another"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109750253"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of carrying out an instruction by a computer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109750420"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the serial execution of computer programs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109750524">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the execution of two or more computer programs by a single computer</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109750677"
	 b:glossaryEntry="data processing in accounting or business management"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109750833">
	<b:glossaryEntry>data processing in which the operations performed are determined by a system of priorities</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109751025">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the execution of low priority programs while higher priority programs are not using the processing system</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109751214">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the execution of a program that preempts the use of the processing system</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109751371">
	<b:glossaryEntry>data processing in which some of the functions are performed in different places and connected by transmission facilities</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109751615"
	 b:glossaryEntry="processing data that is given in the form of chained lists"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109751735"
	 b:glossaryEntry="simultaneous processing by two or more processing units"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109751874"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sequential processing by two or more processing units"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109751991">
	<b:glossaryEntry>data processing fast enough to keep up with an outside process</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109752142">
	<b:glossaryEntry>rapid and efficient processing (storage and printing) of linguistic data for composition and editing</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109752304">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an arithmetic operation performed on floating-point numbers; "this computer can perform a million flops per second"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109752497">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an operation that determines whether one or more of a set of items has a specified property; "they wrote a program to do a table lookup"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109752695">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an operation that segregates items into groups according to a specified criterion; "the bottleneck in mail delivery it the process of sorting"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109752898"
	 b:glossaryEntry="operations that are initiated predictably by a clock"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109753036">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an operation performed on operands in order to obtain the value of a threshold function</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109753207">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(computer science) the operation of accessing information from the computer''s memory</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109753347">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the operation that prepares for the next character to be printed or displayed as the first character on a line</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109753519">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the operation that prepares for the next character to be printed or displayed on the next line</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109753669">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the operation that prepares for the next character to be printed or displayed as the first character on the next line</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109753841"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of initiating combustion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109753950">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ignition of a substance (as oily rags) resulting from an internal oxidation process</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109754102">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the process of declining from a higher to a lower level of effective power or vitality or essential quality</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109754268"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any process serving to define the shape of something"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109754385">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the physical process in which particles are deflected haphazardly as a result of collisions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109754552">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the physical process in which incident radiated energy is retained without reflection or transmission</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109754729">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the reduction of the intensity of radiation as a consequence of absorption and radiation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109754912">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physiology) the effect of a stimulus (on nerves or organs etc.)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109755052"
	 b:glossaryEntry="stimulation of activity in an organism or chemical"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109755159">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(ecology) the process by which a plant or animal becomes established in a new habitat</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109755313">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(ecology) the gradual and orderly process of change in an ecosystem brought about by the progressive replacement of one community by another until a stable climax is established</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109755571"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the process of becoming vitreous"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109755663">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process whereby voters are moved toward nonpartisanship thus weakening the structure of political parties</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109755828">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the formation of stonelike objects within a body organ (e.g., the kidneys)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109755959">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process of taking up or using up or consuming; "they developed paper napkins with greater uptake of liquids"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109756140">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a process of using up or consuming again; "psychopharmacologists discovered that amine reuptake is a process that inactivates monoamine neurotransmitters"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109756361"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a specific measure of amount"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109756579"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an estimated quantity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109757080"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a quantity relative to some purpose"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109757341"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a system of related measures"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109757617">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a decimal system of weights and measures based on the meter and the kilogram and the second</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109757804">
	<b:glossaryEntry>system of measurement based on centimeters and grams and seconds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109757918">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a complete metric system of units of measurement for scientists; fundamental quantities are length (meter) and mass (kilogram) and time (second) and electric current (ampere) and temperature (kelvin) and amount of matter (mole) and luminous intensity (candela)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109758307">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a system of graduating sizes of type in multiples of the point</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109758428">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a system of measurement of information based on the probabilities of the information-bearing events</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109758629">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a data transmission rate; the maximum amount of information (bits/second) that can be transmitted along a channel</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109758798">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(computer science) a data transmission rate (bits/second) for modems</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109758929"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the amount of wood in an area as measured in cords"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109759033"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a measure of the antiknock properties of gasoline"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109759158">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a quantity that is large enough to achieve a purpose or satisfy a desire; "enough is as good as a feast"; "there is more than a sufficiency of lawyers in this country"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109759410">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a quantity sufficient to satisfy; "he ate his fill of potatoes"; " she had heard her fill of gossip"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109759561">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a greater or additional quantity or number or degree or amount: "the more I see of you the more I like you"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109759719">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the greatest number or amount or degree: "she has the most to lose"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109759837">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lowest or smallest in size or degree or importance: "least said soonest mended"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109759968">
	<b:glossaryEntry>smaller in amount or degree: "the less I see of you the better"; "people have lost their heads for less"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109760123">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the smaller in amount or degree esp of two alternatives: "the lesser of the two evils"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109760262"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(in an election) more than half of the votes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109760381">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(in an election with more than 2 options) the number of votes for the candidate or party receiving the greatest number (but less that half of the votes)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109760609">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any division of quantity accepted as a standard of measurement or exchange; "the dollar is the United States unit of currency"; "a unit of wheat is a bushel"; "change per unit volume"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109761387">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the unit of measurement for the proportion of gold in an alloy; 18-karat gold is 75% gold; 24-karat gold is pure gold</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109761564"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a quantity of paper; 24 or 25 sheets"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109761671">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a quantity of paper; 480 or 500 sheets; one ream equals 20 quires</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109761806"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a number in the decimal system"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109761890">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a number representing a quantity assumed to have a fixed value in a specified mathematical context; "the velocity of light is a constant"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109762118">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a constant number that serves as a measure of some property or characteristic</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109762451">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the rate of decrease in the intensity of electromagnetic radiation (as light) as it passes through a given substance; the fraction of incident radiant energy absorbed per unit mass or thickness of an absorber: "absorptance equals 1 minus transmittance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109762827">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the ratio of the drag on a body moving through air to the product of the velocity and the surface area of the body</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109763026">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the ratio of the weight of an object being moved along a surface and the force that maintains contact between the object and the surface</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109763232">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the induction between two circuits; the ratio of the electromotive force in a circuit to the corresponding change of current in a neighboring circuit; usually measured in henries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109763521">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the ratio of the electromotive force produced in a circuit by self-induction to the rate of change of current producing it, expressed in henries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109763759">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) a coefficient that expresses how much of a specified property is possessed by a specified substance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109763940">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) the ratio of the applied stress to the change in shape of an elastic body</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109764191">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the ratio of the change in pressure acting on a volume to the fractional change in volume</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109764339"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the coefficient of elasticity for a shearing force"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109764455">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a coefficient of elasticity applicable to the stretching of a wire</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109764583">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the fractional change in length or area or volume per unit change in temperature at a given constant pressure</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109764777">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the fraction of radiant energy that is reflected from a surface</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109764961">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the fraction of radiant energy that passes through a substance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109765098">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the resistance to flow of a fluid under an applied force</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109765279">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(statistics) a coefficient assigned to elements of a frequency distribution in order to represent their relative importance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109765467">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) the universal constant relating force to mass and distance in Newton''s law of gravitation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109765658">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a concept of quantity derived from zero and units; "every number has a unique position in the sequence"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109766372"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a number that is added to another number"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109766465"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a number to which another number (the addend) is added"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109766572"
	 b:glossaryEntry='the total number counted: "a blood count"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109766755"
	 b:glossaryEntry='a complete number or quantity: "a full complement"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109766862"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the number of red and white corpuscles in a blood sample"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109766976">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a count of troops killed in an operation or time period: "the daily body count increased as the war went on"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109767141"
	 b:glossaryEntry="number of people in a particular group"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109767248">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the number of sperm in an ejaculate; "the sperm count is used as an indicator of male fertility"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109767402">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of the numbers (or symbols) that form a product when multiplied together</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109767549">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the quantity of a particular substance that can dissolve in a particular solvent (yielding a saturated solution)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109767718"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a quantity that has no factor but itself and 1"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109767851">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the prime factors of a quantity are all of the prime quantities that will exactly divide the given quantity</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109768017"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an integer that has no integral factors but itself and 1"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109768132">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a number that expresses the accomplishment of a team or an individual in a game or contest; "the score was 7 to 0"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109768551"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(in golf) a score of one stroke under par on a golf hole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109768660"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(in golf) a score of one stroke over par on a golf hole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109768767"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(in golf) a score of two strokes over par for a hole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109768878"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(in cricket) a score of nothing by a batsman"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109768986"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(in golf) a score of two strokes under par on a golf hole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109769095"
	 b:glossaryEntry='the score needed to win a game; "he is serving for the game"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109769206"
	 b:glossaryEntry='a score of zero in tennis or squash; "it was 40 love"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109769310"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the score needed to win a match"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109769393">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(in golf) the standard number of strokes set for each hole on a golf course, or for the entire course; "a par-5 hole"; "par for this course is 72"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109769589">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the number of wins versus losses and ties a team has had; "at 9-0 they have the best record in their league"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109769750">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a quantity expressed in two different units; "one hour and ten minutes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109769883"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the number designating place in an ordered sequence"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109770029">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the first element in a countable series; "the first of the month"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109770170"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the number of elements in a mathematical set"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109770305">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(in a digital numeration system) the positive integer that is equivalent to one in the next higher counting place; "10 is the radix of the decimal system"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109770518"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a number represented in floating-point notation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109770633"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a number represented in fixed-point notation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109770742"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the number of observations in a given statistical category"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109770877">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the order of an element in Mendeleyev''s table of the elements; equal to the number of protons in the nucleus or electrons in the neutral state of an atom of an element</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109771122">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the atomic number of an extra stable strongly bound atomic nucleus: 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82 or 126</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109771276">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a number equal to the difference between the number of baryons and the number of antibaryons in any subatomic structure; it is conserved in all types of particle interactions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109771510">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a prescribed number; "all the salesmen met their quota for the month"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109771631"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a measure of length"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109771744">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the strength of a magnetic field over a given area</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109771885"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit for measuring absorption"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109771996"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit for measuring acceleration"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109772111"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of measurement for angles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109772436"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a system of units used to measure areas"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109772854"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of measurement of volume or capacity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109773384"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the volume equal to a cube one inch on each side"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109773497"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the volume equal to a cube one foot on each side"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109773610"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit for measuring computer memory"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109773911"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of amount of wood cut for burning; 128 cubic feet"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109774018">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various systems of units for measuring electricity and magnetism</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109774414"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any unit for measuring the force of explosions"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109774557"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of measurement of physical force"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109774761"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of measurement of length"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109775624">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a decimal unit of measurement based on meters and kilograms and seconds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109775807"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a monetary unit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109778024"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(computer science) a unit for measuring computing power"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109778191">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(computer science) a unit for measuring the execution speed of computers; "4 MIPS is 4,000,000 instructions per second"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109778395"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit measuring the intensity of pain"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109778507"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit measuring force per unit area"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109778729"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of measurement for printing"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109778841"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any acoustic unit of measurement"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109779002"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of measurement for telephone use"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109779120"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of measurement for temperature"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109779310">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit used to measure weight; "he placed two weights in the scale pan"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109779736"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of measurement for mass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109779858"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of measurement for viscosity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109779975"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of measurement for work"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109780339"
	 b:glossaryEntry="unit of solar radiation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109780416"
	 b:glossaryEntry="measure of the hardness of a material"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109780514"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a system for measuring the concentration of sugar solutions"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109780630">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the unit of counting in scoring a game or contest; "he scored 20 points in the first half"; "a touchdown counts 6 points"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109780858"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the final point needed to win a set in tennis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109780978"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the final point needed to win a match (especially in tennis)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109781096">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of acoustic absorption equivalent to the absorption by a square foot of a surface that absorbs all incident sound</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109781268"
	 b:glossaryEntry="measurement of angles in radians"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109781363">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an angular unit used in artillery; equal to 1/6400 of a complete revolution</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109781488">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure for arcs and angles; "there are 360 degrees in a circle"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109781695">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a 60th part of a minute of arc; "the treasure is 2 minutes and 45 seconds south of here"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109781867"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of angular distance equal to a 60th of a degree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109782040">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of angular distance equal to one thousandth of a milliradian</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109782184">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of angular distance equal to one thousandth of a radian</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109782342">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the unit of plane angle adopted under the System International d''Unites; equal to the angle at the center of a circle subtended by an arc equal in length to the radius (approximately 57.295 degrees)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109782618"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one-hundredth of a right angle"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109782726"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of angular distance equal to half a quadrant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109782868"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of angular distance equal to 60 degrees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109783006"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a quarter of the circumference of a circle"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109783193"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an angle of 180 degrees"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109783315">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the unit of solid angle adopted under the System International d''Unites</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109783466"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of area equal to one inch by one inch square"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109783583"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of area equal to one foot by one foot square"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109783700"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of area equal to one yard by one yard square"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109783817"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a centare is 1/100th of an are"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109783931"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an area of 640 acres"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109784027"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a land unit of 1 square mile measuring 1 mile on a side"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109784136">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a land unit equal to a quarter of a section (160 acres) and measuring 1/2 mile on a side</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109784286">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of area (4840 square yards) used in English-speaking countries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109784406"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of surface area equal to 100 square meters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109784524">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(abbreviated `ha'') a unit of surface area equal to 100 ares (or 10,000 square meters)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109784682"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a former French unit of area; equal approximately to an acre"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109784795"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(physics) a unit of nuclear cross section"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109784887"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a former Russian unit of area equal to 2.7 acres"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109784992"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a South African unit of measure equal to about 2 acres"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109785099"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a square rod of land"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109785184">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of capacity for liquids (for measuring the volumes of liquids or their containers)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109785456"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of capacity for dry commodities (as fruit or grain)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109785655">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of measurement of capacity for liquids officially adopted in the United States Customary System</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109786009">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a unit of measure for capacity officially adopted in the British Imperial System; British units are both dry & wet]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109786504"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a capacity unit defined in metric terms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109786790"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of dry measure used in Egypt"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109786877">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a liquid measure (with different values) used in some Spanish speaking countries</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109787010"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an ancient Hebrew liquid measure equal to about 10 gallons"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109787138"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a capacity unit used for measuring fresh herring"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109787237">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an ancient Hebrew unit of dry measure equal to about a bushel</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109787376">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an ancient Hebrew unit of capacity equal to 10 baths or 10 ephahs</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109787537"
	 b:glossaryEntry="ancient Hebrew unit of liquid measure = 1.5 gallons"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109787638">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(mining) a unit of volume (equal to 6 cubic feet) used in measuring bodies of ore</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109787779">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the volume of water that would cover 1 acre to a depth of 1 foot; 43,560 cubic feet or 1233.5 cubic meters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109787941"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one twelfth of an acre-foot"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109788024">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a system of units for measuring lumber based on the board_foot</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109788146"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the volume of a piece of wood 1 foot square and 1 inch thick"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109788263"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a board measure = 1980 board feet"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109788351"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of volume (as for sand or gravel)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109788455"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(British) a unit of capacity for grain equal to 80 bushels"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109788564">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Scottish unit of liquid measure equal to 0.9 United States pint</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109788684"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a Turkish liquid unit equal to 1.3 pints"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109788774"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a United States liquid unit equal to 1/60 fluidram"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109788895">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of capacity or volume in the apothecary system equal to one eighth of a fluid ounce</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109789115">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a United States unit of capacity or volume equal to 1.804 cubic inches</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109789294"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a United States liquid unit equal to 4 fluid ounces"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109789434"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a United States liquid unit equal to 8 fluid ounces"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109789573">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a United States liquid unit equal to 16 fluid ounces; two pints equal one quart</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109789741">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a quantity of liquor equal to one fifth of a United States gallon</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109789858">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a United States liquid unit equal to 32 fluid ounces; four quarts equal one gallon</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109790030"
	 b:glossaryEntry="United States liquid unit equal to 4 quarts or 3.785 liters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109790186">
	<b:glossaryEntry>any of various units of capacity; "a barrel of beer is 31 gallons and a barrel of oil is 42 gallons"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109790420">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of measurement of capacity for dry substances officially adopted in the United States Customary System</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109790670">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a United States dry unit equal to 0.5 quart or 33.6 cubic inches</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109790815">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a United States dry unit equal to 2 pints or 67.2 cubic inches</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109790979">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a United States dry measure equal to 8 quarts or 537.605 cubic inches</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109791137">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a United States dry measure equal to 4 pecks or 2152.42 cubic inches</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109791277">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a British imperial capacity measure (liquid or dry) equal to 1/60th fluid dram or 0.059194 cubic centimeters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109791456">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a British imperial capacity measure (liquid or dry) equal to 60 minims or 3.5516 cubic centimeters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109791684">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a British imperial unit of capacity or volume (liquid or dry) equal to 8 fluid drams or 28.416 cubic centimeters (1.734 cubic inches)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109791926">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a British imperial capacity unit (liquid or dry) equal to 5 fluid ounces or 142.066 cubic centimeters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109792116">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a British imperial capacity measure (liquid or dry) equal to 4 gills or 568.26 cubic centimeters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109792301">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a British imperial capacity measure (liquid or dry) equal to 2 pints or 1.136 liters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109792475">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a British imperial capacity measure (liquid or dry) equal to 4 quarts or 4.545 liters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109792717">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a British imperial capacity measure (liquid or dry) equal to 2 gallons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109792857">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a British imperial capacity measure (liquid or dry) equal to 4 pecks</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109793035"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a British unit of capacity equal to 9 imperial gallons"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109793180">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an obsolete British unit of capacity equal to 18 Imperial gallons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109793320"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(British) a quarter of a hundredweight (28 pounds)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109793462"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a British unit of capacity for alcoholic beverages"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109793586">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a British imperial capacity measure (liquid or dry) equal to 36 bushels</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109793712">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a metric measure of volume or capacity equal to a cube 1 millimeter on each edge</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109793893"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of volume equal to one thousandth of a liter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109794113"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of volume equal to one hundredth of a liter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109794283"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of volume equal to one tenth of a liter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109794447">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a metric unit of capacity equal to the volume of 1 kilogram of pure water at 4 degrees centigrade and 760 mm of mercury (or approximately 1.76 pints)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109794734"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of volume or capacity equal to 10 liters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109794930"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of volume or capacity equal to 100 liters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109795098"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of volume or capacity equal to 1000 liters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109795288">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of capacity equal to the volume of a cube one kilometer on each edge</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109795462">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of measurement of information (from Binary+digIT); the amount of information in a system having two equiprobable states; "there are 8 bits in a byte"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109795705">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(computer science) an error detection procedure in which a bit (0 or 1) added to each group of bits so that it will have either an odd number of 1''s or an even number of 1''s; e.g., if the parity is odd then any group of bits that arrives with an even number of 1''s must contain an error</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109796057"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a small byte"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109796150">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a sequence of 8 bits (enough to represent one character of alphanumeric data) processed as a single unit of information</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109796377">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the minimum track length that can be assigned to store information; unless otherwise specified a sector of data consists of 512 bytes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109796601">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[a sector or group of sectors that function as the smallest data unit permitted; "since blocks are often defined as a single sector, the terms `block'' and `sector'' are sometimes used interchangeably"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109796888"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a block (usually one sector) that cannot reliably hold data"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109797003">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a group of sectors on a magnetic disk that can be reserved for the use of a particular file</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109797175">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(computer science) the part of a hard disk that is dedicated to a particular operating system or application and accessed as a single unit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109797369">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a word is a string of bits stored in computer memory; "large computers use words up to 64 bits long"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109797558"
	 b:glossaryEntry="1024 bytes of information"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109797715"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one million bits"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109797785"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of information equal to one million (1,048,576) bytes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109797959">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of information equal to one billion (1,000,000,000) bytes or one thousand megabytes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109798167">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of information equal to one trillion (1,000,000,000,000) bytes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109798335">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the capacity of a circuit component to store charge</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109798534">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the quantity of electricity (determined by the amount of an electric current and the time for which it flows)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109798802">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of a material''s ability to conduct an electrical charge</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109798948">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the amount of electric charge flowing past a circuit point at a specific time</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109799169"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the reciprocal of capacitance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109799277"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an electromagnetic unit of magnetic intensity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109799424">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the amount of flux per unit of cross sectional area</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109799606"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a measure of the strength of a magnetic field per unit area"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109799778">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the property of an electric circuit by which an electromotive force is induced in it</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109799991"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a measure of the visible electromagnetic radiation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109800188"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of measurement of magnetomotive force"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109800357">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of the potential energy of a unit charge at a give point in a circuit relative to a reference point (ground)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109800608"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a measure of electric power"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109800836"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a measure of radioactivity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109801035"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the reciprocal of conductance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109801180"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of capacitance equal to one trillionth of a farad"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109801311"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of capacitance equal to one thousandth of a farad"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109801462">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the capacitance of a capacitor that has an equal and opposite charge of 1 coulomb on each plate and a voltage difference of 1 volt between the plates</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109801705"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a capacitance unit equal to one billion farads"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109801824">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of electrical charge equal to the amount of charge transferred by a current of 1 ampere in 1 second</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109802042"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of electrical charge equal to 10 coulombs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109802165"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of charge equal to 60 coulombs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109802300"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of charge equal to 3600 coulombs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109802416"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of conductance equal to the reciprocal of an ohm"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109802552">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[the basic unit of electric current adopted under the System International d''Unites; "a typical household circuit carries 15 to 50 amps"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109802788"
	 b:glossaryEntry="one thousandth of an ampere"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109802897"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of current equal to 10 amperes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109803016">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a former unit of electric current (slightly smaller than the SI ampere)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109803163"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of elastance equal to the reciprocal of a farad"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109803269">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of magnetic field strength equal to one-hundred-thousandth of an oersted</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109803419"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the magnetic field strength 1 cm from a unit magnetic pole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109803550">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a cgs unit of magnetic flux equal to the flux perpendicular to an area of 1 square centimeter in a magnetic field of 1 gauss</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109803752"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of magnetic flux equal to 100,000,000 maxwells"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109803881">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of magnetic flux density equal to one millionth of a gauss</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109804022">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of magnetic flux density equal to 1 maxwell per square centimeter</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109804184">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of magnetic flux density equal to one weber per square meter</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109804322"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of inductance equal to one billionth of a henry"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109804449"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of inductance equal to one thousandth of a henry"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109804580">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of inductance in which an induced electromotive force of one volt is produced when the current is varied at the rate of one ampere per second</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109804822"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a measure of illumination"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109804983"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a measure of luminance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109805102"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a measure of luminous flux per unit area"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109805225"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a measure of luminous intensity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109805381">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the intensity of light falling on a photographic film or plate; "he used the wrong exposure"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109805564"
	 b:glossaryEntry="exposure to too much light or for too long a time"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109805672"
	 b:glossaryEntry="exposure to too little light or for too short a time"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109805784">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of illuminance on a surface that is everywhere 1 foot from a point source of 1 candle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109805933">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a cgs unit of illumination equal to the brightness of a perfectly diffusing surface that emits or reflects one lumen per square centimeter</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109806129">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of illumination equal to 1 lumen per square meter; 0.0929 foot candle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109806279">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of illumination equal to 1 lumen per square centimeter; 10,000 phots equal 1 lux</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109806436">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a luminance unit equal to 1 candle per square meter measured perpendicular to the rays from the source</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109806588"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a former luminance unit equal to one lumen per square foot"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109806712">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of luminous flux equal to the amount of light given out through a solid angle of 1 steradian by a point source of 1 candela intensity radiating uniformly in all directions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109806947">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the basic unit of luminous intensity adopted under the System International d''Unites; equal to 1/60 of the luminous intensity per square centimeter of a blackbody radiating at the temperature of 2,046 degrees Kelvin</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109807248">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a former international unit of luminous intensity; now replaced by the candela</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109807393"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of magnetomotive force equal to 0.7958 ampere-turns"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109807515">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of magnetomotive force equal to the magnetomotive force produced by the passage of 1 ampere through 1 complete turn of a coil; equal to 1.257 gilberts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109807730">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of magnetic moment of a molecular or atomic or subatomic particle</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109807857"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of potential equal to one-hundred-millionth of a volt"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109807989"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of potential equal to one thousandth of a volt"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109808122">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of potential equal to the potential difference between two points on a conductor carrying a current of 1 ampere when the power dissipated between the two points is 1 watt; equivalent to the potential difference across a resistance of 1 ohm when 1 ampere of current flows through it</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109808522"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of potential equal to a thousand volts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109808646">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a wave number characteristic of the wave spectrum of each element</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109808799"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the reciprocal of the wavelength of a wave"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109808917">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a power unit equal to the power dissipated when 1 abampere flows across a potential difference of 1 abvolt (one ten-thousandth of a milliwatt)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109809131"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of power equal to one thousandth of a watt"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109809274">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of power equal to 1 joule per second; the power dissipated by a current of 1 ampere flowing across a resistance of 1 ohm</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109809513"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of power equal to 1000 watts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109809646"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of power equal to one million watts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109809762"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of power equal to 746 watts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109809896">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of electrical power in an AC circuit equal to the power dissipated when 1 volt produces a current of 1 ampere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109810095"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of electrical power equal to 1000 volt-amperes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109810229"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of radioactivity equal to one thousandth of a curie"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109810363">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of radioactivity equal to the amount of a radioactive isotope that decays at the rate of 37,000,000,000 disintegrations per second</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109810576">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of radiation exposure; the dose of ionizing radiation that will produce 1 electrostatic unit of electricity in 1 cc of dry air</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109810768">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit strength of a radioactive source equal to one million disintegrations per second</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109810912">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(Roentgen Equivalent Man) the dosage of ionizing radiation that will cause the same amount of injury to human tissue as 1 roentgen of X-rays</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109811108">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of absorbed ionizing radiation equal to 100 ergs per gram of irradiated material</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109811245"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of resistance equal to a billionth of an ohm"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109811367">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of electrical resistance equal to the resistance between two points on a conductor when a potential difference of one volt between them produces a current of one ampere</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109811630"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of resistance equal to one million ohms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109811748">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of explosive power (of an atomic weapon) equal to that of 1000 tons of TNT</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109811886">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a measure of explosive power (of an atomic weapon) equal to that of one million tons of TNT</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109812031">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of force equal to the force that imparts an acceleration of 1 cm/sec/sec to a mass of 1 gram</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109812200">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of force equal to the force that imparts an acceleration of 1 m/sec/sec to a mass of 1 kilogram; equal to 100,000 dynes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109812421"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of force equal to 1000 newtons"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109812530">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of force equal to the force that imparts an acceleration of 1 foot/sec/sec to a mass of 1 pound; equal to 0.1382 newtons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109812717">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a nontechnical unit of force equal to the mass of 1 pound with an acceleration of free fall equal to 32 feet/sec/sec</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109812892">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of force equal to the force exerted by gravity; used to indicate the force to which a body is subjected when it is accelerated</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109813079">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of gravitational acceleration equal to one centimeter per second per second (named after Galileo)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109813233"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a scale from 0 to 12 for the force of the wind"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109813340"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a linear unit used for astronomical distances"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109813554"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a linear unit of distance in metric terms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109813912"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a linear unit of distance used in navigation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109814077">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of length (in United States and Britain) equal to one twelfth of a foot</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109814306">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a linear unit of length equal to 12 inches or a third of a yard; "he is six feet tall"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109814505">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used only in combinations) the height or length of something in feet; "he is a six-footer" or "the golfer sank a 40-footer" or "his yacht is a 60-footer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109814712">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of length equal to 3 feet; defined as 91.44 centimeters; originally taken to be the average length of a stride</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109814982"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(British) a linear measure of 16.5 feet"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109815124"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of length equal to 220 yards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109815270"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of length equal to 1760 yards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109815475"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of length equal to half of 1 mile"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109815602"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of length equal to a quarter of 1 mile"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109815737">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an obsolete unit of distance of variable length (usually 3 miles)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109815874">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a linear unit (1/40 inch) used to measure diameter of buttons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109816006"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a former unit of length for cloth equal to 1/16 of a yard"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109816114"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a Russian unit of length (71 cm)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109816200">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an Indian unit of length having different values in different localities</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109816329">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a Spanish unit of length (about a yard) having different values in different localities</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109816467"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a Russian unit of length (1.067 km)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109816554"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a nautical unit of depth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109816662"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of length"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109816841"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of length (22 yards)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109816948"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of length (100 ft)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109817036"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an ancient unit of length based on the length of the forearm"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109817148"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the length of breadth of a finger used as a linear measure"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109817302"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the length of your body"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109817383"
	 b:glossaryEntry='the most extreme possible amount or value; "voltage peak"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109817538">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of length equal to 4 inches; used in measuring horses; "the horse stood 20 hands"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109817677"
	 b:glossaryEntry="any unit of length based on the breadth of the human hand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109817824">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the length or height based on the size of a human or animal head; "he is two heads taller than his little sister"; "his horse won by a head"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109818015"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of length of thread or yarn"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109818118"
	 b:glossaryEntry="Chinese distance measure; approximately 0.5 kilometers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109818221"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a unit of length equal to 1/100 of a chain"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109818333">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the number of opening per inch of a screen; measures size of particles; "a 100 mesh screen" or "100 mesh powdered cellulose"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109818527">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of length equal to one thousandth of an inch; used to specify thickness (e.g., of sheets or wire)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109818700"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a Swedish unit of length equivalent to 10 km"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109818810"
	 b:glossaryEntry="an ancient Roman unit of length equivalent to 1620 yards"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109818930">
	<b:glossaryEntry>an ancient Roman unit of length (4.85 English feet) measured as the distance from the heel of one foot to the heel of the same foot when next it touches the ground</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109819150"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a modern version of the Roman pace now taken to be 5 feet"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109819268">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the length of a single step in marching (taken to be 30 inches for quick time or 36 inches for double time)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109819435"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a linear unit based on the length or width of the human hand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109819546">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of length based on the width of the expanded human hand (usually taken as 9 inches)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109819687">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the distance that light travels in a vacuum in 1 year; 5.88 trillion miles or 9.46 trillion kilometers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109819903">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the distance light travels in a vacuum in one hour; approximately one billion kilometers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109820067">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the distance light travels in a vacuum in one minute; approximately 18 million kilometers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109820234">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the distance light travels in a vacuum in one second; approximately 300,000 kilometers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109820398">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of length used for distances within the solar system; equal to the mean distance between the Earth and the Sun (approximately 93 million miles or 150 million kilometers)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109820643">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of astronomical length based on the distance from Earth at which stellar parallax is 1 second of arc; equivalent to 3.262 light years</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109820845">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a metric unit of length equal to one quadrillionth of a meter</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109821003"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of length equal to one trillionth of a meter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109821181">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a metric unit of length equal to one ten billionth of a meter (or 0.0001 micron); used to specify wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109821432"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of length equal to one billionth of a meter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109821645"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of length equal to one millionth of a meter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109821819"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of length equal to one thousandth of a meter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109821990"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of length equal to one hundredth of a meter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109822160"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of length equal to one tenth of a meter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109822324">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the basic unit of length adopted under the System International d''Unites (approximately 1.094 yards)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109822526"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of length equal to ten meters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109822711"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of length equal to 100 meters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109822867">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a metric unit of length equal to 1000 meters (or 0.621371 miles)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109823050"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a metric unit of length equal to 10,000 meters"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109823191"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a nautical unit of length (15 ft)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109823285">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a linear unit of measurement (equal to 6 feet) for water depth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109823426">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a unit of length used in navigation; equivalent to the distance spanned by one second of arc in latitude; 1,852 meters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109823674">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a British unit of length equivalent to 1,853.18 meters (6,082 feet)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109823860">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a former British unit of length once used in navigation; equivalent to 1828.8 meters (6000 feet)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109824018"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the amount that can be bought for a halfpenny"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109824136"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the amount that can be bought for a penny"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109824246">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the basic monetary unit in many countries; equal to 100 cents</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109824793">
	<b:glossaryEntry>the basic monetary unit in many countries; equal to 100 centimes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;109825306">
