	<b:glossaryEntry>bearing an autograph; "an autographed copy of his latest book"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302077951">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of a contract or will or other document) having a signature written at the end; "the subscribed will"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302078110">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking a signature; "the message was typewritten and unsigned"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302078228">
	<b:glossaryEntry>important in effect or meaning; "a significant change in tax laws"; "a significant change in the Constitution"; "a significant contribution"; "significant details"; "statistically significant"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302078742">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of very great significance "deciding to drop the atom bomb was a very big decision"; "a momentous event"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302078908">
	<b:glossaryEntry>highly significant or important especially bringing about or marking the beginning of a new development or era; "epochal decisions made by Roosevelt and Churchill"; "an epoch-making discovery"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302079169">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sufficiently significant to affect the whole world; "earthshaking proposals"; "the contest was no world-shaking affair"; "the conversation...could hardly be called world-shattering"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302079444">
	<b:glossaryEntry>serving as or based on evidence; "evidential signs of a forced entry"; "its evidentiary value"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302079609">
	<b:glossaryEntry>far-reaching and thoroughgoing in effect especially on the nature of something; "the fundamental revolution in human values that has occurred"; "the book underwent fundamental changes"; "committed the fundamental error of confusing spending with extravagance"; "profound social changes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302079964">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(grammar) of a word in a phrase; the word playing the same grammatical role in the sentence that the phrase itself plays; "the head noun in the phrase `fresh fish'' is `fish,'' which is the direct object in the sentence `I bought fresh fish''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302080258">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fairly large or important in effect; influential; "played a large role in the negotiations"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302080401">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[of outstanding significance; "Einstein''s monumental contributions to physics"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302080535">
	<b:glossaryEntry>worthy of notice; "a noteworthy fact is that her students rarely complain"; "a remarkable achievement"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302080707"
	 b:glossaryEntry='effective; producing a desired effect; "the operative word"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302080828">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of momentous or ominous significance; "such a portentous...monster raised all my curiosity"- Herman Melville; "a prodigious vision"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302081029"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not important or noteworthy"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302081256">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relating to the peripheral and unimportant aspects of life; "a hole-and-corner life in some obscure community"- H.G.Wells</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302081468">
	<b:glossaryEntry>too small to make a significant difference; "inappreciable fluctuations in temperature"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302081615">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having little importance; "losing his job was no light matter"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302081729">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of little substance or significance; "a few superficial editorial changes"; "only trivial objections"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302081898">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(statistics) too closely correlated to be attributed to chance and therefore indicating a systematic relation; "the interaction effect is significant at the .01 level"; "no significant difference was found"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302082162"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(statistics) attributable to chance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302082258"
	 b:glossaryEntry='reduced to silence; "the silenced crowd waited expectantly"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302082390">
	<b:glossaryEntry>manifesting or subjected to suppression; "a suppressed press"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302082508"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not silenced"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302082577">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) especially of leaf shapes; having no divisions or subdivisions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302083239"
	 b:glossaryEntry="narrow and long; as pine leaves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302083379"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; narrowing to a slender point"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302083498"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; having a short sharply pointed tip"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302083623"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; tapering gradually into a long taillike tip"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302083737"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302083821">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; narrowly triangular, wider at the apex and tapering toward the base</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302083974">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; suggesting a capital delta, with a point at the apex</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302084097"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having the shape of the head of an ax or cleaver"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302084215"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; in the form of an ellipse"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302084330"
	 b:glossaryEntry='shaped like a sword blade; "the iris has an ensiform leaf"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302084500">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; like a spear point, with flaring pointed lobes at the base</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302084666">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; shaped like a lance head; narrow; pointed at each end</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302084823"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; long and narrow"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302084919"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; having curvature suggestive of a lyre"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302085026"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; rounded at the apex"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302085115">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; having a broad rounded apex and a tapering base</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302085238">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; having a somewhat elongated form with approximately parallel sides</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302085392">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; egg-shaped with the narrower end at the base</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302085507"
	 b:glossaryEntry="circular or nearly circular"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302085621"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; egg-shaped with the broader end at the base"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302085733"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302085834">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; round, with the stem attached near the center of the lower surface rather than the margin, as a nasturtium leaf e.g.</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302086037">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf having the base united around, and apparently pierced by, the stem</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302086170"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302086256">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; like an arrow head without flaring base lobes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302086421">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; having a broad rounded apex and a narrow base</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302086574"
	 b:glossaryEntry="without lobes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302086641">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) especially of leaf shapes; composed of several similar parts or lobes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302087278"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having two lobes; "a bilobate leaf"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302087390"
	 b:glossaryEntry='growing in two parts or in pairs; "binate leaves"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302087492"
	 b:glossaryEntry="divided into two portions almost to the base"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302087592"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; having doubly pinnate leaflets (as ferns)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302087706"
	 b:glossaryEntry="pinnatifid with the segments also pinnatifid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302087809"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having one or more incisions reaching nearly to the midrib"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302087931"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a pinnate leaflet; having only one pair of leaflets"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302088041">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a compound leaf; consisting of divisions that are themselves compound</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302088170"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; pinnate with a pair of leaflets at the apex"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302088322"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sharply and deeply indented"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302088403">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having deeply indented margins but with lobes not entirely separate from each other</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302088547"
	 b:glossaryEntry="a leaf shape; pinnate with a single leaflet at the apex"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302088676">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; having leaflets or lobes radiating from a common point</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302088815"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; palmately cleft rather than lobed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302088922">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a margin incised almost to the base so as to create distinct divisions or lobes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302089061">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; having radiating lobes, each deeply cleft or divided</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302089183">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a leaf shape; featherlike; having leaflets on each side of a common axis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302089323">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cleft nearly to the midrib in broad divisions not separated into distinct leaflets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302089462">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cleft nearly to the midrib in narrow divisions not separated into distinct leaflets</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302089603"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; having five leaflets"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302089701"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; consisting of three leaflets or sections"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302089812"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a leaf shape; having three leaflets"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302089936"
	 b:glossaryEntry="divided into three lobes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302090040"
	 b:glossaryEntry="thrice pinnate"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302090125"
	 b:glossaryEntry="bipinnatifid with segments pinnatifid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302090222">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not complex or complicated or involved; "a simple problem"; "simple mechanisms"; "a simple design"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302090553">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being the ultimate or elemental constituents of anything; "the elemental stuff of...out of which the many forms of life have been molded"- Jack London; "the ultimate ingredients of matter"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302090808"
	 b:glossaryEntry="unrealistically simple"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302090904">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[having only one part or element; "a simplex word has no affixes and is not part of a compound--like `boy'' compared with `boyish'' or `house'' compared with `houseboat''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302091124">
	<b:glossaryEntry>reduced in complexity; "a useful if somewhat simplified classification system"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302091259">
	<b:glossaryEntry>representing the furthest possible extent of analysis or division into parts; "a feeling is a simple and undecomposable mental state"- G.S.Brett; "this weight of evidence is something mystical and unanalyzable"-M.R.Cohen</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302091555">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking complexity; "small and uncomplicated cars for those really interested in motoring"; "an unsophisticated machine"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302091753">
	<b:glossaryEntry>complicated in structure; consisting of interconnected parts; "a complex set of variations based on a simple folk melody"; "a complex mass of diverse laws and customs"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302092226"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of being partitioned"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302092326">
	<b:glossaryEntry>highly involved or intricate; "the Byzantine tax structure"; "convoluted legal language"; "convoluted reasoning"; "an intricate labyrinth of refined phraseology"; "the plot was too involved"; "a knotty problem"; "got his way by labyrinthine maneuvering"; "Oh, what a tangled web we weave"- Sir Walter Scott; "tortuous legal procedures"; "tortuous negotiations lasting for months"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302092842">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(zoology) composed of many distinct individuals united to form a whole or colony; "coral is a colonial organism"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302093020">
	<b:glossaryEntry>difficult to analyze or understand; "a complicated problem"; "complicated Middle East politics"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302093173"
	 b:glossaryEntry="consisting of separate interconnected parts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302093272">
	<b:glossaryEntry>consisting of two or more substances or ingredients or elements or parts; "soap is a compound substance"; "housetop is a compound word"; "a blackberry is a compound fruid"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302093498"
	 b:glossaryEntry='extremely intricate; usually in phrase "gordian knot"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302093605"
	 b:glossaryEntry="linked or locked closely together as by dovetailing"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302093757">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resembling a labyrinth in form or complexity; "a labyrinthine network of tortuous footpaths"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302093930">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having many parts or aspects; "the multiplex problem of drug abuse"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302094053"
	 b:glossaryEntry='becoming more intricate or complex; "a thickening plot"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302094165">
	<b:glossaryEntry>open and genuine; not deceitful; "he was a good man, decent and sincere"; "felt sincere regret that they were leaving"; "sincere friendship"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302094593"
	 b:glossaryEntry='undertaken in good faith; "a bona fide offer"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302094694">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[sincerely or intensely felt; "a cordial regard for his visitor''s comfort"; "a cordial abhorrence of waste"; "a fervent hope"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302094882">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[earnest; "one''s dearest wish"; "devout wishes for their success"; "heartfelt condolences"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302095053"
	 b:glossaryEntry='without dissimulation; frank; "my honest opinion"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302095155">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not pretended; sincerely felt or expressed; "genuine emotion"; "her interest in people was unfeigned"; "true grief"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302095346">
	<b:glossaryEntry>with unconditional and enthusiastic devotion; "heart-whole friendship"; "gave wholehearted support to her candidacy"; "wholehearted commitment"; "demonstrated his whole-souled allegiance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302095621">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking sincerity; "a charming but thoroughly insincere woman"; "their praise was extravagant and insincere"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302096019"
	 b:glossaryEntry="attempting to win favor from influential people by flattery"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302096184">
	<b:glossaryEntry>unpleasantly and excessively suave or ingratiating in manner or speech; "buttery praise"; "gave him a fulsome introduction"; "an oily sycophantic press agent"; "oleaginous hypocrisy"; "smarmy self-importance"; "the unctuous Uriah Heep"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302096523">
	<b:glossaryEntry>concealing under a false appearance with the intent to deceive; "dissimulative arts"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302096697">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deliberately deceptive; "hollow (or false) promises"; "false pretenses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302096829"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not genuine; "feigned sympathy"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302096914">
	<b:glossaryEntry>based on pretense; deceptively pleasing; "the gilded and perfumed but inwardly rotten nobility"; "meretricious praise"; "a meretricious argument"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302097138">
	<b:glossaryEntry>professing feelings or virtues one does not have; "hypocritical praise"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302097268">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grammatical number category referring to a single item or unit</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302097385">
	<b:glossaryEntry>grammatical number category referring to two or more items or units</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302097523">
	<b:glossaryEntry>a grammatical number category referring to two items or units (as opposed to one item (singular) or more than one item (plural)); "ancient Greek had the dual form but it has merged with the plural form in modern Greek"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302097792">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being or denoting a numerical quantity but not order; "cardinal numbers"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302098747">
	<b:glossaryEntry>indicating the absence of any or all units under consideration; "a zero score"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302098880">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[used of a single unit or thing; not two or more; "`ane'' is Scottish"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302099012"
	 b:glossaryEntry='being one more than one; "he received two messages"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302099122"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than two"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302099207"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than three"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302099292"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than four"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302099375"
	 b:glossaryEntry="denoting a quantity consisting of six items or units"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302099514"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than six"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302099599"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than seven"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302099687">
	<b:glossaryEntry>denoting a quantity consisting of one more than eight and one less than ten</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302099822"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than nine"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302099905"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than ten"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302099991"
	 b:glossaryEntry="denoting a quantity consisting of 12 items or units"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302100114"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than twelve"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302100207"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than thirteen"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302100301"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than fourteen"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302100393"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than fifteen"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302100485"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than sixteen"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302100580"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than seventeen"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302100677"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than eighteen"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302100771"
	 b:glossaryEntry="denoting a quantity consisting of 20 items or units"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302100893"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being one more than twenty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302100987"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being two more than twenty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302101082"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being three more than twenty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302101182"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being four more than twenty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302101279"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being five more than twenty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302101375"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being six more than twenty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302101470"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being seven more than twenty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302101570"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being eight more than twenty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302101671"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being nine more than twenty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302101768"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being ten more than twenty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302101858"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being ten more than thirty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302101957"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being ten more than forty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302102043"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being ten more than fifty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302102143"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being ten more than sixty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302102233"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being ten more than seventy"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302102337"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being ten more than eighty"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302102426"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being ten more than ninety"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302102542"
	 b:glossaryEntry="denoting a quantity consisting of 500 items or units"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302102663"
	 b:glossaryEntry="denoting a quantity consisting of 1,000 items or units"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302102815"
	 b:glossaryEntry="denoting a quantity consisting of 10,000 items or units"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302102929">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(in Roman numerals, C written with a macron over it) denoting a quantity consisting of 100,000 items or units</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302103122">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(in Roman numerals; M written with a macron over it) denoting a quantity consisting of 1,000,000 items or units</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302103299">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(U.S.) denoting a quantity consisting of one thousand million items or units; (Britain) denoting a quantity consisting of one million million items or units</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302103521"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(U.S.) one million million; (Britain) one quintillion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302103642">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very large indeterminate number; "a zillion people were there"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302103770">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being or denoting a numerical order in a series; "ordinal numbers"; "held an ordinal rank of seventh"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302104717"
	 b:glossaryEntry="indicating an initial point or origin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302104805"
	 b:glossaryEntry="indicating the beginning unit in a series"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302104904">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the first in position in space or time or degree or magnitude</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302105047">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the second and just before the fourth in position</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302105183">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the third and just before the fifth in position or time or degree or magnitude; "the quaternary period of geologic time extends from the end of the tertiary period to the present"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302105452">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the fourth and just before the sixth in position</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302105576">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the fifth and just before the seventh in position</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302105701">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the sixth and just before the eighth in position</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302105827">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the seventh and just before the ninth in position</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302105953">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the eighth and just before the tenth in position</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302106077">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the ninth and just before the eleventh in position</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302106204">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the tenth and just before the twelfth in position</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302106333">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the eleventh and just before the thirteenth in position</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302106467"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the twelfth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302106572"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the thirteenth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302106680">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming next after the fourteenth and just before the sixteenth in position</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302106817"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the fifteenth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302106923"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the sixteenth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302107031"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the seventeenth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302107140"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the eighteenth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302107248"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(informal) last in an indefinitely numerous series"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302107376"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the nineteenth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302107483"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the twentieth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302107592"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the twenty-first in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302107705"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the twenty-second in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302107818"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the twenty-third in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302107931"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the twenty-fourth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302108044"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the twenty-fifth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302108156"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the twenty-sixth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302108270"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the twenty-seventh in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302108385"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the twenty-eighth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302108498"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming next after the twenty-ninth in position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302108607"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ordinal number of forty in counting order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302108714"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ordinal number of fifty in counting order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302108821"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ordinal number of sixty in counting order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302108928"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ordinal number of seventy in counting order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302109039"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ordinal number of eighty in counting order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302109148"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ordinal number of ninety in counting order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302109257"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ordinal number of one hundred in counting order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302109385"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ordinal number of one thousand in counting order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302109503"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ordinal number of one million in counting order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302109610"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ordinal number of one billion in counting order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302109717"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the ordinal number of one trillion in counting order"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302109826"
	 b:glossaryEntry="last or greatest in an indefinitely large series"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302109924"
	 b:glossaryEntry="written as for a film or play or broadcast"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302110031">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not furnished with or using a script; "unrehearsed and unscript spot interviews"; "unscripted talk shows"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302110211">
	<b:glossaryEntry>said or done without having been planned or written in advance; "he made a few ad-lib remarks"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302110384"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of being sunk"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302110460">
	<b:glossaryEntry>incapable of being sunk; "they thought the Titanic was unsinkable"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302110583">
	<b:glossaryEntry>existing alone or consisting of one entity or part or aspect or individual; "upon the hill stood a single tower"; "had but a single thought which was to escape"; "a single survivor"; "a single serving"; "a single lens"; "a single thickness"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302111041">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(anatomy) occurring singly; not one of a pair; "the azygous muscle of the uvula"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302111184">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[being one in number--a single unit or thing; "one person is going"; "her one thought was to win"; "I''m just one player on the team"; "one day is just like the next"; "seen one horse and you''ve seen them all"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302111444">
	<b:glossaryEntry>designed for or restricted to a single person; "a one-man show"; "a one-person tent"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302111616">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being the only one; single and isolated from others; "the lone doctor in the entire county"; "a lonesome pine"; "an only child"; "the sole heir"; "the sole example"; "a solitary instance of cowardice"; "a solitary speck in the sky"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302111949">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being the single appropriate individual of a kind; only; "the one horse that could win this race"; "the one person I could marry"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302112131">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[the single one of its kind; "a singular example"; "the unique existing example of Donne''s handwriting"; "a unique copy of an ancient manuscript"; "certain types of problems have unique solutions"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302112390">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[constituting a class of its own; unique; "a history book sui generis"; "sui generis works like Mary Chestnut''s Civil War diary"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302112575">
	<b:glossaryEntry>consisting of or involving a single element or component; "in a unary operation in a mathematical system one element is used to yield a single result"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302112777">
	<b:glossaryEntry>based on the system of having only one member from each district (as of a legislature); "a uninominal electoral system"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302112966">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or involving or consisting of more than one part or entity or individual; "multiple birth"; "multiple ownership"; "made multiple copies of the speech"; "his multiple achievements in public life"; "her multiple personalities"; "a pineapple is a multiple fruit"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302113683">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) formed of separate units in a cluster; "raspberries are aggregate fruits"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302113821"
	 b:glossaryEntry='occurring in pairs; "a bigeminal pulse"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302113916">
	<b:glossaryEntry>consisting of two (units or components or elements or terms) or based on two; "a binary star is a system in which two stars revolve around each other"; "a binary compound"; "the binary number system has two as its base"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302114189">
	<b:glossaryEntry>twice as great or many; "ate a double portion"; "the dose is doubled"; "a twofold increase"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302114353">
	<b:glossaryEntry>consisting of or involving two parts or components usually in pairs; "an egg with a double yolk"; "a double (binary) star"; "double doors"; "dual controls for pilot and copilot"; "duple (or double) time consists of two (or a multiple of two) beats to a measure"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302114682">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having more than one decidedly dissimilar aspects or qualities; "a double (or dual) role for an actor"; "the office of a clergyman is twofold; public preaching and private influence"- R.W.Emerson; "every episode has its double and treble meaning"-Frederick Harrison</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302115038">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used technically of a device or process) having two parts; "a duplex transaction"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302115173">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(used in combination) multiplied by a specified number; "`fold'' is a combing form in expressions like `a fiftyfold increase''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302115349">
	<b:glossaryEntry>many and varied; having many features or forms; "manifold reasons"; "our manifold failings"; "manifold intelligence"; "the multiplex opportunities in high technology"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302115582">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(prefix) consisting of more than one; "`multi'' is a prefix in words like `multimillionaire'' and `multistory house''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302115749">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having three units or components or elements; "a ternary operation"; "a treble row of red beads"; "overcrowding made triple sessions necessary"; "triple time has three beats per measure"; "triplex windows"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302116036">
	<b:glossaryEntry>three times as great or many; "a claim for treble (or triple) damages"; "a threefold increase"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302116204">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being three in one; used especially of the Christian Trinity; "a triune God"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302116333">
	<b:glossaryEntry>four times as great or many; "a fourfold increase in the dosage"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302116464">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having four units or components; "quadruple rhythm has four beats per measure"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302116625">
	<b:glossaryEntry>consisting of or especially arranged in sets of four; "quaternate leaves"; "a quaternary compound"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302116793"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having five units or components"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302116891"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having six units or components"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302116986"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having seven units or components"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302117085"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having eight units or components"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302117183"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having nine units or components"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302117279"
	 b:glossaryEntry="containing ten or ten parts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302117369">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany; of flowers) having more than the usual number of petals in crowded or overlapping arrangements; "double chrysanthemums have many rows of petals and are usually spherical or hemispherical"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302117618">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany; of flowers) having usually only one row or whorl of petals; "single chrysanthemums resemble daisies and may have more than one row of petals"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302117821">
	<b:glossaryEntry>offering several alternative answers from which the correct one is to be chosen; or consisting of such questions; "multiple-choice questions"; "a multiple-choice test"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302118050">
	<b:glossaryEntry>offering a series of statements each of which is to be judged as true or false; "a true-false test"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302118206">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of roads) having a single lane for traffic in both directions; when vehicles meet one must pull off the road to let the other pass</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302118395"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of roads and highways) having two or more lanes for traffic"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302118583">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a median strip or island between lanes of traffic moving in opposite directions; "a divided highway"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302118756"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a lane for traffic in each direction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302118854">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a lane for traffic in each direction and a center lane for passing</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302118984"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having two lanes for traffic in each direction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302119086"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a specified size"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302119269"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used of nail size; 2 1/2 in or 6.4 cm long"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302119368"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used of nail size; 1 3/8 in or 3.8 cm long"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302119466"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used of nail size; 2 3/4 in or 7 cm long"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302119562">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used in combination) "the economy-size package"; "average-sized houses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302119685"
	 b:glossaryEntry="arranged according to size"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302119764"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used of nail size; 1 1/8 in long"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302119853"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not fashioned to sizes; "unsized gloves; one size fits all"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302119984"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not arranged according to size"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302120069"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having the surface treated or coated with sizing"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302120169">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not having the surface treated or coated with sizing; "unsized paper"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302120292">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or showing or requiring special skill; "only the most skilled gymnasts make an Olympic team"; "a skilled surgeon has many years of training and experience"; "a skilled reconstruction of her damaged elbow"; "a skilled trade"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302120864">
	<b:glossaryEntry>highly skilled; "an accomplished pianist"; "a complete musician"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302120998">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or showing knowledge and skill and aptitude; "adept in handicrafts"; "an adept juggler"; "an expert job"; "a good mechanic"; "a practiced marksman"; "a proficient engineer"; "a lesser-known but no less skillful composer"; "the effect was achieved by skillful retouching"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302121389"
	 b:glossaryEntry='expert in skulduggery; "an arch criminal"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302121484">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(baseball or basketball or football) used of a player skilled in stealing the ball or robbing a batter of a hit; "a ball-hawking center fielder"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302121687">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or revealing supreme mastery or skill; "a consummate artist"; "consummate skill"; "a masterful speaker"; "masterful technique"; "a masterly performance of the sonata"; "a virtuoso performance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302121980">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(informal) marked by great skill or facility; "cool maneuvers on the parallel bars"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302122114">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[marked by great skill especially in meticulous technique; "a surgeon''s delicate touch"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302122255">
	<b:glossaryEntry>superior in skill or ability or accomplishment; "a fine violinist"; "a fine athlete"; "a fine mind"; "the scupture showed the fine hand of a master"; "a fine performance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302122476">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(informal) performed or performing with unusually great skill and daring and energy; "a hot drummer"; "he''s hot tonight"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302122646">
	<b:glossaryEntry>highly skilled or proficient" "a master plumber"; "a master thief"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302122768"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(slang) excellent; "famous for a mean backhand"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302122866">
	<b:glossaryEntry>proficient and confident in performance; "promising playwrights...sure-handed enough to turn out top-drawer scripts"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302123040">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having acquired necessary skills by e.g. undergoing a course of study; "a trained nurse"; "a trained voice"; "trained manpower"; "psychologically trained workers"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302123256">
	<b:glossaryEntry>competent in many areas and able to turn with ease from one thing to another; "a versatile writer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302123410">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not having or showing or requiring special skill or proficiency; "unskilled in the art of rhetoric"; "an enthusiastic but unskillful mountain climber"; "unskilled labor"; "workers in unskilled occupations are finding fewer and fewer job opportunities"; "unskilled workmanship"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302123958"
	 b:glossaryEntry='showing lack of art; "an artless translation"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302124057">
	<b:glossaryEntry>poorly done; "a botchy piece of work"; "it was an unskillful attempt"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302124204"
	 b:glossaryEntry='spoiled through incompetence or clumsiness; "a bungled job"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302124327">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing lack of skill or aptitude; "a bungling workman"; "did a clumsy job"; "his fumbling attempt to put up a shelf"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302124533">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not carefully or expertly made; "managed to make a crude splint"; "a crude cabin of logs with bark still on them"; "rough carpentry"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302124725">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[of a person unable to do something skillfully; "I''m hopeless at mathematics"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302124856"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used of unskilled work (especially domestic work)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302124975"
	 b:glossaryEntry='clumsy and unskilled; "a big stupid lubberly fellow"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302125082"
	 b:glossaryEntry="impaired in skill by neglect"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302125183">
	<b:glossaryEntry>possessing or requiring limited skills; "semiskilled dockworkers"; "a semiskilled job"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302125330">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relating to or having facility in the use of words; "a good poet is a verbal artist"; "a merely verbal writer who sacrifices content to sound"; "verbal aptitude"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302125544">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relating to or having ability to think in or work with numbers; "tests for rating numerical aptitude"; "a mathematical whiz"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302125757">
	<b:glossaryEntry>concerned with numbers rather than words; "the numerical part of the test"; "the nonverbal factor"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302125911">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of texture; large-grained or rough to the touch; "coarse meal"; "coarse sand"; "a coarse weave"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302126167"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not having a fine texture; "coarse-grained wood"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302126276">
	<b:glossaryEntry>composed of or covered with relatively large particles; "granular sugar"; "gritty sand"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302126495"
	 b:glossaryEntry='made grainy or formed into granules; "granulated sugar"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302126607">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of textures) full of small openings or gaps; "an open texture"; "a loose weave"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302126746">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of texture; being small-grained or smooth to the touch or having fine particles; "wood with a fine grain"; "fine powdery snow"; "fine rain"; "batiste is a cotton fabric with a fine weave"; "covered with a fine film of dust"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302127164">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of textiles; "a close weave"; "smooth percale with a very tight weave"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302127294">
	<b:glossaryEntry>dense or compact in structure or texture, as a wood composed of small-diameter cells; "fine-grained birch"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302127475"
	 b:glossaryEntry='resembling flour in fine powdery texture; "a floury clay"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302127585">
	<b:glossaryEntry>consisting of fine particles; "powdered cellulose"; "powdery snow"; "pulverized sugar is prepared from granulated sugar by grinding"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302127826">
	<b:glossaryEntry>have fine or very small constituent particles; "a small misty rain"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302127945">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of extremely fine size or texture; "superfine sugar"; "a superfine file"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302128073">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by or emitting or filled with smoke; "smoky rafters"; "smoky chimneys"; "a smoky fireplace"; "a smoky corridor"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302128297"
	 b:glossaryEntry='darkened by smoke; "blackened rafters"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302128391"
	 b:glossaryEntry='emitting smoke in great volume; "a smoking fireplace"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302128498"
	 b:glossaryEntry='containing smoke; "smoke-filled rooms"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302128595">
	<b:glossaryEntry>emitting or containing little or no smoke; "smokeless factory stacks"; "smokeless fuel"; "a smokeless environment"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302128783"
	 b:glossaryEntry='free of smoke; "smoke-free offices and restaurants"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302128891">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being such as to cause things to slip or slide; "slippery sidewalks"; "a slippery bar of soap"; "the streets are still slippy from the rain"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302129239">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a smooth or slippery quality; "the skin of cephalopods is thin and lubricious"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302129381">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of surfaces especially of cooking utensils) permitting easy removal of adherent food particles; "a frying pan with a nonstick surface"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302129571">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[made slick by e.g. ice or grease; "sidewalks slick with ice"; "roads are slickest when rain has just started and hasn''t had time to wash away the oil"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302129773"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being a smooth continuous motion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302129859">
	<b:glossaryEntry>covered with or resembling slime; "a slimy substance covered the rocks"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302129991">
	<b:glossaryEntry>moving as on a slippery surface; "his slipping and slithering progress over the ice"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302130143">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a slippery surface or quality ; "slithery mud"; "slithery eels"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302130268"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not slippery; not likely to slip or skid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302130420"
	 b:glossaryEntry='designed to reduce or prevent skidding; "nonskid tires"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302130529"
	 b:glossaryEntry='designed to reduce or prevent slipping; "a nonslip surface"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302130642"
	 b:glossaryEntry="smeared with oil or grease to reduce friction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302130754"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not lubricated"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302130839">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a surface free from roughness or bumps or ridges or irregularities; "smooth skin"; "a smooth tabletop"; "smooth fabric"; "a smooth road"; "water as smooth as a mirror"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302131300"
	 b:glossaryEntry='used especially of fabrics; "uncreased trousers"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302131417">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of surfaces) conducive to rapid speeds; "a fast road"; "grass courts are faster than clay"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302131559">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resembling glass in smoothness and shininess and slickness; "the glassy surface of the lake"; "the pavement was...glassy with water"- Willa Cather</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302131758">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a smooth, gleaming surface; "glossy auburn hair"; "satiny gardenia petals"; "sleek black fur"; "silken eyelashes"; "silky skin"; "a silklike fabric"; "slick seals and otters"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302132056"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having little or no hair; "his smooth hairless young face"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302132169">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resembling marble in smoothness; "skin of marmoreal smoothness"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302132300">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used especially of skin; "his cheeks were unlined"; "his unseamed face"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302132447"
	 b:glossaryEntry="smooth and soft to sight or hearing or touch or taste"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302132563">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resembling or covered with wax; "as shiny as a waxy pippin"; "the ceraceous surface of the leaves"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302132734">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or caused by an irregular surface; "trees with rough bark"; "rough ground"; "a rough ride"; "rough skin"; "rough blankets"; "his unsmooth face"; "unsmooth writing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302133561"
	 b:glossaryEntry="causing abrasion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302133643"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of paint or varnish; having the appearance of alligator hide"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302133771">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[resembling the rough bark of a tree; "the rattlesnake''s barky scales"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302133892">
	<b:glossaryEntry>topographically very uneven; "broken terrain"; "rugged ground"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302134016"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(botany) of leaves; appearing puckered as if blistered"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302134124"
	 b:glossaryEntry='characterized by rough motion; "a bumpy ride"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302134249">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used of skin roughened as a result of cold or exposure; "chapped lips"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302134395"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of textiles; having parallel raised lines"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302134515">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of roads) made of logs laid down crosswise; "a corduroy road"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302134644"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of the surface of shells) having a rough, rib-like texture"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302134766"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having hills and crags; "hilly terrain"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302134890">
	<b:glossaryEntry>treated so as to have a permanently wrinkled appearance; "crushed velvet"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302135017">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of textiles; having a rough surface; "a sweater knitted of nubbly homespun yarns"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302135192">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) used especially of leaves or bracts; overlapping or layered as scales or shingles</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302135351"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(biology) rough to the touch; covered with scales or scurf"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302135502"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(botany) covered with tiny scales"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302135592">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used especially of skin) "their lined faces were immeasurably sad"; "a seamed face"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302135737"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used of paved surfaces having holes or pits"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302135857"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used especially of fabrics; having puckers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302135954">
	<b:glossaryEntry>abounding in rocks or stones; "rocky fields"; "stony ground"; "bouldery beaches"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302136117"
	 b:glossaryEntry="somewhat rough"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302136186"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(botany) of leaves; ridged or wrinkled"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302136277"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having the abrasive texture of sandpaper"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302136374"
	 b:glossaryEntry="covered with scabs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302136445">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a very rough nap or covered with hanging shags; "junipers with shagged trunks"; "shaggy rugs"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302136608">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having surface roughness; "a textured wall of stucco"; "a rough-textured tweed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302136759">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of skin) covered with warts or projections that resemble warts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302136897">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having long narrow shallow depressions (as grooves or wrinkles) in the surface; "furrowed fields"; "his furrowed face lit by a warming smile"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302137183">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having thin parallel channels; "canaliculate leafstalks of certain plants"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302137316">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shaped into alternating parallel grooves and ridges; "the surface of the ocean was rippled and corrugated"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302137479"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having grooves or tracks cut or incised; "grooved records"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302137591"
	 b:glossaryEntry='full of ruts; "rutty farm roads"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302137684">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked with wrinkles or furrows; "her ancient wrinkled cheeks"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302137811">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not marked with shallow depressions or furrows; "an unfurrowed field"; "unfurrowed cheeks"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302137976"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not marked with wrinkles; "unwrinkled cheeks"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302138087">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) of the margin of a leaf shape; not broken up into teeth</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302138330">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of leaves or petals) having a smooth edge; not broken up into teeth or lobes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302138460"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a slightly undulating margin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302138548">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a strongly waved margin alternately concave and convex</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302138663"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a sinuate margin and rippled surface"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302138761"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having no notches"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302138846">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) of the margin of a leaf shape; having the edge cut or fringed or scalloped</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302139359"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having sharp angles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302139433"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having toothlike projections that are themselves toothed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302139545"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having double saw-teeth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302139624"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a margin or fringe of hairlike projections"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302139738"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a margin with rounded scallops"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302139841"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having a margin with small rounded teeth; "a crenulate leaf"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302139970"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wavy or notched and curled very irregularly"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302140068"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having toothlike projections in the margin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302140164"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a very finely toothed margin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302140257"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a notched tip"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302140334">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having an irregularly notched or toothed margin as though gnawed</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302140487"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a fringe of slender processes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302140579">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having edges irregularly and finely slashed; "a lacinate leaf"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302140706"
	 b:glossaryEntry='irregularly slashed and jagged as if torn; "lacerate leaves"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302140821"
	 b:glossaryEntry="like a comb"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302140888">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having incised margins with the lobes or teeth pointing toward the base; as dandelion leaves</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302141036"
	 b:glossaryEntry="notched like a saw with teeth pointing toward the apex"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302141189"
	 b:glossaryEntry="minutely serrated"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302141262"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having spines"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302141329">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a firearm; having rifling or internal spiral grooves inside the barrel</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302141455">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a firearm; not having rifling or internal spiral grooves inside the barrel</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302141600">
	<b:glossaryEntry>living together or enjoying life in communities or organized groups; "human beings are social animals"; "spent a relaxed social evening"; "immature social behavior"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302141943">
	<b:glossaryEntry>denoting or deriving from or distinctive of the ways of living built up by a group of people; "influenced by ethnic and cultural ties"- J.F.Kennedy; "ethnic food"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302142169">
	<b:glossaryEntry>seeking and enjoying the company of others; "a gregarious person who avoids solitude"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302142311">
	<b:glossaryEntry>occurring among or involving several people; "interpersonal situations in which speech occurs"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302142465">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[not seeking or given to association; being or living without companions; "the unsocial disposition to neglect one''s neighbors"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302142808">
	<b:glossaryEntry>isolated from others; "could be alone in a crowded room"; "was alone with her thoughts"; "I want to be alone"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302142972">
	<b:glossaryEntry>unwilling or unable to conform to normal standards of social behavior; "criminal behavior or conduct that violates the rights of other individuals is antisocial"; "crimes...and other asocial behavior"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302143239">
	<b:glossaryEntry>given to avoiding association with others; "bears are asocial secretive animals"; "are you asocial or do you just enjoy living in the Antarctic?"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302143438">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by or preferring solitude in mode of life; "the eremitic element in the life of a religious colony"; "a lone wolf"; "a man of a solitary disposition"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302143677">
	<b:glossaryEntry>enjoyed or performed alone; "a lonely existence"; "his lonely room"; "took a solitary walk"; "enjoyed her solitary dinner"; "solitary pursuits such as reading"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302143903">
	<b:glossaryEntry>withdrawn from society; seeking solitude; "lived an unsocial reclusive life"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302144057">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having accompaniment or companions or escort; "there were lone gentlemen and gentlemen accompanied by their wives"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302144240">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without accompaniment or companions; "the soloist sang unaccompanied"; "it had dramatic energy unaccompanied by an adequate sense of dramatic form"; "unaccompanied women were excluded"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302144538">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking companions or companionship; "he was alone when we met him"; "she is alone much of the time"; "the lone skier on the mountain"; "a lonely fisherman stood on a tuft of gravel"; "a lonely soul"; "a solitary traveler"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302144848">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cut off or left behind; "an isolated pawn"; "several stranded fish in a tide pool"; "travelers marooned by the blizzard"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302145045">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking accompaniment or a guard or escort; "unattended women"; "problems unattended with danger"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302145199">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tending to form a group with others of the same kind; "gregarious bird species"; "man is a gregarious animal"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302145455"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of plants) growing in dense clumps or tufts"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302145577"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of plants) growing close together but not in dense mats"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302145689">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of birds and animals) tending to move or live together in groups or colonies of the same kind; "ants are social insects"; "the herding instinct in sheep or cattle"; "swarming behavior in bees"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302145962"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not gregarious"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302146089">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of plants and animals; not growing or living in groups or colonies; "solitary bees"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302146255"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having or joined by a seam or seams"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302146361"
	 b:glossaryEntry="fastened with stitches"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302146453">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not having or joined by a seam or seams; "seamless stockings"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302146623"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(of rugs or carpets) woven full width; "a broadloom rug"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302146735">
	<b:glossaryEntry>knitted in tubular form; "circular-knit sweaters without seams"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302146858"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having no seams; "an unseamed garment made of pastic"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302146966">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of the more skilled contestants) selectively arranged in the draw for position a tournament so that they meet each other in later rounds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302147156"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not seeded; used of players of lesser skill"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302147254"
	 b:glossaryEntry='full of seeds; "as seedy as a fig"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302147376">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or supplied with seeds; "a seeded breadfruit"; "seeded rolls"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302147497">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(combining form) having seeds as specified; "many-seeded"; "black-seeded"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302147623"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lacking seeds; "seedless grapefruit"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302147750"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having the seeds extracted; "seeded raisins"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302147847">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of fruits having stones) having the stone removed; "stoneless dried dates"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302147978">
	<b:glossaryEntry>provided with shutters or shutters as specified; often used in combination; "a church with a shuttered belfry and spire"; "green-shuttered cottages"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302148200"
	 b:glossaryEntry="with shutters closed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302148273">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not provided with shutters or having the shuttered open; "unshuttered windows"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302148409">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made with sleeves or sleeves especially as specified; often used in combination; "sleeved garments"; "short-sleeved"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302148579"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having no sleeves; "sleeveless summer dresses"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302148682">
	<b:glossaryEntry>inclined to or conducive to companionship with others; "a sociable occasion"; "enjoyed a sociable chat"; "a sociable conversation"; "Americans are sociable and gregarious"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302149088"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(informal) inclined to club together; "a clubbable man"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302149210">
	<b:glossaryEntry>effusively sociable; "a clubbish set"; "we got rather clubby"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302149335"
	 b:glossaryEntry='suggestive of companionship; "a companionable pet"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302149445">
	<b:glossaryEntry>occupied with or fond of the pleasures of good company; "a convivial atmosphere at the reunion"; "a woman of convivial nature"; "he was a real good-time Charlie"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302149674"
	 b:glossaryEntry="at ease in talking to others"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302149785">
	<b:glossaryEntry>composed of sociable people or formed for the purpose of sociability; "a purely social club"; "the church has a large social hall"; "a social director"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302149989">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not inclined to society or companionship; "an unsociable nature...shy and reserved"; "generally unsociable except with intimate friends"; "unsociable behavior"; "an unsociable neighborhood"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302150325">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[shunning contact with others; "standoffish and antisocial"; "he''s not antisocial; just shy"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302150473"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not disposed to seek company; "a lonely ungregarious person"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302150592"
	 b:glossaryEntry='disposed of to a purchaser; "this merchandise is sold"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302150733">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sold in excess of available supply especially season tickets; "the opera season was oversubscribed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302150893">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sold completely in advance; "had a sold-out house for both performances"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302151020">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not disposed of by purchase; "the house has been on the market almost a year and is still unsold"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302151170">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a sole or soles especially as specified; used in combination; "half-soled"; "rubber-soled"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302151319"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having no sole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302151388">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of definite shape and volume; firm; neither liquid nor gaseous; "ice is water in the solid state"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302151735"
	 b:glossaryEntry="changed into a solid mass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302151830"
	 b:glossaryEntry="formed by the coalescence of particles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302151923"
	 b:glossaryEntry="congealed into jelly"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302152018">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used of solid substances in contrast with liquid ones; "dry weight"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302152135">
	<b:glossaryEntry>partly solid; having a rigidity and viscosity intermediate between a solid and a liquid; "a semisolid mixture"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302152301">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(electronics) consisting of semiconductor materials and components and related devices</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302152445">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characteristic of or relating to the physical properties of solid materials especially electromagnetic or thermodynamic or structural properties of crystalline solids</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302152669">
	<b:glossaryEntry>existing as or have characteristics of a liquid; especially tending to flow; "water and milk and blood are liquid substances"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302152973">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characteristic of a fluid; capable of flowing and easily changing shape</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302153104"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of being liquefied"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302153202"
	 b:glossaryEntry='reduced to a liquid state; "liquefied petroleum gas"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302153322"
	 b:glossaryEntry="somewhat liquid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302153394">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relating to or resembling or consisting of water; "a watery grave"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302153513">
	<b:glossaryEntry>existing as or having characteristics of a gas; "steam is water is the gaseous state"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302153814"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resembling air or having the form of air"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302153919">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in the form of ultramicroscopic solid or liquid particles dispersed or suspended in air or gas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302154071">
	<b:glossaryEntry>drawn off in the form of vapor; "evaporated molecules boil off"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302154191"
	 b:glossaryEntry="relating to or containing gas bubbles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302154289"
	 b:glossaryEntry="converted into a gas or vapor"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302154399"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resembling gass"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302154466"
	 b:glossaryEntry='resembling or characteristic of vapor; "vaporous clouds"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302154637">
	<b:glossaryEntry>entirely of one substance with no holes inside; "solid silver"; "a solid block of wood"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302154830"
	 b:glossaryEntry='being the same substance throughout; "massive silver"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302154937"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not hollowed out"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302155010">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not solid; having a space or gap or cavity; "a hollow wall"; "a hollow tree"; "hollow cheeks"; "his face became gaunter and more hollow with each year"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302155340">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being or suggesting a cavern; "vast cavernous chambers hollowed out of limestone"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302155477">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a sunken area; "hunger gave their faces a sunken look"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302155613"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having nothing inside; "an empty sphere"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302155705"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(pathology) like a reed or tube"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302155815"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having a cavity within; "canoe made of a hollowed log"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302155924"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having hollow tubes (as for the passage of fluids)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302156039">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of a substance) capable of being dissolved in some solvent (usually water)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302156204"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of dissolving"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302156296"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of melting"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302156385"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of a substance) not easily dissolved"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302156496"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not soluble"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302156566">
	<b:glossaryEntry>susceptible of solution or of being solved or explained; "the puzzle is soluble"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302156754"
	 b:glossaryEntry="possible to answer"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302156829"
	 b:glossaryEntry='such problems are perfectly solvable"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302156934">
	<b:glossaryEntry>admitting of no solution or explanation; "an insoluble doubt"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302157087">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not easily solved; "an apparantly insolvable problem"; "public finance...had long presented problems unsolvable or at least unsolved"- C.L.Jones</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302157328">
	<b:glossaryEntry>explained or answered; "mysteries solved and unsolved; problems resolved and unresolved"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302157480">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not solved; "many crimes remain unsolved"; "many problems remain unresolved"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302157624">
	<b:glossaryEntry>quantifier; used with either mass nouns or plural count nouns to indicate an unspecified number or quantity; "have some milk"; "some roses were still blooming"; "having some friends over"; "some apples"; "some paper"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302158002">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[one or some or every or all without specification; "give me any peaches you don''t want"; "not any milk is left"; "any child would know that"; "pick any card"; "any day now"; "cars can be rented at almost any airport"; "at twilight or any other time"; "beyond any doubt"; "need any help we can get"; "give me whatever peaches you don''t want"; "no milk whatsoever is left"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302158449">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used with count nouns) two considered together; the two; "both girls are pretty"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302158584">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used with count nouns) of an indefinite number more than 2 or 3 but not many; "several letters came in the mail"; "several people were injured in the accident"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302158801">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[quantifier; used with either mass nouns or plural count nouns for indicating a complete or almost complete lack or zero quantity of; "we have no bananas"; "no eggs left and no money to buy any"; "have you no decency?"; "did it with no help"; "I''ll get you there in no time"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302159180">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(used with singular count nouns) colloquial for `not a'' or `not one'' or `never a''; "heard nary a sound"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302159337">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having no measurable or otherwise determinable value; "the goal is zero population growth"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302159481">
	<b:glossaryEntry>quantifier; used with either mass or count nouns to indicate the whole number or amount of or every one of a class; "we sat up all night"; "ate all the food"; "all men are mortal"; "all parties are welcome"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302159857">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[all and only; "it''s all the home he ever had"; "it''s all the food he''s going to get"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302159998">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used of count nouns) every one considered individually; "each person is mortal"; "each party is welcome"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302160157">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used of count nouns) each and all of the members of a group considered singly and without exception; "every person is mortal"; "every party is welcome"; "had every hope of success"; "every chance of winning"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302160420"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(used as intensive) every; "every last one of you"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302160530">
	<b:glossaryEntry>each and all of a series of entities or intervals as specified; "every third seat"; "every two hours"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302160686">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or appealing to those having worldly knowledge and refinement and savoir faire; "sophisticated young socialites"; "a sophisticated audience"; "a sophisticated lifestyle"; "a sophisticated book"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302161126">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[very sophisticated especially because of surfeit; versed in the ways of the world; "the blase traveler refers to the ocean he has crossed as `the pond''"; "the benefits of his worldly wisdom"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302161378"
	 b:glossaryEntry='possessing sound knowledge; "well-informed readers"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302161503">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing a high degree of refinement and the assurance that comes from wide social experience; "his polished manner"; "maintained an urbane tone in his letters"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302161745"
	 b:glossaryEntry="experienced in and wise to the ways of the world"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302161852">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[marked by or showing unaffected simplicity and lack of guile or worldly experience; "a teenager''s naive ignorance of life"; "the naive assumption that things can only get better"; "this naive simple creature with wide friendly eyes so eager to believe appearances"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302162355">
	<b:glossaryEntry>exhibiting childlike simplicity and credulity; "childlike trust"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302162497">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing a lack of judgment or experience; "so credulous he believes everything he reads"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302162641">
	<b:glossaryEntry>easily deceived or tricked; "at that early age she had been gullible and in love"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302162798">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking basic knowledge; "how can someone that age be so ignorant?"; "inexperienced and new to the real world"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302162979">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[lacking in sophistication or worldliness; "a child''s innocent stare"; "his ingenuous explanation that he would not have burned the church if he had not thought the bishop was in it"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302163227"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lacking subtlety and insight; "a simple-minded argument"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302163343">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[not wise in the ways of the world; "either too unsophisticated or too honest to promise more than he could deliver"; "this helplessly unworldly woman"- Kate O''Brien]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302163581">
	<b:glossaryEntry>financially secure and safe; "sound investments"; "a sound economy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302163862">
	<b:glossaryEntry>financially sound; "a good investment"; "a secure investment"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302164003"
	 b:glossaryEntry='financially secure and functioning well; "a healthy economy"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302164117">
	<b:glossaryEntry>firm and dependable; subject to little fluctuation; "the economy is stable"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302164245"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not sound financially; "unsound banking practices"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302164511">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[not financially safe or secure; "a bad investment"; "high risk investments"; "anything that promises to pay too much can''t help being risky"; "speculative business enterprises"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302164782"
	 b:glossaryEntry='involving substantial risk; "long odds"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302164872">
	<b:glossaryEntry>outside the bounds of legitimate or ethical business practices; "wildcat currency issued by irresponsible banks"; "wildcat stock speculation"; "a wildcat airline"; "wildcat life insurance schemes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302165122">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in good condition; free from defect or damage or decay; "a sound timber"; "the wall is sound"; "a sound foundation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302165307">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of good quality and condition; solidly built; "a solid foundation"; "several substantial timber buildings"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302165488">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not in good condition; damaged or decayed; "an unsound foundation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302165644"
	 b:glossaryEntry="eaten away as by acid or oxidation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302165733">
	<b:glossaryEntry>damaged by decay; hence unsound and useless; "rotten floor boards"; "rotted beams"; "a decayed foundation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302165911"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of a liquid) giving off bubbles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302166074">
	<b:glossaryEntry>emitting or filled with bubbles as from carbonation or fermentation; "bubbling champagne"; "foamy (or frothy) beer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302166295"
	 b:glossaryEntry="supplied with carbon dioxide"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302166387"
	 b:glossaryEntry="hissing and bubbling"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302166469"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having carbonation (especially artificially carbonated)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302166581"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not effervescent"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302166695"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having lost effervescence; "flat beer"; "a flat cola"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302166799"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not having carbonation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302166896">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used of wines and waters; charged naturally or artificially with carbon dioxide; "sparkling wines"; "sparkling water"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302167084"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not sparkling; "a still wine"; "still mineral water"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302167206">
	<b:glossaryEntry>developed or designed for a special activity or function; "a specialized tool"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302167414">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) exhibiting biological specialization; adapted during development to a specific function or environment</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302167587">
	<b:glossaryEntry>adapted to or reserved for a particular purpose; "a special kind of paint"; "a special medication for arthritis"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302167753"
	 b:glossaryEntry='showing focused training; "specialist training"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302167875">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not specialized or modified for a particular purpose or function</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302168035">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) not biologically differentiated or adapted to a specific function or environment; "the hedgehog is a primitive and generalized mammal"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302168237"
	 b:glossaryEntry="displaying animation, vigor, or liveliness"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302168640">
	<b:glossaryEntry>full of rough and exuberant animal spirits; "boisterous practical jokes"; "knockabout comedy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302168803"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lively and spirited; "a dashing hero"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302168904"
	 b:glossaryEntry="joyously unrestrained"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302169009">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing courage; "the champion is faced with a feisty challenger"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302169145"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used of boisterous girls"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302169249">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by a lightly pert and exuberant quality; "a certain irreverent gaiety and ease of manner"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302169438"
	 b:glossaryEntry='full of zest or vigor; "a racy literary style"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302169544"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a proud and unbroken spirit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302169635"
	 b:glossaryEntry="recovering readily from adversity, depression, or the like"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302169749">
	<b:glossaryEntry>smart and fashionable; "snappy conversation"; "some sharp and whipping lines"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302169890">
	<b:glossaryEntry>full of spirit and vitality; "a sprightly young girl"; "a sprightly dance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302170020">
	<b:glossaryEntry>vigorous and active; "a vibrant group that challenged the system"; "a charming and vivacious hostess"; "a vivacious folk dance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302170213"
	 b:glossaryEntry="marked by spirited enjoyment"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302170312">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking ardor or vigor or energy; "a spiritless reply to criticism"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302170580">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing little or no emotion or animation; "a woman who became active rather than apathetic as she grew older"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302170746">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without vigor or zest or energy; "an insipit and bloodless young man"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302170871">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by low spirits; showing no enthusiasm; "a dispirited and divided Party"; "reacted to the crisis with listless resignation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302171068"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(archaic) devoid of courage or enthusiasm"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302171165"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lacking spirit or sincere effort; "a thin smile"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302171264">
	<b:glossaryEntry>happening or arising without apparent external cause; "spontaneous laughter"; "spontaneous combustion"; "a spontaneous abortion"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302171503">
	<b:glossaryEntry>unthinking; as if prompted by instinct; "an instinctive mistrust of bureaucrats"; "offering to help was as instinctive as breathing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302171693">
	<b:glossaryEntry>proceeding from natural feeling or impulse without external stimulus; "an impulsive gesture of affection"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302171867">
	<b:glossaryEntry>brought about or caused; not spontaneous; "a case of steroid-induced weakness"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302172017">
	<b:glossaryEntry>called forth from a latent or potential state by stimulation; "evoked potentials"; "an elicited response"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302172186">
	<b:glossaryEntry>uttered through the medium of speech or characterized by speech; sometimes used in combination; "a spoken message"; "the spoken language"; "a soft-spoken person"; "sharp-spoken"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302172506"
	 b:glossaryEntry='communicated in words; "frequently uttered sentiments"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302172639">
	<b:glossaryEntry>using speech rather than writing; "an oral tradition"; "an oral agreement</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302172775"
	 b:glossaryEntry='expressed in spoken words; "a verbal contract"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302172874">
	<b:glossaryEntry>expressed orally; "a viva-voce report"; "the film had good word-of-mouth publicity"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302173029">
	<b:glossaryEntry>set down in writing in any of various ways; "written evidence"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302173415">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of handwriting) having the letters slanting from left down to right</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302173554"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having successive letter joined together; "cursive script"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302173666">
	<b:glossaryEntry>written formally in a large clear script, as a deed or other legal document</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302173797"
	 b:glossaryEntry='written or drawn or engraved; "graphic symbols"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302173926"
	 b:glossaryEntry="written by hand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302173999">
	<b:glossaryEntry>written wholly in the handwriting of the signer; "a holographic will"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302174126">
	<b:glossaryEntry>written (by handwriting, printing, engraving, or carving) on or in a surface</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302174258"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having words written out in full by hand; "longhand writing"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302174373">
	<b:glossaryEntry>written in print characters or produced by means of e.g. a printing press</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302174508"
	 b:glossaryEntry='written hastily or carelessly; "his scrawled signiture"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302174618"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(archaic or poetic) copied in handwriting"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302174715"
	 b:glossaryEntry='written in abbreviated or symbolic form; "shorthand notes"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302174829">
	<b:glossaryEntry>taken down in writing especially from notes or dictated or recorded information</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302174966"
	 b:glossaryEntry="written on a typewriter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302175055">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[produced or delivered by the voice; often used in combination; "a frequently voiced opinion"; "voiced consonants such as `b'' and `g'' and `z''"; "loud-voiced teenagers"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302175310">
	<b:glossaryEntry>using the voice in speech; "vocal communication"; "either silent or vocal prayers"; "vocal noises"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302175469"
	 b:glossaryEntry='using the voice in song; "vocal music"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302175566">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[not using the voice; "unvoiced thoughts"; "unvoiced consonants such as `p'' and `k'' and `s'']]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302175759"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not rendered in song; "their dirge was unsung"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302175858">
	<b:glossaryEntry>spoken in soft hushed tones (without vibrations of the vocal cords); "a long whispered conversation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302176014"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(linguistics) of speech sounds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302176106"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(linguistics) of speech sounds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302176210"
	 b:glossaryEntry='systematically collected and written down; "written laws"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302176339">
	<b:glossaryEntry>enacted by a legislative body; "statute law"; "codified written laws"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302176476">
	<b:glossaryEntry>based on custom rather than documentation; "an unwritten law"; "rites...so ancient that they well might have had their unwritten origins in Aurignacian times"- J.L.T.C.Spence</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302176724"
	 b:glossaryEntry='based on common law; "a common-law right"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302176825"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(phonology) of liquids and nasals"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302176923"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(phonology) of liquids and nasals"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302177041">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being or containing or characterized by vowels; "vocalic sounds"; "the Gaelic language being uncommonly vocalic"- Walter Scott</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302177239">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[having characteristics of a vowel sound; "the vowellike nature of `r''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302177365">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being or marked by or containing or functioning as a consonant; "consonantal sounds"; "a consonantal Hebrew text"; "consonantal alliteration"; "a consonantal cluster"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302177589">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being stopped; "if we pick up our pace he may be stoppable"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302177715"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not capable of being stopped; "as unstoppable as the wind"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302177849"
	 b:glossaryEntry='hard to defeat; "an unbeatable ball team"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302177947">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[consisting of a syllable or syllables; constituting a syllable or the nucleus of a syllable; (of a consonant sound) not accompanied in the same syllable by a vowel sound; "the syllabic `l'' in `riddle'' or the syllabic `n'' in `botany'' when pronounced `bot-n-y''"; (of a vowel sound) dominating the other vowel sounds in a syllable as being the first vowel in a falling diphthong; "the syllabic `o'' in `oi''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302178513"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having or characterized by or consisting of two syllables"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302178627"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having or characterized by or consisting of one syllable"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302178742"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having or characterized by or consisting of eight syllables"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302178860"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having or characterized by or consisting of five syllables"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302178978">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or characterized by words of more than three syllables</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302179098"
	 b:glossaryEntry="pronounced in syllables"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302179177">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not forming a syllable or the nucleus of a syllable; (of a consonant sound) accompanied in the same syllable by a vowel sound as the `n'' in `botany'' when pronounced `bot-ny''; (of a vowel sound) dominated by other vowel sounds in a syllable; i.e. being the second vowel in a falling diphthong as the `i'' in `oi''</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302179576"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not articulated in syllables"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302179662">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of verse) having lines based on number of syllables rather than on rhythmical arrangement of stresses or quantities</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302179887"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having or consisting of lines of ten syllables"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302179992">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or consisting of line of eight syllables; "octosyllabic verse"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302180120">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of verse) having a metric system based on stress rather than syllables or quantity; "accentual poetry is based on the number of stresses in a line"; "accentual rhythm"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302180362">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of verse) having a metric system based on relative duration of syllables; "in typical Greek and Latin verse of the classical period the rhymic system is based on some arrangement of long and short elements"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302180646">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resistant to change of position or condition; "a stable ladder"; "a stable peace"; "a stable relationship"; "stable prices"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302180948">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not liable to fluctuate or especially to fall; "stocks are still firm"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302181094"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lasting a long time without change; "a lasting relationship"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302181208"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(chemistry, physics, biology) resistant to change"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302181311"
	 b:glossaryEntry="made stable or firm"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302181387">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking stability or fixity or firmness; "unstable political conditions"; "the tower proved to be unstable in the high wind"; "an unstable world economy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302181829"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being where earthquake waves arrive simultaneously"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302181947"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(used of boats) inclined to heel over easily under sail"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302182080"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lacking order or stability; "these unsettled times"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302182199">
	<b:glossaryEntry>liable to lead to sudden change or violence; "an explosive issue"; "a volatile situation with troops and rioters eager for a confrontation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302182405">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(chemistry, physics, biology) readily undergoing change or breakdown</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302182526">
	<b:glossaryEntry>inclined to shake as from weakness or defect; "a rickety table"; "a wobbly chair with shaky legs"; "the ladder felt a little wobbly"; "the bridge still stands though one of the arches is wonky"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302182798"
	 b:glossaryEntry='liable to rock; "on high rocky heels"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302182887"
	 b:glossaryEntry="subject to or caused by an earthquake or earth vibration"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302183007"
	 b:glossaryEntry="unstable by being overloaded at the top"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302183102">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of structures or institutions) having lost stability; failing or on the point of collapse; "a tottering empire"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302183270">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by or composed of disconnected parts or sounds; (music) cut short crisply; "staccato applause"; "a staccato command"; "staccato notes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302183499">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by sudden changes in subject and sharp transitions; "abrupt prose"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302183640">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(music) without breaks between notes; smooth and connected; "a legato passage"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302183780">
	<b:glossaryEntry>written for or performed on the stage; "a staged version of the novel"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302183903"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not performed on the stage"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302184002"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not performed; "the author of numerous unperformed plays"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302184117">
	<b:glossaryEntry>established or widely recognized as a model of authority or excellence; "a standard reference work"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302184397">
	<b:glossaryEntry>generally accepted or used; "accepted methods of harmony and melody"; "three accepted types of pump"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302184552">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of recognized authority or excellence; "the definitive work on Greece"; "classical methods of navigation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302184742">
	<b:glossaryEntry>reduced to the simplest and most significant form possible without loss of generality; "a basic story line"; "a canonical syllable pattern"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302184955">
	<b:glossaryEntry>according to or characteristic of a casebook or textbook; typical; "a casebook schizophrenic"; "a textbook example"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302185136">
	<b:glossaryEntry>adhering to established standards and principles; "a classic proof"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302185257"
	 b:glossaryEntry="serving as a basis for evaluation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302185360">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not standard; not accepted as a model of excellence; "a nonstandard text"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302185491">
	<b:glossaryEntry>conforming to or constituting a standard of measurement or value; or of the usual or regularized or accepted kind; "windows of standard width"; "standard sizes"; "the standard fixtures"; "standard brands"; "standard operating procedure"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302185926">
	<b:glossaryEntry>constructed with standardized units or dimensions allowing flexibility and variety in use; "modular furniture"; "modular homes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302186107"
	 b:glossaryEntry='dealing with or based on norms; "a normative judgment"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302186217">
	<b:glossaryEntry>conforming to a standard or pattern; "following the regular procedure of the legislature"; "a regular electrical outlet"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302186391">
	<b:glossaryEntry>prescribed by or according to regulation; "regulation army equipment"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302186517">
	<b:glossaryEntry>brought into conformity with a standard; "standardized education"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302186641"
	 b:glossaryEntry='routine; "a stock answer"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302186718">
	<b:glossaryEntry>varying from or not adhering to a standard; "nonstandard windows"; "envelopes of nonstandard sizes"; "nonstandard lengths of board"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302186961"
	 b:glossaryEntry='falling short of some prescribed norm; "substandard housing"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302187102"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not based on a norm"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302187180">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(linguistics) conforming to the established language usage of educated native speakers; "standard English" (American); "received standard English is sometimes called the King''s English" (British)]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302187477">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(linguistics) judged to be in conformity with approved usage; "acceptable English usage"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302187633">
	<b:glossaryEntry>based on or prescribing a norm or standard; "normative grammar"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302187767">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(linguistics) not conforming to the language usage of a prestige group within a community; "a nonstandard dialect is one used by uneducated speakers or socially disfavored groups"; "the common core of nonstandard words and phrases in folk speech"- A.R.Dunlap</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302188119"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(linguistics; informal) "so-called bad grammar"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302188216">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(linguistics) not conforming to standard usage; "the following use of `access'' was judged unacceptable by a panel of linguists; `You can access your cash at any of 300 automatic tellers''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302188475"
	 b:glossaryEntry='consisting of or containing starch; "starchy foods"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302188598"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resembling starch"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302188707"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking starch"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302188778">
	<b:glossaryEntry>abounding with or resembling stars; "a starry night"; "starry eyes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302188952">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shining with brilliant points of light like stars; "sparkling snow"; "sparkling eyes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302189093"
	 b:glossaryEntry='resembling a star; "they saw a starlike object in the sky"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302189206"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lighted only by stars; "the starlit darkness"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302189305">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not starry; having no stars or star-like objects; "dark starless nights"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302189432"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being provided with adequate nourishment"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302189654"
	 b:glossaryEntry='fed on corn; "corn-fed livestock"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302189742">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(informal) having consumed enough food or drink; "a full stomach"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302189871"
	 b:glossaryEntry="properly nourished"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302189961"
	 b:glossaryEntry="too well nourished"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302190033">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of certain animals) nourished on dead or decaying animal matter</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302190168">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of some plants or fungi) nourished on dead or decaying organic matter</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302190296">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of livestock) kept and fed in a stall in order to fatten for the market</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302190424"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not being provided with adequate nourishment"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302190617"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being without food"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302190690">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not getting adequate food; "gaunt underfed children"; "badly undernourished"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302190848"
	 b:glossaryEntry="suffering from lack of food"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302190940"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not fed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302190999"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not nourished"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302191070">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not subject to change or variation especially in behavior; "a steady beat"; "a steady job"; "a steady breeze"; "a steady increase"; "a good steady ballplayer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302191479">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[consistent in performance or behavior; "dependable in one''s habits"; "a steady-going family man"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302191661">
	<b:glossaryEntry>occurring at fixed intervals; "a regular beat"; "the even rhythm of his breathing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302191804">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(of especially a person''s physical features) not shaking or trembling; "his voice was firm and confident"; "a firm step"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302191975">
	<b:glossaryEntry>intent and directed steadily; "had her gaze fastened on the stranger"; "a fixed expresson"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302192128">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not showing abrupt variations; "spoke in a level voice"; "she gave him a level look"- Louis Auchincloss</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302192296">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made steady or constant; "the noise became a steadied roaring"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302192413"
	 b:glossaryEntry='certain not to fail; "a sure hand on the throttle"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302192514"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not liable to stumble or fall; "on surefooted donkeys"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302192636">
	<b:glossaryEntry>subject to change or variation especially in behavior; "her unsteady walk"; "his hand was unsteady as he poured the wine"; "an unsteady voice"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302193139">
	<b:glossaryEntry>affected by involuntary jerky muscular contractions; resembling a spasm; "convulsive motions"; "his body made a spasmodic jerk"; "spastic movements"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302193366"
	 b:glossaryEntry="unsteady in speech or action"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302193450"
	 b:glossaryEntry="shining unsteadily"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302193539"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having unpredictable ups and downs; "fluctuating prices"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302193653">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a slight and rapid trembling motion; "a fluttering heartbeat"; "palpitant movements rather than violent eruptions"; "my palpitating heart"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302193881"
	 b:glossaryEntry="walking unsteadily"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302193989">
	<b:glossaryEntry>affected with palsy or uncontrollable tremor; "hands weak and palsied"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302194113">
	<b:glossaryEntry>vibrating slightly and irregularly; as e.g. with fear or cold or like the leaves of an aspen in a breeze; "a quaking bog"; "the quaking child asked for more"; "quivering leaves of a poplar tree"; "with shaking knees"; "seemed shaky on her feet"; "sparkling light from the shivering crystals of the chandelier"; "trembling hands"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302194549">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(of the voice) quivering as from weakness or fear; "the old lady''s quavering voice"; "spoke timidly in a tremulous voice"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302194738"
	 b:glossaryEntry="shaking convulsively or violently"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302194828">
	<b:glossaryEntry>unsteady in gait as from infirmity or old age; "a tottering skeleton of a horse"; "a tottery old man"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302194995">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of sound) fluctuating unsteadily; "a low-pitched wobbling sound"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302195115">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a stem or stems or having a stem as specified; often used in combination; "stemmed goblets"; "long-stemmed roses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302195289"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not having a stem; "stemless glassware"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302195401"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having the stem removed; "stemmed berries"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302195497">
	<b:glossaryEntry>rousing or quickening activity or the senses; "a stimulating discussion"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302195807">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stimulating interest or thought; "a challenging hypothesis"; "a thought-provoking book"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302195972">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stimulating discussion or exciting controversy; "a provocative novel"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302196110">
	<b:glossaryEntry>engagingly stimulating or provocative; "a piquant wit"; "salty language"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302196244">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of arousing enthusiasm or excitement; "a rousing sermon"; "stirring events such as wars and rescues"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302196417">
	<b:glossaryEntry>causing quivering or shivering as by cold or fear or electric shock; "a thrilling wind blew off the frozen lake"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302196585"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not stimulating"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302196781">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking stimulating characteristics; uninteresting; "a bland little drama"; "a flat joke"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302196929">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking interest or stimulation; dull and lifeless; "a dry book"; "a dry lecture filled with trivial details"; "dull and juiceless as only book knowledge can be when it is unrelated to...life"- John Mason Brown</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302197201">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking significance or liveliness or spirit or zest; "a vapid conversation"; "a vapid smile"; "a bunch of vapid schoolgirls"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302197378">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of depressing physiological or psychological activity or response by a chemical agent</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302197600">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tending to soothe or tranquilize; "valium has a tranquilizing effect"; "took a hot drink with sedative properties before going to bed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302197828"
	 b:glossaryEntry='inducing stupor or narcosis; "narcotic drugs"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302197942">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tending to relax or relieve muscular or nervous tension; "a relaxant drug"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302198071"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sleep inducing"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302198181">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of arousing or accelerating physiological or psychological activity or response by a chemical agent</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302198454"
	 b:glossaryEntry="stimulating or acting on the adrenal cortex"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302198586">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stimulating the central nervous system; "an analeptic drug stimulates the central nervous system"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302198739"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of drugs e.g.) able to excite or stimulate"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302198863">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used of physical stimuli) serving to stimulate or excite; "an irritative agent"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302199013"
	 b:glossaryEntry='that stimulates; "stimulant phenomena"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302199121"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(biology) having a mouth or mouthlike opening"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302199240"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of an opening that resembles a mouth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302199332"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(biology) having no mouth or mouthlike opening"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302199447">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a sting or the capacity to sting; "stinging insects"; "stinging nettles"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302199581"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having no sting; "stingless bees"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302199670">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having no deviations; "straight lines"; "straight roads across the desert"; "straight teeth"; "straight shoulders"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302199947"
	 b:glossaryEntry='in a straight line; "pearly teeth evenly aligned"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302200050">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not bent; "looking for an unbent nail"; "trees with straight unbent trunks make the best lumber"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302200199"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not twisted; formerly twisted but now straight"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302200301">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or marked by bends or angles; not straight or aligned; "crooked country roads"; "crooked teeth"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302200817">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used of arms and legs) bent outward with the joint away from the body; "a tailor sitting with legs akimbo"; "stood with arms akimbo"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302201007"
	 b:glossaryEntry='full of twists and turns; "anfractuous cliffs"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302201111"
	 b:glossaryEntry="curved down like an eagle''s beak"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302201207">
	<b:glossaryEntry>turned or twisted toward one side; "a...youth with a gorgeous red necktie all awry"- G.K.Chesterton</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302201398">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not remaining rigid or straight; "tried to support his weight on a bending cane"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302201532"
	 b:glossaryEntry='altered from an originally straight condition; "a bent wire"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302201643">
	<b:glossaryEntry>strained or wrenched out of normal shape; "old trees with contorted branches"; "scorched and distorted fragments of steel"; "trapped under twisted steel girders"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302201882">
	<b:glossaryEntry>twisted (especially as in pain or struggle); "his mad contorted smile"; "writhed lips"; "my writhen features"- Walter scott</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302202081"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of changing the direction (of a light or sound wave)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302202211">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used of old persons or old trees; covered with knobs or knots; "gnarled and knotted hands"; "a knobbed stick"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302202412"
	 b:glossaryEntry='characterized by malposition; "crooked malposed teeth"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302202521"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wavy and twisting"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302202593">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by repeated turns and bends; "a tortuous road up the mountain"; "winding roads are full of surprises"; "had to steer the car down a twisty track"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302202830">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used especially of timbers or boards; bent out of shape usually by moisture; "the floors were warped and cracked"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302202996">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used especially of trees; growing in a shape determined by the prevailing winds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302203131"
	 b:glossaryEntry='bent to one side; "a wry neck"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302203211"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having short sharp turns or angles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302203298"
	 b:glossaryEntry="without curves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302203403">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(architecture) not arcuate; having straight horizontal beams or lintels (rather than arches)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302203562"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having no curves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302203645">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not straight; having or marked by a curve or smoothly rounded bend; "the curved tusks of a walrus"; "his curved lips suggested a smile but his eyes were hard"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302204101">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(architecture) forming or resembling an arch; "an arched ceiling"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302204266">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by or following a curved line; "curvilinear tracery"; "curvilinear motion"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302204426"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resembling an eel in being long and thin and sinuous"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302204532">
	<b:glossaryEntry>curved like a sickle; "a falcate leaf"; "falcate claws"; "the falcate moon"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302204677"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having curves; "a settee with only one curvy end"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302204787"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having turns or windings; "the flexuous bed of the stream"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302204900"
	 b:glossaryEntry="bent into or having an inward curve"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302205002"
	 b:glossaryEntry="curved backward or inward"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302205094"
	 b:glossaryEntry="curved into a half circle"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302205178">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resembling a serpent in form; "a serpentine wall"; "snaky ridges in the sand"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302205320"
	 b:glossaryEntry='curved or curving in and out; "wiggly lines"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302205437"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a succession of waves or curves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302205532"
	 b:glossaryEntry="curving upward"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302205601">
	<b:glossaryEntry>curled or wound (especially in concentric rings or spirals); "a coiled snake ready to strike"; "the rope lay coiled on the deck"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302205944"
	 b:glossaryEntry="in the shape of a coil"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302206092">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) rolled longitudinally upon itself:" a convolute petal"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302206211"
	 b:glossaryEntry='rolled or coiled together; "a convoluted shell"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302206315"
	 b:glossaryEntry='drawn up into a ball; "she lay curled up in a big armchair"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302206439"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of some shells) closely coiled so that the axis is obscured"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302206554">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) especially of petals or leaves in bud; having margins rolled inward</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302206694"
	 b:glossaryEntry="like the pattern basic to the human fingerprint"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302206804">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wound or wrapped around something; "hair twined around her fingers"; "bulky with twisted stitches around the edges"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302206982"
	 b:glossaryEntry="put in a coil"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302207047"
	 b:glossaryEntry="no longer coiled"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302207165"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not curled; "lay uncurled on the bed"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302207257"
	 b:glossaryEntry='no longer wound; "the thread has come unwound"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302207357">
	<b:glossaryEntry>honest and morally upright; "I just want a straight answer to the question"; "straight dealing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302207615">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without concealment or deception; honest; "their business was open and aboveboard"; "straightforward in all his business affairs"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302207819"
	 b:glossaryEntry="free of deceit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302207903"
	 b:glossaryEntry="conventionally moral and upright"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302207998"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not straight; dishonest or immoral or evasive"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302208197"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking honesty and oblivious to what is honorable"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302208318">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by deception; "achieved success in business only by underhand methods"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302208474">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[bearing a stress or accent; "an iambic foot consists of an unstressed syllable followed by a stressed syllable as in `delay''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302208726"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used of syllables"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302208815"
	 b:glossaryEntry='spoken with emphasis; "an emphatic word"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302208923">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(prosody) used of syllables that are unaccented or of relatively long duration</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302209052">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(music or poetry) ending on an accented beat or syllable; "a masculine cadence"; "the masculine rhyme of `annoy, enjoy''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302209228">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not bearing a stress or accent; "short vowels are unstressed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302209431">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(music or poetry) ending on an unaccented beat or syllable; "a feminine ending"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302209565">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(prosody) used of syllables that are unaccented or of relatively brief duration</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302209696">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used of vowels or syllables; pronounced with little or no stress; "a syllable that ends in a short vowel is a light syllable"; "a weak stress on the second syllable"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302209933"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not emphasized"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302210004">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used of syllables; "a tonic syllables carries the main stress in a word"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302210139"
	 b:glossaryEntry='used of syllables; "an atonic syllable carries no stress"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302210262">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having strength or power greater than average or expected; "a strong radio signal"; "strong medicine"; "a strong man"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302210810">
	<b:glossaryEntry>with full strength; "his beardown performance in the exhibition game"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302210937"
	 b:glossaryEntry='made greater or stronger; "beefed-up sales efforts"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302211044">
	<b:glossaryEntry>possessing physical strength and weight; rugged and powerful; "a hefty athlete"; "a muscular boxer"; "powerful arms"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302211243"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a thick short powerful neck"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302211334">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resembling a bullock in strength and power; "thick bullocky shoulders</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302211458">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very strong or vigorous; "strong winds"; "a hard left to the chin"; "a knockout punch"; "a severe blow"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302211632">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of heavy boots; set with or having the soles protected with hobnails; "hobnailed boots"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302211785">
	<b:glossaryEntry>exhibiting strength or hardness like that of iron; "ironlike determination"; "ironlike nerves"; "ironlike discipline of the Marines"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302211972">
	<b:glossaryEntry>strong enough to knock down or overwhelm; "a knock-down blow"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302212104"
	 b:glossaryEntry='readily noticed; "a noticeable resemblance"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302212204">
	<b:glossaryEntry>given added strength or support; "reinforced concrete contains steel bars or metal netting"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302212367">
	<b:glossaryEntry>strong enough to withstand intellectual challenge; "the experiment yielded robust results"; "a robust faith"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302212528">
	<b:glossaryEntry>powerful; "a stiff current"; "a stiff breeze"; "a stiff drink"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302212642">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by great force or energy; "vehement deluges of rain"; "vehement clapping"; "a vehement defense"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302212806">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by energy and vigor; "a virile and ever stronger free society"; "a new and virile leadership"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302212966">
	<b:glossaryEntry>strongly and firmly constructed; "a well-knit argument"; "a well-knit theatrical production"; "well-knit athletes"; "a sailor short but well-set"- Alexander Hamilton</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302213198">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having little physical or spiritual strength; "a weak radio signal"; "a weak link"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302213673">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking vigor or energy; "an anemic attempt to hit the baseball"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302213800"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking strength or vigor"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302213917">
	<b:glossaryEntry>designed to break or tear or come apart easily; "breakaway highway signposts"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302214050">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking strength or vigor; "damning with faint praise"; "faint resistance"; "feeble efforts"; "a feeble voice"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302214221">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pathetically lacking in force or effectiveness; "a feeble excuse"; "a lame argument"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302214365">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking in strength or firmness or resilience; "flaccid muscles"; "took his lax hand in hers"; "gave a limp handshake"; "a limp gesture as if waving away all desire to know" G.K.Chesterton; "a slack grip"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302214644">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having little substance or significance; "a flimsy excuse"; "slight evidence"; "a tenuous argument"; "a thin plot"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302214837">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking solidity or strength; "a flimsy table"; "flimsy construction"; "a fragile link with the past"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302215001">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of inferior workmanship and materials; "mean little jerry-built houses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302215139"
	 b:glossaryEntry="weak in willpower"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302215240">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of light) lacking in intensity or brightness; dim or feeble; "the pale light of a half moon"; "a pale sun"; "the late afternoon light coming through the el tracks fell in pale oblongs on the street"; "a pallid sky"; "the pale (or wan) stars"; "the wan light of dawn"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302215573">
	<b:glossaryEntry>inferior in strength or significance; "a puny physique"; "puny excuses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302215695">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being wounded or hurt; "vulnerable parts of the body"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302215816">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking strength or vigor; "a washy handshake"; "washy prose"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302215929"
	 b:glossaryEntry="made weak or weaker"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302216003">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tenaciously unwilling or marked by tenacious unwillingness to yield</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302216323">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) stubbornly obstructive and unwilling to cooperate; "unions...have never been as bloody-minded about demarcation as the shipbuilders"- Spectator</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302216551"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(British slang) obstreperous"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302216642"
	 b:glossaryEntry="obstinate and stupid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302216747">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stubbornly unyielding; "dogged persistence"; "dour determination"; "the most vocal and pertinacious of all the critics"; "a mind not gifted to discover truth but tenacious to hold it"- T.S.Eliot; "men tenacious of opinion"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302217079"
	 b:glossaryEntry="difficult to deal with"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302217175">
	<b:glossaryEntry>devoting full strength and concentrated attention to; "made continued and determined efforts to find and destroy enemy headquarters"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302217364">
	<b:glossaryEntry>unreasonably rigid in the face of argument or entreaty or attack</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302217494"
	 b:glossaryEntry='haughtily stubborn; "a stiff-necked old Boston Brahmin"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302217608"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a determined will"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302217708">
	<b:glossaryEntry>willing to be taught or led or supervised or directed; "the docile masses of an enslaved nation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302217947">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very docile; "tame obedience"; "meek as a mouse"- Langston Hughes</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302218070">
	<b:glossaryEntry>like or suggestive of a sheep in docility or stupidity or meekness or timidity</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302218215">
	<b:glossaryEntry>inclined to yield to argument or influence or control; "a timid yielding person"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302218350">
	<b:glossaryEntry>subject or submissive to authority or the control of another; "a subordinate kingdom"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302218601"
	 b:glossaryEntry='subject to a ruling authority; "the ruled mass"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302218700">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being under the power or sovereignty of another or others; "subject peoples"; "a dependent prince"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302218864">
	<b:glossaryEntry>compliant and obedient to authority; "editors and journalists who express opinions in print that are opposed to the interests of the rich are dismissed and replaced by subservient ones"-G. B. Shaw</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302219118"
	 b:glossaryEntry="willing to submit without resistance to authority; deferent"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302219234">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not submissive to authority; "a history of insubordinate behavior"; "insubordinate boys"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302219472">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wilfully obstinate; stubbornly disobedient; "a contumaceous witness is subject to punishment"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302219624"
	 b:glossaryEntry='unwilling to submit to authority; "unruly teenagers"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302219743">
	<b:glossaryEntry>disposed to or in a state of mutiny; "the men became mutinous and insubordinate"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302219878">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resisting control or authority; "temperamentally rebellious"; "a rebellious crew"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302220016">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having succeeded or being marked by a favorable outcome; "a successful architect"; "a successful business venture"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302220457">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very lively and profitable; "flourishing businesses"; "a palmy time for stockbrokers"; "a prosperous new business"; "doing a roaring trade"; "a thriving tourist center"; "did a thriving business in orchids"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302220786"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having achieved eminence; "an eminent physician"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302220888"
	 b:glossaryEntry='holding office; "the in party"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302220970">
	<b:glossaryEntry>successful or assured of success; "now I am a made man forever"- Christopher Marlowe</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302221105">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(baseball) of a game (or the pitching) in which a pitcher allows the opponent no hits; "a no-hit pitcher"; "a no-hit game"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302221280"
	 b:glossaryEntry="yielding positive results"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302221362">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having achieved success or recognition by your own efforts; "a self-made millionaire"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302221503">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(baseball) unofficial championship title for player who heads the league in batting average and home runs and runs batted in</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302221686">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(horse racing) of a horse that has won the Kentucky Derby and the Belmont Stakes and the Preakness Stakes races</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302221856"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having won; "the victorious entry"; "the winning team"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302221977"
	 b:glossaryEntry='bringing success; "the winning run"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302222066">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not successful; having failed or having an unfavorable outcome</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302222511"
	 b:glossaryEntry='tried unsuccessfully; "attempted murder"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302222607">
	<b:glossaryEntry>disappointingly unsuccessful; "disappointed expectations and thwarted ambitions"; "their foiled attempt to capture Calais"; "many frustrated poets end as pipe-smoking teachers"; "his best efforts were thwarted"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302222934"
	 b:glossaryEntry="doomed to extinction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302223049"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking resources (or any prospect of resources)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302223156">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having acquired or gained nothing; "the returned from the negotiations empty-handed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302223312">
	<b:glossaryEntry>unable to meet financial obligations; "a failing business venture"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302223441"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(of a batter) "he went hitless for three innings"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302223544"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not victorious; "on the losing team"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302223636">
	<b:glossaryEntry>out of power; especially having been unsuccessful in an election; "now the Democrats are out"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302223782"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having no points scores; "a scoreless inning"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302223904">
	<b:glossaryEntry>acting to defeat its own purpose; "it is self-defeating...to ignore the progress of events"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302224056">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of persons; marked by failure to realize full potentialities; "unfulfilled and uneasy men"; "unrealized dreams and ambitions"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302224252"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not one of the first three in a race or competition"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302224358">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having no wins; "the team had a very disappointing winless season"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302224478">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a quantity that can fulfill a need or requirement but without being abundant; "sufficient food"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302224723">
	<b:glossaryEntry>enough to meet a purpose; "an adequate income"; "the food was adequate"; "a decent wage"; "enough food"; "food enough"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302224914">
	<b:glossaryEntry>just sufficient; "the library had a spare but efficient look"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302225027"
	 b:glossaryEntry='sufficient to provide comfort; "a comfortable salary"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302225138">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a quantity not able to fulfill a need or requirement; "insufficient funds"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302225466">
	<b:glossaryEntry>no longer sufficient; "supplies are low"; "our funds are depleted"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302225593">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not sufficient to meet a need; "an inadequate income"; "a poor salary"; "money is short"; "on short rations"; "food is in short supply"; "short on experience"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302225823"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(metallurgy) low in mineral content; "a lean ore"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302225923">
	<b:glossaryEntry>containing little excess; "a lean budget"; "a skimpy allowance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302226046">
	<b:glossaryEntry>less than the correct or legal or full amount often deliberately so; "a light pound"; "a scant cup of sugar"; "regularly gives short weight"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302226257"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not enough; hard to find; "meat was scarce during the war"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302226368"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(informal) short; "eleven is one shy of a dozen"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302226469"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not enough"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302226536"
	 b:glossaryEntry="containing sugar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302226713"
	 b:glossaryEntry='encrusted with sugar or syrup; "candied grapefruit peel"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302226838"
	 b:glossaryEntry="with honey added"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302226926"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resembling honey"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302226998"
	 b:glossaryEntry="with sweetening added"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302227093"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not containing sugar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302227234"
	 b:glossaryEntry="with no sugar added"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302227309"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not made sweet"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302227381">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of or characteristic of high rank or importance; "a superior officer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302227703">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used of behavior or attitude) characteristic of those who treat others with condescension</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302227888">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of the highest rank; used of persons; "the commanding officer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302228043">
	<b:glossaryEntry>standing above others in quality or position; "people in high places"; "the high priest"; "eminent members of the community"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302228228">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[greatest in importance or degree or significance or achievement; "our greatest statesmen"; "the country''s leading poet"; "a preeminent archeologist"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302228457">
	<b:glossaryEntry>at an elevated level in rank or importance; "a high-level official"; "a high-level corporate briefing"; "upper-level management"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302228671">
	<b:glossaryEntry>majestic in manner or bearing; superior to mundane matters; "his majestic presence"; "olympian detachment"; "olympian beauty and serene composure"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302228883"
	 b:glossaryEntry='controlling; "master race"; "master plan"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302228980"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of higher rank or status or value"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302229073">
	<b:glossaryEntry>superior in rank or accomplishment; "the upper half of the class"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302229190"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of or characteristic of low rank or importance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302229453">
	<b:glossaryEntry>low or inferior in station or quality; "a humble cottage"; "a lowly parish priest"; "a modest man of the people"; "small beginnings"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302229669">
	<b:glossaryEntry>fairly poor to not very good; "has an indifferent singing voice"; "has indifferent qualifications for the job"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302229837">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(usually preceded by `no)'' lower in esteem; "no less a person than the king himself"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302229980">
	<b:glossaryEntry>at a low level in rank or importance; "a low-level job"; "low-level discussions"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302230116"
	 b:glossaryEntry='intermediate in rank or position; "middle-level management"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302230234">
	<b:glossaryEntry>decisively surpassed by something else so as to appear to be of a lower class</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302230368">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of high or superior quality or performance; "superior wisdom derived from experience"; "superior math students"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302230912">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(informal) of the highest quality; "an ace reporter"; "a crack shot"; "a first-rate golfer"; "a super party"; "played top-notch tennis"; "an athlete in tiptop condition"; "she is absolutely tops"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302231224"
	 b:glossaryEntry='unusually good; outstanding; "a banner year for the company"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302231337">
	<b:glossaryEntry>selected or chosen for special qualifications; "the blue-ribbon event of the season"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302231491">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(informal) exceptionally good; "a boss hand at carpentry"; "his brag cornfield"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302231628">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of surpassing excellence; "a brilliant performance"; "a superb actor"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302231762">
	<b:glossaryEntry>clearly superior or having the attributes of a winner; "a champion at teaching"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302231896">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of superior grade; "choice wines"; "fine wines" "prime beef"; "prize carnations"; "quality paper"; "select peaches"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302232109">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of the highest quality; "made an excellent speech"; "the school has excellent teachers"; "a first-class mind"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302232288">
	<b:glossaryEntry>superior to the average; "in fine spirits"; "a fine student"; "a fine summer day"; "made good grades"; "morale was good"; "had good weather for the parade"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302232501">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of the highest quality or value; "gilt-edged securities"; "gilt-edged credentials"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302232640"
	 b:glossaryEntry="highest in quality"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302232741">
	<b:glossaryEntry>distinguished from others in excellence; "did outstanding work in human relations"; "an outstanding war record"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302232910">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or reflecting superior quality or value; "premium gasoline at a premium price"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302233049">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(India) absolutely first class and genuine; "pukka sahib"; "pukka quarters with a swarm of servants"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302233209">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by exceptional merit; "had shining virtues and few faults"; "a shining example"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302233349">
	<b:glossaryEntry>highest in excellence or achievement; "supreme among musicians"; "a supreme endeavor"; "supreme courage"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302233507"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(British) excellent; best possible"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302233619">
	<b:glossaryEntry>exceeding or surpassing usual limits especially in excellence</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302233752"
	 b:glossaryEntry="skillfully constructed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302233830"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of low or inferior quality"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302234253">
	<b:glossaryEntry>below average in quality or performance; "a bad chess player"; "a bad recital"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302234381">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used of metals) consisting of or alloyed with inferior metal; "base coins of aluminum"; "a base metal"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302234535"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(informal) of very poor quality"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302234676">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resembling cardboard especially in flimsiness; "apartments with cardboard walls"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302234821">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cheap and shoddy; "cheapjack moviemaking...that feeds on the low taste of the mob"- Judith Crist</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302234991">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of low or inferior quality or value; "of what coarse metal ye are molded"- Shakespeare; "produced...the common cloths used by the poorer population"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302235201"
	 b:glossaryEntry="made coarse or crude by lack of skill"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302235294">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of the kind or quality used in commerce; average or inferior; "commercial grade of beef"; "commercial oxalic acid"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302235465">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of very poor quality or condition; "deplorable housing conditions in the inner city"; "woeful treatment of the accused"; "woeful errors of judgment"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302235714">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(usually preceded by `no'') lower in quality; "no less than perfect"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302235832"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of inferior quality"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302235907">
	<b:glossaryEntry>moderate to inferior in quality; "they improved the quality from mediocre to above average"; "he would make a poor spy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302236102">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British informal) very poor in quality; "ropey food"; "a ropey performance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302236237">
	<b:glossaryEntry>inferior in size or quality; "scrawny cattle"; "scrubby cut-over pine"; "old stunted thorn trees"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302236408">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of inferior status or quality; "a second-class citizen"; "second-class accommodations"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302236553"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of lesser quality than second-rate"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302236644"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used of beef; usable but inferior"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302236750">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(astronomy) having an orbit farther from the sun than the Earth''s orbit; "Mars and Jupiter are the closest in of the superior planets"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302236939">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(astronomy) having an orbit between the sun and the Earth''s orbit; "Mercury and Venus are inferior planets"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302237101"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lying immediately above or on something else"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302237257">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(geology) lying or leaning on something else; "an incumbent geological formation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302237394"
	 b:glossaryEntry='placed on or over something else; "an overlying image"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302237519">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lying or resting on and exerting pressure on something else; "superincumbent layers of dead plants cut off the air and arrested decomposition"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302237722"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lying nearby but lower; "hills and subjacent valleys"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302237849"
	 b:glossaryEntry="located beneath or below"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302237930">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(printing) written or printed above and to one side of another character</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302238089">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(printing) written or printed below and to one side of another character</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302238246">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(printing) written or printed immediately following another character and aligned with it</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302238408">
	<b:glossaryEntry>under observation or under the direction of a superintendent or overseer; "supervised play"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302238556">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not supervised or under constant observation; "the school maintains unsupervised study halls during free periods"; "reliable workers are generally unsupervised"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302238793">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not watched; "she dashed out leaving the bar unattended"; "a fire left unattended"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302238932">
	<b:glossaryEntry>held up or having the weight borne especially from below; "supported joints in a railroad track have ties directly under the rail ends"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302239285"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having a base; "firmly based ice"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302239370"
	 b:glossaryEntry="held up by braces or buttresses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302239467">
	<b:glossaryEntry>supported from above; "the child dabbled his dangling feet in the water"; "fuchsias in hanging pots"; "pendent bunches of grapes"; "a suspended fireplace"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302239722">
	<b:glossaryEntry>supported on gimbals and remaining steady or level when the base tips</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302239846">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(chemistry) of a liquid; floating on the surface above a sediment or precipitate; "the supernatant fat was skimmed off"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302240023">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of undissolved particles in a fluid) supported or kept from sinking or falling by buoyancy and without apparent attachment; "suspended matter such as silt or mud..."; "dust particles suspended in the air"; "droplets in suspension in a gas"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302240338">
	<b:glossaryEntry>supported from below especially resting on a track instead of suspended from above; "underhung sliding doors"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302240503">
	<b:glossaryEntry>supported from above especially in a vehicle having springs attached to the axle from below</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302240651">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not held up or borne; "removal of the central post left the roof unsupported"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302240822"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having no straps; "a strapless evening gown"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302240922"
	 b:glossaryEntry="without braces or props"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302241000">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sustained or maintained by aid (as distinct from physical support); "a club entirely supported by membership dues"; "well-supported allegations"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302241344"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having backing; "a claim backed up by strong evidence"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302241463">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a basis; often used as combining terms; "a soundly based argument"; "well-founded suspicions"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302241625"
	 b:glossaryEntry='being founded on; "criticisms based on ignorance"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302241729">
	<b:glossaryEntry>supported or established by evidence or proof; "the substantiated charges"; "a verified case"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302241908"
	 b:glossaryEntry="formally supported especially by public statement"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302242012"
	 b:glossaryEntry='supported financially; "a privately financed project"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302242120">
	<b:glossaryEntry>encouraged or promoted in growth or development; "dreams of liberty nourished by the blood of patriots cannot easily be given up"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302242316">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having partial financial support from public funds; "lived in subsidized public housing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302242461">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not sustained or maintained by nonmaterial aid; "unsupported accusations"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302242682">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without a basis in reason or fact; "baseless gossip"; "the allegations proved groundless"; "idle fears"; "unfounded suspicions"; "unwarranted jealousy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302242934"
	 b:glossaryEntry="unsupported by other people"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302243045"
	 b:glossaryEntry="unsupported by other evidence"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302243153"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not promoted for fostered"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302243236"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having help; often used as a combining form"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302243378">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relying on an engine for propulsion in addition to muscle power; "a motor-assisted bicycle"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302243530">
	<b:glossaryEntry>supplementing or replacing manual effort; "power brakes"; "power-assisted steering"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302243685"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking help"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302243790">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of the eye or ear e.g.) without the aid of an optical or acoustical device or instrument; "visible to the naked eye"; "clearly audible to the unaided ear"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302244007">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being without physical aid; "he could not rise unaided from a sitting position"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302244140">
	<b:glossaryEntry>furnishing support or assistance; "a supportive family network"; "his family was supportive of his attempts to be a writer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302244428">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relating to something that is added but is not essential; "an ancillary pump"; "an adjuvant discipline to forms of mysticism"; "The mind and emotions are auxilliary to each other"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302244735"
	 b:glossaryEntry="serving to certify or endorse authoritatively"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302244840"
	 b:glossaryEntry='serving to support or corroborate; "collateral evidence"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302245115">
	<b:glossaryEntry>serving to prove or demonstrate; "the oath of office is...demonstrative of the legislative opinion on on this subject"- John Marshall</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302245314">
	<b:glossaryEntry>furnishing support and encouragement; "the anxious child needs supporting and accepting treatment from the teacher"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302245500"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not furnishing support or assistance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302245649"
	 b:glossaryEntry="that confounds or contradicts or confuses"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302245764"
	 b:glossaryEntry="establishing as invalid or untrue"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302245872">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being surmounted or overcome; "situations of measurable and surmountable danger"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302246058"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of being surmounted or excelled"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302246167">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not capable of being surmounted or overcome; "insurmountable disadvantages"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302246374">
	<b:glossaryEntry>incapable of being surmounted or excelled; "insuperable odds"; "insuperable heroes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302246531">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[taken unawares or suddenly and feeling wonder or astonishment; "surprised by her student''s ingenuity"; "surprised that he remembered my name"; "a surprised expression"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302246844">
	<b:glossaryEntry>filled with the emotional impact of overwhelming surprise or shock; "an amazed audience gave the magician a standing ovation"; "I stood enthralled, astonished by the vastness and majesty of the cathedral"; "astounded viewers wept at the pictures from the Oklahoma City bombing"; "stood in stunned silence"; "stunned scientists found not one but at least three viruses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302247311">
	<b:glossaryEntry>as if struck dumb with astonishment and surprise; "a circle of policement stood dumbfounded by her denial of having seem the accident"; "the flabbergasted aldermen were speechless"; "was thunderstruck by the news of his promotion"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302247656"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(colloq) with eyes or mouth open in surprise"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302247782">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(British informal expletive) "Well I''m jiggered!"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302247886">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[excited by sudden surprise or alarm and making a quick involuntary movement; "students startled by the teacher''s quiet return"; "the sudden fluttering of the startled pigeons"; "her startled expression"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302248143">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not surprised or expressing surprise; "that unsuprised obstinate look on his face"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302248299">
	<b:glossaryEntry>causing surprise or wonder or amazement; "the report shows a surprising lack of hard factual data"; "leaped up with surprising agility"; "she earned a surprising amount of money"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302248624">
	<b:glossaryEntry>surprising greatly; "she does an amazine amount of work"; "the dog was capable of astonishing tricks"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302248793">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[so remarkably different or sudden as to cause momentary shock or alarm; "Sydney''s startling new Opera House"; "startling news"; "startling earthquake shocks"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302249006">
	<b:glossaryEntry>causing great astonishment and consternation; "the strike came as a stunning protest against management"; "a stunning defeat"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302249186">
	<b:glossaryEntry>causing surprise or amazement by not being expected; "the curtains opened to reveal a completely unexpected scene"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302249357"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not causing surprise"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302249490"
	 b:glossaryEntry="looked forward to as probable"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302249574"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not startling"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302249645">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(often followed by `of'' or `to'') yielding readily to or capable of; "susceptible to colds"; "susceptible of proof"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302250087">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having an allergy or peculiar or excessive susceptibility (especially to a specific factor); "allergic children"; "hypersensitive to pollen"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302250365">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(`capable'' is usually followed by `of'' or `to'') possibly accepting or permitting; "a passage capable of misinterpretation"; "open to interpretation"; "an issue open to question"; "the time is fixed by the director and players and therefore subject to much variation"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302250714"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being susceptible to persuasion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302250842">
	<b:glossaryEntry>susceptible to being led or directed; "fictile masses of people ripe for propaganda"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302250990">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(often followed by `to'') likely to be affected with; "liable to diabetes"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302251160">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made susceptible; "because of conditions in the mine, miners are predisposed to lung disease"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302251311">
	<b:glossaryEntry>readily reacting to suggestions and influences; "a responsive student"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302251438">
	<b:glossaryEntry>susceptible or responsive to suggestion; "suggestible young minds"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302251562"
	 b:glossaryEntry="susceptible to temptation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302251643"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not vaccinated"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302251716">
	<b:glossaryEntry>susceptible to criticism or persuasion or temptation; "vulnerable to bribery"; "an argument vulnerable to refutation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302251890"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not susceptible to"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302252092"
	 b:glossaryEntry="relating to or conferring immunity (to disease or infection)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302252217"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having been rendered unsusceptible to a disease"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302252333">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[not being susceptible to or admitting of something (usually followed by `of)''; "incapable of solution"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302252494"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not susceptible to persuasion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302252596">
	<b:glossaryEntry>easily impressed or influenced; "an impressionable youngster"; "an impressionable age"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302252779">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being influenced or formed; "the plastic minds of children"; "a pliant nature"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302252931"
	 b:glossaryEntry="easily impressed emotionally"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302253017">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not sensitive or susceptible to impression; "an unimpressionable mind"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302253150">
	<b:glossaryEntry>responsible to a higher authority; "amenable to a small-scale form of enquiry"; "amenable data"; "is it to be contended that the heads of departments are not amenable to the laws?"- John Marshall</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302253454">
	<b:glossaryEntry>liable to be called to account; "you are answerable for this debt"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302253591">
	<b:glossaryEntry>susceptible to suggestion; "a personality responsive to your desires"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302253729">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being or liable to be subjected to judgment or test; "elasticity of fluids is not subject to simple measurement at present"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302253934"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not amenable; "unamenable to persuasion"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302254049"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not susceptible to suggestion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302254137">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of persons) freed from or not subject to an obligation or liability (as e.g. taxes) to which others or other things are subject; "a beauty somehow exempt from the aging process"; "exempt from jury duty"; "only the very poorest citizens should be exempt from income taxes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302254516">
	<b:glossaryEntry>granted exemption; "one of the excused jurors planned to write a book"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302254640">
	<b:glossaryEntry>secure against; "immune from taxation as long as he resided in Bermuda"; "immune from criminal prosecution"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302254800">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not subject to usual rules or penalties; "a privileged statement"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302254922"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of persons) not exempt from an obligation or liability"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302255087"
	 b:glossaryEntry='subject to legal action; "liable to criminal charges"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302255196">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not exempt from paying taxes; "after training they became productivek taxpaying citizens"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302255341"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not excused; "too many unexcused absences"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302255439">
	<b:glossaryEntry>planned or scheduled for some certain time or times; "the scheduled meeting"; "the scheduled flights had to be cancelled because of snow"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302255650">
	<b:glossaryEntry>regularly scheduled for fixed times; "at a regular meeting of the PTA"; "regular bus departures"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302255800">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not scheduled or not on a regular schedule; "an unscheduled meeting"; "the plane made an unscheduled stop at Gander for refueling"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302256042">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made necessary by sudden occurrence demanding immediate remedy; "the plane made an emergency landing"; "an emergency session to consider aid for the earthquake victims"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302256266">
	<b:glossaryEntry>added to a regular schedule; "a special holiday flight"; "put on special buses for the big game"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302256424">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made necessary by an unexpected situation or emergency; "a forced landing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302256564"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(used of wines) having a sweet taste"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302256688">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of wines) not sweet because of decomposition of sugar during fermentation; "a dry white burgundy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302256908"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of champagne) extremely dry"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302256987"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of champagne) moderately dry"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302257076"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a pleasant taste (as of sugar)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302257255"
	 b:glossaryEntry="overly sweet"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302257353"
	 b:glossaryEntry="somewhat sweet"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302257422"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a sharp biting taste"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302257662"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sour or bitter in taste"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302257768"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tasting like vinegar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302257863"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tasting sour like acid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302257962"
	 b:glossaryEntry="made slightly acid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302258037"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tasting sour like a lemon"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302258152"
	 b:glossaryEntry="slightly sour to the taste"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302258232"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having turned bad"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302258356"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having undergone fermentation; "hard cider"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302258462"
	 b:glossaryEntry='in an unpalatable state; "sour milk"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302258564"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not having turned bad"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302258676"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not soured or preserved; "sweet milk"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302258773"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not having undergone fermentation; "sweet cider"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302258887">
	<b:glossaryEntry>believed likely; "a suspected thief"; "a suspected infection"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302259004">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not suspected or believed likely; "remained unsuspected as the head of the spy ring"; "he was able to get into the building unspotted and unsuspected"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302259248">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not foreseen; "unsuspected difficulties arose"; "unsuspected turnings in the road"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302259387">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not known to exist; "things obscurely felt surged up from unsuspected depths in her"- Edith Wharton</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302259540"
	 b:glossaryEntry='possessing sweep; "the sleek swept wings of the plane"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302259682">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(especially of aircraft wings) angled rearward from the point of attachment; "aircraft with sweptback wings"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302259846">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of an aircraft) having sweptback wings; "a sweptwing aircraft"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302259965">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not swept or having sweep; "a boxy little plane with square unswept wings"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302260093">
	<b:glossaryEntry>bound by or stated on oath; "now my sworn friend and then mine enemy"- Shakespeare</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302260263">
	<b:glossaryEntry>asserted as true under oath; "a statement attested by witnesses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302260391"
	 b:glossaryEntry='bound by an oath; "a bound official"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302260479">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not bound by or stated on oath; "the witness stands unsworn"; "unsworn testimony"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302260614">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having similarity in size, shape, and relative position of corresponding parts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302260960"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having identical parts on each side of an axis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302261127">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing both bilateral and radial symmetry; "some sea anemones are biradial"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302261258"
	 b:glossaryEntry="shaped like a cross"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302261344">
	<b:glossaryEntry>symmetrically arranged; "even (or regular) features; "a regular polygon"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302261477">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(mathematics, logic) such that the arguments or roles can be interchanged; "the arguments of the symmetric relation, `is a sister of,'' are interchangeable"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302261694"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of a triangle) having two sides of equal length"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302261798">
	<b:glossaryEntry>arranged like rays or radii; radiating from a common center; "radial symmetry"; "a starlike or stellate arrangement of petals"; "many cities show a radial pattern of main highways"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302262042">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a symmetrical arrangement of radiating parts about a central point</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302262182"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(crystallography) having three-fold symmetry"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302262296">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by asymmetry in the spatial arrangement or placement of parts or components</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302262529"
	 b:glossaryEntry="out of proportion in shape"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302262641">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(mathematics or logic) such that the terms of an expression cannot be interchanged without changing the meaning; "the arguments of the symmetric relation, `is the father of'', are noninterchangeable"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302262904"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking symmetry"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302262994">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) capable of division into symmetrical halves by any longitudinal plane passing through the axis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302263193">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a radial form; "starfish are actinoid--that is, they are radially symmetrical"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302263333">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) capable of division into symmetrical halves by only one longitudinal plane passing through the axis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302263541">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[expressing or feeling or resulting from sympathy or compassion or friendly fellow feelings; disposed toward; "sympathetic to the students'' cause"; "a sympathetic observer"; "a sympathetic gesture"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302263885">
	<b:glossaryEntry>feeling or expressing sympathy; "made commiserative clicking sounds with his tongue"- Kenneth Roberts</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302264046">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[showing empathy or ready comprehension of others'' states; "a sensitive and empathetic school counselor"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302264217"
	 b:glossaryEntry="expressing sympathy"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302264287">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not sympathetic or disposed toward; "unsympathetic officialdom"; "people unsympathetic to the revolution"; "his dignity made him seem aloof and unsympathetic"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302264577">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not showing or expressing sympathy; "an uncharitable and unsympathizing attitude"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302264719">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of characters in literature or drama) evoking empathic or sympathetic feelings; "the sympathetic characters in the play"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302264931">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of characters in literature or drama) tending to evoke antipathetic feelings; "all the characters were peculiarly unsympathetic"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302265159">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of biological species or speciation) occurring in the same or overlapping geographical areas</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302265308">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of biological species or speciation) occurring in areas isolated geographically from one another</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302265462">
	<b:glossaryEntry>concerned with phenomena (especially language) at a particular period without considering historical antecedents; "synchronic linguistics"; "descriptive linguistics"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302265716">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used of the study of a phenomenon (especially language) as it changes through time; "diachronic linguistics"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302265894">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[occurring or existing at the same time or having the same period or phase; "recovery was synchronous with therapy"- Jour.A.M.A.; "a synchronous set of clocks"; "the synchronous action of a bird''s wings in flight"; "synchronous oscillations"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302266362"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of the same period"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302266464"
	 b:glossaryEntry="existing at the same time"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302266559">
	<b:glossaryEntry>occurring or operating at the same time; "a series of coincident events"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302266780">
	<b:glossaryEntry>occurring in the same period of time; "a rise in interest rates is often contemporaneous with an increase in inflation"; "the composer Salieri was contemporary with Mozart"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302267029">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(computer science) of or relating to the simultaneous performance of multiple operations; "parallel processing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302267212"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of taxa) occurring in the same period of geological time"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302267326">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[operating in unison; "the synchronized flapping of a bird''s wings"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302267451">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not synchronous; not occurring or existing at the same time or having the same period or phase</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302267676"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of taxa) occurring in different geologic times"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302267781">
	<b:glossaryEntry>chronologically misplaced; "English public schools are anachronistic"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302267937"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not occurring together"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302268053">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(computer science) of or relating to the sequential performance of multiple operations; "serial processing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302268227">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(digital communication) pertaining to a transmission technique that requires a common clock signal (a timing reference) between the communicating devices in order to coordinate their transmissions</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302268481">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(digital communication) pertaining to a transmission technique that does not require a common clock between the communicating devices; timing signals are derived from special characters in the data stream itself</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302268751"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(grammar) connected by a conjunction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302268842"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(grammar) lacking conjunctions"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302268928"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of words) meaning the same or nearly the same"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302269067"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of words) expressing closely related meanings"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302269167">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of words) interchangeable in a given context without changing the import of the expression</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302269318"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of words: having opposite meanings"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302269499">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[of words so related that each is the negation of the other; "`male'' and `female'' are complementary terms"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302269664">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[of words so related that both cannot be true and both cannot be false; "`perfect'' and `imperfect'' are contradictory terms"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302269846">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[of words so related that both cannot be true but both may be false; "`hot'' and `cold'' are contrary terms"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302270006">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[of words so related that one contrasts with the other; "`rich'' and `hard-up'' are contrastive terms"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302270178">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[of words so related that one reverses the relation denoted by the other; "`parental'' and `filial'' are converse terms"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302270350">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by order and planning; "the investigation was very systematic"; "a systematic administrator"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302270603">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by system; in good order; "everything is in order"; "his books are always just so"; "things must be exactly so"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302270787"
	 b:glossaryEntry='reduced to a system; "systematized knowledge"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302270891"
	 b:glossaryEntry="arranged or displayed systematically in table form"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302270995">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking systematic arrangement or method or organization; "unsystematic and fragmentary records"; "he works in an unsystematic manner"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302271242">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not done according to a system; "works in an unsystematized manner"; "unsystematized and fragmentary records"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302271412"
	 b:glossaryEntry="habitually reserved and uncommunicative"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302271578"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(British colloquial) not inclined to conversation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302271685"
	 b:glossaryEntry="temperamentally disinclined to talk"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302271789">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by a ready flow of speech; "she is an extremely voluble young woman who engages in soliloquies not conversations"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302272089"
	 b:glossaryEntry='making speech sounds; "a babbling baby"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302272183">
	<b:glossaryEntry>talking idly or incoherently; "blithering (or blathering) idiot"; "jabbering children"; "gabbling housewives"; "a babbling hospital inmate"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302272426">
	<b:glossaryEntry>full of trivial conversation; "kept from her housework by gabby neighbors"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302272606"
	 b:glossaryEntry='talking foolishly; "prattling children"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302272701">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or showing a sense of what is fitting and considerate in dealing with others; "she was tactful enough not to shatter his illusion"; "a tactful remark eased her embarrassment"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302272972">
	<b:glossaryEntry>unobtrusively perceptive and sympathetic; "a discerning editor"; "a discreet silence"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302273125">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking or showing a lack of what is fitting and considerate in dealing with others; "in the circumstances it was tactless to ask her age"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302273366">
	<b:glossaryEntry>great in vertical dimension; high in stature; "tall people"; "tall buildings"; "tall trees"; "tall ships"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302273720">
	<b:glossaryEntry>accommodating the full height of the human figure; "a full-length mirror"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302273851">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tall and thin and having long slender limbs; "a gangling teenager"; "a lanky kid transformed almost overnight into a handsome young man"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302274067">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a specified height; "five feet tall"; "five feet in height"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302274192"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having long legs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302274289"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of plants) having tall spindly stems"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302274393">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of relatively great height; "a race of long gaunt men"- Sherwood Anderson; "looked out the long French windows"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302274555"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of size and dignity suggestive of a statue"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302274664"
	 b:glossaryEntry="somewhat tall"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302274731">
	<b:glossaryEntry>low in stature; not tall; "his was short and stocky"; "short in stature"; "a short smokestack"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302275003">
	<b:glossaryEntry>short and thick; as e.g. having short legs and heavy musculature; "some people seem born to be square and chunky"; "a dumpy little dumpling of a woman"; "dachshunds are long lowset dogs with drooping ears"; "a little church with a squat tower"; "a squatty red smokestack"; "a stumpy ungainly figure"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302275400">
	<b:glossaryEntry>representing only the upper half of the body; "a half-length portrait"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302275528"
	 b:glossaryEntry="slang; well below average height"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302275649"
	 b:glossaryEntry="short and fat"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302275724">
	<b:glossaryEntry>brought from wildness into a domesticated state; "tame animals"; "fields of tame blueberries"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302276002">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tamed or trained to obey; "a horse broken to the saddle"; "this old nag is well broken in"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302276157">
	<b:glossaryEntry>no longer in the natural state; developed by human care and for human use; "cultivated roses"; "cultivated blackberries"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302276334">
	<b:glossaryEntry>easily handled or managed; "a gentle old horse, docile and obedient"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302276464">
	<b:glossaryEntry>converted or adapted to domestic use; "domestic animals"; "domesticated plants like maize"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302276624">
	<b:glossaryEntry>brought from wildness; "the once inhospitable landscape is now tamed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302276745">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in a natural state; not tamed or domesticated or cultivated; "wild geese"; "edible wild plants"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302276991"
	 b:glossaryEntry='wild and menacing; "a ferocious dog"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302277088"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not subdued or trained for service or use; "unbroken colts"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302277202"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not domesticated; "a few undomesticated horses left"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302277315">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very restrained or quiet; "a tame Christmas party"; "she was one of the tamest and most abject creatures imaginable with no will or power to act but as directed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302277563">
	<b:glossaryEntry>quieted and brought under control; "children were subdued and silent"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302277686">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by extreme lack of restraint or control; "wild ideas"; "wild talk"; "wild originality"; "wild parties"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302277918"
	 b:glossaryEntry="completely unordered and unpredictable and confusing"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302278037">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by uncontrolled excitement or emotion; "a crowd of delirious baseball fans"; "something frantic in their gaiety"; "a mad whirl of pleasure"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302278280">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[affected with or marked by frenzy or mania uncontrolled by reason; "a frenzied attack"; "a frenzied mob"; "the prosecutor''s frenzied denunciation of the accused"- H.W.Carter; "outbursts of drunken violence and manic activity and creativity"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302278583">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perceptible by the senses especially the sense of touch; "skin with a tangible roughness"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302278763">
	<b:glossaryEntry>producing a sensation of touch; "tactile qualities"; "the tactual luxury of stroking silky human hair"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302278929">
	<b:glossaryEntry>incapable of being perceived by the senses especially the sense of touch; "the intangible constituent of energy"- James Jeans</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302279142">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of especially business assets) having physical substance and intrinsic monetary value ; "tangible property like real estate"; "tangible assets such as machinery"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302279395">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of property) fixed or immovable; "real property consists of land and buildings; real estate"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302279539"
	 b:glossaryEntry='capable of being realized; "realizable benefits of the plan"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302279656">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of especially business assets) not having physical substance or intrinsic productive value; "intangible assets such as good will"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302279843"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having or showing or conforming to good taste"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302280052"
	 b:glossaryEntry='aesthetically pleasing; "an artistic flower arrangement"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302280197">
	<b:glossaryEntry>exhibiting restrained good taste; "the room is pleasant and understated"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302280360"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking aesthetic or social taste"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302280612">
	<b:glossaryEntry>unrestrained and crudely rich; "barbaric use of color or ornament"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302280733">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tastelessly showy; "a flash car"; "a flashy ring"; "garish colors"; "a gaudy costume"; "loud sport shirts"; "a meretricious yet stylish book"; "tawdry ornaments"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302281056">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[providing sophisticated amusement by virtue of having artificially (and vulgarly) mannered or banal or sentimental qualities; "they played up the silliness of their roles for camp effect"; "campy Hollywood musicals of the 1940''s"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302281344">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in violation of good taste even verging on the indecent; "an indelicate remark"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302281480"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a display that is tawdry or vulgar"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302281599"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having flavor"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302282135">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extremely pleasing to the taste; sweet and fragrant; "a nectarous drink"; "ambrosial food"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302282305">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of the four basic taste sensations; sharp and disagreeable; like the taste of quinine</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302282447"
	 b:glossaryEntry="somewhat bitter"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302282518"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a taste that is a mixture of bitterness and sweetness"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302282648"
	 b:glossaryEntry='appealing to refined taste; "choice wine"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302282742">
	<b:glossaryEntry>especially pleasing to the taste; "a dainty dish to set before a kind"; "a tasty morsel"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302282891"
	 b:glossaryEntry="extremely pleasing to the sense of taste"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302283064"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having been given flavor (as by seasoning)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302283184"
	 b:glossaryEntry="full of flavor"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302283338"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tasting or smelling richly of or as of fruit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302283435">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by richness and fullness of flavor; "a rich ruby port"; "full-bodied wines"; "a robust claret"; "the robust flavor of fresh-brewed coffee"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302283654"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having a taste like that of grapes; "a grapey wine"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302283766"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having the flavor of nuts; "a nutty sherry"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302283871"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having an agreeably pungent taste"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302283993">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of the four basic taste sensations; like the taste of sea water</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302284119"
	 b:glossaryEntry='tasting of smoke; "smoky sausages"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302284205">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of the four basic taste sensations; like the taste of vinegar or lemons</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302284331">
	<b:glossaryEntry>one of the four basic taste sensations; very pleasant; like the taste of sugar or honey</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302284470"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having the taste of wine; "a rich winy taste"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302284574"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking flavor"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302284770">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking taste or flavor or tang; "a bland diet"; "insipid hospital food"; "flavorless supermarket tomatoes"; "vapid beer"; "vapid tea"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302285020"
	 b:glossaryEntry="without flavoring added"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302285143"
	 b:glossaryEntry="without salt or seasoning"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302285236">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of goods or funds) subject to taxation; "taxable income"; "nonexempt property"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302285471">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being assessed especially for the purpose of taxation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302285592"
	 b:glossaryEntry='subject to import tax; "dutiable imports"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302285688">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(British) liable to payment of rates or taxes; "the ratable value of property"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302285831"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not exempt from tax; "the gift will be subject to taxation"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302285947">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of goods or funds) not subject to taxation; "the funds of nonprofit organizations are nontaxable"; "income exempt from taxation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302286214"
	 b:glossaryEntry='exempt from duty; "duty-free liquor"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302286306">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of goods or funds) not taxed; "tax-exempt bonds"; "an untaxed expense account"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302286463"
	 b:glossaryEntry='British; "unratable properties"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302286550">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not extreme in behavior; "temperate in his habits"; "a temperate response to an insult"; "temperate in his eating and drinking"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302286805">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by temperance in indulgence; "abstemious meals"; "a light eater"; "a light smoker"; "ate a light supper"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302286984">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by avoidance of extravagance or extremes; "moderate in his demands"; "restrained in his response"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302287156"
	 b:glossaryEntry='excessive in behavior; "intemperate rage"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302287345"
	 b:glossaryEntry='prodigious; "big spender"; "big eater"; "heavy investor"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302287465">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a drinker or drinking; indulging intemperately; "does a lot of hard drinking"; "a heavy drinker"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302287626">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extravagant or extreme; "the inhumanity of his untempered principles"- M.S.Dworkin</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302287795">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of weather or climate) free from extremes; mild; or characteristic of such weather or climate; "a temperate region"; "the temperate zones"; "temperate plants"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302288046"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not varying; "an equable climate"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302288133">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of weather or climate) not mild; subject to extremes; "an intemperate climate"; "intemperate zones"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302288309"
	 b:glossaryEntry='taut or rigid; stretched tight; "tense piano strings"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302288468"
	 b:glossaryEntry='too tightly strung; "an overstrung archery bow"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302288572">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pulled or drawn tight; "taut sails"; "a tight drumhead"; "a tight rope"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302288702"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not taut or rigid; not stretched or held tight; "a lax rope"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302288938"
	 b:glossaryEntry="hanging down (as from exhaustion or weakness)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302289057"
	 b:glossaryEntry='hanging limply; "a spaniel with floppy ears"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302289154">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[not tense or taut; "the old man''s skin hung loose and gray"; "slack and wrinkled skin"; "slack sails"; "a slack rope"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302289331">
	<b:glossaryEntry>loosely articulated or constructed; "a loose-jointed paragraph"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302289454"
	 b:glossaryEntry='made less tense or rigid; "his relaxed muscles"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302289555"
	 b:glossaryEntry="free from tension"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302289630">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(phonetics) pronounced with relatively tense tongue muscles (e.g., the vowel sound in `beat'')</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302289793"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(phonetics) especially tense; especially in some dialects"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302289908">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(phonetics) pronounced with muscles relatively relaxed (e.g., the vowel sound in `bet'')</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302290045"
	 b:glossaryEntry="in or of a state of physical or nervous tension"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302290342">
	<b:glossaryEntry>brought to a state of great tension; "all wound_up for a fight"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302290470"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a situation; characterized by or causing suspense"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302290610">
	<b:glossaryEntry>subjected to great tension; stretched tight; "the skin of his face looked drawn and tight"; "her nerves were taut as the strings of a bow"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302290807"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being in a tense state; easily upset"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302290994">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of a situation) exceptionally tense; "an atmosphere electric with suspicion"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302291126"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(physiology) of two or more muscles; having equal tension"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302291238">
	<b:glossaryEntry>under great stress; "her voice was strained as she asked the question"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302291363">
	<b:glossaryEntry>nor relaxed; "his life was drawing to a close in baffled zeal and unrelaxed strain"- U.B.Phillips</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302291516">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(informal) tense with excitement as from a rush of adrenaline; "we were really pumped up for the race"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302291700">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without strain or anxiety; "gave the impression of being quite relaxed"; "a relaxed and informal discussion"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302291962">
	<b:glossaryEntry>free and relaxed in manner; "rather degage after the nervousness he had shown at dinner"- Edmund Wilson</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302292118">
	<b:glossaryEntry>unhurried and relaxed; "an easygoing pace"; "a mellow conversation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302292262">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not placed under psychological stress; "the campaign would not leave party loyalties unstrained"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302292415">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of living tissue) in a state of abnormally high tension; "hypertonic muscle tissue"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302292556"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of living tissue) lacking normal tone or tension"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302292661"
	 b:glossaryEntry='of or relating to the local vicinity; "territorial waters"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302292813">
	<b:glossaryEntry>restricted to the geographic area under a particular jurisdiction; "the jurisdictional limits of a state"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302292979">
	<b:glossaryEntry>related or limited to a particular region; "a regional dialect"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302293097">
	<b:glossaryEntry>outside territorial limits or jurisdiction; "fishing in extraterritorial waters"; "enjoying exterritorial privileges and rights"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302293304">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) displaying territoriality; defending a territory from intruders; "territorial behavior"; "strongly territorial birds"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302293489">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) not displaying territoriality; "a nonterritorial species"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302293617">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having the property of softening or fusing when heated and of hardening and becoming rigid again when cooled; "thermoplastic materials can be remelted and cooled time after time without undergoing any appreciable chemical change"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302293906">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having the property of becoming permanently hard and rigid when heated or cured; "the phenol resins and plastics were the original synthetic thermosetting materials"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302294143">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not thin; of relatively great extent from one surface to the opposite usually in the smallest of the three solid dimensions; "a thick board"; "a thick sandwich"; "spread a thick layer of butter"; "thick coating of dust"; "thick warm blankets"; or of a specific thickness; "an inch thick"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302294680">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relatively thick from top to bottom; "deep carpets"; "deep snow"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302294795"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having a relatively large diameter; "a fat rope"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302294893">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a thickness made up of four layers or strands; "four-ply yarns"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302295018"
	 b:glossaryEntry='made of fabric having considerable thickness; "a heavy coat"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302295130">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made of layers of fabric held together by patterned stitching</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302295245"
	 b:glossaryEntry='made or having become thick; "thickened bronchial arteries"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302295360">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a thickness made up of three layers or strands; "three-ply cloth"; "three-ply yarn"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302295506"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a thickness made up of two layers or strands"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302295611">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of relatively small extent from one surface to the opposite or in cross section; "thin wire"; "a thin chiffon blouse"; "a thin book"; "a thin layer of paint"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302296125">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(crystallography) composed of thin flat plates resembling a knife blade; "bladed arsenopyrite"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302296272">
	<b:glossaryEntry>long and slender with a very small internal diameter; "a capillary tube"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302296411">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) flattened laterally along the whole length (e.g., certain leafstalks or flatfishes)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302296568">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) flattened downward as if pressed from above or flattened along the dorsal and ventral surfaces</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302296728"
	 b:glossaryEntry='as thin and soft as chiffon; "chiffon taffeta"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302296828">
	<b:glossaryEntry>so thin as to transmit light; "a hat with a diaphanous veil"; "filmy wings of a moth"; "gauzy clouds of dandelion down"; "gossamer cobwebs"; "sheer silk stockings"; "transparent chiffon"; "vaporous silks"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302297174"
	 b:glossaryEntry="thin in diameter; resembling a thread"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302297307">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very thin in gauge or diameter; "fine hairs"; "a tenuous thread"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302297432">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very thin and insubstantial; "thin paper"; "flimsy voile"; "light summer dresses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302297574">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physics) extremely fine or thin, as in a spectral line split into two or more components; "hyperfine structure"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302297742">
	<b:glossaryEntry>thin and paperlike; "papery leaves"; "wasps that make nests of papery material"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302297874"
	 b:glossaryEntry='long and thin; resembling a ribbon; "ribbonlike noodles"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302297997">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of cloth; thin and loosely woven; "the coat has a sleazy lining"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302298114">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having little width in proportion to the length or height; "a slender pole"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302298243">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relatively dense in consistency; "thick cream"; "thick soup"; "thick smoke"; "thick fog"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302298599">
	<b:glossaryEntry>thickened or coalesced in soft thick lumps (such as clogs or clots); "clotted blood"; "seeds clogged together"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302298773"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of coagulating and becoming thick"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302298871">
	<b:glossaryEntry>transformed from a liquid into a soft semisolid or solid mass; "coagulated blood"; "curdled milk"; "grumous blood"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302299084"
	 b:glossaryEntry="thick like cream"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302299172">
	<b:glossaryEntry>permitting little if any light to pass through because of denseness of matter; "dense smoke"; "heavy fog"; "impenetrable gloom"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302299374"
	 b:glossaryEntry="thick like gelatin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302299475"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having or suggesting a viscous consistency; "heavy cream"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302299584"
	 b:glossaryEntry="forming viscous or glutinous threads"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302299699"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a relatively high resistance to flow"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302299805">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made thick in consistency; "flour-thickened gravy"; "dust-thickened saliva"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302299936">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relatively thin in consistency or low in density; not viscous; "air is thin at high altitudes"; "a thin soup"; "skimmed milk is much thinner than whole milk"; "thin oil"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302300228"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having thin consistency; "a tenuous fluid"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302300324">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having low density; "rare gasses"; "lightheaded from the rarefied mountain air"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302300476"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of being conceived or imagined or considered"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302300674">
	<b:glossaryEntry>possible to ponder; "space flight to other galaxies becomes more cogitable"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302300818">
	<b:glossaryEntry>possible to conceive or imagine; "that is one possible answer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302300962"
	 b:glossaryEntry="able to be inferred on slight grounds"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302301082"
	 b:glossaryEntry="incapable of being conceived or considered"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302301236"
	 b:glossaryEntry="totally unlikely"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302301362">
	<b:glossaryEntry>exhibiting or characterized by careful thought; "a thoughtful paper"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302301631">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deeply absorbed in thought; "as distant and bemused as a professor listening to the prattling of his freshman class"; "lost in thought"; "a preoccupied frown"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302301888"
	 b:glossaryEntry="persistently or morbidly thoughtful"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302302073"
	 b:glossaryEntry='given to cogitation; "he looked at me with cogitative eyes"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302302203">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resulting from careful thought; "the paper was well thought out"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302302357">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by careful consideration or reflection; "a deliberate decision"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302302484">
	<b:glossaryEntry>involved in or characterized by deliberation and discussion and examination; "a deliberative body"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302302641">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing lack of careful thought; "the debate turned into thoughtless bickering"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302302850">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without proper consideration or reflection; "slovenly inconsiderate reasoning"; "unconsidered words"; "prejudice is the holding of unconsidered opinions"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302303078"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not exhibiting or characterized by careful thought"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302303215"
	 b:glossaryEntry="careful and diligent in the use of resources"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302303421">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[avoiding waste; "an economical meal"; "an economical shopper"; "a frugal farmer"; "a frugal lunch"; "a sparing father and a spending son"; "sparing in their use of heat and light"; "stinting in bestowing gifts"; "thrifty because they remember the great Depression"; (`scotch'' is used only informally)]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302303817"
	 b:glossaryEntry="thrifty in small matters only"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302303903">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by thriftiness; "wealthy by inheritance but saving by constitution"- Ellen Glasgow</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302304052">
	<b:glossaryEntry>searching for and salvaging anything useful from discarded material; "scavenging larvae"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302304197"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tending to squander and waste"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302304353"
	 b:glossaryEntry='recklessly wasteful; "prodigal in their expenditures"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302304502"
	 b:glossaryEntry="unwise in dealing with large sums"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302304595"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wasteful of resources"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302304688">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by good order and cleanliness in appearance or habits; "a tidy person"; "a tidy house"; "a tidy mind"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302305063">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of persons) neat and smart in appearance; "a clean-cut and well-bred young man"; "the trig corporal in his jaunty cap"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302305252"
	 b:glossaryEntry='clean or orderly; "her neat dress"; "a neat room"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302305352">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing care in execution; "neat homework"; "neat handwriting"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302305465">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by or adhering to method or system; "a clean orderly man"; "an orderly mind"; "an orderly desk"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302305621"
	 b:glossaryEntry='neat and tidy; "a small ruly beard"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302305707">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of places; characterized by order and neatness; free from disorder; "even the barn was shipshape"; "a trim little sailboat"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302305905"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having been made especially tidy; "slicked up for visitors"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302306021"
	 b:glossaryEntry='neatly arranged; not disorderly; "the room is straight now"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302306135">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having nothing extraneous; "an uncluttered room"; "the unlittered shoulders of the road"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302306294">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not neat and tidy; "careless and untidy in her personal habits"; "an untidy living room"; "untidy and casual about money"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302306720"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used especially of women"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302306830">
	<b:glossaryEntry>filled or scattered with a disorderly accumulation of objects or rubbish; "the storm left the driveway littered with sticks and debris"; "his library was a cluttered room with piles of books on every chair"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302307103">
	<b:glossaryEntry>in disarray; extremely disorderly; "her clothing was disheveled"; "powder-smeared and frowzled"; "a rumpled unmade bed"; "a bed with tousled sheets"; "his brown hair was tousled, thick, and curly"- Al Spiers</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302307412"
	 b:glossaryEntry='in utter disorder; "a disorderly pile of clothes"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302307578">
	<b:glossaryEntry>negligent of neatness especially in dress and person; habitually dirty and unkempt; "filled the door with her frowzy bulk"; "frowzy white hair"; "slovenly appearance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302307817">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[dirty and disorderly; "a mussy fussy bedroom"; "a child''s messy eating habits"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302307955">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[lacking neatness or order; "the old man''s scraggly beard"; "a scraggly little path to the door"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302308105"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lacking neatness or order; "a sloppy room"; "sloppy habits"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302308217"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lacking stiffness in form or posture; "a slouchy sweater"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302308328">
	<b:glossaryEntry>spreading out carelessly (as if wandering) in different directions; "sprawling handwriting"; "straggling branches"; "straggly hair"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302308539">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not properly maintained; "an unkempt garden"; "native vistas and unkempt rambling paths"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302308681">
	<b:glossaryEntry>neat and smart in appearance; well cared for; "the manager was a beautifully groomed young man"; "his horse was always groomed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302308970">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of hair or clothing) groomed with a brush; "with shining hair neatly brushed"; "the freshly brushed clothes hung in the closet"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302309152"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(used of hair) neat and tidy; "a nicely kempt beard"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302309270">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of hair) made smooth by applying a sticky or glossy substance; "black hair plastered with pomade"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302309434"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(of hair) groomed with pomade; "pomaded hair"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302309533">
	<b:glossaryEntry>well-groomed and neatly tailored; especially too well-groomed; "sleek figures in expensive clothes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302309684">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not neat and smart in appearance; "he was wrinkled and ungroomed, with a two-day beard"; "ungroomed hair"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302309917">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used of hair; thick and poorly groomed; "bushy locks"; "a shaggy beard"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302310049">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of hair or clothing) not brushed; "snarled unbrushed hair"; "the suit was wrinkled and unbrushed, as if it had been slept in"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302310231"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not neat or cared for; slovenly; "his unkempt appearance"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302310342">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of hair) made tidy with a comb; "with hair combed to the side"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302310458">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of hair) not combed; "he was unwashed and uncombed with his clothes half buttoned"; "wild unkempt hair"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302310653"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not capable of being combed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302310737"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not neatly combed; wild unkempt hair"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302310828"
	 b:glossaryEntry='furnished with or made of wood or timbers; "timbered walls"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302310978">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having exposed wood framing with spaces filled with masonry, as in Tudor architecture</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302311137">
	<b:glossaryEntry>framed by exposed timbered; "a magnificently timbered old barn"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302311260"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lacking timbers; "an untimbered boat"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302311354">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[having or given a name or title; "many paintings are titled simply `Untitled''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302311557">
	<b:glossaryEntry>known or spoken of as; "though she is named Katherine, she is called Kitty"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302311696">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[given or having a specified name; "they called his name Jesus"; "forces...which Empedocles called `love'' and `hate''"; "an actor named Harold Lloyd"; "a building in Cardiff named the Temple of Peace"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302311955">
	<b:glossaryEntry>designated by a name or title; "Edward VIII--later styled Duke of Windsor"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302312082">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[given a title or identifying name; "the book entitled `A Tale of Two Cities''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302312214">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[not having a title or name; "his still untitled autobiography"; "many paintings are titled simply `Untitled''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302312378">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or distinguished by a tone; often used in combination; "full-toned"; "silver-toned"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302312520">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking in tone or expression; "his toneless mechanical voice"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302312637">
	<b:glossaryEntry>provided with or resembling a tongue; often used in combination; "tongued shoes"; "tongued boards"; "toungued lightning"; "long-tongued"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302312827"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lacking a tongue; "tongueless moccasins"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302312924">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a tip; or having a tip as specified (used in combination); "a rubber-tipped cane"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302313083">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a cigar or cigarette; having a tip designed to filter the smoke; "filter-tipped cigarettes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302313237"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not provided with a special tip; "untipped cigarettes"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302313346">
	<b:glossaryEntry>depleted of strength or energy; "tired mothers with crying babies"; "too tired to eat"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302313736">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(informal) very tired; "was all in at the end of the day"; "so beat I could flop down and go to sleep anywhere"; "bushed after all that exercise"; "I''m dead after that long trip"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302314002">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[physically and mentally fatigued; "`aweary'' is archaic"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302314118"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tired to the point of exhaustion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302314238">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tired of the world; "bored with life"; "strolled through the museum with a bored air"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302314389">
	<b:glossaryEntry>exhausted as a result of long-time stress; "she was burned-out before she was 30"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302314539">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing the wearing effects of overwork or care or suffering; "looking careworn as she bent over her mending"; "her face was drawn and haggard from sleeplessness"; "that raddled but still noble face"; "shocked to see the worn look of his handsome young face"- Charles Dickens</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302314904"
	 b:glossaryEntry="weak from exhaustion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302314990">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[drained of energy or effectiveness; completely exhausted; "the day''s shopping left her exhausted"; "was fagged and sweaty"; "the trembling of his played out limbs"; "felt completely washed-out"; "only worn-out horses and cattle"; "you look worn out"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302315377"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having sore or tired feet"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302315457">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[exhausted; "my father''s words had left me jaded and depressed"- William Styron]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302315597"
	 b:glossaryEntry='worn out from stress or strain; "run ragged"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302315694"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tired by travel"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302315767"
	 b:glossaryEntry="with energy unrestored"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302315858"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(British informal) exhausted or worn out"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302315952">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not tired; refreshed as by sleeping or relaxing; "came back rested from her vacation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302316144"
	 b:glossaryEntry="with restored energy"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302316258"
	 b:glossaryEntry="with unreduced energy"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302316355">
	<b:glossaryEntry>able to be tolerated or endured; "the climate is at least tolerable"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302316515">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being borne though unpleasant; "sufferable punishment"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302316674">
	<b:glossaryEntry>impossible to tolerate or endure; "an intolerable degree of sentimentality"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302316897">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very difficult to accept or bear; "the bitter truth"; "a bitter sorrow"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302317021">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used of persons or their behavior; "impossible behavior"; "insufferable insolence"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302317205"
	 b:glossaryEntry='impossible to bear; "unbearable pain"; "unendurable agony"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302317334"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not able to be supported or defended"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302317430">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing respect for the rights or opinions or practices of others</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302317623"
	 b:glossaryEntry="inclined to be excessively tolerant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302317714"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not opinionated"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302317785"
	 b:glossaryEntry="unwilling to tolerate difference of opinion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302317939">
	<b:glossaryEntry>blindly and obstinately attached to some creed or opinion and intolerant toward others; "a bigoted person"; "an outrageously bigoted point of view"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302318140"
	 b:glossaryEntry="incapable of compromise or flexibility"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302318239">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(music) having tonality; i.e. tones and chords organized in relation to one tone such as a keynote or tonic</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302318488"
	 b:glossaryEntry="set to a key or tone"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302318560">
	<b:glossaryEntry>based on or using the five tones and two semitones of the major or minor scales of Western music</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302318711">
	<b:glossaryEntry>using more than one key or tonality simultaneously; "exciting rhythms and polytonal harmonies"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302318861">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or characterized or distinguished by tone or a specific tone; often used in combination; "full-toned"; "shrill-toned"; "deep-toned"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302319051">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relating to or being the keynote of a major or minor scale; "tonic harmony"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302319178">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(music) characterized by avoidance of traditional Western tonality</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302319307">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having teeth especially of a certain number or type; often used in combination; "saw-toothed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302319598"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having serrated serrations"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302319680"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having protruding upper front teeth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302319786"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having cogs; "a cogged wheel"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302319868"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having fine teeth set close together; "a fine-toothed comb"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302320005"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having widely spaced teeth; "his gap-toothed grin"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302320113"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having teeth that resemble sabers; "a saber-toothed tiger"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302320262"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resembling a tooth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302320336"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having or showing prominent teeth; "a toothy smile"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302320440">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking teeth; "most birds are toothless"; "a toothless old crone"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302320598"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having few if any teeth; "anteaters are edentate animals"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302320733"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having lost teeth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302320807"
	 b:glossaryEntry='situated at the top or highest position; "the top shelf"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302321042"
	 b:glossaryEntry="situated at an apex"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302321114">
	<b:glossaryEntry>forming or providing a crown or summit; "the crowning star on a Christmas tree"; "her hair was her crowning glory"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302321283">
	<b:glossaryEntry>at or nearest to the top; "the uppermost book in the pile"; "on the topmost step"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302321439"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the topmost one of two"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302321513">
	<b:glossaryEntry>situated at the bottom or lowest position; "the bottom drawer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302321703"
	 b:glossaryEntry='farthest down; "bottommost shelf"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302321818"
	 b:glossaryEntry="the bottom one of two"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302321891"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lower; "gnawed his nether lip"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302321974"
	 b:glossaryEntry='located on a side; "side fences"; "the side porch"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302322132"
	 b:glossaryEntry='toward a full side; "a broadside attack"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302322228">
	<b:glossaryEntry>situated at or extending to the side; "the lateral branches of a tree"; "shot out sidelong boughs"- Tennyson</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302322401"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a top of a specified character"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302322545"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a flat or flattened upper surface"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302322654"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having a lid; "milk in a heavy lidded mug"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302322749"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a lid with a spiral groove"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302322841"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having no top; "a topless jar"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302322943"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having no lid; "a lidless container"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302323033"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a bottom of a specified character"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302323200">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of trousers) having legs that flare at the bottom; "bell-bottomed trousers"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302323350">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a bottom of copper or sheathed with copper; "copper-bottomed pots"; "a copper-bottomed ship"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302323511"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having a flat bottom; "a flat-bottomed boat"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302323629"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having a rounded bottom; "round-bottom flasks"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302323751">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having no bottom; "bottomless pajamas consisting simply of a long top opening down the front"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302323901"
	 b:glossaryEntry='computer; "top-down programming"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302323988"
	 b:glossaryEntry='computer; "bottom-up programming"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302324077">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of or existing at or near the geographic equator; "equatorial Africa"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302324239"
	 b:glossaryEntry="distributed throughout the tropics"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302324343">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relating to or situated in or characteristic of the tropics (the region on either side of the equator); "tropical islands"; "tropical fruit"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302324547">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of or existing at or near a geographical pole or within the Arctic or Antarctic Circles; "polar regions"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302324775"
	 b:glossaryEntry="located or found throughout a polar region"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302324875">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not tropical; "the disease occurs in a tropical and a nontropical form"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302325004"
	 b:glossaryEntry="at or near the north pole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302325096"
	 b:glossaryEntry="at or near the south pole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302325191"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(law) having made a legally valid will before death"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302325296">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(law) having made no legally valid will before death or not disposed of by a legal will; "he died intestate"; "intestate property"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302325482"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having come into contact"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302325596">
	<b:glossaryEntry>touched lightly in passing; grazed against; "of all the people brushed against in a normal day on a city street I remember not a one"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302325783">
	<b:glossaryEntry>scraped or touched lightly in passing; "his grazed and bleeding arm proved he had been in the line of fire"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302325943"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not having come in contact"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302326025"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tough to cut or chew"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302326205"
	 b:glossaryEntry="difficult to chew"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302326302"
	 b:glossaryEntry="requiring much chewing"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302326376"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resembling or made to resemble leather; tough but pliable"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302326527"
	 b:glossaryEntry="full of sinews; especially impossible to chew"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302326658">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or resembling fibers especially fibers used in making cordage such as those of jute</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302326811"
	 b:glossaryEntry='easy to cut or chew; "tender beef"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302327006"
	 b:glossaryEntry="easy to cut or chew"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302327091"
	 b:glossaryEntry='tender and brittle; "crisp potato chips"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302327192"
	 b:glossaryEntry="made of or easily forming flakes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302327285">
	<b:glossaryEntry>containing a large amount of shortening; therefore tender and easy to crumble or break into flakes; "shortbread is a short crumbly cookie"; "a short flaky pie crust"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302327502"
	 b:glossaryEntry='made tender as by marinating or pounding; "tenderized meat"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302327618"
	 b:glossaryEntry='physically toughened; "the tough bottoms of his feet"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302327843"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having skin made tough and thick through wear"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302327956">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made tough by habitual exposure; "hardened fishermen"; "a peasant, dark, lean-faced, wind-inured"- Robert Lynd; "our successors...may be graver, more inured and equable men"- V.S.Pritchett</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302328217">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tanned and coarsened from being outdoors; "a weather-beaten face"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302328343"
	 b:glossaryEntry='physically untoughened; "tender feet"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302328483">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[easily hurt; "soft hands"; "a baby''s delicate skin"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302328596">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not given to gentleness or sentimentality; "a tough character"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302328782"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tough and callous by virtue of experience"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302328908">
	<b:glossaryEntry>facing facts or difficulties realistically and with determination</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302329048">
	<b:glossaryEntry>given to sympathy or gentleness or sentimentality; "a tender heart"; "a tender smile"; "tender loving care"; "tender memories"; "a tender mother"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302329300"
	 b:glossaryEntry='showing a care; "a caring mother"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302329399"
	 b:glossaryEntry="given to or marked by sentiment or sentimentality"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302329506">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of or relating to or caused by a toxin or poison; "suffering from exposure to toxic substances"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302329743">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extremely poisonous or injurious; producing venom; "venomous snakes"; "a virulent insect bite"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302329912"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having the qualities or effects of a poison"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302330022"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not producing or resulting from poison"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302330214"
	 b:glossaryEntry="counteracting a toxin or poison"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302330301"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not producing any toxic effects"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302330387"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not producing poison"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302330466"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not producing venom; "nonvenomous snakes"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302330565">
	<b:glossaryEntry>easily managed (controlled or taught or molded); "tractable young minds"; "gold is tractable"; "the natives...being...of an intelligent tractable disposition"- Samuel Butler</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302330974">
	<b:glossaryEntry>ready and willing to be taught; "docile pupils eager for instruction"; "teachable youngsters"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302331132"
	 b:glossaryEntry="easily influenced"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302331215"
	 b:glossaryEntry='easily dealt with; "manageable problems"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302331312"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of being tamed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302331399">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not tractable; difficult to manage or mold; "an intractable disposition"; "intractable pain"; "the most intractable issue of our era"; "intractable metal"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302331809">
	<b:glossaryEntry>stopping short and refusing to go on; "a balking "; "a balky mule"; "a balky customer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302331957">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by stubborn resistance to and defiant of authority or guidance; "a recalcitrant teenager"; "everything revolves around a refractory individual genius"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302332186">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(med) not responding to treatment; "a stubborn infection"; "a refractory case of acne"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302332341"
	 b:glossaryEntry='difficult to solve or alleviate; "uncontrollable pain"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302332471"
	 b:glossaryEntry="difficult or impossible to shape or work"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302332569">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of a restaurant meal) complete but with limited choices and at a fixed price</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302332717">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of a restaurant meal) having unlimited choices with a separate price for each item</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302332857">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being traced or tracked; "a traceable riverbed"; "the traceable course of an ancient wall"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302333026">
	<b:glossaryEntry>incapable of being traced or tracked down; "an untraceable source"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302333150">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having tracks; "new snow tracked by rabbits"; "tracked vehicles"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302333304">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having caterpillar treads or tracks on the wheels; "a caterpillar-tracked earthmover"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302333469">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having caterpillar treads on the rear and wheels in front; "half-track armored vehicles"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302333629">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having no tracks; "a trackless trolley"; "the trackless snowy meadow"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302333754"
	 b:glossaryEntry="traveled over or through; sometimes used as a combining term"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302333887">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[subject to much traffic or travel; "the region''s most heavily traveled highways"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302334030"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not traveled over or through; "untraveled roads"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302334167">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not traveled over or through; "untraveled roads"; "an untraversed region"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302334298"
	 b:glossaryEntry='made neat and tidy by trimming; "his neatly trimmed hair"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302334433">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cut or trimmed by clipping; "a handsome man with a clipped moustache"; "clipped hedges"; "close-clipped lawns"; "a clipped poodle"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302334617"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not trimmed; "shaggy untrimmed locks"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302334736"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not clipped; "unclipped rosebushes"; "unclipped hair"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302334845">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having an end or small portion cut off; "birds with clipped wings cannot fly"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302334976"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not clipped; without a portion cut off; "unclipped wings"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302335089">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cut off as by clipping or biting off; "trees with cropped tops"; "cropped hedges"; "her short cropped hair"; "the cropped grass where sheep had browsed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302335367">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of animals) having ears or tail cut short; "doberman pinschers with docked tails and ears"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302335521"
	 b:glossaryEntry='cut very short; "close-cropped hair"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302335617">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of plants or trees) having the top or outer parts cut off; "lopped-off branches"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302335764">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of plants or trees) shaped by having superfluous branches or shoots trimmed back or cut off; "abundant fruit from properly pruned vines and fruit trees"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302335970">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not subjected to cutting or clipping or browsing; "uncropped hair"; "a dog with uncropped ears"; "tall uncropped grass"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302336181"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not fed on by animals; "an unbrowsed meadow"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302336281"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(of animal tails or ears) cut short; "an undocked tail"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302336391">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by or indicative of distress or affliction or danger or need; "troubled areas"; "fell into a troubled sleep"; "a troubled expression"; "troubled teenagers"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302336957">
	<b:glossaryEntry>troubled persistently especially with petty annoyances; "harassed working mothers"; "a harried expression"; "her poor pestered father had to endure her constant interruptions"; "the vexed parents of an unruly teenager"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302337269">
	<b:glossaryEntry>causing or fraught with or showing anxiety; "spent an anxious night waiting for the test results"; "cast anxious glances behind her"; "those nervous moments before takeoff"; "an unquiet mind"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302337543"
	 b:glossaryEntry="pounded or hit repeatedly by storms or adversities"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302337711">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(archaic) full of cares or anxiety; "Thou art careful and troubled about many things"-Luke 10.41</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302337861">
	<b:glossaryEntry>burdened by cares; "all ye that labor and are heavy-laden"-Matt.11:28</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302338001">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made troubled or apprehensive or distressed in appearance; "his face was clouded with unhappiness"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302338153">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[afflicted with or marked by anxious uneasiness or trouble or grief; "too upset to say anything"; "spent many disquieted moments"; "distressed about her son''s leaving home"; "lapsed into disturbed sleep"; "worried parents"; "a worried frown"; "one last worried check of the sleeping children"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302338544">
	<b:glossaryEntry>facing or experiencing financial trouble or difficulty; "the troubled car industry"; "distressed companies need loans and technical advice"; "financially hard-pressed Mexican hotels are lowering their prices"; "we were hard put to meet the mortgage paymentng"; "it was apparent that the magazine was in trouble"; "found themselves in a bad way financially"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302339017"
	 b:glossaryEntry='marked by distress; "a fraught mother-daughter relationship"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302339131">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tormented or harassed by nightmares or unreasonable fears; "hagridden...by visions of an imminent heaven or hell upon earth"- C.S.Lewis</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302339347">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing emotional affliction or disquiet; "her expression became progressively more haunted"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302339493">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(often followed by `with'' or used in combination) troubled by or encroached upon in large numbers; "waters infested with sharks"; "shark-infested waters"; "the locust-overrun countryside"; "drug-plagued streets"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302339779">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a serious nonfinancial problem; "in trouble with the police"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302339906">
	<b:glossaryEntry>suffering severe physical strain or distress; "he dropped out of the race, clearly distressed and having difficulty breathing"; "the victim was in a bad way and needed immediate attention"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302340180">
	<b:glossaryEntry>engaged in a struggle to overcome especially poverty or obscurity; "a financially struggling theater"; "struggling artists"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302340360"
	 b:glossaryEntry='troubled by pain or loss; "suffering refugees"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302340462"
	 b:glossaryEntry='full of trouble; "these are troublous times"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302340562">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not beset by troubles or disturbance or distress; "seemed untroubled by doubts of any kind"; "untroubled sleep"; "a kind untroubled face"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302340900">
	<b:glossaryEntry>free of trouble and worry and care; "the carefree joys of childhood"; "carefree millionaires, untroubled financially"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302341084">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by freedom from troubling thoughts; especially e.g. guilt; "a clear conscience"; "looked at her questioner with clear untroubled eyes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302341284"
	 b:glossaryEntry='undisturbed by dreams; "a sound and dreamless sleep"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302341392">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without problems or difficulties; "NASA reported a trouble-free launch"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302341522">
	<b:glossaryEntry>easy in mind; not worried; "the prisoner seems entirely unconcerned as to the outcome of the examination"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302341685">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not interfered with or disturbed; "food supplies were allowed through unmolested"; "he could pursue his studies undisturbed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302341880">
	<b:glossaryEntry>consistent with fact or reality; not false; "the story is true"; "it is undesirable to believe a proposition when there is no ground whatever for supposing it true"- B. Russell; "the true meaning of the statement"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302342342">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being or reflecting the essential or genuine character of something; "her actual motive"; "a literal solitude like a desert"- G.K.Chesterton; "a genuine dilemma"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302342583">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(logic) of a proposition; necessarily true or logically certain</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302342715"
	 b:glossaryEntry='based on fact; "factual accuracy"; "a factual account"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302342823">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[conforming to truth; "I wouldn''t have told you this if it weren''t so"; "a truthful statement"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302342979">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[infallible or unfailing; "a sure (or true) sign of one''s commitment"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302343098">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not in accordance with the fact or reality or actuality; "gave false testimony under oath"; "false tales of bravery"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302343428"
	 b:glossaryEntry='intentionally untrue; "a mendacious statement"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302343531"
	 b:glossaryEntry='plausible but false; "a specious claim"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302343625"
	 b:glossaryEntry='concocted with intent to deceive; "trumped-up charges"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302343739">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not according with the facts; "unfortunately the statement was simply untrue"; "the facts as reported were wrong"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302343913">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[inclined to believe or confide readily; full of trust; "great brown eye, true and trustful"- Nordhoff & Hall]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302344159">
	<b:glossaryEntry>willing to entrust personal matters; "first she was suspicious, then she became confiding"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302344305"
	 b:glossaryEntry='tending to trust; "she had an open and trusting nature"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302344415"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not suspicious; "deceiving the unsuspecting public"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302344540">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or showing distrust; "a man of distrustful nature"; "my experience...in other fields of law has made me distrustful of rules of thumb generally"- B.N.Cardozo; "vigilant and distrustful superintendence"- Thomas Jefferson</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302344950">
	<b:glossaryEntry>believing the worst of human nature and motives; having a sneering disbelief in e.g. selflessness of others</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302345143">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by or given to doubt; "a skeptical attitude"; "a skeptical listener"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302345311">
	<b:glossaryEntry>suspicious or unduly suspicious or fearful of being displaced by a rival; "a jealous lover"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302345483"
	 b:glossaryEntry="openly distrustful and unwilling to confide"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302345625"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(used of men) having deep-seated distrust of women"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302345731"
	 b:glossaryEntry="unduly suspicious"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302345809">
	<b:glossaryEntry>worthy of trust or belief; "a trustworthy report"; "an experienced and trustworthy traveling companion"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302346141">
	<b:glossaryEntry>conforming to fact and therefore worthy of belief; "an authentic account by an eyewitness"; "reliable information"; "a faithful source"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302346354"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having an acceptable credit rating; "a responsible borrower"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302346487">
	<b:glossaryEntry>worthy of being depended on; "a dependable worker"; "an honest working stiff"; "a reliable source of information"; "he was true to his word"; "I would be true for there are those who trust me"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302346766"
	 b:glossaryEntry="based on trust"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302346835">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of persons) worthy of trust or confidence; "a sure (or trusted) friend"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302346968"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not worthy of trust or belief; "an untrustworthy person"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302347221">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by insincerity or deceit; evasive; "a devious character"; "shifty eyes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302347369"
	 b:glossaryEntry='of businesses and businessmen; "a fly-by-night operation"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302347493">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not to be trusted; "how extraordinarily slippery a liar the camera is"- James Agee</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302347639"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not to be trusted"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302347728"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a tire; having an inner tube"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302347811"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of a tire; not needing an inner tube"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302347902">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having tucked or being tucked; "tightly tucked blankets"; "a fancy tucked shirt"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302348035">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking tucks or not being tucked; "the sheet came untucked"; "plain untucked shirt front"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302348180"
	 b:glossaryEntry="moved around an axis or center"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302348353">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being in such a position that top and bottom are reversed; "a quotation mark is sometimes called an inverted comma"; "an upside-down cake"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302348560">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having been turned so that the bottom is no longer the bottom; "an overturned car"; "the upset pitcher of milk"; "sat on an upturned bucket"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302348776">
	<b:glossaryEntry>turned inside out and resewn; "the reversed collar looked as good as new"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302348904"
	 b:glossaryEntry="turned in a circle around an axis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302349002"
	 b:glossaryEntry="with the inside surface on the outside"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302349120"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not turned; "left no stone unturned"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302349247"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of fabric or clothing"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302349332"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of objects having a top and bottom"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302349429">
	<b:glossaryEntry>exhibiting the qualities or characteristics that identify a group or kind or category; "a typical American girl"; "a typical suburban community"; "the typical car owner drives 10,000 miles a year"; "a painting typical of the Impressionist school"; "a typical romantic poem"; "a typical case of arteritis"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302349895">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being or serving as an illustration of a type; "the free discussion that is emblematic of democracy"; "an action exemplary of his conduct";</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302350112">
	<b:glossaryEntry>serving to represent or typify; "representative moviegoers"; "a representative modern play"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302350264">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[often used as intensifiers; "a regular morass of details"; "a regular nincompoop"; "he''s a veritable swine"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302350443">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) conforming to the definitive criteria of a natural group; "the horseshoe crab is not a true (or typical) crab"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302350614">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deviating from or not conforming to type; "atypical behavior..not the accepted type of response that we expect from children"- G.E.Gardner</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302350891">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not exemplifying a class; "I soon tumbled to the fact that my weekends were atypical"; "behavior quite unrepresentative (or atypical) of the profession"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302351106">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(sports) with hand brought forward and up from below shoulder level; "an underhand pitch"; "an underhand stroke"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302351299">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(sports) with hand brought forward and down from above shoulder level; "an overhand pitch"; "an overhand stroke"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302351507">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(cricket) with the arm swung round at shoulder height; "round-arm bowling"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302351637"
	 b:glossaryEntry='on the surface; "surface materials of the moon"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302351846">
	<b:glossaryEntry>on or above the surface of the ground; "aboveground nuclear testing"; "surface instruments for detecting oil deposits"; "surface transportation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302352048"
	 b:glossaryEntry='at surface level; "a grade crossing"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302352151">
	<b:glossaryEntry>constructed at ground level; "grade-constructed accesses to the freeway"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302352287">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of mines and mining) worked from the exposed surface; "opencast mining"; "an opencut iron mine"; "a strip mine"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302352475"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being or operating on or near the surface of the earth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302352585"
	 b:glossaryEntry='beneath the surface; "subsurface materials of the moon"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302352787">
	<b:glossaryEntry>under the level of the ground; "belowground storage areas"; "underground caverns"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302352940"
	 b:glossaryEntry="beneath the surface of the sea"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302353037"
	 b:glossaryEntry='beneath the surface of the water; "submerged rocks"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302353169">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being or operating under the surface of the earth; "subterranean passages"; "a subsurface flow of water"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302353332"
	 b:glossaryEntry='located or originating from above; "an overhead crossing"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302353498">
	<b:glossaryEntry>operating or operated overhead especially on elevated cables; "aerial conveyers for transporting raw materials"; "aerial cable cars"; "an aerial walkway between the buildings"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302353726">
	<b:glossaryEntry>raised above ground level; on elevated rails; "elevated railway"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302353845">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being immersed in water or functioning while submerged; "a submersible pump"; "a submergible electric frying pan"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302354041"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not submersible or submergible"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302354149">
	<b:glossaryEntry>filled with or marked by tears; "tearful eyes"; "tearful entreaties"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302354361">
	<b:glossaryEntry>filled or brimming with tears; "swimming eyes"; "watery eyes"; "sorrow made the eyes of many grow liquid"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302354539"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having eyes blurred as with tears; "sad and misty-eyed"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302354651"
	 b:glossaryEntry="with eyes full of tears"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302354753"
	 b:glossaryEntry="liable to sniffle"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302354861"
	 b:glossaryEntry="liable to weep easily"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302354934"
	 b:glossaryEntry="free from tears"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302355033"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not shedding tears; "dry sobs"; "with dry eyes"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302355130">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of trade unions; "the union movement"; "union negotiations"; "a union-shop clause in the contract"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302355316"
	 b:glossaryEntry='requiring union membership; "a closed shop"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302355415">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being a member of or formed into a labor union; "organized labor"; "unionized workers"; "a unionized shop"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302355589">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not belonging to or not allowing affiliation with a trade union; "nonunion carpenters"; "a nonunion contractor"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302355791">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not requiring union membership; "an open shop employs nonunion workers"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302355916">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not affiliated in a trade union; "the workers in the plant were unorganized"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302356065"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(biology) producing only one offspring at a time"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302356169"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(biology) producing more than one offspring at a time"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302356298"
	 b:glossaryEntry="producing two offspring at a time"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302356397"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a single pole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302356472"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having two poles"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302356560">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or concerned with polarities or contrasts; "a Janus-faced view of history"; "a Janus-faced policy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302356723">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by unity; being or joined into a single entity; "presented a united front"; "The United States of America"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302357220">
	<b:glossaryEntry>united by being of the same opinion; "agreed in their distrust of authority"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302357367"
	 b:glossaryEntry="united in a confederacy or league"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302357483">
	<b:glossaryEntry>joined together into a whole; "United Industries"; "the amalgamated colleges constituted a university"; "a consolidated school"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302357716">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cohering or tending to cohere; well integrated; "a cohesive organization"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302357844"
	 b:glossaryEntry="joined together especially in a pair or pairs"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302357968">
	<b:glossaryEntry>bound in close association; "conjunct influences"; "conjunct ideas"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302358090"
	 b:glossaryEntry="united under a central government"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302358190"
	 b:glossaryEntry="formed or united into a whole"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302358324">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(usually followed by `with'') united in effort as if in a league; "they found out that some policemen were in league with the criminals"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302358518">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having the indivisible character of a unit; "a unitary action"; "spoke with one voice"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302358667">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(linguistics) pertaining to a feature of speech that extends over more than a single speech sound</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302358825">
	<b:glossaryEntry>bound together by or as if by a strong rope; especially as by a bond of affection; "people tied by blood or marriage"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302358993">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not parted by conflict of opinion; "presented an undivided front"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302359114"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not divided by partitions"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302359199">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(zoology) having a body that is not divided into segments; "unsegmented worms"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302359350"
	 b:glossaryEntry='separated into parts or pieces; "opinions are divided"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302359962">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not living together as man and wife; "decided to live apart"; "maintaining separate households"; "they are separated"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302360157"
	 b:glossaryEntry='consisting of two chambers; "the bicameral heart of a fish"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302360288"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(botany) divided into two lobes; "a bifid petal"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302360388">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resembling a fork; divided or separated into two branches; "the biramous appendages of an arthropod"; "long branched hairs on its legson which pollen collects"; "a forked river"; "a forked tail"; "forked lightning"; "horseradish grown in poor soil may develop prongy roots"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302360767">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) divided into five parts; specifically, having each floral whorl consist of five (or a multiple of five) members; "pentamerous flowers"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302360968">
	<b:glossaryEntry>divided into or made up of two parts; "socially bifurcated populations"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302361096">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) divided into or containing two cells or chambers; "having a bilocular capsule"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302361253"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having compartmental chambers; "a spiral chambered seashell"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302361369"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used of hooves"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302361456">
	<b:glossaryEntry>divided or dividing into two sharply distinguished parts or classifications</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302361589">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having been divided; having the unity destroyed; "Congress...gave the impression of...a confusing sum of disconnected local forces"-Samuel Lubell; "a league of disunited nations"- E.B.White; "a fragmented coalition"; "a split group"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302361914"
	 b:glossaryEntry='taken apart at the joints; "a disjointed fowl"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302362017">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(zoology) having deep constrictions separating head, thorax, and abdomen, as in insects</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302362159">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[of writing or narration; divided into or composed of episodes; "the book is episodic and the incidents don''t always hang together"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302362344">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(zoology) having the body divided into successive metameres or segments, as in earthworms or lobsters</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302362525">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of windows; divided by vertical bars or piers usually of stone; "mullioned windows"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302362664"
	 b:glossaryEntry="divided by partitions"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302362761">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having prongs or tines; usually used in combination; "a three-tined fork"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302362896">
	<b:glossaryEntry>consisting of or divided into sections; "a sectional sofa"; "sectioned plates"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302363042">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(linguistics) divided or organized into speech segments or isolable speech sounds</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302363179"
	 b:glossaryEntry='being divided or separated; "split between love and hate"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302363288">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[without agreement; "were in disagreement on the question of women''s rights"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302363428">
	<b:glossaryEntry>divided again after a first division, e.g. of a tract of land, divided into lots</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302363565">
	<b:glossaryEntry>disrupted by the pull of contrary forces; "torn between love and hate"; "torn by conflicting loyalties"; "torn by religious dissensions"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302363752">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) of unlike parts or organs; growing closely attached; "a calyx adnate to the ovary"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302363897">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(biology) of similar parts or organs; closely joined or united; "a connate tomato flower"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302364040">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(zoology) used of mollusks, especially gastropods, as snails etc.</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302364178"
	 b:glossaryEntry="univalve"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302364247"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(zoology) used of mollusks having two shells (as clams etc.)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302364390"
	 b:glossaryEntry="bivalve"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302364484">
	<b:glossaryEntry>moving or going or growing upward; "the ascending plane"; "the ascending staircase"; "the ascending stems of chickweed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302364788"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sloping upward"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302364878">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tending or directed upward; "rooted and ascendant strength like that of foliage"- John Ruskin</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302365051">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) growing or extending upward; "an assurgent stem or leaf"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302365172"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(heraldry) rising from the sea; "a seahorse assurgent"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302365282">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used especially of plants; having a tendency to climb; "a climbing fern"; "plants of a creeping or scandent nature"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302365466">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extending or moving toward a higher place; "the up staircase"; "a general upward movement of fish"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302365628"
	 b:glossaryEntry="coming down or downward"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302365855"
	 b:glossaryEntry="sloping down rather steeply"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302365970"
	 b:glossaryEntry="going down by steps"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302366046"
	 b:glossaryEntry="going or coming down"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302366136">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extending or moving from a higher to a lower place; "the down staircase"; "the downward course of the stream"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302366313">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of rain) falling lightly in very small drops; "a raw drizzing rain"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302366437">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming down freely under the influence of gravity; "the eerie whistle of dropping bombs"; "falling rain"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302366606">
	<b:glossaryEntry>falling in drops or as if falling like rain; "watched the raining apple blossoms"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302366741">
	<b:glossaryEntry>maintaining an erect position; "standing timber"; "many buildings were still standing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302366892">
	<b:glossaryEntry>suddenly losing an upright position; "they ran from the falling tree"; "a falling wall crushed the car"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302367085">
	<b:glossaryEntry>falling over as if from top-heaviness or lack of support; "a toppling pile of books"; "toppling empires"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302367244">
	<b:glossaryEntry>pitching headlong with a rolling or twisting movement; "a violent tumbling fall"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302367379">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of a heavenly body) becoming visible above the horizon; "the rising sun"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302367505">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of a heavenly body) disappearing below the horizon; "the setting sun"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302367629">
	<b:glossaryEntry>advancing or becoming higher or greater in degree or value or status; "a rising trend"; "a rising market"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302367823"
	 b:glossaryEntry='characterized by rising prices; "a bull market"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302367924">
	<b:glossaryEntry>getting higher or more vigorous; "its an up market"; "an improving economy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302368060">
	<b:glossaryEntry>becoming lower or less in degree or value; "a falling market"; "falling incomes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302368230">
	<b:glossaryEntry>becoming progressively lower; "steadily declining incomes"; "the down trend in the real estate market"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302368398">
	<b:glossaryEntry>falling rapidly; "dropping prices"; "dropping rate of production"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302368518">
	<b:glossaryEntry>preceding the climax especially of a dramatic or narrative plot; "the rising action"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302368673">
	<b:glossaryEntry>rising progressively; "the building suspense"; "the heightening drama"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302368818">
	<b:glossaryEntry>coming after the climax especially of a dramatic or narrative plot; "the falling action"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302368978">
	<b:glossaryEntry>manifesting or attended by a disappointing or ineffective conclusion; "everything after the discovery of the murderer was anticlimactic"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302369174">
	<b:glossaryEntry>designed for high-income consumers; "he turned up in well-cut clothes...and upmarket felt hats"- New Yorker</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302369354">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relating to people with good incomes; "an upscale neighborhood"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302369471"
	 b:glossaryEntry="designed for low-income consumers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302369579">
	<b:glossaryEntry>intended for people with low incomes; "mass-produced downscale versions of high-priced fashions"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302369731">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(grammar) designating a verb that requires a direct object to complete the meaning</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302369870">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(grammar) designating a verb that does not require or cannot take a direct object</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302370010">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being put into another form or style or language; "substances readily translatable to the American home table"; "his books are eminently translatable"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302370230">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not capable of being put into another form or style or language; "an untranslatable idiom"; "untranslatable art"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302370403">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or resembling hoofs; "horses and other hoofed animals"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302370549">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or resembling claws or nails; "unguiculate animals"; "an unguiculate flower petal"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302370747">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a claw or claws; often used as a combining form; "sharp-clawed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302370870"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resembling a claw"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302370942">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being or moving higher in position or greater in some value; being above a former position or level; "the anchor is up"; "the sun is up"; "he lay face up"; "he is up by a pawn"; "the market is up"; "the corn is up"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302371403">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having the leading position or higher score in a contest; "he is ahead by a pawn"; "the leading team in the pennant race"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302371603">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used of an anchor) hanging clear of the bottom; "anchors aweigh"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302371721">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(golf) in match play a side that stands as many holes ahead as there are holes remaining to be played; "he was dormie three and still lost the match"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302371931">
	<b:glossaryEntry>directed toward heaven or the sky; "the soul in its heavenward flight"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302372068">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of e.g. celestial bodies) above the horizon; "the risen sun"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302372181">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of growing vegetation) having just emerged from the ground; "the corn is sprouted"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302372319">
	<b:glossaryEntry>heading in any direction that is conventionally up; "upbound shipping lanes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302372449">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(sports) away from the defending teams''s end of the playing field</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302372568">
	<b:glossaryEntry>directed up; "the cards were face upward"; "an upward stroke of the pen"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302372693">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being or moving lower in position or less in some value; "lay face down"; "the moon is down"; "our team is down by a run"; "down by a pawn"; "the stock market is down today"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302373097">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having the lower score or lagging position in a contest; "behind by two points"; "the 8th inning found the home team trailing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302373290">
	<b:glossaryEntry>heading in any direction that is conventionally down; "a downbound channel"; "the downbound train"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302373444"
	 b:glossaryEntry='directed downward; "a downcast glance"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302373537">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(sports) toward or in the defending team''s end of the playing field; "he threw to a downfield receiver"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302373696">
	<b:glossaryEntry>on or toward a surface regarded as a base; "he lay face downward"; "the downward pull of gravity"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302373852">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having dropped by the force of gravity; "fallen leaves covered the forest floor"; "sat on a fallen tree trunk"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302374015"
	 b:glossaryEntry='being below the horizon; "the moon is set"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302374110">
	<b:glossaryEntry>caused to fall to the ground; "the thrown rider got back on his horse"; "a thrown wrestler"; "a ball player thrown for a loss"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302374289">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of the back half of a stage; "she crossed to the upstage chair forcing the lead to turn his back to the audience"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302374456"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of the front half of a stage"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302374540">
	<b:glossaryEntry>on or of upper floors of a building; "the upstairs maid"; "an upstairs room"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302374681">
	<b:glossaryEntry>on or of lower floors of a building; "the downstairs (or downstair) phone"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302374842">
	<b:glossaryEntry>on the floor closest to level with the ground; "the ground-floor entrance is kept locked"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302374990"
	 b:glossaryEntry="in the direction against a stream''s current"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302375088"
	 b:glossaryEntry="in the direction of a stream''s current"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302375183">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of or located in the upper part of a town; "uptown residential areas"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302375305">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of or located in the lower part of a town especially the business center; "downtown Manhattan"; "delinquents roaming the downtown streets"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302375498">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[employed in accomplishing something; "the principle of surprise is the most used and misused of all the principles of war"- H.H.Arnold & I.C.Eaker]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302375731">
	<b:glossaryEntry>currently being used; "robots are in use throughout industry"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302375845"
	 b:glossaryEntry="put to use"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302375910">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used incorrectly or carelessly or for an improper purpose; "misused words are often laughable but one weeps for misused talents"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302376128">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used improperly or excessively especially drugs; "an abused substance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302376251">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of persons; taken advantage of; "after going out of his way to help his friend get the job he felt not appreciated but used"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302376473">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being of use or service; "the girl felt motherly and useful"; "a useful job"; "a useful member of society"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302376874">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of service or assistance; "a child who is helpful around the house can save the mother many steps"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302377026"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having multiple uses; "a multipurpose tool"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302377128"
	 b:glossaryEntry="possible to use again"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302377231"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having a beneficial use; "a serviceable kitchen gadget"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302377344"
	 b:glossaryEntry='able to be put to use; "usable byproducts"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302377449">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having utility often to the exclusion of values; "plain utilitarian kitchenware"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302377587"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of being put to a profitable or practical use"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302377697">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having no beneficial use or incapable of functioning usefully; "a kitchen full of useless gadgets"; "she is useless in an emergency"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302378027"
	 b:glossaryEntry='disposed of as useless; "waste paper"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302378162">
	<b:glossaryEntry>producing no result or effect; "a futile effort"; "the therapy was ineffectual"; "an otiose undertaking"; "an unavailing attempt"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302378380"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not worth using"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302378464"
	 b:glossaryEntry="impossible to use"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302378564">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being the best possible; characterized by ideal perfection; "the dim utopian future"; "utopian idealists"; "recognized the utopian nature of his hopes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302378841">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not practical or realizable; speculative; "airy theories about socioeconomic improvement"; "visionary schemes for getting rich"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302379045"
	 b:glossaryEntry="represented in the abstract rather than as they really are"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302379168">
	<b:glossaryEntry>as bad as can be; characterized by human misery; "AIDS is one of the dystopian harbingers of the global villages"- Susan Sontag</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302379369">
	<b:glossaryEntry>well grounded in logic or truth or having legal force; "a valid inference"; "a valid argument"; "a valid contract"; "a valid license"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302379680"
	 b:glossaryEntry='executed with proper legal authority; "a binding contract"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302379792">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having legal efficacy or force; "a sound title to the property"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302379915"
	 b:glossaryEntry='in accordance with reason or logic; "a logical conclusion"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302380040">
	<b:glossaryEntry>reflects weight of sound argument or evidence; "a sound argument"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302380184"
	 b:glossaryEntry='declared or made legally valid; "a validated claim"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302380291">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[having no cogency or legal force; "invalid reasoning"; "an invalid driver''s license"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302380573">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not capable of being collected; "a bad (or uncollectible) debt"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302380702">
	<b:glossaryEntry>containing or based on a fallacy; "fallacious reasoning"; "an unsound argument"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302380848">
	<b:glossaryEntry>erroneous and usually accidental; "a false start"; "a false alarm"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302380966"
	 b:glossaryEntry="deprived of legal force"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302381059"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking legal force"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302381147"
	 b:glossaryEntry="plausible but misleading"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302381241">
	<b:glossaryEntry>plausible but false; "specious reasoning"; "the spurious inferences from obsolescent notions of causality"- Ethel Albert</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302381427">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having great material or monetary value especially for use or exchange; "another human being equally valuable in the sight of God"; "a valuable diamond"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302381796"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having incalculable monetary worth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302381899">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of high worth or cost; "diamonds, sapphires, rubies, and emeralds are precious stones"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302382040"
	 b:glossaryEntry='of great worth or quality; "a rich collection of antiques"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302382149">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used of gemstones having less commercial value than precious stones; "such semiprecious stones as amethyst, garnet, jade, and tourmaline"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302382345">
	<b:glossaryEntry>held in great esteem for admirable qualities especially of an intrinsic nature; "a valued friend"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302382495">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a specified value; "a house valued at a million dollars"; "not worth his salt"; "worth her weight in gold"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302382678"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lacking in excellence or value; "a worthless idler"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302382947">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of no value; "an empty chaffy book by a foolish chaffy fellow"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302383062">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without merit; "a sorry horse"; "a sorry excuse"; "a lazy no-count, good-for-nothing goldbrick"; "the car was a no-good piece of junk"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302383331">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not worth considering; "he considered the prize too paltry for the lives it must cost"; "piffling efforts"; "a trifling matter"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302383535"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of no real value"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302383606">
	<b:glossaryEntry>serving no useful purpose; having no excuse for being; "otiose lines in a play"; "advice is wasted words"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302383799">
	<b:glossaryEntry>cheap and inferior; of no value; "rubbishy newspapers that form almost the sole reading of the majority"; "trashy merchandise"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302383989"
	 b:glossaryEntry='gaudy and showy but valueless; "trumpery ornaments"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302384104"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of no value"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302384171">
	<b:glossaryEntry>liable to or capable of change; "rainfall in the tropics is notoriously variable"; "variable winds"; "variable expenses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302384454">
	<b:glossaryEntry>subject to change; "a changeable climate"; "the weather is uncertain"; "unsettled weather with rain and hail and sunshine coming one right after the other"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302384690">
	<b:glossaryEntry>taking on different forms; "eyes...of that baffling protean gray which is never twice the same"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302384839"
	 b:glossaryEntry='continuously varying; "taffeta with shifting colors"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302384946"
	 b:glossaryEntry='changeable or inconstant; "versatile moods"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302385045">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not liable to or capable of change; "an invariable temperature"; "an invariable rule"; "his invariable courtesy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302385304">
	<b:glossaryEntry>persistent in occurrence and unvarying in nature; "maintained a constant temperature"; "a constant beat"; "principles of unvarying validity"; "a steady breeze"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302385564"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(of rules) stringently enforced; "hard-and-fast rules"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302385687">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(mathematics) unaffected by a designated operation or transformation</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302385811">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[characterized by variety; "immigrants'' varied ethnic and religious traditions"; "his work is interesting and varied"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302386160">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having many aspects; "a many-sided subject"; "a multifaceted undertaking"; "multifarious interests"; "the multifarious noise of a great city"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302386388"
	 b:glossaryEntry='of all varieties or forms or kinds; "omnifarious reading"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302386503"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having a variety of colors"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302386615"
	 b:glossaryEntry="varying in form or shape"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302386694">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by diversity or difference; "the varying angles of roof slope"; "nature is infinitely variable"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302386861">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having great diversity or variety; "his various achievements are impressive"; "his vast and versatile erudition"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302387039"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking variety"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302387175"
	 b:glossaryEntry="marked by tedious repetition"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302387300">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or as if having a veil or concealing cover; "a veiled dancer"; "a veiled hat"; "veiled threats"; "veiled insults" ; "the night-veiled landscape"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302387540">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[having the head and face covered; "the bride''s veiled head"; "veiled Muslim women"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302387676"
	 b:glossaryEntry='deliberately concealed as if with a veil; "disguised threats'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302387792">
	<b:glossaryEntry>revealed; especially by having a veil removed; "a new generation of unveiled women in Iran"; "applauding the unveiled statue of Winston Churchill"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302388029">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made known (especially something secret or concealed); "the disclosed purpose of their wicked plan"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302388184"
	 b:glossaryEntry='stripped of drapery; "the undraped statue"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302388281"
	 b:glossaryEntry='exposed to air; "a well ventilated room"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302388450"
	 b:glossaryEntry='open to or abounding in fresh air; "airy rooms"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302388556"
	 b:glossaryEntry='supplied with louvers for ventilation; "a louvered door"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302388667">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physiology) used of tissues or especially blood; supplied with oxygen by respiration</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302388819">
	<b:glossaryEntry>supplied with a vent or vents for intake of air or discharge of gases</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302388941"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not ventilated; "stuffy unventilated rooms"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302389133">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking fresh air; "a dusty airless attic"; "the dreadfully close atmosphere"; "hot and stuffy and the air was blue with smoke"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302389341"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(British informal) poorly ventilated"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302389429"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(physiology) used of blood; not supplied with oxygen"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302389552"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not provided with vents"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302389630">
	<b:glossaryEntry>thoroughly acquainted with and skilled in something through study or experience; "well versed in classical languages"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302389816">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking knowledge or skill; "unversed in the jargon of the social scientist"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302389958">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(zoology) having a backbone or spinal column; "fishes and amphibians and reptiles and birds and mammals are verbetrate animals</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302390141">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(zoology) lacking a backbone or spinal column; "worms are an example of invertebrate animals"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302390293">
	<b:glossaryEntry>that can be violated; "a violable rule"; "a violable contract"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302390410">
	<b:glossaryEntry>that cannot be transgressed or dishonored; "the person of the king is inviolable"; "an inviolable oath"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302390606"
	 b:glossaryEntry="impossible to assail"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302390699"
	 b:glossaryEntry='impossible to not honor; "an unbreakable promise"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302390806">
	<b:glossaryEntry>acting with or marked by or resulting from great force or energy or emotional intensity; "a violent attack"; "a violent person"; "violent feelings"; "a violent rage"; "felt a violent dislike"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302391249">
	<b:glossaryEntry>resembling a convulsion in being sudden and violent; "a convulsive rage"; "convulsive laughter"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302391401">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by extreme and violent energy; "a ferocious beating"; "fierce fighting"; "a furious battle"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302391583">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by violent and forceful activity or movement; very intense; "the fighting became hot and heavy"; "a hot engagement"; "a raging battle"; "the river became a raging torrent"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302391827">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(informal) extremely violent; "a knock-down-and-drag-out fight"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302391980">
	<b:glossaryEntry>violently urging on by whipping or flogging; "looked at the lashing riders"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302392109">
	<b:glossaryEntry>horrible in fierceness or savagery; "lurid crimes"; "a lurid life"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302392227">
	<b:glossaryEntry>displaying raging violence; often destructive; "the hot rampageous horses of my will"- W.H.Auden</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302392380">
	<b:glossaryEntry>violent and lawless; "the more ruffianly element"; "tough street gangs"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302392515">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characteristic of someone who employs terrorism (especially as a political weapon); "terrorist activity"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302392675"
	 b:glossaryEntry="abstaining (on principle) from the use of violence"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302392836">
	<b:glossaryEntry>peacefully resistant in response to injustice; "passive resistance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302392968"
	 b:glossaryEntry="morally excellent"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302393166"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not capable of sin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302393241"
	 b:glossaryEntry="free from sin"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302393331"
	 b:glossaryEntry="morally bad in principle or practice"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302393618"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having the nature of vice"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302393715">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(of an actor or role) being or playing the villain; "Iago is the heavy role in `Othello''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302393856">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by iniquity; wicked because it is believed to be a sin; "iniquitous deeds"; "he said it was sinful to wear lipstick"; "ungodly acts"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302394078">
	<b:glossaryEntry>insusceptible of reform; "vicious irreclaimable boys"; "irredeemable sinners"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302394260">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extremely wicked; "nefarious schemes"; "a villainous plot"; "a villainous band of thieves"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302394419">
	<b:glossaryEntry>liable to sin; "a frail and peccable mortal"- Sir Walter Scott</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302394546">
	<b:glossaryEntry>perceptible especially by the eye; or open to easy view; "a visible object"; "visible stars"; "mountains visible in the distance"; "a visible change of expression"; "visible files"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302394916">
	<b:glossaryEntry>readily apparent to the eye; "angry for no apparent reason"; "had no visible means of support"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302395065">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of a celestial body) continually visible above the horizon during the entire 360 degrees of daily travel; "a circumpolar star"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302395250">
	<b:glossaryEntry>at or within a reasonable distance for seeing; "not a policeman in sight"; "kept the monkey in view"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302395415">
	<b:glossaryEntry>able to be seen; "be sure of it; give me the ocular proof"- Shakespeare; "a visual presentation"; "a visual image"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302395591">
	<b:glossaryEntry>including everything visible in one view; "a panoptic aerial photograph of the missile base"; "a panoptic stain used in microscopy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302395790">
	<b:glossaryEntry>visible only with a telescopic; "a bright star with a telescopic companion"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302395922">
	<b:glossaryEntry>impossible or nearly impossible to see; imperceptible by the eye; "the invisible man"; "invisible rays"; "an invisible hinge"; "invisible mending"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302396316"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not easily seen or noticed; "blind stitching"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302396428"
	 b:glossaryEntry="made invisible by means of protective coloring"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302396532">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not accessible to view; "concealed (or hidden) damage"; "in stormy weather the stars are out of sight"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302396714">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or employing wavelengths longer than light but shorter than radio waves; lying outside the visible spectrum at its red end; "infrared radiation"; "infrared photography"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302396944">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[giving no light; "lightless stars `visible'' only to radio antennae"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302397067"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not resulting in vision; "nonvisual stimuli"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302397167">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or employing wavelengths shorter than light but longer than X-rays; lying outside the visible spectrum at its violet end; "ultraviolet radiation"; "an ultraviolet lamp"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302397400"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not seen or perceived; "unseen natural resources"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302397502"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(zoology) producing living young"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302397609"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(zoology) egg-laying"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302397739">
	<b:glossaryEntry>good at incubating eggs especially a fowl kept for that purpose; "a brood hen"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302397891"
	 b:glossaryEntry='physiologically ready to incubate eggs; "a broody hen"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302397998">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(zoology) producing living young from eggs that hatch within the body</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302398145">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(chemistry) evaporating readily at normal temperatures and pressures; "volatile oils"; "volatile solvents"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302398342"
	 b:glossaryEntry="used of substances"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302398459"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not volatilizing readily; "a nonvolatile acid"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302398582">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of your own free will or design; not forced or compelled; "man is a voluntary agent"; "participation was voluntary"; "voluntary manslaughter"; "voluntary generosity in times of disaster"; "voluntary social workers"; "a voluntary confession"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302399058"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not by chance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302399130"
	 b:glossaryEntry='of your own accord; "a free act of the will"; "free choice"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302399240">
	<b:glossaryEntry>done by design; "the insult was intentional"; "willful disobedience"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302399385"
	 b:glossaryEntry='done of your own accord; "a freewill offering"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302399486"
	 b:glossaryEntry='voluntarily assumed or endured; "self-imposed exile"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302399597">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not brought about by coercion or force; "the confession was uncoerced"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302399744">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without payment; "the soup kitchen was prmarily by unpaid helpers"; "a volunteer fire department"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302399909">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not subject to the control of the will; "involuntary manslaughter"; "involuntary servitude"; "an involuntary shudder"; "It (becoming a hero) was involuntary. They sank my boat"- John F.Kennedy</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302400314">
	<b:glossaryEntry>compelled forcibly by an outside agency; "mobs goaded by blind hatred"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302400446"
	 b:glossaryEntry='forced or compelled; "promised to abolish forced labor"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302400554"
	 b:glossaryEntry="without conscious volition"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302400638"
	 b:glossaryEntry="without deliberate volition"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302400720"
	 b:glossaryEntry="in spite of contrary volition"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302400805">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physiology) controlled by individual volition; "voluntary motions"; "voluntary muscles"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302400949">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physiology) controlled by the autonomic nervous system; without conscious control; "involuntary muscles"; "gave an involuntary start"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302401195">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physiology) without volition or conscious control; "the automatic shrinking of the pupils of the eye in strong light"; "a reflex knee jerk"; "sneezing is reflexive"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302401440">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physiology) relating to or controlled by the autonomic nervous system; "autonomic reflexes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302401588">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(physiology) used of involuntary bodily functions; "vegetative functions such as digestion or growth or circulation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302401761"
	 b:glossaryEntry='susceptible to attack; "a vulnerable bridge"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302402114">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not defended or capable of being defended; "an open city"; "open to attack"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302402281">
	<b:glossaryEntry>vulnerable to danger especially of discredit or suspicion; "she found herself in a compromising situation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302402446"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lacking protection or support; "a defenseless child"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302402570">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of flora or fauna) in imminent danger of extinction; "an endangered species"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302402704"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not safe from attack"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302402788"
	 b:glossaryEntry='capable of being penetrated; "penetrable defenses"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302402895">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of flora or fauna) likely in the near future to become endangered; "the spotted owl is a threatened species, not yet an endangered one"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302403088">
	<b:glossaryEntry>subjected to enemy attack or censure; "an official under fire for mismanagement"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302403246">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking protection or a guard; "an unguarded gate"; "his unguarded queen was open to attack"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302403408">
	<b:glossaryEntry>immune to attack; impregnable; "gunners raked the beach from invulnerable positions on the cliffs"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302403763">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having no weak points; "an airtight defense"; "an airtight argument"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302403886">
	<b:glossaryEntry>able to resist the explosive force of bombs and shells; "bombproof shelter"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302404030"
	 b:glossaryEntry="capable of being defended"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302404125"
	 b:glossaryEntry="dug in"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302404188">
	<b:glossaryEntry>able to withstand attack; "an impregnable fortress"; "fortifications that made the frontier inviolable"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302404410">
	<b:glossaryEntry>set so close together as to be invulnerable to penetration; "in tight formation"; "a tight blockade"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302404562">
	<b:glossaryEntry>beyond the reach of criticism or attack or impeachment; "for the first time criticism was directed at a hitherto untouchable target"- Newsweek</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302404762">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or surrounded by walls or walls of a specified kind; sometimes used as a combining term; "the ancient walled city of Jerico"; "thick-walled cells"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302404968">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking walls; "the wall-less ground floor is open to the winds"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302405088">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[desired or wished for or sought; "couldn''t keep her eyes off the wanted toy"; "a wanted criminal"; "a wanted poster"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302405383">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wanted intensely; "the child could no longer resist taking one of the craved cookies"; "it produced the desired effect"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302405565"
	 b:glossaryEntry='wanted by the police; "a hot suspect"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302405652"
	 b:glossaryEntry="greatly desired"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302405751">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being searched for; "the most sought-after item was the silver candelabrum"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302405897">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not wanted; not needed; "tried to give away unwanted kittens"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302406211">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being discarded or renounced or relinquished; "abdicable responsibilites"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302406351">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[thrown away; "wearing someone''s cast-off clothes"; "throwaway children living on the streets"; "salvaged some thrown-away furniture"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302406585"
	 b:glossaryEntry="excluded from a society"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302406675">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(India) not belonging to or having been expelled from a caste and thus having no place or status in society; "the foreigner was a casteless person"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302406889"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not required or requested; "uncalled-for suggestions"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302407001">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not claimed or called for by an owner or assignee; "unclaimed luggage"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302407127"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not desired; "an undesired result"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302407228"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not welcome; "unwelcome publicity"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302407344">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or producing a comfortable and agreeable degree of heat or imparting or maintaining heat; "a warm body"; "a warm room"; "a warm climate"; "a warm coat"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302407643">
	<b:glossaryEntry>moderately warm; "he hates lukewarm coffee"; "tepid bath water"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302407769"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having been warmed up; "a cup of warmed milk"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302407867"
	 b:glossaryEntry='imparting heat; "a warming fire"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302407953">
	<b:glossaryEntry>neither warm or very cold; giving relief from heat; "a cool autumn day"; "a cool room"; "cool summer dresses"; "cool drinks"; "a cool breeze"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302408307"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cooled by air conditioning"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302408395"
	 b:glossaryEntry='cooled by a flow of air; "an air-cooled engine"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302408499"
	 b:glossaryEntry='uncomfortably cool; "a chill wind"; "chilly weather"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302408612">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made or become cool or made cool as specified; often used as a combining form; "put the cooled soup in the refrigerator"; "air-cooled"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302408799">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[providing coolness; "a cooling breeze"; "`caller'' is a Scottish term as in `a caller breeze''"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302408955"
	 b:glossaryEntry="cooled in advance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302409028">
	<b:glossaryEntry>kept cool or designed to be kept cool by means of water especially circulating water; "a water-cooled engine"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302409196">
	<b:glossaryEntry>psychologically warm; friendly and responsive; "a warm greeting"; "a warm personality"; "warm support"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302409421">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing warm and heartfelt friendliness; "gave us a cordial reception"; "a hearty welcome"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302409574">
	<b:glossaryEntry>psychologically cool; unfriendly or unresponsive or showing dislike; "relations were cool and polite"; "a cool reception"; "cool to the idea of higher taxes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302409908">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking warmth of feeling; "a chilly greeting"; "an unfriendly manner"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302410044">
	<b:glossaryEntry>aloof or indifferent; "was unresponsive to her passionate advances"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302410170">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(color) inducing the impression of warmth; used especially of reds and oranges and yellows; "warm reds and yellows and orange"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302410365"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(color) bold and intense; "hot pink"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302410451">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(color) inducing the impression of coolness; used especially of greens and blues and violets; "cool greens and blues and violets"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302410649"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(color) giving no sensation of warmth; "a cold bluish gray"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302410759">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(zoology) having warm blood (in animals whose body temperature is internally regulated)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302410923">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of birds and mammals; having constant and relatively high body temperature</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302411086">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(zoology) having cold blood (in animals whose body temperature is not internally regulated)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302411254">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of animals except birds and mammals; having body temperature that varies with the environment</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302411442">
	<b:glossaryEntry>marked by warmth of feeling like kindness and sympathy and generosity; "gave a warmhearted welcome to the stranger"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302411633"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking in sympathy or feeling"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302411757">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking warmth and generosity of spirit; "a brittle and calculating woman"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302411885">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of being washed without injury; "washable woolens"; "acrylic blankets are both warm and washable"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302412063">
	<b:glossaryEntry>treated so as to be easily or quickly washed and dried and requiring little or no ironing; "a wash-and-wear shirt"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302412248"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not washable"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302412318"
	 b:glossaryEntry='treated with wax; "waxed floors"; "waxed mustache"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302412420"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not waxed; "the unwaxed floor"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302412504">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of the moon) becoming full; "the waxing moon passes from new to full"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302412627"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(of the moon) "after full moon comes the waning moon"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302412733"
	 b:glossaryEntry='becoming greater or larger; "increasing prices"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302413143"
	 b:glossaryEntry="tending to increase velocity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302413245"
	 b:glossaryEntry="marked or produced by accretion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302413335"
	 b:glossaryEntry="growing by accretion"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302413411">
	<b:glossaryEntry>increasing or having the power to increase especially in size or amount or degree; "muscle-building exercises"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302413604">
	<b:glossaryEntry>increasing or growing quickly like a balloon; "ballooning prices caused a panic"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302413741">
	<b:glossaryEntry>increasing in area or volume or bulk or range; "an expanding universe"; "an expanding economy"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302413891">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[increasing suddenly and with uncontrolled rapidity; "the world''s exploding population"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302414033">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having a gradual increase in width; "flared nostrils"; "a skirt flaring from the waist"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302414183">
	<b:glossaryEntry>increasing in size or degree or amount; "her growing popularity"; "growing evidence of a world depression"; "a growing city"; "growing businesses"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302414383">
	<b:glossaryEntry>growing by taking over and incorporating adjacent territories; "the Russian Empire was a typical incorporative state"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302414560"
	 b:glossaryEntry='increasing in length; "the lengthening days of February"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302414674"
	 b:glossaryEntry="making as great as possible"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302414758">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tending or having the power to multiply or increase in number or quantity or degree; "the multiplicative tendency of proportional representation"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302414964">
	<b:glossaryEntry>making the profit as great as possible; "the profit-maximizing price</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302415096"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gradually advancing in extent"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302415183">
	<b:glossaryEntry>increasing in quantity or value; "a cost-raising increase in the basic wage rate"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302415318"
	 b:glossaryEntry='increasing in amount or degree; "rising prices"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302415418"
	 b:glossaryEntry="becoming less or smaller"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302415661"
	 b:glossaryEntry="decreasing in amount or intensity"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302415760"
	 b:glossaryEntry='growing weaker; "declining powers of body and mind"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302415867">
	<b:glossaryEntry>tending to decrease or cause a decrease in value; "a depreciating currency"; "depreciatory effects on prices"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302416065">
	<b:glossaryEntry>causing to decrease in importance or value; "detractive influences on the volume of investment"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302416217">
	<b:glossaryEntry>becoming smaller or less or appearing to do so; "diminishing returns"; "his diminishing respect for her"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302416379"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gradually decreasing until little remains"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302416502"
	 b:glossaryEntry='losing color; "the paling photographs"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302416602"
	 b:glossaryEntry='decreasing in amount or degree; "falling temperature"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302416709">
	<b:glossaryEntry>becoming less in dimension or degree; "the lessening distance between us"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302416838"
	 b:glossaryEntry="music"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302416936"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gradually increasing in tempo"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302417023"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gradually increasing in volume"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302417109"
	 b:glossaryEntry="music"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302417243"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gradually decreasing in tempo and broadening in manner"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302417354"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gradually decreasing in tempo and volume"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302417448"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gradually decreasing in volume"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302417549"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gradually decreasing in tempo"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302417660">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(economics) associated with or tending to cause increases in inflation; "inflationary prices"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302417812">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(economics) associated with or tending to cause decreases in consumer prices or increases in the purchasing power of money; "deflationary measures"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302418018">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[freed of dependence on something especially (for mammals) mother''s milk; "the just-weaned calf bawled for its mother"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302418188">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not weaned; "some children remain unweaned until their second or third birthdays"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302418324">
	<b:glossaryEntry>suitable for wear or able to be worn; "wearable evening clothes"; "a wearable hearing aid"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302418469">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not suitable for wear or able to be worn; "shoes so dilapidated as to be unwearable"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302418628">
	<b:glossaryEntry>considered inappropriate for or unattractive on a particular person; "an unbecoming style"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302418775">
	<b:glossaryEntry>abounding with or resembling weeds; "a weedy path"; "weedy plants that take over a garden"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302418917"
	 b:glossaryEntry='free from weeds; "a weedless garden"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302419026"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having had weeds removed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302419103">
	<b:glossaryEntry>giving pleasure or satisfaction or received with pleasure or freely granted; "a welcome relief"; "a welcome guest"; "made the children feel welcome"; "you are welcome to join us"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302419425"
	 b:glossaryEntry='freely permitted or invited; "invited guests"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302419533"
	 b:glossaryEntry="gladly and cordially received or admitted"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302419632"
	 b:glossaryEntry="conventional response to thanks meaning `freely granted''"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302419749">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not welcome; not giving pleasure or received with pleasure; "unwelcome publicity"; "unwelcome interruptions"; "unwelcome visitors"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302420007">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without being invited; "an unwanted intrusion"; "uninvited guests"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302420150">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[in good health especially after having suffered illness or injury; "appears to be entirely well"; "the wound is nearly well"; "a well man"; "I think I''m well; at least I feel well"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302420489">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[being in good health; "he''s feeling all right again"; "I''m fine; how are you?"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302420630"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having no symptoms of illness or disease"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302420743">
	<b:glossaryEntry>freed from illness or injury; "the patient appears cured"; "the incision is healed"; "appears to be entirely recovered"; "when the recovered patient tries to remember what occurred during his delirium"- Normon Cameron</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302421033"
	 b:glossaryEntry='feeling healthy and free of aches and pains; "I feel good"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302421145">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not in good physical or mental health; "ill from the monotony of his suffering"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302421803"
	 b:glossaryEntry="grievously affected especially by disease"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302421911"
	 b:glossaryEntry="affected by ague"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302421980">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[somewhat ill or prone to illness; "my poor ailing grandmother"; "feeling a bit indisposed today"; "you look a little peaked"; "feeling poorly"; "a sickly child"; "is unwell and can''t come to work"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302422284"
	 b:glossaryEntry="experiencing motion sickness"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302422386"
	 b:glossaryEntry="confined to bed (by illness)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302422510">
	<b:glossaryEntry>suffering from or suggesting a liver disorder or gastric distress</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302422649"
	 b:glossaryEntry="suffering from or prone to bronchitis"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302422743">
	<b:glossaryEntry>afflicted with or or associated with pulmonary tuberculosis; "a consumptive patient"; "a consumptive cough"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302422908">
	<b:glossaryEntry>returning to health after illness or debility; "convalescent children are difficult to keep in bed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302423079"
	 b:glossaryEntry="experiencing delirium"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302423172"
	 b:glossaryEntry="suffering from diabetes"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302423250">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or causing a whirling sensation; liable to falling; "had a dizzy spell"; "a dizzy pinnacle"; "had a headache and felt giddy"; "a giddy precipice"; "feeling woozy from the blow on his head"; "a vertiginous climb up the face of the cliff"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302423575"
	 b:glossaryEntry="suffering from dyspepsia"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302423655">
	<b:glossaryEntry>weak and likely to lose consciousness; "suddenly felt faint from the pain"; "was sick and faint from hunger"; "felt light in the head"; "a swooning fit"; "light-headed with wine"; "light-headed from lack of sleep"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302423954"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having or affected by a fever"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302424049">
	<b:glossaryEntry>experiencing odd bodily sensations; "told the doctor about the funny sensations in her chest"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302424194"
	 b:glossaryEntry="suffering from gout"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302424265"
	 b:glossaryEntry="put out of action (by illness)"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302424364"
	 b:glossaryEntry='ill and usually confined; "laid up with a bad cold"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302424472"
	 b:glossaryEntry="undergoing menstruation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302424563"
	 b:glossaryEntry="feeling nausea; feeling about to vomit"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302424683"
	 b:glossaryEntry="afflicted with scrofula"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302424763"
	 b:glossaryEntry="inclined to sneeze"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302424834"
	 b:glossaryEntry='suffering from spastic paralysis; "a spastic child"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302424939">
	<b:glossaryEntry>constituting or afflicted with or caused by tuberculosis or the tubercle bacillus; "a tubercular child"; "tuberculous patients"; "tubercular meningitis"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302425162"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not healed; "an unhealed wound"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302425248"
	 b:glossaryEntry='mildly physically distressed; "an upset stomach"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302425348">
	<b:glossaryEntry>covered or soaked with a liquid such as water; "a wet bathing suit"; "wet sidewalks"; "wet paint"; "wet weather"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302425924"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wet with dew"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302425997"
	 b:glossaryEntry='sprinkled over; "glistening grass besprent with raindrops"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302426122">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of soil) soft and watery; "the ground was boggy under foot"; "a marshy coastline"; "miry roads"; "wet mucky lowland"; "muddy barnyard"; "quaggy terrain"; "the sloughy edge of the pond"; "swampy bayous"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302426436">
	<b:glossaryEntry>unpleasantly cool and humid; "a clammy handshake"; "clammy weather"; "a dank cellar"; "dank rain forests"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302426601">
	<b:glossaryEntry>slightly wet; "clothes damp with perspiration"; "a moist breeze"; "eyes moist with tears"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302426759">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[wet through and through; thoroughly wet; "stood at the door drenched (or soaked) by the rain"; "a shirt saturated with perspiration"; "his shoes were sopping (or soaking)"; "the speaker''s sodden collar"; "soppy clothes"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302427091">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having liquid falling in drops; "in the dripping fog"; "the scene where he is singing in the rain in dripping clothes"; "there was little shelter under the dripping trees"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302427317"
	 b:glossaryEntry='wet with light rain; "a sad drizzly day"; "a wet drippy day"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302427440">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wet from being plunged into liquid; "a dunked doughnut"; "fell into the pool and came up soused"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302427598">
	<b:glossaryEntry>containing or characterized by a great deal of water vapor; "humid air"; "humid weather"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302427738"
	 b:glossaryEntry='wet with mist; "the misty evening"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302427824">
	<b:glossaryEntry>hot or warm and humid; "muggy weather"; "the steamy tropics"; "sticky weather"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302427972">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being wet with perspiration; "the perspiring runners"; "his sweating face"; "sweaty clothes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302428141">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wet with secreted or exuded moisture such as sweat or blood or tears; "wiped his reeking neck"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302428309">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of weather) wet by periods of rain; "showery weather"; "rainy days"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302428439">
	<b:glossaryEntry>soaked with moisture; "a soggy lawn"; "the flood left waterlogged fields"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302428578">
	<b:glossaryEntry>emitting moisture in the form of vapor or mist; "a steaming kettle"; "steaming towels"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302428719">
	<b:glossaryEntry>moist as with undried perspiration and with clothing sticking to the body; "felt sticky and chilly at the same time"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302428888">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of a glutinous liquid such as paint) not completely dried and slightly sticky to the touch; "tacky varnish"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302429048"
	 b:glossaryEntry="still wet or moist"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302429120">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wet as from washing; sometimes used in combination; "rain-washed"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302429238"
	 b:glossaryEntry='filled with water; "watery soil"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302429323">
	<b:glossaryEntry>free from liquid or moisture; lacking natural or normal moisture or depleted of water; or no longer wet; "dry land"; "dry clothes"; "a dry climate"; "dry splintery boards"; "a dry river bed"; "the paint is dry"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302429799">
	<b:glossaryEntry>dried out by heat or excessive exposure to sunlight; "a vast desert all adust"; "land lying baked in the heat"; "parched soil"; "the earth was scorched and bare"; "sunbaked salt flats"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302430075">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking sufficient water or rainfall; "an arid climate"; "a waterless well; "miles of waterless country to cross"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302430251">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without a trace of moisture; as dry as a weathered bone; "bone-dry leaves are a fire hazard"; "a drier to get the clothes bone dry"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302430454">
	<b:glossaryEntry>thoroughly dried out; "old boxes of desiccated Cuban cigars"; "dried-out boards beginning to split"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302430622">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not still wet; "the ink has dried"; "a face marked with dried tears"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302430742"
	 b:glossaryEntry='depleted of water; "a dried-up water hole"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302430839">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(used especially of vegetation) having lost all moisture; "dried-up grass"; "the desert was edged with sere vegetation"; "shriveled leaves on the unwatered seedlings"; "withered vines"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302431128">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or keeping the feet or shoes dry; "a land bridge over which man and beasts could have crossed dry-shod"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302431293"
	 b:glossaryEntry='lacking rain; "a rainless month"; "rainless skies"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302431398"
	 b:glossaryEntry="somewhat arid"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302431466"
	 b:glossaryEntry='needing moisture; "thirsty fields under a rainless sky"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302431575">
	<b:glossaryEntry>producing or secreting milk; "a wet nurse"; "a wet cow"; "lactating cows"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302431728">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having recently calved and therefore able to give milk; "the cow is fresh"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302431854"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not producing milk; "a dry cow"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302431953"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having no milk; "milkless breasts"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302432042">
	<b:glossaryEntry>supporting or permitting the legal production and sale of alcoholic beverages; "a wet candidate running on a wet platform"; "a wet county"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302432230">
	<b:glossaryEntry>opposed to or prohibiting the production and sale of alcoholic beverages; "the dry vote led by preachers and bootleggers"; "a dry state"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302432416">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(chemistry) combined with water; especially water of crystallization</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302432538">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(chemistry) without water; especially water of crystallization</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302432656"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having wheels; often used in combination"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302432768"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having three wheels; "a three-wheeled bike"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302432871">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having no wheels or having no wheeled vehicles; "dragging a wheelless stoneboat filled with rocks"; "wheelless societies"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302433048">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[of or designating work or workers of relatively high status whose duties call for well-groomed appearance; "the coal miner''s son aspired to a white-collar occupation as a bookkeeper"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302433361">
	<b:glossaryEntry>appropriate for or engaged in office work; "clerical skills"; "a clerical job"; "the clerical staff"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302433516">
	<b:glossaryEntry>engaged in by members of a profession; "professional occupations include medicine and the law and teaching"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302433682">
	<b:glossaryEntry>receiving a salary; "salaried members of the staff as distinct from hourly workers"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302433820">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of or designating work or workers in occupations not requiring well-groomed appearance</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302434036">
	<b:glossaryEntry>employed in industry; "the industrial classes"; "industrial work"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302434158">
	<b:glossaryEntry>doing or requiring physical work; "manual labor"; "manual laborer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302434280">
	<b:glossaryEntry>working for hourly wages rather than fixed (e.g. annual) salaries; "working-class occupations include manual as well as industrial labor"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302434492">
	<b:glossaryEntry>conducive to or characteristic of physical or moral well-being; "wholesome attitude"; "wholesome appearance"; "wholesome food"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302434800"
	 b:glossaryEntry='providing nourishment; "good nourishing food"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302434951">
	<b:glossaryEntry>promoting health; healthful; "a healthy diet"; "clean healthy air"; "plenty of healthy sleep"; "healthy and normal outlets for youthful energy"; "the salubrious mountain air and water"- C.B.Davis; "carrots are good for you"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302435259">
	<b:glossaryEntry>providing abundant nourishment; "a hearty meal"; "good solid food"; "ate a substantial breakfast"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302435444">
	<b:glossaryEntry>favorable to health of mind or body; "not the most salubrious campsite"; "one of the less salubrious suburbs"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302435610">
	<b:glossaryEntry>detrimental to physical or moral well-being; "unwholesome food"; "unwholesome habits like smoking"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302435928"
	 b:glossaryEntry="detrimental to health"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302436034">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking in nutritive value; "the jejune diets of the very poor"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302436166">
	<b:glossaryEntry>suggesting an unhealthy mental state; "morbid interest in death"; "morbid curiosity"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302436303">
	<b:glossaryEntry>causing or able to cause nausea; "a nauseating smell"; "nauseous offal"; "a sickening stench"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302436517">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having great (or a certain) extent from one side to the other; "wide roads"; "a wide necktie"; "wide margins"; "three feet wide"; "a river two miles broad"; "broad shoulders"; "a broad river"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302437091"
	 b:glossaryEntry='broad in the beam; "a beamy cargo ship"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302437182">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[as from an altitude or distance; "a bird''s-eye survey"; "a panoramic view"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302437325">
	<b:glossaryEntry>occurring or extending throughout a city; "citywide bussing"; "a citywide strike"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302437461">
	<b:glossaryEntry>occurring or extending throughout a county; "a countywide war on drugs"; "countywide elections"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302437613">
	<b:glossaryEntry>occurring or extending throughout a country or nation; "the event aroused nationwide interest"; "a countrywide fund-raising campaign"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302437817">
	<b:glossaryEntry>above or covering a wide area; "the covering darkness"; "shadow of the overhanging crag"; "the dark overspreading clouds"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302438023"
	 b:glossaryEntry='extending relatively far inward; "a deep border"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302438122"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resembling a fan"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302438192"
	 b:glossaryEntry='wide from side to side; "a heavy black mark"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302438296">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a specified width; "a yard in width"; "three feet in breadth"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302438434">
	<b:glossaryEntry>spreading over a wide area; "under the spreading chestnut tree"; "the spreading circle of lamplight"; "wide-spreading branches"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302438634">
	<b:glossaryEntry>occurring or extending throughout a state; "the statewide recycling program"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302438766">
	<b:glossaryEntry>extending far; "beyond the misty gray of the rain he saw the stretching hutment"; "wide-spreading plains"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302438948">
	<b:glossaryEntry>taking in or moving over (or as if over) a wide area; often used in combination; "a sweeping glance"; "a wide-sweeping view of the river"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302439140">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(motion pictures) projected on a screen with much greater width than height</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302439276"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not wide; "a narrow bridge"; "a narrow line across the page"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302439551"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of circumstances) tending to constrict freedom"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302439684">
	<b:glossaryEntry>reduced in size as by squeezing together; "his narrowed eyes"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302439800"
	 b:glossaryEntry='very narrow; "a thin line across the page"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302439903"
	 b:glossaryEntry='(archaic) strict and severe; "strait is the gate"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302440005">
	<b:glossaryEntry>becoming gradually narrower; "long tapering fingers"; "trousers with tapered legs"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302440164"
	 b:glossaryEntry='great in degree; "won by a wide margin"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302440272"
	 b:glossaryEntry='more than adequate; "the home team had a comfortable lead"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302440388">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very limited in degree; "won by a narrow margin"; "a narrow escape"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302440526">
	<b:glossaryEntry>just barely adequate or within a lower limit; "a bare majority"; "a marginal victory"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302440676"
	 b:glossaryEntry='easy to handle or use or manage; "a large but wieldy book"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302440800">
	<b:glossaryEntry>difficult to use or handle or manage because of size or weight or shape; "we set about towing the unwieldy structure into the shelter"; "almost dropped the unwieldy parcel"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302441078">
	<b:glossaryEntry>difficult to handle or manage especially because of shape; "an awkward bundle to carry"; "a load of bunglesome paraphernalia"; "clumsy wooden shoes"; "the cello, a rather ungainly instrument for a girl"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302441367">
	<b:glossaryEntry>difficult to handle or use especially because of size or weight; "a cumbersome piece of machinery"; "cumbrous protective clothing"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302441565"
	 b:glossaryEntry='wearing a wig; "the judges all wigged and robed"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302441702"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wearing a wig popular for men in the 17th and 18th centuries"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302441829"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wearing a small hairpiece to cover partial baldness"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302441934"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not wearing a wig"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302442005">
	<b:glossaryEntry>disposed or inclined toward; "a willing participant"; "willing helpers"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302442328"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having given consent; "consenting adults"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302442426"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having made preparations; "prepared to take risks"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302442566">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[(`lief'' is archaic) very willing; "was lief to go"; "glad to help"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302442693"
	 b:glossaryEntry='mentally disposed; "he was ready to believe her"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302442793"
	 b:glossaryEntry='with deliberate intention; "a volitional act"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302442913"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not reluctant"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302442989"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not disposed or inclined toward; "an unwilling assistant"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302443228">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[of especially an attitude; "gave grudging consent"; "grudging acceptance of his opponent''s victory"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302443382">
	<b:glossaryEntry>unwillingness to do something contrary to your custom; "a reluctant smile"; "loath to admit a mistake"; "unwilling to face facts"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302443582">
	<b:glossaryEntry>without deliberate intent; "my heart with unwilled love grew warm"- George Macdonald</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302443737"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having or as if having wings; "the winged feet of Mercury";'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302443994"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(biology) having or resembling wings"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302444113">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having winglike extensions; "alate leaves"; "alate seeds of a maple tree"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302444247">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[formed or shaped like a bat''s wing; "a dress with batwing sleeves"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302444367">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(especially of certain insects) having very short or rudimentary wings</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302444512">
	<b:glossaryEntry>capable of or engaged in flight; "the bat is a flying animal"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302444626"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of birds) especially having the flight feathers"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302444729"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(heraldry) with wings extended in a flying position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302444837"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resembling a wing in shape or position"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302444930"
	 b:glossaryEntry="lacking wings"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302445034"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of insects) without wings"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302445125"
	 b:glossaryEntry="incapable of flying"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302445201">
	<b:glossaryEntry>equipped with wire or wires especially for electric or telephone service; "a well-wired house"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302445401">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having hidden electronic eavesdropping devices; "wired hotel rooms"; "even the car is bugged"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302445547">
	<b:glossaryEntry>wired together to an alarm system; "all the window alarms are connected"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302445675"
	 b:glossaryEntry='stiffened or reinforced with wire; "stiffened brassieres"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302445788"
	 b:glossaryEntry='having no wires; "a wireless security system"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302445888">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or prompted by wisdom or discernment; "a wise leader"; "a wise and perceptive comment"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302446176"
	 b:glossaryEntry="infinitely wise"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302446262">
	<b:glossaryEntry>acutely insightful and wise; "much too perspicacious to be taken in by such a spurious argument"; "observant and thoughtful, he was given to asking sagacious questions"; "a source of valuable insights and sapient advice to educators"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302446577"
	 b:glossaryEntry="resembling an owl; solemn and wise in appearance"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302446678"
	 b:glossaryEntry="having wisdom that comes with age and experience"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302446777">
	<b:glossaryEntry>devoid of good sense or judgment; "foolish remarks"; "a foolish decision"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302447138">
	<b:glossaryEntry>so devoid of wisdom or good sense as to be laughable; "the absurd excuse that the dog ate his homework"; "ask a nonsensical question and get a nonsensical answer"; "a contribution so small as to be derisory"; "it is ludicrous to call a cottage a mansion"; "a preposterous attempt to turn back the pages of history"; "her conceited assumption of universal interest in her rather dull children was ridiculous"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302447663"
	 b:glossaryEntry="complacently or inanely foolish"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302447787">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(informal terms) "gave me a cockamamie reason for not going"; "wore a goofy hat"; "a silly idea"; "some wacky plan for selling more books"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302448058">
	<b:glossaryEntry>absurd or silly because unlikely; "fond hopes of becoming President"; "fond fancies"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302448193">
	<b:glossaryEntry>very foolish; "harebrained ideas"; "took insane risks behind the wheel"; "a completely mad scheme to build a bridge between two mountains"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302448404">
	<b:glossaryEntry>poorly conceived or thought out; "an ill-conceived plan to take over the company"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302448557">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[lacking sense or discretion; "his rattlebrained crackpot ideas"; "how rattlepated I am! I''ve forgotten what I came for"- Glenway Westcott]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302448794">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing or resulting from lack of judgment or wisdom; "an unwise investor is soon impoverished"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302448942"
	 b:glossaryEntry="affirmed as true by a witness"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302449063">
	<b:glossaryEntry>bearing the signature of a witness attesting to something''s legality</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302449203"
	 b:glossaryEntry="witnessed at first hand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302449288"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not affirmed as true by a witness"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302449415">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not bearing the signature of a witness; "an unattested legal document"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302449542"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not witnessed at first hand"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302449626">
	<b:glossaryEntry>covered with growing trees and bushes etc; "wooded land"; "a heavily wooded tract"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302449977">
	<b:glossaryEntry>abounding in trees; "an arboreous landscape"; "violets in woodsy shady spots"; "a woody area near the highway"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302450174">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[covered with or consisting of bushes or thickets; "brushy undergrowth"; "`bosky'' is a literary term"; "a bosky park leading to a modest yet majestic plaza"- Jack Beatty]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302450403"
	 b:glossaryEntry="covered with brambles and ferns and other undergrowth"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302450518">
	<b:glossaryEntry>covered with forest; "efforts to protect forested lands of the northwest"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302450646"
	 b:glossaryEntry="overgrown with tropical vegetation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302450733"
	 b:glossaryEntry="abounding in usually unwanted vegetation"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302450829"
	 b:glossaryEntry='abounding in rushes; "a rushy marsh"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302450917">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sparsely covered with stunted trees or vegetation and underbrush; "open scrubby woods"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302451068">
	<b:glossaryEntry>relating to or characteristic of wooded regions; "a shady sylvan glade"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302451201">
	<b:glossaryEntry>covered with growing timber; "thickly timbered ridges clothed with loblolly pine and holly"; "hills timbered up to their summits"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302451385">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characteristic or suggestive of woods; "a fresh woodsy fragrance"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302451503"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not wooded"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302451633"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not covered with forest; "unforested lands"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302451733"
	 b:glossaryEntry='without trees; "an untimbered area"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302451825">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made of or containing or resembling wood; "woody plants"; "perennial herbs with woody stems"; "a woody taste"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302452184"
	 b:glossaryEntry="made of wood of the ash tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302452264"
	 b:glossaryEntry="made of wood of the aspen tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302452346"
	 b:glossaryEntry='made of bamboo; "bamboo furniture"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302452433"
	 b:glossaryEntry="consisting of or made of wood of the beech tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302452534"
	 b:glossaryEntry="consisting of or made of wood of the birch tree"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302452652"
	 b:glossaryEntry='consisting of or made of cedar; "carved cedarn doors"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302452758"
	 b:glossaryEntry='made of fir or pine; "a plain deal table"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302452850">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(botany) consisting of or containing lignin or xylem; "ligneous (or woody) tissue"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302452987">
	<b:glossaryEntry>consisting of or made of wood of the oak tree; "a solid oak table"; "the old oaken bucket"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302453135">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of a plant; having a woody base that does not die down each year</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302453259">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made or consisting of (entirely or in part) or employing wood; "a wooden box"; "an ancient cart with wooden wheels"; "wood houses"; "a wood fire"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302453467"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not woody; not consisting of or resembling wood"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302453605"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(botany) characteristic of a nonwoody herb or plant part"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302453718"
	 b:glossaryEntry="like a pulp; not having stiffness"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302453803">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characteristic of or devoted to the temporal world; "worldly goods and advancement"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302454066">
	<b:glossaryEntry>concerned with worldly necessities of life (especially money); "he wrote the book primarily for economic reasons"; "gave up the large house for economic reasons"; "in economic terms they are very privileged"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302454328">
	<b:glossaryEntry>concerned with worldly rather than spiritual interests; "material possessions"; "material wealth"; "material comforts"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302454501"
	 b:glossaryEntry="marked by materialism"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302454611">
	<b:glossaryEntry>concerned with the world or worldly matters; "mundane affairs"; "he developed an immense terrestrial practicality"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302454793"
	 b:glossaryEntry='of the material world; "temporal possessions of the church"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302454907">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not concerned with the temporal world or swayed by mundane considerations; "was unworldly and did not greatly miss worldly rewards"- Sheldon Cheney</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302455218">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characterized by ascetic solitude; "the eremitic element in the life of a religious colony"; "his hermitic existence"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302455414">
	<b:glossaryEntry>of communal life sequestered from the world under religious vows</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302455584">
	<b:glossaryEntry>concerned with or affecting the spirit or soul; "a spiritual approach to life"; "spiritual fulfillment"; "spiritual values"; "unearthly love"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302455793"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not mercenary; not influenced by financial gains"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302455899">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made or constructed by interlacing threads or strips of material or other elements into a whole; "woven fabrics"; "woven baskets"; "the incidents woven into the story"; "folk songs woven into a symphony"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302456154">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not woven; "tapa cloth is an unwoven fabric made by pounding bark into a thin sheet"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302456328">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made by combining fibers with a binder using heat and pressure; "felt is a felted cloth"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302456469">
	<b:glossaryEntry>made by intertwining threads in a series of connected loops rather than by weaving; "knitted garments"; "a hand-knitted sweater"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302456651">
	<b:glossaryEntry>characteristic of or imbued with the attitude of a philosopher or based on philosophy; "that breadth of outlook that distinguishes the philosophic mind"; "their differences were philosophical"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302456935">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[concerned with or suggestive of ideas; "an ideological argument"; "ideological application of a theory"; "the drama''s symbolism was very ideological"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302457154"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not philosophical"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302457251">
	<b:glossaryEntry>affected by wear; damaged by long use; "worn threads on the screw"; "a worn suit"; "the worn pockets on the jacket"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302457831">
	<b:glossaryEntry>at an advanced stage of erosion (pronounced as one syllable); "aged rocks"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302457956"
	 b:glossaryEntry="worn by rubbing or friction"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302458038"
	 b:glossaryEntry='damaged especially by hard usage; "his battered old hat"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302458149">
	<b:glossaryEntry>worn and broken down by hard use; "a creaky shack"; "a decrepit bus...its seats held together with friction tape"; "a flea-bitten sofa"; "a run-down neighborhood"; "a woebegone old shack"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302458437">
	<b:glossaryEntry>worn or shabby from overuse or (of pages) from having corners turned down; "a somewhat dog-eared duke...a bit run down"- Clifton Fadiman "an old book with dog-eared pages"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302458672"
	 b:glossaryEntry="worn away as by water or ice or wind"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302458771"
	 b:glossaryEntry='worn away or tattered along the edges; "frayed cuffs"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302458877">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having many worn or threadbare spots in the nap; "a mangy carpet"; "a mangy old fur coat"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302459027">
	<b:glossaryEntry>worn or eaten away by or as if by moths; "moth-eaten blankets"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302459146"
	 b:glossaryEntry='worn out; "a played out deck of cards"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302459241">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[being or dressed in clothes that are worn or torn; "clothes as ragged as a scarecrow''s"; "a ragged tramp"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302459399">
	<b:glossaryEntry>worn until no longer useful; "battered trumpets and raddled radios"; "worn-out shoes with flapping soles"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302459569">
	<b:glossaryEntry>showing signs of wear and tear; "a ratty old overcoat"; "shabby furniture"; "an old house with dirty windows and tatty curtains"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302459779">
	<b:glossaryEntry>shabby and untidy; "a surge of ragged scruffy children"; "he was soiled and seedy and fragrant with gin"- Mark Twain</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302459957">
	<b:glossaryEntry>worn or faded from being on display in a store; "shopworn merchandise at half price"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302460109">
	<b:glossaryEntry>worn to shreds; or wearing torn or ragged clothing; "a man in a tattered shirt"; "the tattered flag"; "tied up in tattered brown paper"; "a tattered barefoot boy";</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302460328">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having the nap worn away so that the threads show through; "threadbare rugs"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302460461">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of pages) worn or soiled by thumb and fingers by frequent handling or turning; "well-thumbed pages of the dictionary"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302460633"
	 b:glossaryEntry="eaten (or as if eaten) by worms"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302460743"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of rocks especially) worn smooth by the action of water"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302460855">
	<b:glossaryEntry>worn by exposure to the weather; "a house of weathered shingles"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302461006"
	 b:glossaryEntry="showing signs of much wear or use"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302461095"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not affected by wear"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302461222"
	 b:glossaryEntry='unaffected by use or exposure; "it looks like new"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302461322"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not worn away by exposure"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302461416">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having worth or merit or value; being honorable or admirable; "a worthy fellow"; "no student deemed worthy, and chosen for admission, would be kept out for lack of funds"- Nathan Pusey; "worthy of acclaim"; "orthy of consideration"; "a worthy cause"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302462042">
	<b:glossaryEntry>worthy of high praise; "applaudable efforts to save the environment"; "a commendable sense of purpose"; "laudable motives of improving housing conditions"; "a significant and praiseworthy increase in computer intelligence"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302462362">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[worthy of often limited commendation; "the student''s effort on the essay--though not outstanding--was creditable"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302462532">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having sufficient worth; "an idea worth considering"; "a cause deserving or meriting support"; "the deserving poor" (often used ironically)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302462755"
	 b:glossaryEntry='worthy of imitation; "exemplary behavior"; "model citizens"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302462881">
	<b:glossaryEntry>used as a title of respect; "my honorable colleague"; "our worthy commanding officer"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302463035"
	 b:glossaryEntry='worthy of honor; "an honored name"; "our honored dead"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302463154">
	<b:glossaryEntry>deserving reward or praise; "a lifetime of meritorious service"; "meritorious conduct"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302463310"
	 b:glossaryEntry='worthy of notice; "a noteworthy advance in cancer research"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302463436">
	<b:glossaryEntry>suitable for or worthy of quotation; "a quotable slogan"; "his remarks are not quotable in mixed company"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302463596">
	<b:glossaryEntry xml:space='preserve'><![CDATA[worthy of respect or dedication; "saw motherhood as woman''s sacred calling"]]></b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302463724">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having worth or merit or value; "a valuable friend"; "a good and worthful man"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302463868">
	<b:glossaryEntry>sufficiently valuable to justify the investment of time or interest; "a worthwhile book"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302464013">
	<b:glossaryEntry>lacking in value or merit; "dispel a student whose conduct is deemed unworthy"; "unworthy of forgiveness"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302464317"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not deserving; "the undeserving poor"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302464449">
	<b:glossaryEntry>not merited; "unmerited treatment of a potentially fine subject"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302464569"
	 b:glossaryEntry='without merit; "protect...from unmeritorious criticism"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302464684"
	 b:glossaryEntry="being injured unjustly or outrageously"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302464848"
	 b:glossaryEntry='subjected to an injustice; "the aggrieved party"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302464962"
	 b:glossaryEntry="wrongfully subjugated and oppressed"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302465080"
	 b:glossaryEntry="treated dishonorably"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302465165"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not unjustly or outrageously wronged or offended"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302465305"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not treated unjustly or offensively"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302465396">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being deficient in moisture; "deserts provide xeric environments"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302465549">
	<b:glossaryEntry>adapted to a xeric (or dry) environment; "cacti are xerophytic plants"; "xerophytic adaptations"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302465702">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or characterized by excessive moisture; "a hydric habitat"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302465874">
	<b:glossaryEntry>growing wholly or partially in water; "water lilies are hydrophytic"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302466000">
	<b:glossaryEntry>requiring an abundance of moisture; "some tropical ferns are hygrophytic"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302466131">
	<b:glossaryEntry>having or characterized by moderate or a well-balanced supply of moisture; "mesic habitats"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302466310">
	<b:glossaryEntry>being or growing in or adapted to a moderately moist environment; "mesophytic habitats"; "mesophytic plants"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302466475">
	<b:glossaryEntry>associated with or divided into zones; "a zonal pattern of cell structure"; "zonal division"</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302466637"
	 b:glossaryEntry="of the nature of a zone"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302466713"
	 b:glossaryEntry='not divided into zones; "azonal heating"'/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302466824"
	 b:glossaryEntry="not restricted to any particular zone or region"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302466924"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of mosses) having the archegonia at the top of the stem"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302467038"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of mosses) having the archegonia on short lateral branches"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302467157"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of limbs and feet) adapted for running"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302467252"
	 b:glossaryEntry="(of limbs and feet) adapted for digging"/>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302467347">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(ichthyology) possessing a symmetrical tail that extends beyond the end of the vertebral column (as in most bony fishes)</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302467524">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(ichthyology) possessing a tail with the upper lobe larger than the lower and with the vertebral column prolonged into the upper lobe</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302467716">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of the feet of some animals) having the digits connected by a thin fold of skin</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302467867">
	<b:glossaryEntry>(of the feet of water birds) having three toes connected by a thin fold of skin</b:glossaryEntry>
</rdf:Description>
<rdf:Description rdf:about="&a;302468000">
